window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"must-know.html","t":"Must Know Quick Reference","secs":[{"s":"Must Know Quick Reference","a":"","k":["Exam Prep","Everything the instructor explicitly said would be tested or heavily hinted. A ★ means it was said out loud."]},{"s":"Week 1▾","a":"week-1","k":["Week 1▾","Topic Name","★ A short, quick-reference bullet (about 20 words).","An unstarred bullet keeps its attribution: she hinted this would show up.","Not Tested","Record every \"you don't need to memorize this\" the moment it's said."]}]},{"f":"week1-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 1","Part 1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","Part 2A — Acute Coronary Syndrome","Part 2B — Myocardial Infarction","Part 3 — Acute MI Pharmacology","Part 4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 1: the four topic pages that follow, in the order they were taught. Topic 1: myocarditis, pericarditis and cardiac tamponade (Part 1). Topic 2: acute coronary syndrome …"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade▾","a":"part-1-myocarditis-pericarditis-cardiac-tamponade","k":["Part 1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade▾","Topic 1, the first lecture of NUR 406: myocarditis, then the pericardium, how inflammation leads to pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade, the bedside signs, pulsus paradoxus, and treatment.","Myocarditis","Think back to normal A&P: the myocardium is the muscle layer of the heart, the muscle cells. Myocarditis is an inflammatory disease of the myocardium.","Range of severity — A wide variety of symptoms: it can be a pretty mild disorder with little to no discomfort, all the way up to heart failure and a lethal condition.","What happens to the cells — The inflammation comes with degeneration and necrosis of the cardiac myocytes. The inflammation causes direct injury to the myocytes, and over time it can create fibrosis and scarring that lead to structural chan…","Conduction — We often see a disruption in conduction through the heart. The tissue changes, inflammatory cytokines and T cell activation in the myocardium can result in abnormal electrical conduction.","Cause — Typically viruses. In the United States and Europe, viruses are the leading cause of myocarditis. The Coxsackie virus A and B are the majority of cases.","Treatment — Depends on how severe the symptoms are. Treated by giving antivirals; anti-inflammatories can also be used.","The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid","The pericardium is a sac-like membrane that surrounds the heart and the roots of the major blood vessels, and it helps the heart function properly. Between its inner and outer layers is the pericardial space.","Normal — A small amount, about 15 to 50 mL. — Acts like a lubricant between the inner and outer layers, for normal function of the heart.","Pericardial effusion — When the pericardium is inflamed, fluid accumulates in excess of what is normal. Effusion is that excess fluid accumulation. — Can compress the heart chambers and limit cardiac filling.","Cardiac tamponade — About 200 mL. — The fluid compresses the heart. The chambers are restricted and can't stretch and fill with blood as they normally would.","Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency. The fluid has to be removed quickly, because the heart chambers can't stretch and fill with blood.","Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade","The lecture describes what acute pericarditis looks like, then what changes once it progresses to cardiac tamponade and cardiac output falls.","Acute pericarditis","Chest pain: a sharp pain that worsens with deep breathing.","Fever, because of the inflammatory process.","Dyspnea (the lecture glosses it as \"some painful breathing\").","Pericardial friction rub on auscultation: a scratching sound, almost like rubbing on a balloon.","Muffled heart sounds, because you are listening to the heart pump through a layer of fluid.","ECG changes: elevations and also depressions.","Pulsus paradoxus is also named as a sign of acute pericarditis.","Cardiac tamponade","Signs of decreased cardiac output: hypotension, and the usual signs of a patient who is not being perfused appropriately, such as confusion and dizziness.","Jugular vein distension, from blood backing up in the system because of the decreased cardiac output.","Muffled heart sounds, from listening through a layer of fluid.","Pulsus paradoxus occurs in about 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","Pulsus Paradoxus","What it is — A decrease in the systolic blood pressure when the patient breathes in (with inspiration).","Why it's a \"paradox\" — Typically your blood pressure should increase a little bit when you breathe in. Seeing a drop instead is the paradox.","What it indicates — The heart is not pumping properly, and something is impacting cardiac filling.","How you see it — Most clearly with an arterial line (an IV catheter in an artery), which gives real-time blood pressure: you can watch the pressure change as the patient breathes in.","How often — About 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","The recording garbles the exact size of the systolic drop (\"a decrease in the systolic blood pressure of more and more\"), so no number is given here. If the slide shows a value, it can be added.","Pericarditis on the ECG","The ECG in pericarditis shows elements of ST depression as well as ST elevation. In some cases it looks similar to a patient having an MI, but the clinical symptoms will be different.","Treating Pericarditis","NSAIDs (anti-inflammatories) — Anti-inflammatories are used. Aspirin may be seen occasionally.","Corticosteroids — \"Give or take\": sometimes they work really well, sometimes not so much.","Colchicine — Typically a gout medication, but recently found to work well for pericarditis that is not responding to NSAIDs.","Pericardiocentesis — For cardiac tamponade caused by the pericarditis. A needle is put directly into the pericardium and the fluid is drawn out. Because tamponade is a medical emergency, this happens quickly."]},{"s":"Part 2A — Acute Coronary Syndrome▾","a":"part-2a-acute-coronary-syndrome","k":["Part 2A — Acute Coronary Syndrome▾","Topic 2, first half: what ACS is, the spectrum from Prinzmetal's angina to STEMI, what makes plaque unstable and why it ruptures, unstable angina, and how ACS differs from stable angina.","What ACS Is","Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)","An umbrella term for situations in which the blood supply to the heart muscle is suddenly blocked or compromised.","It is certainly a medical emergency.","The blockage can come and go, or it can be sudden and complete. Either way, cardiac tissue is dying.","ACS covers three conditions: unstable angina, non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), and ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).","The three have a similar pathophysiology, so the signs and symptoms can look similar. The ECG is what helps determine the next step: send the patient to the cath lab for reperfusion, or treat medically?","\"Time is tissue.\" The instructor says it several times: treat the blockage as quickly as possible to save as much tissue as possible.","The ACS Spectrum","The slide shows a progression from a normal vessel to complete occlusion. Stable angina has been covered before; ACS is the part of the picture where stable angina has become unstable.","Asymptomatic plaque — Some buildup of atherosclerotic plaque. — Nothing. They don't know it's there unless we go looking for it.","Stable angina — Atherosclerotic plaque buildup. — Signs and symptoms with exertion (strenuous activity).","Unstable angina — The clot is able to stay really small, and blood supply is still somewhat maintained. — Any new or changing chest pain.","NSTEMI — A larger buildup; blood flow is even more decreased. Some blood still gets through, but not enough. — A myocardial infarction without a change in the ST segment on the ECG.","STEMI — The vessel is completely and totally occluded by the clot. — A myocardial infarction with elevation of the ST segment on the ECG.","On the other side of the spectrum slide sits Prinzmetal's variant angina, which is a vasospasm of the coronary artery rather than plaque buildup (next section).","Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina","Mechanism — The coronary artery vasospasms. It is not necessarily related to atherosclerotic plaque buildup; the spasm reduces oxygenated blood flow to the cardiac tissue. The spasms can increase a person's risk for myocardial ischemia.","Cause — A lot of times, damage to the endothelium of the coronary vessel. The spasms narrow the artery and decrease blood flow.","Plaque — These patients may or may not have atherosclerotic plaque.","When it happens — Oftentimes at rest: sleeping, sitting around, minimal activity. These are not the people who are overexerting themselves.","ECG — May show ST elevation. Monitor closely to see whether treatment is working and the ECG changes return to normal.","Treatment — Oftentimes nitrates, because they relax the spasm.","Outlook — Usually benign, but it can cause some serious dysrhythmias to be on the lookout for.","What Makes Plaque Unstable","Usually, when plaque becomes unstable, people develop ACS. These are people who already have atherosclerotic plaque in their coronary vessels.","Stable plaque","A pretty thick fibrous cap on top of the fat, which contributes to its stability.","Unstable plaque","The lipid core (the fat inside the vessel) gets bigger.","As the core grows, the fibrous cap gets thinner.","There is a component of inflammation; C-reactive protein (CRP) is a marker of inflammation in the body.","Smooth muscle cells proliferate into the intima (the lecture describes the intima as the middle layer of the blood vessel). These cells create the fibrous cap and bring in inflammatory components, increasing the risk of plaque rupture.","Assessing Unstable Plaque","A study that puts dye into the vessel shows how much narrowing is happening, so we can roughly estimate whether the lipid core is larger or smaller.","CRP levels show how much inflammation is going on.","Why Plaque Ruptures","Rupture risk is higher with unstable plaque: a large lipid core, a thin fibrous cap, and ongoing inflammation. The slide walks through the theory step by step.","1. Trigger — Psychological stress, exercise, or circadian rhythms stimulate the sympathetic nervous system.","2. Sympathetic response — Epinephrine and norepinephrine are released: blood pressure and heart rate rise, and the heart works harder with stronger contractions.","3. Force on the vessels — Pushing out more blood with stronger contractions puts more force on the vessels, including the coronary vessels themselves.","4. Rupture — That force can lead to rupture of the plaque.","5. Platelets — Platelets come to the site and adhere to the ruptured plaque. They release substances that attract more platelets and can contribute to vasospasm.","6. Thrombus — A thrombus forms in that same area.","Unstable Angina","What counts as unstable angina","Chest pain occurring for the first time. Consider it unstable immediately, until proven otherwise; it is a medical emergency because no pattern or history has been established.","In a patient who normally has stable angina, pain that is more severe than normal: for example, they have taken their first nitro and five minutes later the pain hasn't stopped or is getting worse.","Pain that is different: usually in the chest, but this time down one arm, down both arms, or radiating into the jaw.","It may happen at rest; the person may be awakened in the middle of the night.","What's happening in the vessel","The plaque ruptures and a thrombus forms. That rupture and clot formation is the real worry.","The size of the clot and of the blockage determine how much blood gets past to perfuse the tissue on the other side. A 30% occluded artery may still allow some flow around a clot; a 90% blockage leaves little room.","If there is no infarction, the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved, and some tissue was still perfused.","The ECG changes and then goes back to normal, and we typically don't see troponin changes.","Unstable angina is a medical emergency. Teach patients to recognize the signs, recognize it as an emergency, and call 911 right now. They should most definitely not drive themselves to the ER.","Stable Angina vs ACS","Teach patients the difference so they can identify it and seek care. The big difference is the pain: its severity and its duration.","Stable angina","Pain should last about 5 minutes, and be gone by maybe about 15 minutes.","May feel like a little bit of chest tightness.","Flares up with strenuous activity.","ACS","Chest pain lasts a lot longer.","No relief with nitroglycerin.","More severe: the sensation of an elephant sitting on your chest.","Other symptoms: diaphoresis, shortness of air, nausea and vomiting.","An impending sense of doom: they know something is not right but can't pinpoint it. Take them very, very seriously.","Signs in Men and Women","Research over roughly the last 50 years shows different signs and symptoms of ACS between men and women. These symptoms aren't limited to one or the other.","Typically associated with men","Chest pain.","Tingling or discomfort in the back, arms, neck or jaw.","Other symptoms (often reported by women)","Dizziness, nausea, vomiting, tiredness.","Heartburn: a lot of women with ACS say they took some Tums that morning because they thought it was heartburn.","Some of these can feel similar to stable angina. The particular concern is when the experience changes or gets worse."]},{"s":"Part 2B — Myocardial Infarction▾","a":"part-2b-myocardial-infarction","k":["Part 2B — Myocardial Infarction▾","Topic 2, second half: the signs of MI, what an acute MI is, what happens to heart tissue over time, the zones of damage, STEMI vs NSTEMI, and the LAD.","Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction","What the lecture lists","Diaphoresis and dyspnea.","Extreme anxiety; restless, can't sit still, moving around.","Impending sense of doom (\"I know something's wrong\") without being able to pinpoint it.","Levine sign: the fist to the chest, the \"Hollywood\" heart attack sign.","Pallor and weak pulses, from perfusion issues because the heart isn't working right.","Retrosternal (behind the sternum) crushing chest pain that can radiate to several parts of the body; the elephant on the chest.","Acute Myocardial Infarction","What an acute MI is","Ruptured plaque plus thrombus that severely reduces or completely blocks blood flow to the heart muscle.","Think of ACS as a continuum; MI is at the end of that continuum.","It is ACS with prolonged ischemia that results in the death of myocardial tissue: an irreversible ischemic necrosis. These cells are never going to function properly again.","Infarction happens either because blood flow disruption goes on over a very long period of time, or because of a complete and total blockage.","How much tissue is lost depends on: where the infarction is (smaller or larger vessel, left or right side of the heart), how long blood supply has been affected, and how complete the blockage is. Partial flow over a few days can damage tiss…","MIs are classified by ECG findings: STEMI and NSTEMI.","ECG — Changes, then eventually goes back to normal. — Changes that stay; a repeat ECG won't go back to normal.","Troponin — Typically no change in cardiac enzymes. — Increases. Serial troponins trend up during the infarction, then trend down after the occlusion has been rectified.","Tissue — No infarction: the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved. — Death of myocardial tissue, which is irreversible.","Ischemia, Infarction & ATP","Ischemia","When the oxygen supply does not meet the heart's metabolic demands.","Infarction","Tissue death that results from that ischemia.","Why ATP Matters","ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a molecule that stores and transfers energy in cells; it is the main energy source for cellular function.","Myocardial cells cannot store ATP. They must be continuously fed ATP, so they need a continuous supply of oxygen to make it.","When oxygen goes down, ATP supply goes down, but the heart can't stop beating while ATP isn't being produced.","What Happens to Heart Tissue Over Time","Acute ischemia — ATP depletes instantly.","1 to 2 minutes — Decreased ability of the heart to contract.","30 minutes up to 4 hours — Irreversible injury to cardiac tissue. By 4 hours, necrosis has begun and the tissue begins to die.","1 to 2 weeks — The necrotic tissue is cleared away; the myocardium is very weak and susceptible to rupture.","About 6 weeks — The necrotic tissue has been replaced by tough scar tissue.","Reperfusion: as quickly as possible, at least within 4 hours. Most guidelines state reperfusion therapy should be done within four hours. Within 30 minutes would be ideal, since irreversible injury can occur that quickly.","Scar tissue is thickened and tougher, so electrical impulses have a harder time traveling through it. That is why ECGs after an MI can show permanent changes.","Zones of Damage","Infarction — Necrosis; dead cells, where the MI happened. — No. The goal is to stop it from increasing.","Injury — Not dead yet, but the tissue has suffered injury. — Some recovery is still possible; it can be perfused and restored to be viable.","Ischemia — The outer zone. — Full recovery is possible if it is perfused again.","The goal is to not extend the size of the infarct: increase oxygen and decrease the demand on the heart. The instructor compares this with an ischemic stroke: the same concept of tissue dying from lack of oxygen, where time is of the essenc…","Three Factors That Decide the Extent of Damage","The location or level of the occlusion in the coronary artery.","The length of time it has been occluded.","The availability of collateral circulation. In people with atherosclerotic plaque the heart can start to develop collateral circulation. The lecture calls it \"typically a bad thing,\" but here it creates ways to bypass the blockage and still…","STEMI vs NSTEMI","The main difference is the ST segment on the ECG.","ST segment — Elevated. — Normal, or a slight ST depression.","QRS complex — Usually a wide QRS that develops over hours. — Normal.","T wave — Peaked, then over time less peaked, then inverted. — Inverted the entire time.","Troponin — Elevated; still elevated after 2 hours, with a sharper increase than in NSTEMI. — Elevated.","Infarct size — Typically larger; more blockage, more tissue dying. — Smaller.","Outcomes — Worse. — Better than STEMI.","The \"tombstone.\" Nurses describe the ST elevation of a STEMI as looking like a tombstone, because the prognosis is not good if it isn't treated very quickly. Seeing it on an ECG should throw up red flags: investigate, and do it quickly.","On the ECG images, an NSTEMI can show ST depression or T wave inversion; a STEMI shows ST elevation.","The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"","Coronary blood supply named in the lecture","The right coronary artery.","The left main artery, which branches into the left circumflex artery and the left anterior descending (LAD) artery.","The LAD supplies almost the entire front side of the heart, much of the sidewall, and most of the left ventricle.","The LAD is the vessel most commonly involved in MI, and one of the most damaging. It is a really large vessel feeding most of the left ventricle, so a blockage there is particularly detrimental and often very lethal if not treated very quic…"]},{"s":"Part 3 — Acute MI Pharmacology▾","a":"part-3-acute-mi-pharmacology","k":["Part 3 — Acute MI Pharmacology▾","Topic 3, the second part of the same ACS PowerPoint: the initial drugs, fibrinolytic therapy with alteplase, nitroglycerin safety, reperfusion injury, and the key takeaways. Diagnostics and surgical reperfusion are covered in med-surg, not …","Initial Drug Treatment","The lecture goes through these drugs twice, once briefly and once as a recap with more detail. Both passes are combined here.","Oxygen — Increases oxygen delivery to the ischemic myocardium. — New research shows that if the patient is not hypoxic, oxygen may not have that benefit. Whether it is ordered depends on the provider.","Morphine — Decreases pain. Some research suggests it dilates blood vessels to help blood flow, and there is some thought that it helps preserve ischemic tissue. — Side effects: respiratory depression and hypotension, so check vital signs an…","Aspirin — Makes platelets less sticky and suppresses platelet aggregation, so the thrombus is less likely to form or get bigger. — Routinely used. Have the patient chew the first dose to speed absorption. Research shows aspirin decreases mo…","Nitroglycerin — Dilates the veins to decrease preload; reduces preload and afterload, which helps limit infarct size. — It does not reduce mortality. Given sublingually, or IV if the patient can maintain their blood pressure.","Beta blockers — Reduce heart rate and contractility (and blood pressure), which reduces overall oxygen demand. This can reduce pain, reduce infarct size and help with mortality. — Only if there are no contraindications.","Thrombolytic agent — The biggest line of treatment. — Only if the patient is eligible, with no contraindications; for example, not after a recent hemorrhagic stroke. Ideally given within about 4 to 6 hours of the chest pain starting. These …","Take vital signs before giving any of these. Note heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory effort before administration. These medications can push someone over the edge if they aren't stable to begin with.","Which Drugs Affect Mortality","Aspirin — Decreases mortality in STEMI.","Beta blockers — Can help with mortality.","Nitroglycerin — Does not reduce mortality (it limits infarct size).","Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)","When it's used","Often used in a STEMI.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) is the first choice for treating a STEMI. If a cath lab is not available, fibrinolytic therapy is effective.","These are the \"clot busters.\"","Alteplase (tPA)","The prototype and the drug of choice; it is the most effective.","Dissolves the clot by converting plasminogen into plasmin; plasmin is what actually dissolves the clot.","Works best when given within 30 to 70 minutes of the onset of symptoms (when the chest pain started).","Drugs Given Alongside","In a STEMI where fibrinolytic therapy is anticipated, clopidogrel is also given at presentation, and heparin might be started.","Anyone who has had fibrinolytic therapy is given heparin and antiplatelet therapy (like clopidogrel) to help prevent new clots from forming. Alteplase dissolves the current clot; the others keep new ones from forming.","The main adverse effect is bleeding. We are dissolving a clot, so watch for bleeding.","The lecture gives two time windows: thrombolytics \"ideally\" within about 4 to 6 hours of chest pain starting, and alteplase working best within 30 to 70 minutes of symptom onset. Both are recorded here exactly as stated.","Nitroglycerin Safety","Sildenafil (Viagra) — Do not give nitroglycerin with it. Both cause vasodilation of the veins and can reduce preload, leading to really profound hypotension.","Other nitrates — Severe hypotension, especially when taken with other nitrates.","Common effects — Headache and flushing.","Route — Sublingual, or IV if the blood pressure tolerates it.","Reperfusion Injury","What the lecture says","When myocardial tissue that has been deprived of oxygen suddenly receives oxygenated blood, it can become stunned, and that can cause heart failure.","Why: white blood cells come to the infarcted, damaged tissue (that's their job), and they release oxidized free radicals that cause the reperfusion injury.","These patients need to be monitored closely.","Also monitor for reperfusion dysrhythmias, such as ventricular tachycardia (VTach) and ventricular fibrillation.","Key Takeaways","Acute MI by the numbers","Acute MI is a leading cause of death in the United States and throughout the world.","A big reason for that mortality is hesitating or delaying care (\"it might pass,\" \"it's not serious\").","With treatment, survival is great: about 90 to 95% for treated patients in the US. Teach patients: if you suspect an MI, seek treatment. Better safe than sorry.","About half of people with MI are younger than 65.","People older than 65 and people with diabetes can have an MI and not know it: a silent or asymptomatic MI, found later, with no signs or symptoms at the time."]},{"s":"Part 4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection▾","a":"part-4-aneurysms-aortic-dissection","k":["Part 4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection▾","Topic 4: what an aneurysm is and why it forms, the types by location and shape, assessing a AAA, and the two big complications, rupture and aortic dissection.","What an Aneurysm Is","Definition and cause","A localized dilation or outpouching of a vessel wall.","A true aneurysm involves all three layers of the arterial wall. There are also false aneurysms (below).","Aneurysms are primarily a result of hypertension. Prolonged, uncontrolled high blood pressure puts constant pressure on the arterial wall: the vessel dilates, the wall weakens, a pouch or bulge forms, and eventually the pouch can burst.","Aneurysms cause turbulent blood flow, which makes them susceptible to rupture.","Most commonly seen in the aorta and the cerebral arteries, but they can occur elsewhere.","Listen for a bruit. Because of the turbulent flow, a stethoscope over an aneurysm may pick up a bruit: a whooshing-type sound.","Risk Factors","Think of anything that damages blood vessels. The primary drivers are atherosclerosis and hypertension.","Named in the lecture","Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Diseases of the blood vessels","Trauma","Tobacco use","Older adult age (over 65)","A hereditary component","Types of Aneurysms","By location","Aortic aneurysms, the most common type: abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA, \"triple A\"), thoracic aortic aneurysm, and dissecting aortic aneurysm.","Cerebral aneurysms: anywhere in the brain.","False or pseudoaneurysms.","By shape","Saccular","Berry (a subtype of saccular)","Fusiform","Saccular — Balloon-shaped. Involves only one side of the vessel wall, not the entire circumference. A wide neck at the base. — Seen on an angiogram (dye is injected and watched). — Not specified separately in the lecture.","Berry — A subtype of saccular: the same pouch or balloon look, but a small neck at the base. — Located at bifurcations. A very common location is the circle of Willis, which connects the anterior and posterior circulation at the base of the…","Fusiform — Involves the entire circumference of the vessel; no longer limited to one wall. Usually a gradual, progressive dilation of the arterial wall. Can have potentially extensive involvement. — Often related to diffuse sclerotic change…","False (pseudo) — A localized dissection or tear in the inner artery wall; the outer artery walls are still intact. Considered a type of hematoma. — Often a result of vascular interventional procedures, such as leakage between a vascular gra…","Berry Aneurysm Surgery","Drain — Takes the blood out of the aneurysm.","Clip — Places a clip around the base of the aneurysm.","Coil — Fills the aneurysm from the inside with a coil.","Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)","Auscultation — Listen with the stethoscope over the abdominal aorta. A bruit there suggests a AAA may be present.","Inspection — In particularly thin patients with low body fat, you may even see a pulsatile mass in the abdomen.","Light palpation — You could feel a mass that might indicate a AAA.","Suspected AAA: no deep palpation. Deep palpation is a risk for rupture, so keep it to light palpation.","The aorta is the largest vessel in the body and supplies a lot of really important places, so the clinical manifestations of an aortic aneurysm depend on where it is located. An aneurysm above the kidneys looks different from one below the …","Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection","Rupture","Especially in the aorta, where the pressure in the vessel is very high.","If a AAA ruptures, the patient will bleed out very, very quickly, with a lot of blood involved.","Aortic dissection (dissecting aneurysm)","A tear in the inner layer of the artery; blood enters between the layers of the artery wall.","The turbulent blood flow tears away at the layers, separating them.","A medical emergency. These patients can also rupture, and without quick intervention it can be fatal.","An aneurysm increases the risk for dissection: a weakened wall can keep weakening, eventually tear, and turn into a dissection.","Not tested: the slides showing the different types of dissections and aneurysms. The instructor said, \"you do not need to know these. So don't memorize them. They're just for you to look at.\"","Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection","Aneurysm","Can be completely asymptomatic.","Aortic dissection","Sudden, severe tearing pain: the most common manifestation.","Most likely felt in the chest, but it can also be in the neck, jaw, back or abdomen.","Clinical Manifestations of Aortic Dissection","Early stages — Blood pressure is elevated, as the body tries to compensate for the loss of blood.","Later stages — Blood pressure is \"in the trash\" and may be completely unattainable, because of significant bleeding. Blood between the walls of the artery isn't in the arteries getting where it needs to be.","Other signs — Syncope, total unresponsiveness, hemiplegia, paralysis of the lower extremities.","Without intervention — Progresses to total cardiovascular collapse, shock and death.","Managing Aortic Dissection","Aortic dissection is a surgical emergency; minutes matter. Emergent evaluation is needed, and surgical intervention is the priority and the most important goal.","Diagnosis — CT scan, or MRA (an MRI with angiogram).","Beta blockers — Decrease heart rate and blood pressure to prevent further tearing or further dissection of the artery.","Nitrates — May also be seen.","Surgery — The most important goal of treatment."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One row per part: the points to have cold before an exam. Every detail is taught in full in the parts above.","1. Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — Myocarditis is mostly viral (Coxsackie A and B) and can disrupt conduction. Normal pericardial fluid is 15–50 mL; about 200 mL compresses the heart into cardiac tamponade, a medical emergen…","2A. Acute Coronary Syndrome — ACS = unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI; time is tissue. Prinzmetal's is vasospasm, often at rest, treated with nitrates. Unstable plaque: big lipid core, thin fibrous cap, inflammation (CRP). First-time chest pai…","2B. Myocardial Infarction — MI is irreversible ischemic necrosis; ECG changes stay and troponin trends up. Myocardial cells can't store ATP: contraction drops in 1–2 minutes, irreversible injury from 30 minutes to 4 hours, so reperfuse with…","3. Acute MI Pharmacology — Take vital signs before every drug. Aspirin: chew the first dose; decreases mortality in STEMI. Morphine: watch respiratory depression and hypotension; may inhibit antiplatelets. Nitroglycerin: reduces preload and…","4. Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection — Primarily from hypertension; turbulent flow, a bruit, rupture risk. Saccular (one side, wide neck), berry (small neck, circle of Willis; drain, clip or coil), fusiform (whole circumference, monitored), fal…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the five parts above: a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week1-pericardial-disease.html","t":"Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","secs":[{"s":"Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1","Myocarditis","The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid","Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade","Pulsus Paradoxus","Pericarditis on the ECG","Treating Pericarditis","Self-Test Flashcards","This is the first lecture of NUR 406, a short voiceover on pericardial disease. It covers myocarditis (inflammation of the heart muscle), then the pericardium itself: how inflammation of the pericardium leads to fluid buildup, pericardial e…"]},{"s":"Myocarditis▾","a":"myocarditis","k":["Myocarditis▾","Think back to normal A&P: the myocardium is the muscle layer of the heart, the muscle cells. Myocarditis is an inflammatory disease of the myocardium.","Range of severity — A wide variety of symptoms: it can be a pretty mild disorder with little to no discomfort, all the way up to heart failure and a lethal condition.","What happens to the cells — The inflammation comes with degeneration and necrosis of the cardiac myocytes. The inflammation causes direct injury to the myocytes, and over time it can create fibrosis and scarring that lead to structural chan…","Conduction — We often see a disruption in conduction through the heart. The tissue changes, inflammatory cytokines and T cell activation in the myocardium can result in abnormal electrical conduction.","Cause — Typically viruses. In the United States and Europe, viruses are the leading cause of myocarditis. The Coxsackie virus A and B are the majority of cases.","Treatment — Depends on how severe the symptoms are. Treated by giving antivirals; anti-inflammatories can also be used."]},{"s":"The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid▾","a":"the-pericardium-pericardial-fluid","k":["The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid▾","The pericardium is a sac-like membrane that surrounds the heart and the roots of the major blood vessels, and it helps the heart function properly. Between its inner and outer layers is the pericardial space.","Normal — A small amount, about 15 to 50 mL. — Acts like a lubricant between the inner and outer layers, for normal function of the heart.","Pericardial effusion — When the pericardium is inflamed, fluid accumulates in excess of what is normal. Effusion is that excess fluid accumulation. — Can compress the heart chambers and limit cardiac filling.","Cardiac tamponade — About 200 mL. — The fluid compresses the heart. The chambers are restricted and can't stretch and fill with blood as they normally would.","Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency. The fluid has to be removed quickly, because the heart chambers can't stretch and fill with blood."]},{"s":"Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade▾","a":"acute-pericarditis-vs-cardiac-tamponade","k":["Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade▾","The lecture describes what acute pericarditis looks like, then what changes once it progresses to cardiac tamponade and cardiac output falls.","Acute pericarditis","Chest pain: a sharp pain that worsens with deep breathing.","Fever, because of the inflammatory process.","Dyspnea (the lecture glosses it as \"some painful breathing\").","Pericardial friction rub on auscultation: a scratching sound, almost like rubbing on a balloon.","Muffled heart sounds, because you are listening to the heart pump through a layer of fluid.","ECG changes: elevations and also depressions.","Pulsus paradoxus is also named as a sign of acute pericarditis.","Cardiac tamponade","Signs of decreased cardiac output: hypotension, and the usual signs of a patient who is not being perfused appropriately, such as confusion and dizziness.","Jugular vein distension, from blood backing up in the system because of the decreased cardiac output.","Muffled heart sounds, from listening through a layer of fluid.","Pulsus paradoxus occurs in about 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade."]},{"s":"Pulsus Paradoxus▾","a":"pulsus-paradoxus","k":["Pulsus Paradoxus▾","What it is — A decrease in the systolic blood pressure when the patient breathes in (with inspiration).","Why it's a \"paradox\" — Typically your blood pressure should increase a little bit when you breathe in. Seeing a drop instead is the paradox.","What it indicates — The heart is not pumping properly, and something is impacting cardiac filling.","How you see it — Most clearly with an arterial line (an IV catheter in an artery), which gives real-time blood pressure: you can watch the pressure change as the patient breathes in.","How often — About 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","The recording garbles the exact size of the systolic drop (\"a decrease in the systolic blood pressure of more and more\"), so no number is given here. If the slide shows a value, it can be added."]},{"s":"Pericarditis on the ECG▾","a":"pericarditis-on-the-ecg","k":["Pericarditis on the ECG▾","The ECG in pericarditis shows elements of ST depression as well as ST elevation. In some cases it looks similar to a patient having an MI, but the clinical symptoms will be different.","ST elevation in MI is covered in Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction. Compare the two if needed"]},{"s":"Treating Pericarditis▾","a":"treating-pericarditis","k":["Treating Pericarditis▾","NSAIDs (anti-inflammatories) — Anti-inflammatories are used. Aspirin may be seen occasionally.","Corticosteroids — \"Give or take\": sometimes they work really well, sometimes not so much.","Colchicine — Typically a gout medication, but recently found to work well for pericarditis that is not responding to NSAIDs.","Pericardiocentesis — For cardiac tamponade caused by the pericarditis. A needle is put directly into the pericardium and the fluid is drawn out. Because tamponade is a medical emergency, this happens quickly.","Aspirin for acute MI is a different use, covered in Acute MI Pharmacology"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-acs-mi.html","t":"Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction","secs":[{"s":"Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 2","What ACS Is","The ACS Spectrum","Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina","What Makes Plaque Unstable","Why Plaque Ruptures","Unstable Angina","Stable Angina vs ACS","Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction","Acute Myocardial Infarction","Ischemia, Infarction & ATP","Zones of Damage","STEMI vs NSTEMI","The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the ACS voiceover: what ACS is, the spectrum from Prinzmetal's angina to STEMI, what makes plaque unstable and why it ruptures, unstable angina, how ACS differs from stable angina, the signs of MI, what happens to heart ti…"]},{"s":"What ACS Is▾","a":"what-acs-is","k":["What ACS Is▾","Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)","An umbrella term for situations in which the blood supply to the heart muscle is suddenly blocked or compromised.","It is certainly a medical emergency.","The blockage can come and go, or it can be sudden and complete. Either way, cardiac tissue is dying.","ACS covers three conditions: unstable angina, non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), and ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).","The three have a similar pathophysiology, so the signs and symptoms can look similar. The ECG is what helps determine the next step: send the patient to the cath lab for reperfusion, or treat medically?","\"Time is tissue.\" The instructor says it several times: treat the blockage as quickly as possible to save as much tissue as possible."]},{"s":"The ACS Spectrum▾","a":"the-acs-spectrum","k":["The ACS Spectrum▾","The slide shows a progression from a normal vessel to complete occlusion. Stable angina has been covered before; ACS is the part of the picture where stable angina has become unstable.","Asymptomatic plaque — Some buildup of atherosclerotic plaque. — Nothing. They don't know it's there unless we go looking for it.","Stable angina — Atherosclerotic plaque buildup. — Signs and symptoms with exertion (strenuous activity).","Unstable angina — The clot is able to stay really small, and blood supply is still somewhat maintained. — Any new or changing chest pain.","NSTEMI — A larger buildup; blood flow is even more decreased. Some blood still gets through, but not enough. — A myocardial infarction without a change in the ST segment on the ECG.","STEMI — The vessel is completely and totally occluded by the clot. — A myocardial infarction with elevation of the ST segment on the ECG.","On the other side of the spectrum slide sits Prinzmetal's variant angina, which is a vasospasm of the coronary artery rather than plaque buildup (next section)."]},{"s":"Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina▾","a":"prinzmetal-s-variant-or-vasospastic-angina","k":["Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina▾","Mechanism — The coronary artery vasospasms. It is not necessarily related to atherosclerotic plaque buildup; the spasm reduces oxygenated blood flow to the cardiac tissue. The spasms can increase a person's risk for myocardial ischemia.","Cause — A lot of times, damage to the endothelium of the coronary vessel. The spasms narrow the artery and decrease blood flow.","Plaque — These patients may or may not have atherosclerotic plaque.","When it happens — Oftentimes at rest: sleeping, sitting around, minimal activity. These are not the people who are overexerting themselves.","ECG — May show ST elevation. Monitor closely to see whether treatment is working and the ECG changes return to normal.","Treatment — Oftentimes nitrates, because they relax the spasm.","Outlook — Usually benign, but it can cause some serious dysrhythmias to be on the lookout for."]},{"s":"What Makes Plaque Unstable▾","a":"what-makes-plaque-unstable","k":["What Makes Plaque Unstable▾","Usually, when plaque becomes unstable, people develop ACS. These are people who already have atherosclerotic plaque in their coronary vessels.","Stable plaque","A pretty thick fibrous cap on top of the fat, which contributes to its stability.","Unstable plaque","The lipid core (the fat inside the vessel) gets bigger.","As the core grows, the fibrous cap gets thinner.","There is a component of inflammation; C-reactive protein (CRP) is a marker of inflammation in the body.","Smooth muscle cells proliferate into the intima (the lecture describes the intima as the middle layer of the blood vessel). These cells create the fibrous cap and bring in inflammatory components, increasing the risk of plaque rupture.","Assessing Unstable Plaque","A study that puts dye into the vessel shows how much narrowing is happening, so we can roughly estimate whether the lipid core is larger or smaller.","CRP levels show how much inflammation is going on."]},{"s":"Why Plaque Ruptures▾","a":"why-plaque-ruptures","k":["Why Plaque Ruptures▾","Rupture risk is higher with unstable plaque: a large lipid core, a thin fibrous cap, and ongoing inflammation. The slide walks through the theory step by step.","1. Trigger — Psychological stress, exercise, or circadian rhythms stimulate the sympathetic nervous system.","2. Sympathetic response — Epinephrine and norepinephrine are released: blood pressure and heart rate rise, and the heart works harder with stronger contractions.","3. Force on the vessels — Pushing out more blood with stronger contractions puts more force on the vessels, including the coronary vessels themselves.","4. Rupture — That force can lead to rupture of the plaque.","5. Platelets — Platelets come to the site and adhere to the ruptured plaque. They release substances that attract more platelets and can contribute to vasospasm.","6. Thrombus — A thrombus forms in that same area."]},{"s":"Unstable Angina▾","a":"unstable-angina","k":["Unstable Angina▾","What counts as unstable angina","Chest pain occurring for the first time. Consider it unstable immediately, until proven otherwise; it is a medical emergency because no pattern or history has been established.","In a patient who normally has stable angina, pain that is more severe than normal: for example, they have taken their first nitro and five minutes later the pain hasn't stopped or is getting worse.","Pain that is different: usually in the chest, but this time down one arm, down both arms, or radiating into the jaw.","It may happen at rest; the person may be awakened in the middle of the night.","What's happening in the vessel","The plaque ruptures and a thrombus forms. That rupture and clot formation is the real worry.","The size of the clot and of the blockage determine how much blood gets past to perfuse the tissue on the other side. A 30% occluded artery may still allow some flow around a clot; a 90% blockage leaves little room.","If there is no infarction, the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved, and some tissue was still perfused.","The ECG changes and then goes back to normal, and we typically don't see troponin changes.","Unstable angina is a medical emergency. Teach patients to recognize the signs, recognize it as an emergency, and call 911 right now. They should most definitely not drive themselves to the ER."]},{"s":"Stable Angina vs ACS▾","a":"stable-angina-vs-acs","k":["Stable Angina vs ACS▾","Teach patients the difference so they can identify it and seek care. The big difference is the pain: its severity and its duration.","Stable angina","Pain should last about 5 minutes, and be gone by maybe about 15 minutes.","May feel like a little bit of chest tightness.","Flares up with strenuous activity.","ACS","Chest pain lasts a lot longer.","No relief with nitroglycerin.","More severe: the sensation of an elephant sitting on your chest.","Other symptoms: diaphoresis, shortness of air, nausea and vomiting.","An impending sense of doom: they know something is not right but can't pinpoint it. Take them very, very seriously.","Signs in Men and Women","Research over roughly the last 50 years shows different signs and symptoms of ACS between men and women. These symptoms aren't limited to one or the other.","Typically associated with men","Chest pain.","Tingling or discomfort in the back, arms, neck or jaw.","Other symptoms (often reported by women)","Dizziness, nausea, vomiting, tiredness.","Heartburn: a lot of women with ACS say they took some Tums that morning because they thought it was heartburn.","Some of these can feel similar to stable angina. The particular concern is when the experience changes or gets worse."]},{"s":"Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction▾","a":"signs-symptoms-of-myocardial-infarction","k":["Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction▾","What the lecture lists","Diaphoresis and dyspnea.","Extreme anxiety; restless, can't sit still, moving around.","Impending sense of doom (\"I know something's wrong\") without being able to pinpoint it.","Levine sign: the fist to the chest, the \"Hollywood\" heart attack sign.","Pallor and weak pulses, from perfusion issues because the heart isn't working right.","Retrosternal (behind the sternum) crushing chest pain that can radiate to several parts of the body; the elephant on the chest."]},{"s":"Acute Myocardial Infarction▾","a":"acute-myocardial-infarction","k":["Acute Myocardial Infarction▾","What an acute MI is","Ruptured plaque plus thrombus that severely reduces or completely blocks blood flow to the heart muscle.","Think of ACS as a continuum; MI is at the end of that continuum.","It is ACS with prolonged ischemia that results in the death of myocardial tissue: an irreversible ischemic necrosis. These cells are never going to function properly again.","Infarction happens either because blood flow disruption goes on over a very long period of time, or because of a complete and total blockage.","How much tissue is lost depends on: where the infarction is (smaller or larger vessel, left or right side of the heart), how long blood supply has been affected, and how complete the blockage is. Partial flow over a few days can damage tiss…","MIs are classified by ECG findings: STEMI and NSTEMI.","ECG — Changes, then eventually goes back to normal. — Changes that stay; a repeat ECG won't go back to normal.","Troponin — Typically no change in cardiac enzymes. — Increases. Serial troponins trend up during the infarction, then trend down after the occlusion has been rectified.","Tissue — No infarction: the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved. — Death of myocardial tissue, which is irreversible."]},{"s":"Ischemia, Infarction & ATP▾","a":"ischemia-infarction-atp","k":["Ischemia, Infarction & ATP▾","Ischemia","When the oxygen supply does not meet the heart's metabolic demands.","Infarction","Tissue death that results from that ischemia.","Why ATP Matters","ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a molecule that stores and transfers energy in cells; it is the main energy source for cellular function.","Myocardial cells cannot store ATP. They must be continuously fed ATP, so they need a continuous supply of oxygen to make it.","When oxygen goes down, ATP supply goes down, but the heart can't stop beating while ATP isn't being produced.","What Happens to Heart Tissue Over Time","Acute ischemia — ATP depletes instantly.","1 to 2 minutes — Decreased ability of the heart to contract.","30 minutes up to 4 hours — Irreversible injury to cardiac tissue. By 4 hours, necrosis has begun and the tissue begins to die.","1 to 2 weeks — The necrotic tissue is cleared away; the myocardium is very weak and susceptible to rupture.","About 6 weeks — The necrotic tissue has been replaced by tough scar tissue.","Reperfusion: as quickly as possible, at least within 4 hours. Most guidelines state reperfusion therapy should be done within four hours. Within 30 minutes would be ideal, since irreversible injury can occur that quickly.","Scar tissue is thickened and tougher, so electrical impulses have a harder time traveling through it. That is why ECGs after an MI can show permanent changes."]},{"s":"Zones of Damage▾","a":"zones-of-damage","k":["Zones of Damage▾","Infarction — Necrosis; dead cells, where the MI happened. — No. The goal is to stop it from increasing.","Injury — Not dead yet, but the tissue has suffered injury. — Some recovery is still possible; it can be perfused and restored to be viable.","Ischemia — The outer zone. — Full recovery is possible if it is perfused again.","The goal is to not extend the size of the infarct: increase oxygen and decrease the demand on the heart. The instructor compares this with an ischemic stroke: the same concept of tissue dying from lack of oxygen, where time is of the essenc…","Three Factors That Decide the Extent of Damage","The location or level of the occlusion in the coronary artery.","The length of time it has been occluded.","The availability of collateral circulation. In people with atherosclerotic plaque the heart can start to develop collateral circulation. The lecture calls it \"typically a bad thing,\" but here it creates ways to bypass the blockage and still…"]},{"s":"STEMI vs NSTEMI▾","a":"stemi-vs-nstemi","k":["STEMI vs NSTEMI▾","The main difference is the ST segment on the ECG.","ST segment — Elevated. — Normal, or a slight ST depression.","QRS complex — Usually a wide QRS that develops over hours. — Normal.","T wave — Peaked, then over time less peaked, then inverted. — Inverted the entire time.","Troponin — Elevated; still elevated after 2 hours, with a sharper increase than in NSTEMI. — Elevated.","Infarct size — Typically larger; more blockage, more tissue dying. — Smaller.","Outcomes — Worse. — Better than STEMI.","The \"tombstone.\" Nurses describe the ST elevation of a STEMI as looking like a tombstone, because the prognosis is not good if it isn't treated very quickly. Seeing it on an ECG should throw up red flags: investigate, and do it quickly.","On the ECG images, an NSTEMI can show ST depression or T wave inversion; a STEMI shows ST elevation.","Pericarditis can also show ST elevation and can look like an MI. See Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade"]},{"s":"The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"▾","a":"the-lad-the-widow-maker","k":["The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"▾","Coronary blood supply named in the lecture","The right coronary artery.","The left main artery, which branches into the left circumflex artery and the left anterior descending (LAD) artery.","The LAD supplies almost the entire front side of the heart, much of the sidewall, and most of the left ventricle.","The LAD is the vessel most commonly involved in MI, and one of the most damaging. It is a really large vessel feeding most of the left ventricle, so a blockage there is particularly detrimental and often very lethal if not treated very quic…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-acute-mi-pharm.html","t":"Acute MI Pharmacology","secs":[{"s":"Acute MI Pharmacology","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 3","Initial Drug Treatment","Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)","Nitroglycerin Safety","Reperfusion Injury","Key Takeaways","Self-Test Flashcards","The second, shorter part of the ACS voiceover. It covers the initial drug treatment of an acute MI (oxygen, morphine, aspirin, nitroglycerin, beta blockers and thrombolytics), fibrinolytic therapy with alteplase, reperfusion injury, and the…","The pathophysiology of ACS and MI is covered in Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction"]},{"s":"Initial Drug Treatment▾","a":"initial-drug-treatment","k":["Initial Drug Treatment▾","The lecture goes through these drugs twice, once briefly and once as a recap with more detail. Both passes are combined here.","Oxygen — Increases oxygen delivery to the ischemic myocardium. — New research shows that if the patient is not hypoxic, oxygen may not have that benefit. Whether it is ordered depends on the provider.","Morphine — Decreases pain. Some research suggests it dilates blood vessels to help blood flow, and there is some thought that it helps preserve ischemic tissue. — Side effects: respiratory depression and hypotension, so check vital signs an…","Aspirin — Makes platelets less sticky and suppresses platelet aggregation, so the thrombus is less likely to form or get bigger. — Routinely used. Have the patient chew the first dose to speed absorption. Research shows aspirin decreases mo…","Nitroglycerin — Dilates the veins to decrease preload; reduces preload and afterload, which helps limit infarct size. — It does not reduce mortality. Given sublingually, or IV if the patient can maintain their blood pressure.","Beta blockers — Reduce heart rate and contractility (and blood pressure), which reduces overall oxygen demand. This can reduce pain, reduce infarct size and help with mortality. — Only if there are no contraindications.","Thrombolytic agent — The biggest line of treatment. — Only if the patient is eligible, with no contraindications; for example, not after a recent hemorrhagic stroke. Ideally given within about 4 to 6 hours of the chest pain starting. These …","Take vital signs before giving any of these. Note heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory effort before administration. These medications can push someone over the edge if they aren't stable to begin with.","Which Drugs Affect Mortality","Aspirin — Decreases mortality in STEMI.","Beta blockers — Can help with mortality.","Nitroglycerin — Does not reduce mortality (it limits infarct size)."]},{"s":"Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)▾","a":"fibrinolytic-therapy-alteplase-tpa","k":["Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)▾","When it's used","Often used in a STEMI.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) is the first choice for treating a STEMI. If a cath lab is not available, fibrinolytic therapy is effective.","These are the \"clot busters.\"","Alteplase (tPA)","The prototype and the drug of choice; it is the most effective.","Dissolves the clot by converting plasminogen into plasmin; plasmin is what actually dissolves the clot.","Works best when given within 30 to 70 minutes of the onset of symptoms (when the chest pain started).","Drugs Given Alongside","In a STEMI where fibrinolytic therapy is anticipated, clopidogrel is also given at presentation, and heparin might be started.","Anyone who has had fibrinolytic therapy is given heparin and antiplatelet therapy (like clopidogrel) to help prevent new clots from forming. Alteplase dissolves the current clot; the others keep new ones from forming.","The main adverse effect is bleeding. We are dissolving a clot, so watch for bleeding.","The lecture gives two time windows: thrombolytics \"ideally\" within about 4 to 6 hours of chest pain starting, and alteplase working best within 30 to 70 minutes of symptom onset. Both are recorded here exactly as stated."]},{"s":"Nitroglycerin Safety▾","a":"nitroglycerin-safety","k":["Nitroglycerin Safety▾","Sildenafil (Viagra) — Do not give nitroglycerin with it. Both cause vasodilation of the veins and can reduce preload, leading to really profound hypotension.","Other nitrates — Severe hypotension, especially when taken with other nitrates.","Common effects — Headache and flushing.","Route — Sublingual, or IV if the blood pressure tolerates it."]},{"s":"Reperfusion Injury▾","a":"reperfusion-injury","k":["Reperfusion Injury▾","What the lecture says","When myocardial tissue that has been deprived of oxygen suddenly receives oxygenated blood, it can become stunned, and that can cause heart failure.","Why: white blood cells come to the infarcted, damaged tissue (that's their job), and they release oxidized free radicals that cause the reperfusion injury.","These patients need to be monitored closely.","Also monitor for reperfusion dysrhythmias, such as ventricular tachycardia (VTach) and ventricular fibrillation."]},{"s":"Key Takeaways▾","a":"key-takeaways","k":["Key Takeaways▾","Acute MI by the numbers","Acute MI is a leading cause of death in the United States and throughout the world.","A big reason for that mortality is hesitating or delaying care (\"it might pass,\" \"it's not serious\").","With treatment, survival is great: about 90 to 95% for treated patients in the US. Teach patients: if you suspect an MI, seek treatment. Better safe than sorry.","About half of people with MI are younger than 65.","People older than 65 and people with diabetes can have an MI and not know it: a silent or asymptomatic MI, found later, with no signs or symptoms at the time."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-aneurysms.html","t":"Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","secs":[{"s":"Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 4","What an Aneurysm Is","Types of Aneurysms","Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)","Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection","Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection","Managing Aortic Dissection","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the aneurysm voiceover: what an aneurysm is and why it forms, risk factors, the types by location and by shape (saccular, berry, fusiform and false aneurysms), assessing an abdominal aortic aneurysm, and the two big compli…"]},{"s":"What an Aneurysm Is▾","a":"what-an-aneurysm-is","k":["What an Aneurysm Is▾","Definition and cause","A localized dilation or outpouching of a vessel wall.","A true aneurysm involves all three layers of the arterial wall. There are also false aneurysms (below).","Aneurysms are primarily a result of hypertension. Prolonged, uncontrolled high blood pressure puts constant pressure on the arterial wall: the vessel dilates, the wall weakens, a pouch or bulge forms, and eventually the pouch can burst.","Aneurysms cause turbulent blood flow, which makes them susceptible to rupture.","Most commonly seen in the aorta and the cerebral arteries, but they can occur elsewhere.","Listen for a bruit. Because of the turbulent flow, a stethoscope over an aneurysm may pick up a bruit: a whooshing-type sound.","Risk Factors","Think of anything that damages blood vessels. The primary drivers are atherosclerosis and hypertension.","Named in the lecture","Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Diseases of the blood vessels","Trauma","Tobacco use","Older adult age (over 65)","A hereditary component"]},{"s":"Types of Aneurysms▾","a":"types-of-aneurysms","k":["Types of Aneurysms▾","By location","Aortic aneurysms, the most common type: abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA, \"triple A\"), thoracic aortic aneurysm, and dissecting aortic aneurysm.","Cerebral aneurysms: anywhere in the brain.","False or pseudoaneurysms.","By shape","Saccular","Berry (a subtype of saccular)","Fusiform","Saccular — Balloon-shaped. Involves only one side of the vessel wall, not the entire circumference. A wide neck at the base. — Seen on an angiogram (dye is injected and watched). — Not specified separately in the lecture.","Berry — A subtype of saccular: the same pouch or balloon look, but a small neck at the base. — Located at bifurcations. A very common location is the circle of Willis, which connects the anterior and posterior circulation at the base of the…","Fusiform — Involves the entire circumference of the vessel; no longer limited to one wall. Usually a gradual, progressive dilation of the arterial wall. Can have potentially extensive involvement. — Often related to diffuse sclerotic change…","False (pseudo) — A localized dissection or tear in the inner artery wall; the outer artery walls are still intact. Considered a type of hematoma. — Often a result of vascular interventional procedures, such as leakage between a vascular gra…","Berry Aneurysm Surgery","Drain — Takes the blood out of the aneurysm.","Clip — Places a clip around the base of the aneurysm.","Coil — Fills the aneurysm from the inside with a coil."]},{"s":"Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)▾","a":"abdominal-aortic-aneurysm-aaa","k":["Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)▾","Auscultation — Listen with the stethoscope over the abdominal aorta. A bruit there suggests a AAA may be present.","Inspection — In particularly thin patients with low body fat, you may even see a pulsatile mass in the abdomen.","Light palpation — You could feel a mass that might indicate a AAA.","Suspected AAA: no deep palpation. Deep palpation is a risk for rupture, so keep it to light palpation.","The aorta is the largest vessel in the body and supplies a lot of really important places, so the clinical manifestations of an aortic aneurysm depend on where it is located. An aneurysm above the kidneys looks different from one below the …"]},{"s":"Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection▾","a":"complications-rupture-aortic-dissection","k":["Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection▾","Rupture","Especially in the aorta, where the pressure in the vessel is very high.","If a AAA ruptures, the patient will bleed out very, very quickly, with a lot of blood involved.","Aortic dissection (dissecting aneurysm)","A tear in the inner layer of the artery; blood enters between the layers of the artery wall.","The turbulent blood flow tears away at the layers, separating them.","A medical emergency. These patients can also rupture, and without quick intervention it can be fatal.","An aneurysm increases the risk for dissection: a weakened wall can keep weakening, eventually tear, and turn into a dissection.","Not tested: the slides showing the different types of dissections and aneurysms. The instructor said, \"you do not need to know these. So don't memorize them. They're just for you to look at.\""]},{"s":"Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection▾","a":"aneurysm-vs-aortic-dissection","k":["Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection▾","Aneurysm","Can be completely asymptomatic.","Aortic dissection","Sudden, severe tearing pain: the most common manifestation.","Most likely felt in the chest, but it can also be in the neck, jaw, back or abdomen.","Clinical Manifestations of Aortic Dissection","Early stages — Blood pressure is elevated, as the body tries to compensate for the loss of blood.","Later stages — Blood pressure is \"in the trash\" and may be completely unattainable, because of significant bleeding. Blood between the walls of the artery isn't in the arteries getting where it needs to be.","Other signs — Syncope, total unresponsiveness, hemiplegia, paralysis of the lower extremities.","Without intervention — Progresses to total cardiovascular collapse, shock and death."]},{"s":"Managing Aortic Dissection▾","a":"managing-aortic-dissection","k":["Managing Aortic Dissection▾","Aortic dissection is a surgical emergency; minutes matter. Emergent evaluation is needed, and surgical intervention is the priority and the most important goal.","Diagnosis — CT scan, or MRA (an MRI with angiogram).","Beta blockers — Decrease heart rate and blood pressure to prevent further tearing or further dissection of the artery.","Nitrates — May also be seen.","Surgery — The most important goal of treatment.","Beta blockers and nitroglycerin in acute MI are a different use, covered in Acute MI Pharmacology"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 1","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics","The Four Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It's a map of Week 1's four topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate; it pulls the whole week …"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-the-four-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics▾","Four pages are easier to hold in your head when you see what each lecture is really about. Topics 2 and 3 are the two halves of one PowerPoint.","The Heart Muscle and Its Sac: Inflammation — Topic 1, Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade (all of it) — The first lecture of 406. Inflammation of the myocardium (myocarditis, usually viral) can range from mild to heart failure and…","The Coronary Arteries: Blockage — Topic 2, Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction (the ACS sections first, then the MI sections) — The longest lecture. Blood supply to the heart muscle is suddenly blocked or compromised, and the si…","Treating the Blockage — Topic 3, Acute MI Pharmacology (all of it) — The second, shorter half of the ACS PowerPoint. Each drug has a job: oxygen raises delivery to the ischemic muscle, morphine eases pain, aspirin keeps the thrombus from gr…","The Artery Wall: Weakening and Tearing — Topic 4, Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection (all of it) — Back out to the vessels. Uncontrolled hypertension weakens the arterial wall until it bulges (an aneurysm), and a weakened wall can tear (dissecti…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order, and read Topic 2 before Topic 3: the pharmacology lecture assumes the plaque, clot and \"time is tissue\" ideas from ACS."]},{"s":"The Four Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-four-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Four Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — Myocarditis is mostly viral (Coxsackie A and B), treated with antivirals and anti-inflammatories depending on severity. Pericarditis brings sharp chest pain that worsens with deep breathin…","2 — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction — ACS is unstable angina, NSTEMI and STEMI, and time is tissue. Unstable plaque (big lipid core, thin cap, inflammation) ruptures under sympathetic stress and a thrombus forms. Unstable an…","3 — Acute MI Pharmacology — Oxygen, morphine (now used more cautiously), chewed aspirin, nitroglycerin and beta blockers, all after checking vital signs. Aspirin decreases mortality; nitroglycerin does not. PCI is first choice for STEMI; al…","4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection — Aneurysms come primarily from hypertension and atherosclerosis, cause turbulent flow and a bruit, and are most common in the aorta and cerebral arteries. Saccular, berry, fusiform and false aneurysms diff…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content. Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why. Section names are in quotation marks.","ST elevation on the ECG — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"STEMI vs NSTEMI\" (elevation in STEMI; normal or slight depression in NSTEMI) — The same page's \"Prinzmetal's Angina\" section (it may show ST elevation, which return…","Aspirin — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Initial Drug Treatment\" (chew the first dose; suppresses platelet aggregation; decreases mortality in STEMI) — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Treating Pericarditis,\" where aspirin may be se…","Nitrates and nitroglycerin — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Initial Drug Treatment\" and \"Nitroglycerin Safety\" (reduces preload and afterload; no sildenafil) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: nitrates relax the spasm in Prinzmetal…","Beta blockers — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Initial Drug Treatment\" (lower heart rate and contractility, so lower oxygen demand) — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: \"Managing Aortic Dissection,\" to decrease heart rate and blood pressure and preven…","Blood pressure and force on the vessel wall — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: \"What an Aneurysm Is\" (uncontrolled hypertension weakens the arterial wall) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"Why Plaque Ruptures\" (sympathetic s…","Atherosclerosis — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"The ACS Spectrum\" and \"What Makes Plaque Unstable\" — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: a primary driver in \"Risk Factors,\" with fusiform aneurysms related to diffuse sclerotic…","Inflammation — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade (inflammation of the myocardium and of the pericardium) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"What Makes Plaque Unstable,\" where inflammation (measured by CRP) makes…","Conduction problems and dysrhythmias — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Myocarditis\" (inflammation disrupts conduction) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: Prinzmetal's can cause serious dysrhythmias, and scar …","Heart failure — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Myocarditis\" (mild all the way up to heart failure) — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Reperfusion Injury,\" where stunned myocardium can cause heart failure.","Poor perfusion and falling blood pressure — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade\" (decreased cardiac output: hypotension, confusion, dizziness, JVD) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial I…","Bleeding — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Fibrinolytic Therapy\" (bleeding is the main adverse effect; no thrombolytic after a recent hemorrhagic stroke) — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: a ruptured AAA bleeds out very quickly; late dissection bleed…","Dye studies and angiography — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: saccular aneurysms on an angiogram, berry aneurysms diagnosed by angiography, dissection by CT or MRA — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"What Makes Plaque Unstabl…"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and safety statements from across the four pages, condensed to a phrase, one row per topic. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation. Click through to a topic for the details behind any …","Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency: the fluid has to be removed quickly (pericardiocentesis), because the chambers can't stretch and fill. Watch for decreased cardiac output: hypotension…","Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction — First-time chest pain is unstable angina until proven otherwise. Unstable angina is a medical emergency: call 911, never drive to the ER. Take an impending sense of doom very seriously. Time…","Acute MI Pharmacology — Take heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory effort before giving any of these drugs. Morphine: respiratory depression and hypotension. Nitroglycerin: never with sildenafil (profound hypotension); IV only if blood…","Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection — No deep palpation of a suspected AAA (rupture risk); light palpation only. A ruptured AAA bleeds out very quickly. Aortic dissection is a surgical emergency where minutes matter; without intervention it progr…","Patterns You'll Notice Across These Rules","\"The emergency is when blood can't get where it needs to go.\" Tamponade stops the heart from filling, a complete clot in a STEMI stops blood reaching the heart muscle, and a rupture or dissection lets blood escape the vessel. The lectures c…","\"The quality of the pain is a clue.\" Pericarditis: sharp and worse with deep breathing. ACS: longer, more severe, an elephant on the chest, no relief with nitroglycerin. Aortic dissection: sudden, severe and tearing. And some don't hurt at …","\"Look-alikes on the ECG.\" Pericarditis can show ST elevation like an MI; Prinzmetal's angina can show ST elevation that returns to normal; unstable angina's ECG changes return to normal while an MI's stay. The ECG alone isn't the whole stor…","\"Check before you give.\" The MI drugs can push an unstable patient over the edge, so vital signs come first, and several drugs carry a condition: morphine if they can tolerate it, IV nitroglycerin if blood pressure tolerates it, beta blocke…","\"Don't let the patient wait.\" Hesitating or delaying care is a big reason for MI deaths, while treated patients survive about 90 to 95% of the time. Teach patients to call 911 for unstable angina and to seek care if they suspect an MI: bett…"]}]},{"f":"week2-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 2","Part 1A — pH, Hydrogen & Acids","Part 1B — How the Body Defends Its pH","Part 2A — ABGs & the Four Acid-Base Disorders","Part 2B — ABG Interpretation","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 2: the two topic pages that follow, in the order they were taught. Topic 1: acid-base balance (Parts 1A–1B). Topic 2: ABGs and ABG interpretation (Parts 2A–2B). It inte…"]},{"s":"Part 1A — pH, Hydrogen & Acids▾","a":"part-1a-ph-hydrogen-acids","k":["Part 1A — pH, Hydrogen & Acids▾","Topic 1, first voiceover (Acid base 1): what pH is, why hydrogen matters, normal serum pH and how pH varies in the body, what pH affects (including oxygen delivery), the survivable limits, and volatile vs non-volatile acids.","What pH Is","pH, acids and bases","pH is the potential, or the power, of a hydrogen ion. It is a scale that identifies whether a solution is more acidic or more alkaline, based on the hydrogen ions present.","The bottom of the scale is more acidic, the middle is neutral, and the higher the number, the more basic (alkaline).","pH is inversely related to hydrogen ion concentration: when hydrogen ions go up, pH goes down (more acidic); when hydrogen ions go down, pH goes up (more basic).","The scale is logarithmic, not linear: each number is 10 times more acidic or alkaline than the one before it. A pH of 5 is 10 times more acidic than a pH of 6; going from 7 to 6 is a tenfold increase in hydrogen ions.","An acid can donate (release) hydrogen ions. A base can accept hydrogen ions; bases have fewer hydrogen ions and give up a hydroxide ion (OH⁻).","A strong acid completely dissociates in solution (gives up all its hydrogen ions). A weak acid only partially dissociates.","Where Common Things Fall on the Scale","Acidic side (lower numbers)","Milk, coffee, tomato, lemon","Stomach acid (hydrochloric acid)","Battery acid","Neutral and alkaline side","Water: 7, neutral","Blood: on the alkaline side; the slide shows it at 8, but it is really a little lower than 8","Baking soda, stomach tablets (like Alka-Seltzer), soap","Drain cleaner: 14","Why Hydrogen Matters","What hydrogen does in the body","Helps maintain cell membranes.","Helps with enzymatic activity.","Is a component of water and keeps the body hydrated.","Helps with energy production.","Is a component of the sugars, proteins, starches and fats we consume.","The body works to stay in homeostasis and functions best within certain ranges, like a heart rate of 60 to 100 or sodium of 135 to 145. pH is one of those ranges.","Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body","Normal serum (blood) pH: 7.35 to 7.45. Most of the body's enzymes function within that very narrow range.","pH isn't the same everywhere. The stomach is the most acidic place in the body, with hydrochloric acid at a pH more like 1. The stomach is uniquely designed for it: mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate at the cell surface to protect t…","Serum (blood) — 7.35 to 7.45","Saliva — 6.5 to 7.5","Stomach — As low as 1.5 on the GI chart (about 1 in the narration), then pH increases as contents move through the GI tract. The GI tract's differences aid digestion.","Skeletal muscle — 6.9 to 7.2","Bone — 7.4","Liver — 7.2","Pleural fluid — 7.6","What pH Affects","Acid-base balance affects","Electrolytes: sodium, potassium in particular, and chloride.","Hormones.","Oxygen transport and delivery, which is greatly affected.","Acidic environment","Hemoglobin gives up oxygen more readily at the tissue.","Alkalotic environment","Hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and doesn't give it to the tissue, which can result in inadequate tissue oxygenation.","The slide shows this on the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve: the relationship between oxygen saturation of the blood and oxygen partial pressure, which shifts as the blood becomes acidotic or alkalotic.","Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits","Below 7.35 — Acidosis.","7.35 to 7.45 — Normal.","Above 7.45 — Alkalosis.","Below 6.8 or above 7.8 — Incompatible with life, because of pH's profound effects on cellular metabolism, enzyme activity and oxygen delivery.","The body can function outside the normal range for a short time, but because pH affects so much (neurologic, respiratory, GI and cardiovascular function), the goal is to get back to normal as quickly as possible.","Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids","Volatile acids","Can be eliminated as carbon dioxide (CO2) gas, by the lungs (pulmonary ventilation).","The only volatile acid in the body is carbonic acid (H2CO3).","Carbonic acid is a weak acid: it does not easily release its hydrogen ion.","In the presence of the enzyme carbonic anhydrase, it readily dissociates into CO2 and water.","Non-volatile acids","Can be eliminated only by the kidneys.","Most are strong acids: they readily release their hydrogen ions.","Examples: lactic, phosphoric, sulfuric, acetoacetic and beta-hydroxybutyric acid (table below).","Lactic acid — A byproduct of anaerobic metabolism (metabolism without oxygen). A little different: it is metabolized by the liver and kidneys, and once oxygen is reestablished to the tissue it can be reconverted and used for energy again.","Phosphoric acid — Made from phosphorus; works with calcium to help with strong bones, and supports kidney function.","Sulfuric acid — Results from protein metabolism.","Acetoacetic acid — Produced by the liver.","Beta-hydroxybutyric acid — Increases with exercise, calorie restriction and fasting. Its measurement is used as a diagnostic for people in DKA (ketoacidosis).","Why the Body Leans Acidic","At baseline, the body produces more acids than bases: from the food we eat and the drinks we consume, from the metabolism of lipids and proteins, and from cellular metabolism, whose waste product is CO2.","We want to avoid alkalosis, especially if a person is ill, because hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and tissue oxygenation suffers. An alkaline pH is also really difficult to correct."]},{"s":"Part 1B — How the Body Defends Its pH▾","a":"part-1b-how-the-body-defends-its-ph","k":["Part 1B — How the Body Defends Its pH▾","Topic 1, second voiceover (Acid base 2): the three lines of defense, the buffer systems, the carbonic acid equation, cellular compensation and potassium, respiratory and renal control, and compensation.","The Three Lines of Defense","1. Chemical buffer systems — Almost instantaneously (seconds) — Primarily intracellular. Chemicals combine with acid or base to resist a change in pH. Makes only small changes; a relatively short, not sustainable fix.","2. Respiratory system — Several minutes to hours — Changes the rate or depth of breathing to blow off or retain CO2. The lungs are the primary controller of the carbonic acid supply.","3. Renal (kidney) control — Several hours to days — The long-term regulator. Excretes hydrogen ions and retains or excretes bicarb. The kidneys are the primary controller of bicarb (the base).","The lungs and kidneys work together. The lungs respond relatively quickly; the kidneys need more time, but their mechanisms are more long-term.","Buffer Systems","Buffers are chemicals that combine with an acid or base to change the pH and bring it back to normal. They can release or accept a hydrogen ion, and they work almost instantaneously, but they make small, short-term corrections only.","Bicarbonate–carbonic acid — The main buffer outside the cells (extracellular fluid) — CO2 from cell metabolism combines with water to form carbonic acid, which carbonic anhydrase breaks down in the lungs and kidneys (see the equation below)…","Phosphate — The main intracellular buffer — Picks up a hydrogen ion inside the cell to balance the pH inside the cell. It can't be measured. You don't need to memorize its equation.","Protein — Primarily intracellular (most proteins are in cells) — Most proteins can buffer. Carboxyl groups act as weak acids and release hydrogen ions; amino groups act as weak bases and accept them. When pH increases, proteins release hydr…","Hemoglobin — A protein buffer in the blood (some call it a fourth buffer system) — As pH increases, hemoglobin loses hydrogen ions, and the reverse when pH decreases. It also binds CO2 to form carbaminohemoglobin, which carries CO2 to the l…","The Carbonic Acid Equation","CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 (carbonic acid) ⇌ H⁺ + HCO3⁻ (bicarb). The instructor: \"You really want to be familiar and comfortable with the equation and know this backwards and forwards.\"","Lungs — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into CO2 and water, and the lungs exhale the CO2.","Kidneys — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into hydrogen ions and bicarb, and the kidneys excrete the hydrogen ions (and can generate bicarb).","The equation goes both directions: carbonic acid can break down into CO2 and water or form from them, and the same on the bicarb and hydrogen side.","A patient retaining CO2, like a COPD patient, pushes the equation in the other direction and increases bicarb.","CO2 also rises with respiratory depression, such as narcotic use or an overdose: the respiratory rate is so low the lungs can't remove the CO2, which changes the pH.","When a patient is acidotic and not compensating through the respiratory system, sodium bicarb is often given as a quick fix to raise the pH. This happens a lot in the ICU.","Cellular Compensation & Potassium","Cells like a neutral electrical balance. Hydrogen and potassium are both positively charged, and potassium is found primarily inside the cell.","In acidosis — Hydrogen ions move into the cell to balance the pH, and potassium moves out of the cell, which can raise blood potassium.","With kidney dysfunction — The kidneys can't get rid of the excess potassium: hyperkalemia, which can cause dysrhythmias.","When pH returns to normal — Hydrogen ions move back into the blood and potassium moves back into the cells.","With working kidneys — The kidneys will already have excreted the excess potassium, so this can lead to hypokalemia (to be revisited with DKA later in the course).","Cellular compensation is a temporary correction of the pH, not a permanent solution.","Respiratory Control of pH","The body produces CO2 continuously, which forms carbonic acid; in the lungs it breaks back down into water and CO2, and the excess CO2 is exhaled.","This works only on volatile acids, not on non-volatile acids like lactic acid.","The lungs adjust serum pH by changing the rate or depth of breathing. The brain and arterial receptors are stimulated by the pH change.","On mechanical ventilation in the ICU, these adjustments can be made through the ventilator settings.","pH too low (acidosis)","Breathing rate or depth increases → more CO2 blown off → less carbonic acid → pH rises.","pH too high (alkalosis)","Breathing rate or depth decreases → CO2 retained → more carbonic acid → pH falls.","Renal Control of pH","The kidneys are the last to alter pH and are the long-term regulators. They can eliminate large amounts of acid by getting rid of hydrogen ions, using an active transport mechanism.","They can also excrete bicarb (a base) if the pH is too high, or retain bicarb if the pH is too low.","Example: in a COPD patient retaining CO2, over time the kidneys compensate by holding on to more bicarb, so this patient has higher CO2 and higher bicarb to balance the serum pH.","Kidney function is extremely important: in renal failure, patients can no longer maintain a normal pH and often take bicarb daily.","Acidosis — Increased — Increased — Raises pH back to normal","Alkalosis — Decreased — Decreased — Lowers pH back to normal","The kidneys handle acidosis better than alkalosis. It is harder for the kidneys to excrete bicarb than to hold on to it.","Compensation","If one side of the equation is the problem, the body pulls toward the other side to rebalance. That movement is compensation: the body's response to an acid-base imbalance, aimed at bringing the pH back to normal. It is evaluated with arter…","Underlying problem is metabolic","The respiratory system compensates.","Underlying problem is respiratory","The renal system compensates, bringing about metabolic changes (metabolic compensation).","Uncompensated — Nothing has changed in response to a pH outside normal limits.","Partial compensation — Bicarb and CO2 levels have changed, but the pH is still outside normal limits.","Complete compensation — Bicarb or CO2 levels are abnormal, but the pH is back within normal limits.","What an ABG Measures","The lecture ends by previewing the ABG: pH, CO2 (PaCO2), PO2, HCO3 (bicarb) and O2 saturation. \"You do need to know these numbers.\" They are taught in the next lecture (Part 2A)."]},{"s":"Part 2A — ABGs & the Four Acid-Base Disorders▾","a":"part-2a-abgs-the-four-acid-base-disorders","k":["Part 2A — ABGs & the Four Acid-Base Disorders▾","Topic 2, first voiceover (ABGs): why ABGs are drawn, the normal values, the key terms, ROME, and each of the four disorders with its ABG pattern, causes, signs and treatment.","Why We Draw ABGs","Purpose of an arterial blood gas","To assess acid-base status, determine the adequacy of oxygenation and ventilation, and decide which treatment would be best.","Arterial blood gives the best representation of the pH and PaCO2 in the body.","A venous blood gas (VBG) can be drawn, but the measurements differ: the CO2 is a bit higher (it's venous blood), which changes the pH measurement. If an arterial draw isn't possible, a VBG can gauge where things stand, but for critically il…","Normal ABG Values","\"You do need to know these numbers.\" pH 7.35–7.45 · PaCO2 35–45 · HCO3 24–29.","pH — 7.35 to 7.45 — The balance of hydrogen ions.","PaCO2 — 35 to 45 — The respiratory parameter: the partial pressure of CO2 in the artery (the carbonic acid that dissolves into CO2 and water).","HCO3 (bicarb) — 24 to 29 — The metabolic parameter. It is calculated on the ABG, not directly measured.","Serum CO2 (BMP) — 24 to 29 — On a regular venous chemistry panel (BMP), the CO2 level is equivalent to the bicarb.","The ABG also reports PO2 and O2 saturation, and the slide compares ABG and VBG values (a VBG reports PvCO2, the partial pressure of CO2 in the vein, with a slightly different pH). The transcript doesn't read those numbers aloud, so they are…","Key Terms","Primary event — The problem that initiates the acid-base imbalance: the root cause. — Hypoventilation, hyperventilation, vomiting, diarrhea.","Primary disorder — What happens as a result of the primary event. — Hypoventilation retains CO2 → respiratory acidosis. Vomiting up stomach acid → metabolic alkalosis.","Compensatory mechanisms — The processes the body uses to bring acid-base balance back, without correcting the underlying cause. — A lung problem → the kidneys compensate (and can keep it up longer, if they function properly). A kidney probl…","Metabolic cause — The bicarb level changes because of metabolic alterations (think kidneys). — Metabolic acidosis and metabolic alkalosis.","Respiratory cause — The carbonic acid (CO2) level changes because of alterations in the lungs. — Respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis.","ROME & the Four Primary Disorders","ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Respiratory: pH and CO2 move in opposite directions (pH up, CO2 down = alkalosis; pH down, CO2 up = acidosis). Metabolic: pH and bicarb move in the same direction (both up = alkalosis; both down …","Metabolic acidosis — Bicarb ↓ — ↓ — CO2 ↓","Metabolic alkalosis — Bicarb ↑ — ↑ — CO2 ↑","Respiratory acidosis — CO2 ↑ — ↓ — Bicarb ↑","Respiratory alkalosis — CO2 ↓ — ↑ — Bicarb ↓","Notice the compensating value always moves in the same direction as the primary change.","Metabolic Alkalosis","Too much bicarb, or not enough carbonic acid (acid).","ABG — pH high; bicarb high (greater than 29); CO2 typically normal if uncompensated, and high once the lungs compensate.","Causes — Excessive baking soda or antacids (Alka-Seltzer): too much base. The lecture links this to hypokalemia, with hydrogen shifting out of the cell and potassium going in. Prolonged vomiting (losing hydrochloric acid, so hydrogen ions).…","Manifestations — CNS over-excitability, tremors, muscle cramps, confusion up to coma, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and respiratory depression (the body holding on to CO2 to counteract the alkalosis).","Cardiac risk — With a high pH, hydrogen ions come out of the cell to buffer, and potassium goes into the cell, so less potassium circulates: hypokalemia, with its classic cardiac issues.","Compensation — The lungs decrease the rate and depth of breathing to hold on to CO2.","Treatment — Treat the cause, such as the vomiting. Drugs that reduce gastric hydrochloric acid secretion: H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors.","Respiratory Alkalosis","The lecture introduces it as a \"bicarb deficit in our extracellular fluid,\" most often caused by hyperventilation (the primary event), with too much CO2 blown off.","ABG — pH high; CO2 low (blowing off CO2); bicarb relatively normal, because the kidneys are not yet compensating.","Why the kidneys can't help acutely — The kidneys take hours to days. A person hyperventilating in a panic attack often passes out; their breathing then regulates and CO2 builds back up.","Causes — Hyperventilation (anxiety, panic attack); increased metabolic demand (fever, infection, sepsis); some medications that increase breathing rate; head injuries, brain lesions or chronic brain injury; pulmonary diseases; ventilator se…","Manifestations — Similar to metabolic alkalosis: CNS over-excitability, light-headedness, syncopal episodes, up to coma.","Treatment — Help retain CO2 and decrease respirations: anti-anxiety medications, calming the patient, breathing into a paper bag (inhaling CO2). On a ventilator, change the settings.","Respiratory Acidosis","Can be acute or chronic. Excess CO2 means too much carbonic acid, so too high a hydrogen ion concentration. Primarily caused by hypoventilation: CO2 rises and pH drops.","Acute (e.g., respiratory arrest)","pH low.","CO2 high.","Bicarb pretty normal: the kidneys haven't had time to compensate.","Chronic (e.g., COPD retaining CO2)","The kidneys have had time to compensate.","Bicarb high.","pH closer to normal, or normal when fully compensated.","The COPD Example: Acute on Chronic","This client's baseline (chronic) — 60 — 32 — Normal (chronic, compensated)","Now with an infection (breathing worse) — 75 — Still 32: the kidneys haven't had a chance to compensate — Drops: more acidic","Causes — New-onset CNS depression and hypoventilation: overdose, found down, too much narcotic or sedative given or taken; respiratory arrest; head injury; anesthesia and post-anesthesia (the body can take longer to metabolize the drugs); p…","Manifestations — Difficulty breathing or respiratory distress; slow, shallow breathing; dysrhythmias and ECG changes; decreased level of consciousness, eventually coma. The patient feels like they can't catch their breath.","Interventions — Improve ventilation (increase the rate and depth of breathing to blow off CO2): stimulate the patient, encourage slow and deep breathing; on a ventilator, increase the respiratory rate or the tidal volume.","Metabolic Acidosis","Too much acid or not enough bicarb: a bicarb deficit in the blood.","ABG — pH low (below 7.35); bicarb low (same direction as pH). CO2 somewhat normal early, before the lungs compensate; once they do, the lungs breathe faster to blow off CO2, lowering it.","Causes — Renal disease or failure (can't excrete hydrogen ions; dialysis patients take daily bicarb); fistulas (bicarb lost with fluid and electrolytes); diabetes, especially type 1, with DKA; lactic acidosis (cells without enough oxygen); …","Manifestations — Lethargy, confusion, headache; tremors, muscle cramps, muscle twitching; paresthesias (tingling of fingers, toes, perioral); hypotension; hyperkalemia (hydrogen ions pulled into the cell, potassium out); warm, flushed skin;…","Compensation — Compensatory hyperventilation: deep breaths called Kussmaul respirations, common in DKA, as the lungs try to blow off CO2 and reduce the acidosis.","Treatment (DKA) — DKA's high ketones are acids. Treat by bringing the blood glucose down with insulin to decrease the acid in the body (more later in the course).","One line in the recording says that if the lungs are compensating, the CO2 \"is going to be increased.\" The rest of the lecture (the arrow table, Kussmaul breathing to blow off CO2, and the worked example of 7.35 / 33 / 20) shows the CO2 goi…"]},{"s":"Part 2B — ABG Interpretation▾","a":"part-2b-abg-interpretation","k":["Part 2B — ABG Interpretation▾","Topic 2, second voiceover (ABG Interpretation): how compensation works, the four levels of compensation, the step-by-step method, every worked example, and the two shortcut methods.","How Compensation Works","Respiratory compensation (lungs)","Triggered by a metabolic problem.","Rapid: within a few minutes to a few hours.","Hyperventilation (blow off CO2) or hypoventilation (conserve CO2), depending on whether it's correcting an acidosis or an alkalosis.","Metabolic compensation (kidneys)","Triggered by a respiratory problem.","Slow: hours to days.","Retains or excretes hydrogen ions and bicarb as needed.","The Four Levels of Compensation","Uncompensated — Abnormal — Only one (the acid or the base component) is abnormal, not both.","Partially compensated — Still abnormal — Both are abnormal: the body has started to respond, moving the second component to pull the pH back.","Compensated — Within normal limits — Still abnormal, but balanced. The imbalance is neutralized, which is not the same as corrected.","Corrected — Within normal limits — Also within normal limits, after whatever caused the imbalance has been corrected.","Not everyone returns to \"corrected.\" A COPD patient holds on to CO2, and their bicarb stays high to balance it; because COPD is chronic, you will never see a fully corrected ABG.","Interpreting an ABG Step by Step","1. pH — Acidic, basic or normal? If the pH is normal but the CO2 and bicarb are not, compensation has occurred. Then decide which way the normal pH leans: treat 7.40 as neutral; 7.35 to 7.40 leans acidic, above 7.40 to 7.45 leans basic (e.g…","2. PaCO2 (35 to 45) — Acidic (high), basic (low) or normal? If it is abnormal and matches the pH, it's a respiratory problem: an inverse relationship with pH (Respiratory Opposite).","3. HCO3 (24 to 29) — A calculated value. Basic (high), acidic (low) or normal? If it matches the pH, it's a metabolic problem: a direct relationship with pH (Metabolic Equal).","4. Compensation — Is the body trying to restore the pH by altering the system that is not involved in the imbalance? If compensation has occurred, that value moves in the same direction as the other component. Use the four levels above.","With compensation, the pH points the way. When the pH is normal, the side it leans toward (acidic or basic) tells you which value is the primary problem and which is compensating.","Worked Examples From the Lecture","Cover the interpretation column with the eye button and work each one yourself. Values are pH / PaCO2 / HCO3.","7.25 / 56 / 24 — Respiratory acidosis (no compensation) — pH down, CO2 up (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.35 / 56 / 35 — Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the high CO2; the high bicarb is compensating.","7.60 / 25 / 24 — Respiratory alkalosis (no compensation) — pH up, CO2 down (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.45 / 30 / 20 — Fully compensated respiratory alkalosis — pH normal but leaning basic, matching the low CO2; the low bicarb is compensating.","7.26 / 40 / 18 — Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — pH down, bicarb down (equal); CO2 normal.","7.35 / 33 / 20 — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the low bicarb; the low CO2 is compensating.","7.46 / 37 / 26 — Metabolic alkalosis (as given in the lecture) — pH basic, CO2 normal; the lecture calls the bicarb of 26 \"on the higher end.\"","Normal pH leaning basic / high CO2 / 30 — Compensated metabolic alkalosis — Leaning basic matches the high bicarb; the high CO2 is compensating.","7.30 / 52 / 30 — Respiratory acidosis, partially compensated — pH and CO2 both acidic; the kidneys are retaining bicarb, but the pH is still abnormal.","7.60 / 31 / 26 — Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Tic-tac-toe: pH and CO2 line up in the basic column; bicarb is normal.","Below 7.35 / 29 / 21 — Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — pH and bicarb both acidic; the CO2 is abnormal (basic), but the pH is not yet normal.","7.52 / 48 / 39 — Metabolic alkalosis, partially compensated — pH and bicarb both basic; CO2 abnormal (acidic); pH still abnormal.","The lecture ends with a slide of extra practice ABGs to work through on your own; their values aren't read aloud in the transcript.","Two Shortcut Methods","\"It's important to know this information and to rationalize all these steps.\" These are extra ways to get there, if they fit how you think.","ABG tic-tac-toe","Draw a board with three columns: Acidic, Normal, Basic.","Place the pH, CO2 and bicarb in the column each one falls in.","The value that lines up with the pH names the problem (CO2 = respiratory, bicarb = metabolic), and the pH's column names acidosis or alkalosis.","If the third value is in the normal column, it's uncompensated.","The instructor's three lines","Draw a line each for pH, CO2 and bicarb, always in that order.","pH: low end Acidic → high end Basic.","CO2: opposite, low is Basic → high is Acidic.","Bicarb: low is Acidic → high is Basic.","Memorize \"AB, BA, AB,\" mark where each value falls, and see what matches the pH."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One row per part: the points to have cold before an exam. Every detail is taught in full in the parts above.","1A. pH, Hydrogen & Acids — More hydrogen ions = lower pH; the scale is logarithmic (each number is 10 times). Normal serum pH 7.35–7.45; below 7.35 is acidosis, above 7.45 alkalosis; below 6.8 or above 7.8 is incompatible with life. Acidic …","1B. How the Body Defends Its pH — Buffers act in seconds, the lungs in minutes to hours, the kidneys in hours to days. Bicarbonate–carbonic acid is the main extracellular buffer; phosphate and protein buffers are intracellular. Know CO2 + H…","2A. ABGs & the Four Disorders — Know the numbers: pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO2 35–45, HCO3 24–29 (BMP CO2 = bicarb). ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Metabolic alkalosis: vomiting, NG suction, loop diuretics, antacids (treat with H2 bloc…","2B. ABG Interpretation — Step 1 pH (7.40 is the midpoint), step 2 PaCO2, step 3 HCO3, step 4 compensation. The value that matches the pH is the problem; the other one moving the same direction is compensating. Uncompensated: one abnormal va…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the four parts above: a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week2-acid-base-balance.html","t":"Acid-Base Balance","secs":[{"s":"Acid-Base Balance","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 1","What pH Is","Why Hydrogen Matters","Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body","What pH Affects","Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits","Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids","The Three Lines of Defense","Buffer Systems","The Carbonic Acid Equation","Cellular Compensation & Potassium","Respiratory Control of pH","Renal Control of pH","Compensation","What an ABG Measures","Self-Test Flashcards","This page combines the two acid-base voiceovers. Part 1 covers what pH is, why hydrogen matters, the normal serum range and how pH varies around the body, what pH affects (including oxygen delivery), the survivable limits, and volatile vs n…"]},{"s":"What pH Is▾","a":"what-ph-is","k":["What pH Is▾","pH, acids and bases","pH is the potential, or the power, of a hydrogen ion. It is a scale that identifies whether a solution is more acidic or more alkaline, based on the hydrogen ions present.","The bottom of the scale is more acidic, the middle is neutral, and the higher the number, the more basic (alkaline).","pH is inversely related to hydrogen ion concentration: when hydrogen ions go up, pH goes down (more acidic); when hydrogen ions go down, pH goes up (more basic).","The scale is logarithmic, not linear: each number is 10 times more acidic or alkaline than the one before it. A pH of 5 is 10 times more acidic than a pH of 6; going from 7 to 6 is a tenfold increase in hydrogen ions.","An acid can donate (release) hydrogen ions. A base can accept hydrogen ions; bases have fewer hydrogen ions and give up a hydroxide ion (OH⁻).","A strong acid completely dissociates in solution (gives up all its hydrogen ions). A weak acid only partially dissociates.","Where Common Things Fall on the Scale","Acidic side (lower numbers)","Milk, coffee, tomato, lemon","Stomach acid (hydrochloric acid)","Battery acid","Neutral and alkaline side","Water: 7, neutral","Blood: on the alkaline side; the slide shows it at 8, but it is really a little lower than 8","Baking soda, stomach tablets (like Alka-Seltzer), soap","Drain cleaner: 14"]},{"s":"Why Hydrogen Matters▾","a":"why-hydrogen-matters","k":["Why Hydrogen Matters▾","What hydrogen does in the body","Helps maintain cell membranes.","Helps with enzymatic activity.","Is a component of water and keeps the body hydrated.","Helps with energy production.","Is a component of the sugars, proteins, starches and fats we consume.","The body works to stay in homeostasis and functions best within certain ranges, like a heart rate of 60 to 100 or sodium of 135 to 145. pH is one of those ranges."]},{"s":"Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body▾","a":"normal-ph-how-it-varies-in-the-body","k":["Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body▾","Normal serum (blood) pH: 7.35 to 7.45. Most of the body's enzymes function within that very narrow range.","pH isn't the same everywhere. The stomach is the most acidic place in the body, with hydrochloric acid at a pH more like 1. The stomach is uniquely designed for it: mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate at the cell surface to protect t…","Serum (blood) — 7.35 to 7.45","Saliva — 6.5 to 7.5","Stomach — As low as 1.5 on the GI chart (about 1 in the narration), then pH increases as contents move through the GI tract. The GI tract's differences aid digestion.","Skeletal muscle — 6.9 to 7.2","Bone — 7.4","Liver — 7.2","Pleural fluid — 7.6"]},{"s":"What pH Affects▾","a":"what-ph-affects","k":["What pH Affects▾","Acid-base balance affects","Electrolytes: sodium, potassium in particular, and chloride.","Hormones.","Oxygen transport and delivery, which is greatly affected.","Acidic environment","Hemoglobin gives up oxygen more readily at the tissue.","Alkalotic environment","Hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and doesn't give it to the tissue, which can result in inadequate tissue oxygenation.","The slide shows this on the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve: the relationship between oxygen saturation of the blood and oxygen partial pressure, which shifts as the blood becomes acidotic or alkalotic."]},{"s":"Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits▾","a":"acidosis-alkalosis-survivable-limits","k":["Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits▾","Below 7.35 — Acidosis.","7.35 to 7.45 — Normal.","Above 7.45 — Alkalosis.","Below 6.8 or above 7.8 — Incompatible with life, because of pH's profound effects on cellular metabolism, enzyme activity and oxygen delivery.","The body can function outside the normal range for a short time, but because pH affects so much (neurologic, respiratory, GI and cardiovascular function), the goal is to get back to normal as quickly as possible."]},{"s":"Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids▾","a":"volatile-vs-non-volatile-acids","k":["Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids▾","Volatile acids","Can be eliminated as carbon dioxide (CO2) gas, by the lungs (pulmonary ventilation).","The only volatile acid in the body is carbonic acid (H2CO3).","Carbonic acid is a weak acid: it does not easily release its hydrogen ion.","In the presence of the enzyme carbonic anhydrase, it readily dissociates into CO2 and water.","Non-volatile acids","Can be eliminated only by the kidneys.","Most are strong acids: they readily release their hydrogen ions.","Examples: lactic, phosphoric, sulfuric, acetoacetic and beta-hydroxybutyric acid (table below).","Lactic acid — A byproduct of anaerobic metabolism (metabolism without oxygen). A little different: it is metabolized by the liver and kidneys, and once oxygen is reestablished to the tissue it can be reconverted and used for energy again.","Phosphoric acid — Made from phosphorus; works with calcium to help with strong bones, and supports kidney function.","Sulfuric acid — Results from protein metabolism.","Acetoacetic acid — Produced by the liver.","Beta-hydroxybutyric acid — Increases with exercise, calorie restriction and fasting. Its measurement is used as a diagnostic for people in DKA (ketoacidosis).","Why the Body Leans Acidic","At baseline, the body produces more acids than bases: from the food we eat and the drinks we consume, from the metabolism of lipids and proteins, and from cellular metabolism, whose waste product is CO2.","We want to avoid alkalosis, especially if a person is ill, because hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and tissue oxygenation suffers. An alkaline pH is also really difficult to correct."]},{"s":"The Three Lines of Defense▾","a":"the-three-lines-of-defense","k":["The Three Lines of Defense▾","1. Chemical buffer systems — Almost instantaneously (seconds) — Primarily intracellular. Chemicals combine with acid or base to resist a change in pH. Makes only small changes; a relatively short, not sustainable fix.","2. Respiratory system — Several minutes to hours — Changes the rate or depth of breathing to blow off or retain CO2. The lungs are the primary controller of the carbonic acid supply.","3. Renal (kidney) control — Several hours to days — The long-term regulator. Excretes hydrogen ions and retains or excretes bicarb. The kidneys are the primary controller of bicarb (the base).","The lungs and kidneys work together. The lungs respond relatively quickly; the kidneys need more time, but their mechanisms are more long-term."]},{"s":"Buffer Systems▾","a":"buffer-systems","k":["Buffer Systems▾","Buffers are chemicals that combine with an acid or base to change the pH and bring it back to normal. They can release or accept a hydrogen ion, and they work almost instantaneously, but they make small, short-term corrections only.","Bicarbonate–carbonic acid — The main buffer outside the cells (extracellular fluid) — CO2 from cell metabolism combines with water to form carbonic acid, which carbonic anhydrase breaks down in the lungs and kidneys (see the equation below)…","Phosphate — The main intracellular buffer — Picks up a hydrogen ion inside the cell to balance the pH inside the cell. It can't be measured. You don't need to memorize its equation.","Protein — Primarily intracellular (most proteins are in cells) — Most proteins can buffer. Carboxyl groups act as weak acids and release hydrogen ions; amino groups act as weak bases and accept them. When pH increases, proteins release hydr…","Hemoglobin — A protein buffer in the blood (some call it a fourth buffer system) — As pH increases, hemoglobin loses hydrogen ions, and the reverse when pH decreases. It also binds CO2 to form carbaminohemoglobin, which carries CO2 to the l…"]},{"s":"The Carbonic Acid Equation▾","a":"the-carbonic-acid-equation","k":["The Carbonic Acid Equation▾","CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 (carbonic acid) ⇌ H⁺ + HCO3⁻ (bicarb). The instructor: \"You really want to be familiar and comfortable with the equation and know this backwards and forwards.\"","Lungs — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into CO2 and water, and the lungs exhale the CO2.","Kidneys — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into hydrogen ions and bicarb, and the kidneys excrete the hydrogen ions (and can generate bicarb).","The equation goes both directions: carbonic acid can break down into CO2 and water or form from them, and the same on the bicarb and hydrogen side.","A patient retaining CO2, like a COPD patient, pushes the equation in the other direction and increases bicarb.","CO2 also rises with respiratory depression, such as narcotic use or an overdose: the respiratory rate is so low the lungs can't remove the CO2, which changes the pH.","When a patient is acidotic and not compensating through the respiratory system, sodium bicarb is often given as a quick fix to raise the pH. This happens a lot in the ICU."]},{"s":"Cellular Compensation & Potassium▾","a":"cellular-compensation-potassium","k":["Cellular Compensation & Potassium▾","Cells like a neutral electrical balance. Hydrogen and potassium are both positively charged, and potassium is found primarily inside the cell.","In acidosis — Hydrogen ions move into the cell to balance the pH, and potassium moves out of the cell, which can raise blood potassium.","With kidney dysfunction — The kidneys can't get rid of the excess potassium: hyperkalemia, which can cause dysrhythmias.","When pH returns to normal — Hydrogen ions move back into the blood and potassium moves back into the cells.","With working kidneys — The kidneys will already have excreted the excess potassium, so this can lead to hypokalemia (to be revisited with DKA later in the course).","Cellular compensation is a temporary correction of the pH, not a permanent solution."]},{"s":"Respiratory Control of pH▾","a":"respiratory-control-of-ph","k":["Respiratory Control of pH▾","The body produces CO2 continuously, which forms carbonic acid; in the lungs it breaks back down into water and CO2, and the excess CO2 is exhaled.","This works only on volatile acids, not on non-volatile acids like lactic acid.","The lungs adjust serum pH by changing the rate or depth of breathing. The brain and arterial receptors are stimulated by the pH change.","On mechanical ventilation in the ICU, these adjustments can be made through the ventilator settings.","pH too low (acidosis)","Breathing rate or depth increases → more CO2 blown off → less carbonic acid → pH rises.","pH too high (alkalosis)","Breathing rate or depth decreases → CO2 retained → more carbonic acid → pH falls."]},{"s":"Renal Control of pH▾","a":"renal-control-of-ph","k":["Renal Control of pH▾","The kidneys are the last to alter pH and are the long-term regulators. They can eliminate large amounts of acid by getting rid of hydrogen ions, using an active transport mechanism.","They can also excrete bicarb (a base) if the pH is too high, or retain bicarb if the pH is too low.","Example: in a COPD patient retaining CO2, over time the kidneys compensate by holding on to more bicarb, so this patient has higher CO2 and higher bicarb to balance the serum pH.","Kidney function is extremely important: in renal failure, patients can no longer maintain a normal pH and often take bicarb daily.","Acidosis — Increased — Increased — Raises pH back to normal","Alkalosis — Decreased — Decreased — Lowers pH back to normal","The kidneys handle acidosis better than alkalosis. It is harder for the kidneys to excrete bicarb than to hold on to it."]},{"s":"Compensation▾","a":"compensation","k":["Compensation▾","If one side of the equation is the problem, the body pulls toward the other side to rebalance. That movement is compensation: the body's response to an acid-base imbalance, aimed at bringing the pH back to normal. It is evaluated with arter…","Underlying problem is metabolic","The respiratory system compensates.","Underlying problem is respiratory","The renal system compensates, bringing about metabolic changes (metabolic compensation).","Uncompensated — Nothing has changed in response to a pH outside normal limits.","Partial compensation — Bicarb and CO2 levels have changed, but the pH is still outside normal limits.","Complete compensation — Bicarb or CO2 levels are abnormal, but the pH is back within normal limits.","The four levels of compensation (adding \"corrected\") are covered in ABGs & ABG Interpretation"]},{"s":"What an ABG Measures▾","a":"what-an-abg-measures","k":["What an ABG Measures▾","The lecture ends by previewing the ABG: pH, CO2 (PaCO2), PO2, HCO3 (bicarb) and O2 saturation. \"You do need to know these numbers.\" They are taught in the next lecture.","Normal ABG values are covered in ABGs & ABG Interpretation"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-abg-interpretation.html","t":"ABGs & ABG Interpretation","secs":[{"s":"ABGs & ABG Interpretation","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 2","Why We Draw ABGs","Normal ABG Values","Key Terms","ROME & the Four Primary Disorders","Metabolic Alkalosis","Respiratory Alkalosis","Respiratory Acidosis","Metabolic Acidosis","How Compensation Works","The Four Levels of Compensation","Interpreting an ABG Step by Step","Worked Examples From the Lecture","Two Shortcut Methods","Self-Test Flashcards","This page combines the ABG voiceover and the ABG interpretation voiceover. It covers why we draw ABGs, the normal values, the key terms (primary event, primary disorder, compensation), the four acid-base disorders with their ABG patterns, c…","pH, buffers, the carbonic acid equation, and how the lungs and kidneys compensate are covered in Acid-Base Balance"]},{"s":"Why We Draw ABGs▾","a":"why-we-draw-abgs","k":["Why We Draw ABGs▾","Purpose of an arterial blood gas","To assess acid-base status, determine the adequacy of oxygenation and ventilation, and decide which treatment would be best.","Arterial blood gives the best representation of the pH and PaCO2 in the body.","A venous blood gas (VBG) can be drawn, but the measurements differ: the CO2 is a bit higher (it's venous blood), which changes the pH measurement. If an arterial draw isn't possible, a VBG can gauge where things stand, but for critically il…"]},{"s":"Normal ABG Values▾","a":"normal-abg-values","k":["Normal ABG Values▾","\"You do need to know these numbers.\" pH 7.35–7.45 · PaCO2 35–45 · HCO3 24–29.","pH — 7.35 to 7.45 — The balance of hydrogen ions.","PaCO2 — 35 to 45 — The respiratory parameter: the partial pressure of CO2 in the artery (the carbonic acid that dissolves into CO2 and water).","HCO3 (bicarb) — 24 to 29 — The metabolic parameter. It is calculated on the ABG, not directly measured.","Serum CO2 (BMP) — 24 to 29 — On a regular venous chemistry panel (BMP), the CO2 level is equivalent to the bicarb.","The ABG also reports PO2 and O2 saturation, and the slide compares ABG and VBG values (a VBG reports PvCO2, the partial pressure of CO2 in the vein, with a slightly different pH). The transcript doesn't read those numbers aloud, so they are…"]},{"s":"Key Terms▾","a":"key-terms","k":["Key Terms▾","Primary event — The problem that initiates the acid-base imbalance: the root cause. — Hypoventilation, hyperventilation, vomiting, diarrhea.","Primary disorder — What happens as a result of the primary event. — Hypoventilation retains CO2 → respiratory acidosis. Vomiting up stomach acid → metabolic alkalosis.","Compensatory mechanisms — The processes the body uses to bring acid-base balance back, without correcting the underlying cause. — A lung problem → the kidneys compensate (and can keep it up longer, if they function properly). A kidney probl…","Metabolic cause — The bicarb level changes because of metabolic alterations (think kidneys). — Metabolic acidosis and metabolic alkalosis.","Respiratory cause — The carbonic acid (CO2) level changes because of alterations in the lungs. — Respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis."]},{"s":"ROME & the Four Primary Disorders▾","a":"rome-the-four-primary-disorders","k":["ROME & the Four Primary Disorders▾","ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Respiratory: pH and CO2 move in opposite directions (pH up, CO2 down = alkalosis; pH down, CO2 up = acidosis). Metabolic: pH and bicarb move in the same direction (both up = alkalosis; both down …","Metabolic acidosis — Bicarb ↓ — ↓ — CO2 ↓","Metabolic alkalosis — Bicarb ↑ — ↑ — CO2 ↑","Respiratory acidosis — CO2 ↑ — ↓ — Bicarb ↑","Respiratory alkalosis — CO2 ↓ — ↑ — Bicarb ↓","Notice the compensating value always moves in the same direction as the primary change."]},{"s":"Metabolic Alkalosis▾","a":"metabolic-alkalosis","k":["Metabolic Alkalosis▾","Too much bicarb, or not enough carbonic acid (acid).","ABG — pH high; bicarb high (greater than 29); CO2 typically normal if uncompensated, and high once the lungs compensate.","Causes — Excessive baking soda or antacids (Alka-Seltzer): too much base. The lecture links this to hypokalemia, with hydrogen shifting out of the cell and potassium going in. Prolonged vomiting (losing hydrochloric acid, so hydrogen ions).…","Manifestations — CNS over-excitability, tremors, muscle cramps, confusion up to coma, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and respiratory depression (the body holding on to CO2 to counteract the alkalosis).","Cardiac risk — With a high pH, hydrogen ions come out of the cell to buffer, and potassium goes into the cell, so less potassium circulates: hypokalemia, with its classic cardiac issues.","Compensation — The lungs decrease the rate and depth of breathing to hold on to CO2.","Treatment — Treat the cause, such as the vomiting. Drugs that reduce gastric hydrochloric acid secretion: H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors.","The hydrogen–potassium shift is covered in Acid-Base Balance"]},{"s":"Respiratory Alkalosis▾","a":"respiratory-alkalosis","k":["Respiratory Alkalosis▾","The lecture introduces it as a \"bicarb deficit in our extracellular fluid,\" most often caused by hyperventilation (the primary event), with too much CO2 blown off.","ABG — pH high; CO2 low (blowing off CO2); bicarb relatively normal, because the kidneys are not yet compensating.","Why the kidneys can't help acutely — The kidneys take hours to days. A person hyperventilating in a panic attack often passes out; their breathing then regulates and CO2 builds back up.","Causes — Hyperventilation (anxiety, panic attack); increased metabolic demand (fever, infection, sepsis); some medications that increase breathing rate; head injuries, brain lesions or chronic brain injury; pulmonary diseases; ventilator se…","Manifestations — Similar to metabolic alkalosis: CNS over-excitability, light-headedness, syncopal episodes, up to coma.","Treatment — Help retain CO2 and decrease respirations: anti-anxiety medications, calming the patient, breathing into a paper bag (inhaling CO2). On a ventilator, change the settings."]},{"s":"Respiratory Acidosis▾","a":"respiratory-acidosis","k":["Respiratory Acidosis▾","Can be acute or chronic. Excess CO2 means too much carbonic acid, so too high a hydrogen ion concentration. Primarily caused by hypoventilation: CO2 rises and pH drops.","Acute (e.g., respiratory arrest)","pH low.","CO2 high.","Bicarb pretty normal: the kidneys haven't had time to compensate.","Chronic (e.g., COPD retaining CO2)","The kidneys have had time to compensate.","Bicarb high.","pH closer to normal, or normal when fully compensated.","The COPD Example: Acute on Chronic","This client's baseline (chronic) — 60 — 32 — Normal (chronic, compensated)","Now with an infection (breathing worse) — 75 — Still 32: the kidneys haven't had a chance to compensate — Drops: more acidic","Causes — New-onset CNS depression and hypoventilation: overdose, found down, too much narcotic or sedative given or taken; respiratory arrest; head injury; anesthesia and post-anesthesia (the body can take longer to metabolize the drugs); p…","Manifestations — Difficulty breathing or respiratory distress; slow, shallow breathing; dysrhythmias and ECG changes; decreased level of consciousness, eventually coma. The patient feels like they can't catch their breath.","Interventions — Improve ventilation (increase the rate and depth of breathing to blow off CO2): stimulate the patient, encourage slow and deep breathing; on a ventilator, increase the respiratory rate or the tidal volume."]},{"s":"Metabolic Acidosis▾","a":"metabolic-acidosis","k":["Metabolic Acidosis▾","Too much acid or not enough bicarb: a bicarb deficit in the blood.","ABG — pH low (below 7.35); bicarb low (same direction as pH). CO2 somewhat normal early, before the lungs compensate; once they do, the lungs breathe faster to blow off CO2, lowering it.","Causes — Renal disease or failure (can't excrete hydrogen ions; dialysis patients take daily bicarb); fistulas (bicarb lost with fluid and electrolytes); diabetes, especially type 1, with DKA; lactic acidosis (cells without enough oxygen); …","Manifestations — Lethargy, confusion, headache; tremors, muscle cramps, muscle twitching; paresthesias (tingling of fingers, toes, perioral); hypotension; hyperkalemia (hydrogen ions pulled into the cell, potassium out); warm, flushed skin;…","Compensation — Compensatory hyperventilation: deep breaths called Kussmaul respirations, common in DKA, as the lungs try to blow off CO2 and reduce the acidosis.","Treatment (DKA) — DKA's high ketones are acids. Treat by bringing the blood glucose down with insulin to decrease the acid in the body (more later in the course).","One line in the recording says that if the lungs are compensating, the CO2 \"is going to be increased.\" The rest of the lecture (the arrow table, Kussmaul breathing to blow off CO2, and the worked example of 7.35 / 33 / 20) shows the CO2 goi…"]},{"s":"How Compensation Works▾","a":"how-compensation-works","k":["How Compensation Works▾","Respiratory compensation (lungs)","Triggered by a metabolic problem.","Rapid: within a few minutes to a few hours.","Hyperventilation (blow off CO2) or hypoventilation (conserve CO2), depending on whether it's correcting an acidosis or an alkalosis.","Metabolic compensation (kidneys)","Triggered by a respiratory problem.","Slow: hours to days.","Retains or excretes hydrogen ions and bicarb as needed."]},{"s":"The Four Levels of Compensation▾","a":"the-four-levels-of-compensation","k":["The Four Levels of Compensation▾","Uncompensated — Abnormal — Only one (the acid or the base component) is abnormal, not both.","Partially compensated — Still abnormal — Both are abnormal: the body has started to respond, moving the second component to pull the pH back.","Compensated — Within normal limits — Still abnormal, but balanced. The imbalance is neutralized, which is not the same as corrected.","Corrected — Within normal limits — Also within normal limits, after whatever caused the imbalance has been corrected.","Not everyone returns to \"corrected.\" A COPD patient holds on to CO2, and their bicarb stays high to balance it; because COPD is chronic, you will never see a fully corrected ABG."]},{"s":"Interpreting an ABG Step by Step▾","a":"interpreting-an-abg-step-by-step","k":["Interpreting an ABG Step by Step▾","1. pH — Acidic, basic or normal? If the pH is normal but the CO2 and bicarb are not, compensation has occurred. Then decide which way the normal pH leans: treat 7.40 as neutral; 7.35 to 7.40 leans acidic, above 7.40 to 7.45 leans basic (e.g…","2. PaCO2 (35 to 45) — Acidic (high), basic (low) or normal? If it is abnormal and matches the pH, it's a respiratory problem: an inverse relationship with pH (Respiratory Opposite).","3. HCO3 (24 to 29) — A calculated value. Basic (high), acidic (low) or normal? If it matches the pH, it's a metabolic problem: a direct relationship with pH (Metabolic Equal).","4. Compensation — Is the body trying to restore the pH by altering the system that is not involved in the imbalance? If compensation has occurred, that value moves in the same direction as the other component. Use the four levels above.","With compensation, the pH points the way. When the pH is normal, the side it leans toward (acidic or basic) tells you which value is the primary problem and which is compensating."]},{"s":"Worked Examples From the Lecture▾","a":"worked-examples-from-the-lecture","k":["Worked Examples From the Lecture▾","Cover the interpretation column with the eye button and work each one yourself. Values are pH / PaCO2 / HCO3.","7.25 / 56 / 24 — Respiratory acidosis (no compensation) — pH down, CO2 up (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.35 / 56 / 35 — Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the high CO2; the high bicarb is compensating.","7.60 / 25 / 24 — Respiratory alkalosis (no compensation) — pH up, CO2 down (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.45 / 30 / 20 — Fully compensated respiratory alkalosis — pH normal but leaning basic, matching the low CO2; the low bicarb is compensating.","7.26 / 40 / 18 — Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — pH down, bicarb down (equal); CO2 normal.","7.35 / 33 / 20 — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the low bicarb; the low CO2 is compensating.","7.46 / 37 / 26 — Metabolic alkalosis (as given in the lecture) — pH basic, CO2 normal; the lecture calls the bicarb of 26 \"on the higher end.\"","Normal pH leaning basic / high CO2 / 30 — Compensated metabolic alkalosis — Leaning basic matches the high bicarb; the high CO2 is compensating.","7.30 / 52 / 30 — Respiratory acidosis, partially compensated — pH and CO2 both acidic; the kidneys are retaining bicarb, but the pH is still abnormal.","7.60 / 31 / 26 — Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Tic-tac-toe: pH and CO2 line up in the basic column; bicarb is normal.","Below 7.35 / 29 / 21 — Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — pH and bicarb both acidic; the CO2 is abnormal (basic), but the pH is not yet normal.","7.52 / 48 / 39 — Metabolic alkalosis, partially compensated — pH and bicarb both basic; CO2 abnormal (acidic); pH still abnormal.","The lecture ends with a slide of extra practice ABGs to work through on your own; their values aren't read aloud in the transcript."]},{"s":"Two Shortcut Methods▾","a":"two-shortcut-methods","k":["Two Shortcut Methods▾","\"It's important to know this information and to rationalize all these steps.\" These are extra ways to get there, if they fit how you think.","ABG tic-tac-toe","Draw a board with three columns: Acidic, Normal, Basic.","Place the pH, CO2 and bicarb in the column each one falls in.","The value that lines up with the pH names the problem (CO2 = respiratory, bicarb = metabolic), and the pH's column names acidosis or alkalosis.","If the third value is in the normal column, it's uncompensated.","The instructor's three lines","Draw a line each for pH, CO2 and bicarb, always in that order.","pH: low end Acidic → high end Basic.","CO2: opposite, low is Basic → high is Acidic.","Bicarb: low is Acidic → high is Basic.","Memorize \"AB, BA, AB,\" mark where each value falls, and see what matches the pH."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 2","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Two Topics","The Two Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It's a map of Week 2, so you can see how the two topics fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate; it pulls the whole week toge…"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Two Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-the-two-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Two Topics▾","Each topic page holds two voiceovers, and each voiceover builds directly on the one before it.","The Scale and the Acids — Topic 1, Acid-Base Balance (the sections from \"What pH Is\" through \"Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids\") — pH is a measure of hydrogen ions, inversely and logarithmically. The serum has to stay between 7.35 and 7.45, b…","The Defenses — Topic 1, Acid-Base Balance (from \"The Three Lines of Defense\" to the end) — Three lines of defense on three timescales: buffers in seconds, lungs in minutes to hours, kidneys in hours to days. The carbonic acid equation ties …","Reading the Gas — Topic 2, ABGs & ABG Interpretation (from \"Why We Draw ABGs\" through \"Metabolic Acidosis\") — The ABG measures the equation's two ends: PaCO2 is the respiratory side and HCO3 is the metabolic side. That gives four disorders,…","Putting It Together — Topic 2, ABGs & ABG Interpretation (from \"How Compensation Works\" to the end) — A four-step method (pH, PaCO2, HCO3, compensation), four levels of compensation, worked examples, and two shortcuts. Everything from the f…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order. The interpretation steps only make sense once ROME and the carbonic acid equation do."]},{"s":"The Two Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-two-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Two Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Acid-Base Balance — More hydrogen ions mean a lower pH, and each step on the scale is tenfold. Serum pH must stay at 7.35–7.45 (below 6.8 or above 7.8 is incompatible with life); acidic blood releases oxygen to tissues, alkalotic blood …","2 — ABGs & ABG Interpretation — Know pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO2 35–45 and HCO3 24–29. ROME sorts the four disorders: respiratory acidosis (hypoventilation, overdose, COPD), respiratory alkalosis (hyperventilation, panic, high vent settings), metab…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content. Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why. Section names are in quotation marks.","The carbonic acid equation — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Carbonic Acid Equation\" (know it backwards and forwards) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: it is why PaCO2 is the respiratory value and HCO3 the metabolic value, and why ROME works.","Compensation — Acid-Base Balance: \"Compensation\" (uncompensated, partial, complete; metabolic problems are compensated by the lungs, respiratory problems by the kidneys) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: \"How Compensation Works\" and \"The Four Le…","Speed of each defense — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Three Lines of Defense\" (seconds, minutes to hours, hours to days) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: respiratory alkalosis shows a normal bicarb because the kidneys can't respond acutely; acute res…","The COPD patient — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Carbonic Acid Equation\" and \"Renal Control of pH\" (retained CO2 pushes bicarb up; the kidneys hold on to bicarb) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: chronic respiratory acidosis, the acute-on-chronic exam…","Potassium shifts — Acid-Base Balance: \"Cellular Compensation & Potassium\" (in acidosis, hydrogen moves in and potassium moves out) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: hyperkalemia in metabolic acidosis; hypokalemia and cardiac issues in metabolic …","DKA and ketones — Acid-Base Balance: beta-hydroxybutyric acid as the DKA diagnostic, and the potassium shift to be revisited with DKA — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: \"Metabolic Acidosis\" (DKA as a cause, Kussmaul respirations, insulin to lower…","Kidney failure and daily bicarb — Acid-Base Balance: \"Renal Control of pH\" (renal failure can't maintain pH; patients take bicarb daily) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: renal failure as a cause of metabolic acidosis, and dialysis or bicarb whe…","Narcotics and respiratory depression — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Carbonic Acid Equation\" (a low respiratory rate means CO2 can't be removed) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: \"Respiratory Acidosis\" (overdose, too much narcotic or sedative, anesthe…","The ventilator — Acid-Base Balance: \"Respiratory Control of pH\" (ventilator settings can be adjusted to affect pH) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: a rate or tidal volume set too high causes respiratory alkalosis; raising them treats respirator…","Stomach acid and gut bicarb — Acid-Base Balance: \"Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body\" (hydrochloric acid in the stomach) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: vomiting and NG suction remove that acid (metabolic alkalosis, treated with H2 blockers…","Lactic acid — Acid-Base Balance: \"Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids\" (anaerobic byproduct; the lungs can't remove it) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: lactic acidosis, shock and cardiac arrest as causes of metabolic acidosis.","Hemoglobin — Acid-Base Balance: \"What pH Affects\" (oxygen release shifts with pH) — The same page's \"Buffer Systems\": hemoglobin as a protein buffer that carries CO2 as carbaminohemoglobin."]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and emphasized statements from both pages, condensed to a phrase, one row per topic. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation. Click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","Acid-Base Balance — A pH below 6.8 or above 7.8 is incompatible with life; outside 7.35–7.45, get back to normal as quickly as possible. Avoid alkalosis in an ill patient (hemoglobin holds on to oxygen). Acidosis can cause hyperkalemia and …","ABGs & ABG Interpretation — Use an ABG, not a VBG, when treatment changes depend on the result in a critically ill patient. Metabolic alkalosis can cause hypokalemia and cardiac issues. Ventilator settings can cause respiratory alkalosis. R…","Patterns You'll Notice Across These Rules","\"Fast fixes are short; slow fixes last.\" Buffers act in seconds but only make small, temporary changes; cellular compensation is temporary; sodium bicarb in the ICU is a quick fix. The lungs act in minutes, but can't compensate for long. Th…","\"The organ that isn't the problem does the compensating.\" A lung problem is compensated by the kidneys (bicarb moves); a metabolic problem is compensated by the lungs (CO2 moves). That's why, in every disorder, the compensating value moves …","\"Breathing is both the cause and the cure.\" Hypoventilation causes respiratory acidosis and is how the lungs compensate for metabolic alkalosis; hyperventilation causes respiratory alkalosis and is how the lungs compensate for metabolic aci…","\"Potassium follows hydrogen in the opposite direction.\" Acidosis drives hydrogen into cells and potassium out (hyperkalemia); alkalosis pulls hydrogen out and potassium in (hypokalemia). Both can affect the heart.","\"Chronic looks calmer than acute.\" A chronic problem gives the kidneys time, so the pH drifts back toward normal (the compensated COPD patient). An acute problem doesn't, so the pH swings (the respiratory arrest, the panic attack, the COPD …"]}]},{"f":"week3-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 3","Part 1 — Migraines","Part 2A — Parkinson's Disease","Part 2B — Parkinson's Pharmacology","Part 3 — Myasthenia Gravis","Part 4 — Multiple Sclerosis","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 3: the four topic pages that follow, in the order they were taught. Topic 1: migraines (Part 1). Topic 2: Parkinson's disease and Parkinson's pharmacology (Parts 2A–2B)…"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Migraines▾","a":"part-1-migraines","k":["Part 1 — Migraines▾","Topic 1: the definition and diagnostic criteria, types, risk factors and triggers, pathophysiology, the four phases, abortive therapy (analgesics, triptans, CGRP antagonists, antiemetics) and preventive therapy.","What a Migraine Is","Migraines are the most common complaint neurologists see day to day. A migraine is an episodic neurologic disorder characterized by a headache that lasts anywhere from 4 to 72 hours.","At least TWO of these","Unilateral head pain (characteristic of migraine)","Throbbing pain","Headache that worsens with activity","Pain rated moderate to severe","Plus at least ONE of these","Nausea and vomiting","Photophobia: sensitivity to (avoidance of) light, such as sunglasses, dark rooms, pulled curtains","Phonophobia: avoidance of sound; everything feels loud and grating","Types of Migraine","With aura — An aura is present (about a third of people with migraines).","Without aura — No aura. The most common kind of migraine.","Chronic — Migraines on about 15 days a month over 3 months. They usually start out episodic, and their frequency increases over time until they reach that threshold.","Risk Factors & Triggers","Risk factors","Family history.","Estrogen and progesterone: females are more susceptible. Migraines can occur more right before and during menstruation, and often decrease during pregnancy and menopause. The theory is that cyclical withdrawal of these hormones over the men…","Other genetic and environmental factors.","Common triggers","Fatigue; sleep disturbances, meaning too little or too much sleep (e.g., 15 hours when that isn't normal)","Missing a meal; overexertion","Weather changes (rainy, cold weather)","Stress; hormonal changes","Alcohol (red wine is a common one)","Bright lights or strong smells (stores with strong odors)","Pathophysiology","Not well understood; there is no well-established, identifiable pathology. Migraines are associated with changes in brain metabolism and blood flow, and current theories involve changes in neurotransmitter levels and blood vessel tone.","1 — Increased neuronal depolarization results in hyperactivity.","2 — Excitatory neurotransmitters are released in the brain.","3 — They activate the vascular system, stimulating vasodilation, which results in the migraine.","The Four Phases of a Migraine","1. Premonitory — Hours or days before the headache; about a third of people with migraines — Fatigue, irritability, loss of concentration, stiff neck, food cravings.","2. Aura — Only in people with aura (about a third); lasts up to about an hour — Visual: shimmering lights, flashing spots, blind spots. Sensory: numbness, tingling, pins and needles. Motor: weakness, difficulty with speech, coordination pro…","3. Headache — 4 to 72 hours — Throbbing pain, typically starting on one side and spreading to the whole head; fatigue, nausea, vomiting, dizziness, hypersensitivity to touch on the head.","4. Recovery — A few hours up to a few days to fully resolve — Irritability, fatigue, depression.","Abortive (Symptomatic) Therapy","Abortive therapy treats the symptoms, and it is more effective when given early in the course of the headache.","Mild to moderate, no severe nausea or vomiting — Basic analgesics: aspirin, acetaminophen, NSAIDs, or a combination. Tried first because they can be really effective, are readily available and are inexpensive. If they don't work alone, a tr…","Moderate to severe, no nausea or vomiting — An oral migraine-specific agent: the triptans, often combined (e.g., sumatriptan with naproxen), since the combination is more effective.","Can't take or tolerate triptans — A CGRP antagonist (calcitonin gene-related peptide antagonist).","Severe nausea and vomiting (any severity of migraine) — Antiemetics are added, such as ondansetron or promethazine (Phenergan).","Triptans (Serotonin 1B/1D Receptor Agonists)","How they work — Promote vasoconstriction of the intracranial blood vessels (vasodilation has a role in causing migraine). They also suppress inflammatory neuropeptides in the brain and block the pain pathway.","Routes — Subcutaneous, PO or intranasal. Nasal spray has fewer side effects than subQ. SubQ works most quickly. PO has side effects similar to subQ, but they occur less often.","Side effects — Injection site reactions (subQ), chest pressure, flushing, weakness, headache; a bad taste with the nasal form. These make sense for a drug that constricts blood vessels.","Avoid in — Ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina.","No triptans for clients with ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina. Triptans work by constricting blood vessels.","CGRP Antagonist: Rimegepant (Nurtec)","Used when triptans aren't effective or are contraindicated: CAD, heart disease, angina, ischemic stroke.","Given PO.","Side effects: GI upset (abdominal pain, nausea, dyspepsia).","A CYP substrate: use caution with any inhibitors or inducers of that system.","Preventive Therapy","When it's used","More than 4 migraines in a month, or","Migraines that last longer than 12 hours.","Drugs named","Beta blockers: propranolol","Tricyclic antidepressants: amitriptyline","Anti-epileptic drugs","Estrogen","Goals of preventive therapy","Reduce migraine frequency, severity and duration.","Improve the response to treatment of acute attacks.","Improve function and reduce disability.","Prevent episodic migraines from turning into chronic migraines."]},{"s":"Part 2A — Parkinson's Disease▾","a":"part-2a-parkinson-s-disease","k":["Part 2A — Parkinson's Disease▾","Topic 2, first voiceover: what PD is, primary vs secondary parkinsonism, risk factors, the dopamine–acetylcholine imbalance, the clinical manifestations and the classic triad, and complications.","What Parkinson's Disease Is","Parkinson's disease (PD)","A complex motor disorder with other systemic non-motor and neurologic symptoms.","Progressive and degenerative: it keeps getting worse, and some symptoms worsen faster than others.","Characterized by tremors, rigidity and bradykinesia. The tremor is notably more present when the muscles are at rest.","A disorder of the basal ganglia, which help produce smooth, coordinated movements (the lecture places them in the cerebellum). The concern is the substantia nigra inside the basal ganglia, which contains the cells that produce dopamine.","Primary vs Secondary Parkinsonism","Parkinsonism is the umbrella term. Parkinson's disease is the leading cause of parkinsonism.","Primary parkinsonism","The etiology is unknown (idiopathic); this is Parkinson's disease.","Also includes genetic components and sporadic onset.","Defining feature: no defining event led to the onset of symptoms.","There is damage to the dopamine-producing cells.","Secondary (acquired) parkinsonism","Results from something else: an event that caused the onset.","Infection, head trauma, drug intoxication.","Some antiemetics and some antidepressants are the most common cause. Symptoms usually go away after the medication is stopped.","Any drug that interferes with dopamine transmission can cause it.","Who Gets Parkinson's Disease","Age — Often begins after about 50; incidence rapidly increases after about 60 and peaks around 70. Typically diagnosed in the elderly, but it can happen young (Michael J. Fox, diagnosed in his early 30s).","Genetics — Familial or genetic forms account for about 10% of primary parkinsonism, with both dominant and recessive patterns.","Sex — Seen more often in males.","Environment — Pesticides, air pollution, farm and agricultural work.","Depression — Can make PD develop earlier, because of its association with dopamine changes.","Head trauma — Makes PD more likely (Muhammad Ali: repeated head trauma from boxing, diagnosed in his late 30s).","Decreased estrogen — A hysterectomy, or anything that decreases estrogen, is a risk factor.","Coffee — A protective factor.","Dopamine, Acetylcholine & the Imbalance","Dopamine","Has both inhibitory and excitatory effects in the brain, depending on the receptor it binds.","In PD, it's the inhibitory effects that are lacking.","Helps control movement and balance by inhibiting unnecessary movements, which improves coordination, balance and muscle control.","Acetylcholine (ACh)","An excitatory neurotransmitter: stimulates muscle movement and can cause uncoordinated movement.","Essential for all muscle function, and works with dopamine. It's all about the balance.","1 — The substantia nigra in the basal ganglia is destroyed.","2 — Dopamine levels decrease, because the dopamine-producing cells are in the substantia nigra.","3 — An imbalance develops: a relative excess of acetylcholine.","4 — Extra muscle movements that dopamine would normally inhibit are no longer inhibited: loss of controlled movement and balance.","Clinical Manifestations","They may develop alone or in combination, and diagnosis often takes a while because onset is insidious. At first it may involve only one side of the body; it typically becomes bilateral as the disease progresses. Later, all of them appear.","Common signs","Bradykinesia: slowness of movement.","Cogwheel rigidity: stiff muscles; movements like bending the elbow aren't smooth.","Resting tremor.","Shuffling (Parkinsonian) gait: they don't lift their feet.","Mask-like expression: loss of small facial movements, a flat affect.","Postural instability.","The classic triad, the hallmark of PD: Tremor, Rigidity, Bradykinesia.","Tremor","Often the first sign. It is a rest tremor: most noticeable when the body part is supported against gravity but not doing purposeful activity, and it decreases with movement.","An early sign is the pill-rolling tremor: the thumb looks like it's rolling a small object against the finger. In the dominant hand, handwriting may suffer.","Worse with stress or concentration, such as trying to control the movement.","Cause — Faulty neurological impulses — Dopamine deficiency and the ACh imbalance","When — Occurs with motor function; fine motor movements are really difficult — Occurs at rest; tends to improve with movement","Other signs — No other manifestations of Parkinson's — Comes with the other manifestations of Parkinson's","Rigidity","Resistance to passive movement: when the provider flexes the client's arm at the elbow, there's unintentional resistance.","Cogwheel rigidity: slow, jerky movements like a cogwheel.","Cause: excess acetylcholine stimulates the muscles continuously, so they never get to relax (sustained contraction). Clients report muscle soreness, aches and pains.","Often unilateral at first, becoming bilateral over time.","Bradykinesia","Loss of automatic movement, or slowness of movement. Arguably the major cause of disability in Parkinson's: a lack of the spontaneous movements we take for granted.","Blinking — Dry eye, more susceptible to abrasion.","Arm swing while walking — Reduced or absent, adding to balance problems.","Swallowing saliva — Drooling.","Facial micro-expressions — Flat affect, mask-like expression.","Postural Instability & Gait","Postural instability: a decreased ability to prevent falling while moving, from impaired postural reflexes.","Gait disturbances: short steps, or shuffling without picking up the feet.","Complications","Dementia — About 40% develop it. Lewy bodies spread from movement areas of the brain into memory and cognition areas. Can also cause hallucinations.","Fatigue — From sleep disturbances: the tremor picks up while lying at rest trying to fall asleep.","Depression — A risk factor and also a complication; they may become dependent on others for the first time.","Anxiety — The second most frequent psychiatric disturbance in PD, after depression.","Decreased mobility — Malnutrition (trouble preparing meals or feeding themselves); aspiration and pneumonia from swallowing problems; UTIs from urinary retention; skin breakdown from sitting more.","Drug-related — The drugs are very effective but come with complications (below)."]},{"s":"Part 2B — Parkinson's Pharmacology▾","a":"part-2b-parkinson-s-pharmacology","k":["Part 2B — Parkinson's Pharmacology▾","Topic 2, second voiceover: the goals of treatment, Sinemet and its loss of effect, adverse effects and interactions, Duopa, the dopamine agonists, rotigotine and apomorphine.","Goals & Strategy of Drug Therapy","Goal: help clients keep motor control as long as possible, decrease symptoms, and maintain as much independence as possible. Because PD is progressive and degenerative, treatment is staving it off as much as possible.","When to start: once symptoms affect the dominant hand or interfere with work, ADLs, or social or leisure activities.","Strategy: correct the imbalance, either by enhancing dopamine (dopaminergic drugs) or by blocking acetylcholine (anticholinergic drugs).","Sinemet (Levodopa-Carbidopa)","The gold standard of treatment for Parkinson's disease.","Levodopa","Once it reaches the brain, it is converted to dopamine and activates the dopamine receptors, increasing dopamine activity.","Carbidopa","Prevents levodopa from being broken down before it reaches the brain, so more dopamine is sustained for longer.","One of the most effective PD drugs, but it takes several months for symptoms to improve while dopamine builds up. Teach clients it can take up to months.","It does not work long-term: as PD progresses, fewer dopaminergic terminals survive to take up levodopa and convert it to dopamine, so the effect is lost.","Loss of Drug Effect","Abrupt loss (\"on-off\" phenomenon) — Like flipping a switch: the drug is working, then suddenly it isn't and the symptoms are back. Impossible to predict, can happen at any point in the dosing interval, and over time the \"off\" episodes get m…","Gradual loss (\"wearing off\") — Each dose wears off sooner and sooner, even as the dose is increased; symptoms gradually return. Differs between individuals. — Shorter dosing intervals (taking it more often), or changes in the dose amount.","Adverse Effects (Mostly From the Levodopa)","Nausea and vomiting — Take with a small amount of food. Eating does reduce absorption, so it's a balance, but it keeps clients taking the drug. Avoid high-protein food.","Dyskinesias — From annoying up to disabling, sometimes so disabling the client needs a different drug.","Postural (orthostatic) hypotension — Teach clients to change positions slowly.","Dysrhythmias — Monitor cardiac function, especially when the dose has been increased over time.","Psychosis — In 20%: hallucinations, nightmares, even paranoia.","Darkened sweat and urine — Not harmful, but annoying; it can stain clothing.","Malignant melanoma — Sinemet can activate malignant melanoma: do a good skin assessment before starting, to identify suspicious lesions.","Decrease levodopa's effect","Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine)","Antipsychotics","Protein","Increase levodopa's effect (to toxicity)","Anticholinergics","MAOIs","Duopa (Levodopa-Carbidopa Infusion)","What it is — Another levodopa-carbidopa combination, as a gel infused into the small intestine through a feeding tube or J-tube. Continuous infusion for up to 16 hours a day; it can be stopped while sleeping at night.","Why — Keeps a consistent, continuous blood level. For clients who don't respond to the PO combination, or whose PO dose doesn't last long enough, or who have more or longer \"off\" periods; it helps minimize on-off episodes.","MAOIs — Do not take within 2 weeks of an MAOI for depression: it can cause levodopa toxicity.","Interactions — Antihypertensives, MAOIs, antipsychotics, metoclopramide, isoniazid, iron, vitamins, high protein. Do a good med rec.","Side effects — Falling asleep without warning (like narcolepsy), so not for clients who drive a truck or pilot an aircraft for a living; orthostatic hypotension; hallucinations (visual, auditory, tactile); unusual urges; depression; dyskine…","Tube risks — Placement is a surgery: infection, tube dislodgement, and the other risks of tube placement.","Dopamine Agonists & Other Drugs","Pramipexole (Mirapex) — A dopamine receptor agonist: it binds and stimulates dopamine (D2) receptors. Usually monotherapy in younger clients early in PD; combined with levodopa-carbidopa as the client ages or the disease advances (levodopa …","Ropinirole (Requip) — A dopamine receptor agonist for idiopathic PD. Exactly how it works isn't known; animal studies show increased nerve impulses in the substantia nigra. — Similar to other Parkinson's drugs; with long-term use, an increa…","Rotigotine (Neupro) — A once-daily patch, brought back into the U.S. in 2012. — Not described in the lecture.","Apomorphine (Apokyn) — A short-acting subQ injection for fast relief: useful during the \"off\" phase of the on-off phenomenon to regain control of symptoms. — Not described in the lecture."]},{"s":"Part 3 — Myasthenia Gravis▾","a":"part-3-myasthenia-gravis","k":["Part 3 — Myasthenia Gravis▾","Topic 3: what MG is, the neuromuscular junction attack, clinical manifestations, myasthenic crisis, immunosuppressants and cholinesterase inhibitors, cholinergic crisis, and how to tell the two crises apart.","What Myasthenia Gravis Is","Myasthenia gravis (MG)","An acquired autoimmune disease characterized by fluctuating weakness of certain muscle groups.","Commonly affected: ocular muscles (the eyes), bulbar muscles (brainstem region), and the limb and respiratory muscle groups.","The weakness comes from an antibody-mediated attack on the postsynaptic membrane of the neuromuscular junction: a breakdown in communication between nerve cells and muscle cells.","How It Progresses","Variable from person to person. It often appears first as a mild, insidious case that's easy to push off (\"just a little muscle weakness\").","It can spontaneously go into remission (back to baseline), stabilize (plateau), or cycle through relapses, stable periods and symptom-free periods lasting weeks to months.","It can also progress severely and rapidly.","Because the course is hard to predict, being honest up front with clients about that is key.","Who Gets It","Rare; more common in females and in people aged 10 to 65 (a broad range).","Why the antibodies attack isn't known; there seems to be a genetic component and possible environmental triggers.","Manifestations often first appear during pregnancy or postpartum, or after exposure to certain types of anesthesia.","What Happens at the Neuromuscular Junction","1 — Antibodies form against the nicotinic acetylcholine postsynaptic receptors at the neuromuscular junction of skeletal muscle; the receptors are no longer recognized as self.","2 — Anti-acetylcholine antibodies attach to the receptor sites and block them from binding acetylcholine.","3 — Eventually the antibodies destroy the receptor sites.","4 — The body makes enough acetylcholine, but there aren't enough sites for it to bind, so nerve impulse transmission to the muscle is diminished.","Acetylcholine is an excitatory neurotransmitter responsible for muscle contraction (the lecture says smooth muscle). It also has a role in vasodilation, increasing body secretions, and slowing heart rate, which matters for the drugs below.","Clinical Manifestations","Pattern — Insidious onset with fluctuating skeletal muscle weakness; clients can feel weakness starting, rest, and the strength comes back. Symptoms can be vague.","Eye, face, mouth, throat, neck — Often affected first: drooling, difficulty chewing and swallowing, which affects nutrition and raises aspiration risk.","Respiratory muscles — Weak diaphragm and intercostal muscles can impair the ability to breathe or ventilate appropriately.","Shoulders and hips — Not typically affected, but muscle fatigue after exercise can occur there.","The video (Carrie) — Slow speech, slow blinking, a tracheostomy to help clear secretions, a possibly weak cough, difficulty swallowing.","Myasthenic Crisis","An acute exacerbation of muscle weakness","Can develop as MG progresses, typically triggered by a stressor: infection, surgery, emotional distress, pregnancy, a menstrual cycle, or inadequate pharmacotherapy or other drugs (not on enough medication, or not taking it appropriately).","In the second video, a simple cold sent Carrie into crisis: difficulty expanding her lungs, difficulty breathing, needing oxygen.","Myasthenic crisis: watch the respiratory muscles. The big concern is respiratory insufficiency, and these clients are at higher risk for respiratory arrest.","Drug Therapy","Drug therapy is not a cure; the hope is to alter and alleviate symptoms. Several administration routes can be used.","Immunosuppressants","MG is autoimmune, so immunosuppressants, particularly steroids, are used frequently to calm the immune response.","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Cholinesterase is the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine.","Blocking it lets acetylcholine stay in the synaptic junction longer, so it can hopefully find a receptor the antibodies haven't damaged.","They also intensify the effects of the acetylcholine released, hopefully increasing muscle strength.","Neostigmine","A cholinesterase inhibitor (anticholinesterase drug), so essentially a cholinergic drug, used to treat MG symptoms.","Facilitates impulse transmission across the neuromuscular junction, increasing cholinergic action.","At therapeutic levels it affects both muscarinic and nicotinic receptors.","Nicotinic receptors","Adrenal and skeletal muscle.","These are the receptors affected by MG.","Muscarinic receptors","Sweat glands and other glands (and the effects on the GI tract, bladder, heart, lungs and eyes below).","Neostigmine stimulates these too, which causes most of its side effects.","Cholinergic vs Anticholinergic Effects","GI tract — Increased motility, diarrhea — Decreased motility, constipation","Secretions — Increased — Decreased (dries secretions)","Bladder — Urinary urgency — The opposite","Heart rate — Slows — Increases","Lungs — Bronchial constriction — The opposite","Pupils — Miosis (constriction) — The opposite","Use the symptoms to pick the drug: symptomatic bradycardia → an anticholinergic like atropine raises the heart rate. Atropine is also used to dry up secretions, for example for clients on comfort care or hospice who are choking on secretion…","SLUDGE: the cholinergic side effects. Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Diaphoresis, GI distress, Emesis.","Adverse Effects of Neostigmine","Muscarinic stimulation","Increased secretions and GI motility (diarrhea).","Urinary urgency.","Slowed heart rate.","Bronchoconstriction: consider carefully before giving to clients with asthma or other lung disease.","Miosis.","Nicotinic (neuromuscular) stimulation","Therapeutic dose: increased muscle contraction, the desired effect.","Toxic dose: decreased contraction; the overstimulated muscles wear out and can fail, leading to a cholinergic crisis.","Cholinergic Crisis","Extreme muscle weakness or paralysis, plus signs of excessive muscarinic stimulation: increased intestinal motility (diarrhea, cramping), bradycardia, pupil constriction, lots of saliva, diaphoresis.","It arises from anticholinesterase medications: essentially a drug toxicity, from excessive acetylcholine accumulation.","Risk of respiratory arrest; the client may need mechanical ventilation.","Antidote for the muscarinic signs: atropine (an anticholinergic).","Teach clients with MG to wear a medical alert bracelet at all times.","Myasthenic Crisis vs Cholinergic Crisis","Both present with respiratory insufficiency and extreme muscle weakness, heading the same direction for opposite reasons.","The problem — Not enough stimulation or acetylcholine — Too much acetylcholine (too much neostigmine)","Why the muscles fail — Too weak, leading to respiratory failure — Overstimulated and worn out, leading to respiratory failure","Typical cause — A stressor, or inadequate medication — Anticholinesterase drug toxicity","After edrophonium — Improves (acetylcholine rises temporarily) — Worsens (already too much acetylcholine)","Edrophonium is a very short-acting cholinesterase inhibitor that raises acetylcholine temporarily. Give it when one of the two crises is suspected: improvement means myasthenic crisis; worsening means cholinergic crisis."]},{"s":"Part 4 — Multiple Sclerosis▾","a":"part-4-multiple-sclerosis","k":["Part 4 — Multiple Sclerosis▾","Topic 4: what MS is, risk factors, pathophysiology, the types of MS, clinical manifestations, disease-modifying drugs, acute relapse treatment and symptom management.","What Multiple Sclerosis Is","Multiple sclerosis (MS)","A chronic, immune-mediated inflammatory disease involving degeneration of CNS myelin (loss of the myelin sheath), scarring (sclerosis or plaque formation) and loss of axons.","A progressive disease, with several types.","Features: lots of inflammation, especially in the white matter of the brain and spinal cord; demyelination of nerve fibers; and scar tissue called gliosis. Glial cells myelinate neurons in the CNS; on imaging, the white spots are areas of s…","Demyelinating disorders damage the myelin sheath and impair neural signal transmission. MS affects the central nervous system; Guillain-Barré affects the peripheral nervous system (a later lecture).","Who Gets MS","The etiology is largely unknown, most likely a combination of genetic predisposition, an immune system problem and an environmental trigger (such as an infection).","Age — Onset typically between 20 and 40.","Sex — More common in females, but males who get MS typically have a more severe, progressive course.","Location — Higher prevalence in moderately cool climates, especially the northern U.S.: above the 40th parallel north and below the 40th parallel south. Areas in between have lower prevalence.","Race — People who are white are more likely to have MS.","Family history — Increases risk.","Possible (associated) — Smoking, vitamin D deficiency, obesity, infection. Not concrete risk factors, but there is evidence of an association.","Pathophysiology","MS is a diffuse, progressive CNS autoimmune inflammatory disease affecting white and gray matter throughout the brain and spinal cord. The attack targets the myelin sheath and involves T lymphocytes and antibodies.","1 — T lymphocytes migrate to the CNS, cross the blood-brain barrier, and increase inflammation and breakdown of the myelin sheath.","2 — An antigen-antibody reaction in the CNS attacks the myelin, releasing inflammatory mediators.","3 — The oligodendrocytes (myelin-producing cells) are lost.","4 — Axons are demyelinated, and plaque and scar tissue form (sclerotic lesions).","5 — The axons die: nerve signals are first delayed, then lost. Neurons die, and the brain atrophies overall.","Normal — An axon wrapped in an intact myelin sheath. — Quick, almost instantaneous transmission.","Inflamed (early) — The nerve fiber is inflamed but intact. — Still transmitted, maybe a little slower; the client may notice some weakness.","Detached — The myelin detaches and the underlying axon is damaged; scarring and axon death follow. — Completely blocked: a permanent loss of function.","By diagnosis, clients are usually quite symptomatic. Looking back, many realize they'd had weakness much longer than they thought, but brushed it off until they couldn't ignore it.","Types of MS","Most types have exacerbations (relapses or flares): a temporary worsening of symptoms, mild or serious, lasting several days to several weeks, which may improve and may be followed by progressively worsening symptoms.","Benign MS — Exacerbations, but after each flare the client returns to baseline: no loss of function.","Relapsing-remitting (RRMS) — The most common type, about 80–90% of cases. Long remissions with a few exacerbations; pretty stable between them, with minimal disability or progression. They don't necessarily return fully to baseline: a littl…","Primary progressive (PPMS) — Gradual, steady progression from the start, with no periods of remission or exacerbation (no flare-ups).","Secondary progressive (SPMS) — Starts with relapses and remissions, with some loss of function each time, then shifts to a more progressive course without remissions.","Progressive relapsing (PRMS) — Gradual progression of symptoms with exacerbations, and fewer remissions overall.","The type is determined by how often exacerbations happen and how long remissions last.","Relapses vary: for about 20% of people they last less than a week; for others 1 to 2 weeks, or 2 weeks to a month.","If relapses get shorter after starting treatment (e.g., from a month down to a week or less), that suggests the medication is working.","Clinical Manifestations","No finding is unique to MS, but some are highly characteristic. The big takeaway: symptoms depend on which nerves are affected.","Common initial symptoms","Paresthesias of the face, trunk and limbs; numbness, tingling, weakness, loss of muscle function.","Optic neuritis and unilateral vision loss.","Impaired gait.","Urinary incontinence.","Some develop seizures.","Symptoms to monitor closely","Cognitive \"cog fog\": difficulty thinking clearly and concentrating, not necessarily a memory problem.","Vision problems.","Depression, related to loss of function.","Fatigue: needs more rest periods.","Pain: chronic (continuous) or acute (with exacerbations); burning or stabbing.","Bowel: constipation most common, possibly incontinence. Bladder: incontinence or retention.","Sexual dysfunction; muscle stiffness or spasms.","Walking and balance issues, vertigo: high fall risk; some eventually need a wheelchair.","Clients with MS are at high risk for falls. Most have walking and balance issues, and some have vertigo.","Goals of Drug Therapy","There is no cure. Goals: slow disability and keep the client functioning at the highest possible capacity.","Decrease relapse frequency, lengthen remissions, and reduce new brain lesions (which lead to more dysfunction and symptoms).","Three jobs: modify the disease process, treat acute exacerbations, and manage symptoms.","Disease-Modifying Drugs","Injectables","Interferon beta (Avonex) — Interferons occur naturally in the immune system and can be pro- or anti-inflammatory. Here they keep pro-inflammatory T cells from crossing the blood-brain barrier, reducing inflammation. Decreases relapse rates …","Glatiramer acetate (Copaxone) — Increases anti-inflammatory T cells, which cross the blood-brain barrier and suppress inflammation. Efficacy similar to interferons. — Injection site reactions. A reaction right after injection: flushing, pal…","Oral Drugs","Fingolimod — Retains lymphocytes in the lymph nodes so they don't migrate across the blood-brain barrier, decreasing brain inflammation. Commonly used for RRMS.","Dimethyl fumarate — Developed specifically for MS. Thought to inhibit immune cells, decreasing inflammation and preventing nerve damage.","Infusions","Natalizumab — Keeps circulating T cells from leaving the blood vessels, so they can't cross the blood-brain barrier. Very effective: reduces relapses by up to 68%. Also used for Crohn's disease. Monotherapy only; used for RRMS. — Most commo…","Alemtuzumab — Reserved for poor response to two or more MS drugs: a last effort, not first-line. — Not described in the lecture.","Mitoxantrone — For secondary progressive MS, progressive relapsing MS, and worsening RRMS that never gets a complete remission. — Not described in the lecture.","Natalizumab and PML: only through the TOUCH program, only as monotherapy, and the PML risk is significantly higher with other immunosuppressants like steroids.","Treating an Acute Relapse","High-dose IV glucocorticoids (\"pulse-dose steroids\") — MS is autoimmune, so very high doses calm the immune response: about 500 mg to 1 g daily for 3 to 5 days (vs 40 to 60 mg for asthma). Not for long-term or frequent use; usually reserved…","IV gamma globulin (IVIG) — For clients who can't tolerate high-dose glucocorticoids.","ACTH (H.P. Acthar Gel) — An adrenocorticotropic hormone gel with prolonged ACTH release after injection. Mechanism not clear, but it treats acute relapses. For clients who can't tolerate glucocorticoids, or when steroids aren't (or weren't)…","Plasmapheresis — A dialysis-like process: blood is removed through a catheter, the plasma is separated from the red cells and replaced with donor plasma, and the blood is reinfused.","Managing MS Symptoms","Symptoms vary from client to client, so medications are chosen based on the presentation.","Urinary frequency — An anticholinergic.","Urinary retention — A cholinergic.","Constipation — Hydration and fiber first, then a bulk-forming laxative.","Fatigue — Amantadine, plus teaching to plan the day around rest periods.","Muscle spasms — Muscle relaxants.","Severe cognitive dysfunction (cog fog) — Donepezil (Aricept), normally an Alzheimer's medication."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One row per part: the points to have cold before an exam. Every detail is taught in full in the parts above.","1. Migraines — 4 to 72 hours; at least two of unilateral, throbbing, worse with activity, moderate to severe, plus at least one of nausea and vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia. Without aura is most common; chronic = 15 days a month for 3 m…","2A. Parkinson's Disease — Substantia nigra loss → low dopamine → relative excess acetylcholine. Triad: rest tremor (pill-rolling, better with movement, worse with stress), rigidity (cogwheel) and bradykinesia (the major cause of disability:…","2B. Parkinson's Pharmacology — Correct the imbalance: boost dopamine or block acetylcholine. Sinemet is the gold standard (levodopa → dopamine; carbidopa protects levodopa); months to work; on-off and wearing-off loss of effect; avoid high …","3. Myasthenia Gravis — Autoimmune attack on nicotinic ACh receptors at the neuromuscular junction (ACh supply is fine). Fluctuating weakness that improves with rest; eyes and face first; chewing and swallowing (aspiration); respiratory musc…","4. Multiple Sclerosis — T cells cross the blood-brain barrier and attack CNS myelin; oligodendrocytes are lost; scarring; axon death means permanent loss. Age 20–40, female (males worse), cool climates, white, family history. RRMS is most c…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the five parts above: a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week3-migraines.html","t":"Migraines","secs":[{"s":"Migraines","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 1","What a Migraine Is","Risk Factors & Triggers","Pathophysiology","The Four Phases of a Migraine","Abortive (Symptomatic) Therapy","Preventive Therapy","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the migraine voiceover: the definition and diagnostic criteria, the types, risk factors and triggers, the (still poorly understood) pathophysiology, the four phases, and the two kinds of drug therapy: abortive (symptomatic…"]},{"s":"What a Migraine Is▾","a":"what-a-migraine-is","k":["What a Migraine Is▾","Migraines are the most common complaint neurologists see day to day. A migraine is an episodic neurologic disorder characterized by a headache that lasts anywhere from 4 to 72 hours.","At least TWO of these","Unilateral head pain (characteristic of migraine)","Throbbing pain","Headache that worsens with activity","Pain rated moderate to severe","Plus at least ONE of these","Nausea and vomiting","Photophobia: sensitivity to (avoidance of) light, such as sunglasses, dark rooms, pulled curtains","Phonophobia: avoidance of sound; everything feels loud and grating","Types of Migraine","With aura — An aura is present (about a third of people with migraines).","Without aura — No aura. The most common kind of migraine.","Chronic — Migraines on about 15 days a month over 3 months. They usually start out episodic, and their frequency increases over time until they reach that threshold."]},{"s":"Risk Factors & Triggers▾","a":"risk-factors-triggers","k":["Risk Factors & Triggers▾","Risk factors","Family history.","Estrogen and progesterone: females are more susceptible. Migraines can occur more right before and during menstruation, and often decrease during pregnancy and menopause. The theory is that cyclical withdrawal of these hormones over the men…","Other genetic and environmental factors.","Common triggers","Fatigue; sleep disturbances, meaning too little or too much sleep (e.g., 15 hours when that isn't normal)","Missing a meal; overexertion","Weather changes (rainy, cold weather)","Stress; hormonal changes","Alcohol (red wine is a common one)","Bright lights or strong smells (stores with strong odors)"]},{"s":"Pathophysiology▾","a":"pathophysiology","k":["Pathophysiology▾","Not well understood; there is no well-established, identifiable pathology. Migraines are associated with changes in brain metabolism and blood flow, and current theories involve changes in neurotransmitter levels and blood vessel tone.","1 — Increased neuronal depolarization results in hyperactivity.","2 — Excitatory neurotransmitters are released in the brain.","3 — They activate the vascular system, stimulating vasodilation, which results in the migraine."]},{"s":"The Four Phases of a Migraine▾","a":"the-four-phases-of-a-migraine","k":["The Four Phases of a Migraine▾","1. Premonitory — Hours or days before the headache; about a third of people with migraines — Fatigue, irritability, loss of concentration, stiff neck, food cravings.","2. Aura — Only in people with aura (about a third); lasts up to about an hour — Visual: shimmering lights, flashing spots, blind spots. Sensory: numbness, tingling, pins and needles. Motor: weakness, difficulty with speech, coordination pro…","3. Headache — 4 to 72 hours — Throbbing pain, typically starting on one side and spreading to the whole head; fatigue, nausea, vomiting, dizziness, hypersensitivity to touch on the head.","4. Recovery — A few hours up to a few days to fully resolve — Irritability, fatigue, depression."]},{"s":"Abortive (Symptomatic) Therapy▾","a":"abortive-symptomatic-therapy","k":["Abortive (Symptomatic) Therapy▾","Abortive therapy treats the symptoms, and it is more effective when given early in the course of the headache.","Mild to moderate, no severe nausea or vomiting — Basic analgesics: aspirin, acetaminophen, NSAIDs, or a combination. Tried first because they can be really effective, are readily available and are inexpensive. If they don't work alone, a tr…","Moderate to severe, no nausea or vomiting — An oral migraine-specific agent: the triptans, often combined (e.g., sumatriptan with naproxen), since the combination is more effective.","Can't take or tolerate triptans — A CGRP antagonist (calcitonin gene-related peptide antagonist).","Severe nausea and vomiting (any severity of migraine) — Antiemetics are added, such as ondansetron or promethazine (Phenergan).","Triptans (Serotonin 1B/1D Receptor Agonists)","How they work — Promote vasoconstriction of the intracranial blood vessels (vasodilation has a role in causing migraine). They also suppress inflammatory neuropeptides in the brain and block the pain pathway.","Routes — Subcutaneous, PO or intranasal. Nasal spray has fewer side effects than subQ. SubQ works most quickly. PO has side effects similar to subQ, but they occur less often.","Side effects — Injection site reactions (subQ), chest pressure, flushing, weakness, headache; a bad taste with the nasal form. These make sense for a drug that constricts blood vessels.","Avoid in — Ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina.","No triptans for clients with ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina. Triptans work by constricting blood vessels.","CGRP Antagonist: Rimegepant (Nurtec)","Used when triptans aren't effective or are contraindicated: CAD, heart disease, angina, ischemic stroke.","Given PO.","Side effects: GI upset (abdominal pain, nausea, dyspepsia).","A CYP substrate: use caution with any inhibitors or inducers of that system."]},{"s":"Preventive Therapy▾","a":"preventive-therapy","k":["Preventive Therapy▾","When it's used","More than 4 migraines in a month, or","Migraines that last longer than 12 hours.","Drugs named","Beta blockers: propranolol","Tricyclic antidepressants: amitriptyline","Anti-epileptic drugs","Estrogen","Goals of preventive therapy","Reduce migraine frequency, severity and duration.","Improve the response to treatment of acute attacks.","Improve function and reduce disability.","Prevent episodic migraines from turning into chronic migraines."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-parkinsons.html","t":"Parkinson's Disease & Parkinson's Pharmacology","secs":[{"s":"Parkinson's Disease & Parkinson's Pharmacology","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 2","What Parkinson's Disease Is","Primary vs Secondary Parkinsonism","Who Gets Parkinson's Disease","Dopamine, Acetylcholine & the Imbalance","Clinical Manifestations","Complications","Goals & Strategy of Drug Therapy","Sinemet (Levodopa-Carbidopa)","Duopa (Levodopa-Carbidopa Infusion)","Dopamine Agonists & Other Drugs","Self-Test Flashcards","This page combines the two Parkinson's voiceovers. Part 1 covers what Parkinson's disease is, primary vs secondary parkinsonism, risk factors, the dopamine–acetylcholine balance, the clinical manifestations (the classic triad, plus postural…"]},{"s":"What Parkinson's Disease Is▾","a":"what-parkinson-s-disease-is","k":["What Parkinson's Disease Is▾","Parkinson's disease (PD)","A complex motor disorder with other systemic non-motor and neurologic symptoms.","Progressive and degenerative: it keeps getting worse, and some symptoms worsen faster than others.","Characterized by tremors, rigidity and bradykinesia. The tremor is notably more present when the muscles are at rest.","A disorder of the basal ganglia, which help produce smooth, coordinated movements (the lecture places them in the cerebellum). The concern is the substantia nigra inside the basal ganglia, which contains the cells that produce dopamine."]},{"s":"Primary vs Secondary Parkinsonism▾","a":"primary-vs-secondary-parkinsonism","k":["Primary vs Secondary Parkinsonism▾","Parkinsonism is the umbrella term. Parkinson's disease is the leading cause of parkinsonism.","Primary parkinsonism","The etiology is unknown (idiopathic); this is Parkinson's disease.","Also includes genetic components and sporadic onset.","Defining feature: no defining event led to the onset of symptoms.","There is damage to the dopamine-producing cells.","Secondary (acquired) parkinsonism","Results from something else: an event that caused the onset.","Infection, head trauma, drug intoxication.","Some antiemetics and some antidepressants are the most common cause. Symptoms usually go away after the medication is stopped.","Any drug that interferes with dopamine transmission can cause it."]},{"s":"Who Gets Parkinson's Disease▾","a":"who-gets-parkinson-s-disease","k":["Who Gets Parkinson's Disease▾","Age — Often begins after about 50; incidence rapidly increases after about 60 and peaks around 70. Typically diagnosed in the elderly, but it can happen young (Michael J. Fox, diagnosed in his early 30s).","Genetics — Familial or genetic forms account for about 10% of primary parkinsonism, with both dominant and recessive patterns.","Sex — Seen more often in males.","Environment — Pesticides, air pollution, farm and agricultural work.","Depression — Can make PD develop earlier, because of its association with dopamine changes.","Head trauma — Makes PD more likely (Muhammad Ali: repeated head trauma from boxing, diagnosed in his late 30s).","Decreased estrogen — A hysterectomy, or anything that decreases estrogen, is a risk factor.","Coffee — A protective factor."]},{"s":"Dopamine, Acetylcholine & the Imbalance▾","a":"dopamine-acetylcholine-the-imbalance","k":["Dopamine, Acetylcholine & the Imbalance▾","Dopamine","Has both inhibitory and excitatory effects in the brain, depending on the receptor it binds.","In PD, it's the inhibitory effects that are lacking.","Helps control movement and balance by inhibiting unnecessary movements, which improves coordination, balance and muscle control.","Acetylcholine (ACh)","An excitatory neurotransmitter: stimulates muscle movement and can cause uncoordinated movement.","Essential for all muscle function, and works with dopamine. It's all about the balance.","1 — The substantia nigra in the basal ganglia is destroyed.","2 — Dopamine levels decrease, because the dopamine-producing cells are in the substantia nigra.","3 — An imbalance develops: a relative excess of acetylcholine.","4 — Extra muscle movements that dopamine would normally inhibit are no longer inhibited: loss of controlled movement and balance."]},{"s":"Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"clinical-manifestations","k":["Clinical Manifestations▾","They may develop alone or in combination, and diagnosis often takes a while because onset is insidious. At first it may involve only one side of the body; it typically becomes bilateral as the disease progresses. Later, all of them appear.","Common signs","Bradykinesia: slowness of movement.","Cogwheel rigidity: stiff muscles; movements like bending the elbow aren't smooth.","Resting tremor.","Shuffling (Parkinsonian) gait: they don't lift their feet.","Mask-like expression: loss of small facial movements, a flat affect.","Postural instability.","The classic triad, the hallmark of PD: Tremor, Rigidity, Bradykinesia.","Tremor","Often the first sign. It is a rest tremor: most noticeable when the body part is supported against gravity but not doing purposeful activity, and it decreases with movement.","An early sign is the pill-rolling tremor: the thumb looks like it's rolling a small object against the finger. In the dominant hand, handwriting may suffer.","Worse with stress or concentration, such as trying to control the movement.","Cause — Faulty neurological impulses — Dopamine deficiency and the ACh imbalance","When — Occurs with motor function; fine motor movements are really difficult — Occurs at rest; tends to improve with movement","Other signs — No other manifestations of Parkinson's — Comes with the other manifestations of Parkinson's","Rigidity","Resistance to passive movement: when the provider flexes the client's arm at the elbow, there's unintentional resistance.","Cogwheel rigidity: slow, jerky movements like a cogwheel.","Cause: excess acetylcholine stimulates the muscles continuously, so they never get to relax (sustained contraction). Clients report muscle soreness, aches and pains.","Often unilateral at first, becoming bilateral over time.","Bradykinesia","Loss of automatic movement, or slowness of movement. Arguably the major cause of disability in Parkinson's: a lack of the spontaneous movements we take for granted.","Blinking — Dry eye, more susceptible to abrasion.","Arm swing while walking — Reduced or absent, adding to balance problems.","Swallowing saliva — Drooling.","Facial micro-expressions — Flat affect, mask-like expression.","Postural Instability & Gait","Postural instability: a decreased ability to prevent falling while moving, from impaired postural reflexes.","Gait disturbances: short steps, or shuffling without picking up the feet."]},{"s":"Complications▾","a":"complications","k":["Complications▾","Dementia — About 40% develop it. Lewy bodies spread from movement areas of the brain into memory and cognition areas. Can also cause hallucinations.","Fatigue — From sleep disturbances: the tremor picks up while lying at rest trying to fall asleep.","Depression — A risk factor and also a complication; they may become dependent on others for the first time.","Anxiety — The second most frequent psychiatric disturbance in PD, after depression.","Decreased mobility — Malnutrition (trouble preparing meals or feeding themselves); aspiration and pneumonia from swallowing problems; UTIs from urinary retention; skin breakdown from sitting more.","Drug-related — The drugs are very effective but come with complications (below)."]},{"s":"Goals & Strategy of Drug Therapy▾","a":"goals-strategy-of-drug-therapy","k":["Goals & Strategy of Drug Therapy▾","Goal: help clients keep motor control as long as possible, decrease symptoms, and maintain as much independence as possible. Because PD is progressive and degenerative, treatment is staving it off as much as possible.","When to start: once symptoms affect the dominant hand or interfere with work, ADLs, or social or leisure activities.","Strategy: correct the imbalance, either by enhancing dopamine (dopaminergic drugs) or by blocking acetylcholine (anticholinergic drugs)."]},{"s":"Sinemet (Levodopa-Carbidopa)▾","a":"sinemet-levodopa-carbidopa","k":["Sinemet (Levodopa-Carbidopa)▾","The gold standard of treatment for Parkinson's disease.","Levodopa","Once it reaches the brain, it is converted to dopamine and activates the dopamine receptors, increasing dopamine activity.","Carbidopa","Prevents levodopa from being broken down before it reaches the brain, so more dopamine is sustained for longer.","One of the most effective PD drugs, but it takes several months for symptoms to improve while dopamine builds up. Teach clients it can take up to months.","It does not work long-term: as PD progresses, fewer dopaminergic terminals survive to take up levodopa and convert it to dopamine, so the effect is lost.","Loss of Drug Effect","Abrupt loss (\"on-off\" phenomenon) — Like flipping a switch: the drug is working, then suddenly it isn't and the symptoms are back. Impossible to predict, can happen at any point in the dosing interval, and over time the \"off\" episodes get m…","Gradual loss (\"wearing off\") — Each dose wears off sooner and sooner, even as the dose is increased; symptoms gradually return. Differs between individuals. — Shorter dosing intervals (taking it more often), or changes in the dose amount.","Adverse Effects (Mostly From the Levodopa)","Nausea and vomiting — Take with a small amount of food. Eating does reduce absorption, so it's a balance, but it keeps clients taking the drug. Avoid high-protein food.","Dyskinesias — From annoying up to disabling, sometimes so disabling the client needs a different drug.","Postural (orthostatic) hypotension — Teach clients to change positions slowly.","Dysrhythmias — Monitor cardiac function, especially when the dose has been increased over time.","Psychosis — In 20%: hallucinations, nightmares, even paranoia.","Darkened sweat and urine — Not harmful, but annoying; it can stain clothing.","Malignant melanoma — Sinemet can activate malignant melanoma: do a good skin assessment before starting, to identify suspicious lesions.","Decrease levodopa's effect","Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine)","Antipsychotics","Protein","Increase levodopa's effect (to toxicity)","Anticholinergics","MAOIs"]},{"s":"Duopa (Levodopa-Carbidopa Infusion)▾","a":"duopa-levodopa-carbidopa-infusion","k":["Duopa (Levodopa-Carbidopa Infusion)▾","What it is — Another levodopa-carbidopa combination, as a gel infused into the small intestine through a feeding tube or J-tube. Continuous infusion for up to 16 hours a day; it can be stopped while sleeping at night.","Why — Keeps a consistent, continuous blood level. For clients who don't respond to the PO combination, or whose PO dose doesn't last long enough, or who have more or longer \"off\" periods; it helps minimize on-off episodes.","MAOIs — Do not take within 2 weeks of an MAOI for depression: it can cause levodopa toxicity.","Interactions — Antihypertensives, MAOIs, antipsychotics, metoclopramide, isoniazid, iron, vitamins, high protein. Do a good med rec.","Side effects — Falling asleep without warning (like narcolepsy), so not for clients who drive a truck or pilot an aircraft for a living; orthostatic hypotension; hallucinations (visual, auditory, tactile); unusual urges; depression; dyskine…","Tube risks — Placement is a surgery: infection, tube dislodgement, and the other risks of tube placement."]},{"s":"Dopamine Agonists & Other Drugs▾","a":"dopamine-agonists-other-drugs","k":["Dopamine Agonists & Other Drugs▾","Pramipexole (Mirapex) — A dopamine receptor agonist: it binds and stimulates dopamine (D2) receptors. Usually monotherapy in younger clients early in PD; combined with levodopa-carbidopa as the client ages or the disease advances (levodopa …","Ropinirole (Requip) — A dopamine receptor agonist for idiopathic PD. Exactly how it works isn't known; animal studies show increased nerve impulses in the substantia nigra. — Similar to other Parkinson's drugs; with long-term use, an increa…","Rotigotine (Neupro) — A once-daily patch, brought back into the U.S. in 2012. — Not described in the lecture.","Apomorphine (Apokyn) — A short-acting subQ injection for fast relief: useful during the \"off\" phase of the on-off phenomenon to regain control of symptoms. — Not described in the lecture."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-myasthenia-gravis.html","t":"Myasthenia Gravis","secs":[{"s":"Myasthenia Gravis","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 3","What Myasthenia Gravis Is","What Happens at the Neuromuscular Junction","Clinical Manifestations","Myasthenic Crisis","Drug Therapy","Cholinergic Crisis","Myasthenic Crisis vs Cholinergic Crisis","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the myasthenia gravis voiceover: what MG is and how it progresses, who gets it, what happens at the neuromuscular junction, the clinical manifestations, myasthenic crisis, the drugs (immunosuppressants and cholinesterase i…"]},{"s":"What Myasthenia Gravis Is▾","a":"what-myasthenia-gravis-is","k":["What Myasthenia Gravis Is▾","Myasthenia gravis (MG)","An acquired autoimmune disease characterized by fluctuating weakness of certain muscle groups.","Commonly affected: ocular muscles (the eyes), bulbar muscles (brainstem region), and the limb and respiratory muscle groups.","The weakness comes from an antibody-mediated attack on the postsynaptic membrane of the neuromuscular junction: a breakdown in communication between nerve cells and muscle cells.","How It Progresses","Variable from person to person. It often appears first as a mild, insidious case that's easy to push off (\"just a little muscle weakness\").","It can spontaneously go into remission (back to baseline), stabilize (plateau), or cycle through relapses, stable periods and symptom-free periods lasting weeks to months.","It can also progress severely and rapidly.","Because the course is hard to predict, being honest up front with clients about that is key.","Who Gets It","Rare; more common in females and in people aged 10 to 65 (a broad range).","Why the antibodies attack isn't known; there seems to be a genetic component and possible environmental triggers.","Manifestations often first appear during pregnancy or postpartum, or after exposure to certain types of anesthesia."]},{"s":"What Happens at the Neuromuscular Junction▾","a":"what-happens-at-the-neuromuscular-junction","k":["What Happens at the Neuromuscular Junction▾","1 — Antibodies form against the nicotinic acetylcholine postsynaptic receptors at the neuromuscular junction of skeletal muscle; the receptors are no longer recognized as self.","2 — Anti-acetylcholine antibodies attach to the receptor sites and block them from binding acetylcholine.","3 — Eventually the antibodies destroy the receptor sites.","4 — The body makes enough acetylcholine, but there aren't enough sites for it to bind, so nerve impulse transmission to the muscle is diminished.","Acetylcholine is an excitatory neurotransmitter responsible for muscle contraction (the lecture says smooth muscle). It also has a role in vasodilation, increasing body secretions, and slowing heart rate, which matters for the drugs below."]},{"s":"Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"clinical-manifestations","k":["Clinical Manifestations▾","Pattern — Insidious onset with fluctuating skeletal muscle weakness; clients can feel weakness starting, rest, and the strength comes back. Symptoms can be vague.","Eye, face, mouth, throat, neck — Often affected first: drooling, difficulty chewing and swallowing, which affects nutrition and raises aspiration risk.","Respiratory muscles — Weak diaphragm and intercostal muscles can impair the ability to breathe or ventilate appropriately.","Shoulders and hips — Not typically affected, but muscle fatigue after exercise can occur there.","The video (Carrie) — Slow speech, slow blinking, a tracheostomy to help clear secretions, a possibly weak cough, difficulty swallowing."]},{"s":"Myasthenic Crisis▾","a":"myasthenic-crisis","k":["Myasthenic Crisis▾","An acute exacerbation of muscle weakness","Can develop as MG progresses, typically triggered by a stressor: infection, surgery, emotional distress, pregnancy, a menstrual cycle, or inadequate pharmacotherapy or other drugs (not on enough medication, or not taking it appropriately).","In the second video, a simple cold sent Carrie into crisis: difficulty expanding her lungs, difficulty breathing, needing oxygen.","Myasthenic crisis: watch the respiratory muscles. The big concern is respiratory insufficiency, and these clients are at higher risk for respiratory arrest."]},{"s":"Drug Therapy▾","a":"drug-therapy","k":["Drug Therapy▾","Drug therapy is not a cure; the hope is to alter and alleviate symptoms. Several administration routes can be used.","Immunosuppressants","MG is autoimmune, so immunosuppressants, particularly steroids, are used frequently to calm the immune response.","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Cholinesterase is the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine.","Blocking it lets acetylcholine stay in the synaptic junction longer, so it can hopefully find a receptor the antibodies haven't damaged.","They also intensify the effects of the acetylcholine released, hopefully increasing muscle strength.","Neostigmine","A cholinesterase inhibitor (anticholinesterase drug), so essentially a cholinergic drug, used to treat MG symptoms.","Facilitates impulse transmission across the neuromuscular junction, increasing cholinergic action.","At therapeutic levels it affects both muscarinic and nicotinic receptors.","Nicotinic receptors","Adrenal and skeletal muscle.","These are the receptors affected by MG.","Muscarinic receptors","Sweat glands and other glands (and the effects on the GI tract, bladder, heart, lungs and eyes below).","Neostigmine stimulates these too, which causes most of its side effects.","Cholinergic vs Anticholinergic Effects","GI tract — Increased motility, diarrhea — Decreased motility, constipation","Secretions — Increased — Decreased (dries secretions)","Bladder — Urinary urgency — The opposite","Heart rate — Slows — Increases","Lungs — Bronchial constriction — The opposite","Pupils — Miosis (constriction) — The opposite","Use the symptoms to pick the drug: symptomatic bradycardia → an anticholinergic like atropine raises the heart rate. Atropine is also used to dry up secretions, for example for clients on comfort care or hospice who are choking on secretion…","SLUDGE: the cholinergic side effects. Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Diaphoresis, GI distress, Emesis.","Adverse Effects of Neostigmine","Muscarinic stimulation","Increased secretions and GI motility (diarrhea).","Urinary urgency.","Slowed heart rate.","Bronchoconstriction: consider carefully before giving to clients with asthma or other lung disease.","Miosis.","Nicotinic (neuromuscular) stimulation","Therapeutic dose: increased muscle contraction, the desired effect.","Toxic dose: decreased contraction; the overstimulated muscles wear out and can fail, leading to a cholinergic crisis."]},{"s":"Cholinergic Crisis▾","a":"cholinergic-crisis","k":["Cholinergic Crisis▾","Extreme muscle weakness or paralysis, plus signs of excessive muscarinic stimulation: increased intestinal motility (diarrhea, cramping), bradycardia, pupil constriction, lots of saliva, diaphoresis.","It arises from anticholinesterase medications: essentially a drug toxicity, from excessive acetylcholine accumulation.","Risk of respiratory arrest; the client may need mechanical ventilation.","Antidote for the muscarinic signs: atropine (an anticholinergic).","Teach clients with MG to wear a medical alert bracelet at all times."]},{"s":"Myasthenic Crisis vs Cholinergic Crisis▾","a":"myasthenic-crisis-vs-cholinergic-crisis","k":["Myasthenic Crisis vs Cholinergic Crisis▾","Both present with respiratory insufficiency and extreme muscle weakness, heading the same direction for opposite reasons.","The problem — Not enough stimulation or acetylcholine — Too much acetylcholine (too much neostigmine)","Why the muscles fail — Too weak, leading to respiratory failure — Overstimulated and worn out, leading to respiratory failure","Typical cause — A stressor, or inadequate medication — Anticholinesterase drug toxicity","After edrophonium — Improves (acetylcholine rises temporarily) — Worsens (already too much acetylcholine)","Edrophonium is a very short-acting cholinesterase inhibitor that raises acetylcholine temporarily. Give it when one of the two crises is suspected: improvement means myasthenic crisis; worsening means cholinergic crisis."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-multiple-sclerosis.html","t":"Multiple Sclerosis","secs":[{"s":"Multiple Sclerosis","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 4","What Multiple Sclerosis Is","Who Gets MS","Pathophysiology","Types of MS","Clinical Manifestations","Goals of Drug Therapy","Disease-Modifying Drugs","Treating an Acute Relapse","Managing MS Symptoms","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the multiple sclerosis voiceover: what MS is, risk factors, the pathophysiology from T cells to axon death, the types of MS, the clinical manifestations, disease-modifying drugs, treatment of an acute relapse, and symptom …"]},{"s":"What Multiple Sclerosis Is▾","a":"what-multiple-sclerosis-is","k":["What Multiple Sclerosis Is▾","Multiple sclerosis (MS)","A chronic, immune-mediated inflammatory disease involving degeneration of CNS myelin (loss of the myelin sheath), scarring (sclerosis or plaque formation) and loss of axons.","A progressive disease, with several types.","Features: lots of inflammation, especially in the white matter of the brain and spinal cord; demyelination of nerve fibers; and scar tissue called gliosis. Glial cells myelinate neurons in the CNS; on imaging, the white spots are areas of s…","Demyelinating disorders damage the myelin sheath and impair neural signal transmission. MS affects the central nervous system; Guillain-Barré affects the peripheral nervous system (a later lecture)."]},{"s":"Who Gets MS▾","a":"who-gets-ms","k":["Who Gets MS▾","The etiology is largely unknown, most likely a combination of genetic predisposition, an immune system problem and an environmental trigger (such as an infection).","Age — Onset typically between 20 and 40.","Sex — More common in females, but males who get MS typically have a more severe, progressive course.","Location — Higher prevalence in moderately cool climates, especially the northern U.S.: above the 40th parallel north and below the 40th parallel south. Areas in between have lower prevalence.","Race — People who are white are more likely to have MS.","Family history — Increases risk.","Possible (associated) — Smoking, vitamin D deficiency, obesity, infection. Not concrete risk factors, but there is evidence of an association."]},{"s":"Pathophysiology▾","a":"pathophysiology","k":["Pathophysiology▾","MS is a diffuse, progressive CNS autoimmune inflammatory disease affecting white and gray matter throughout the brain and spinal cord. The attack targets the myelin sheath and involves T lymphocytes and antibodies.","1 — T lymphocytes migrate to the CNS, cross the blood-brain barrier, and increase inflammation and breakdown of the myelin sheath.","2 — An antigen-antibody reaction in the CNS attacks the myelin, releasing inflammatory mediators.","3 — The oligodendrocytes (myelin-producing cells) are lost.","4 — Axons are demyelinated, and plaque and scar tissue form (sclerotic lesions).","5 — The axons die: nerve signals are first delayed, then lost. Neurons die, and the brain atrophies overall.","Normal — An axon wrapped in an intact myelin sheath. — Quick, almost instantaneous transmission.","Inflamed (early) — The nerve fiber is inflamed but intact. — Still transmitted, maybe a little slower; the client may notice some weakness.","Detached — The myelin detaches and the underlying axon is damaged; scarring and axon death follow. — Completely blocked: a permanent loss of function.","By diagnosis, clients are usually quite symptomatic. Looking back, many realize they'd had weakness much longer than they thought, but brushed it off until they couldn't ignore it."]},{"s":"Types of MS▾","a":"types-of-ms","k":["Types of MS▾","Most types have exacerbations (relapses or flares): a temporary worsening of symptoms, mild or serious, lasting several days to several weeks, which may improve and may be followed by progressively worsening symptoms.","Benign MS — Exacerbations, but after each flare the client returns to baseline: no loss of function.","Relapsing-remitting (RRMS) — The most common type, about 80–90% of cases. Long remissions with a few exacerbations; pretty stable between them, with minimal disability or progression. They don't necessarily return fully to baseline: a littl…","Primary progressive (PPMS) — Gradual, steady progression from the start, with no periods of remission or exacerbation (no flare-ups).","Secondary progressive (SPMS) — Starts with relapses and remissions, with some loss of function each time, then shifts to a more progressive course without remissions.","Progressive relapsing (PRMS) — Gradual progression of symptoms with exacerbations, and fewer remissions overall.","The type is determined by how often exacerbations happen and how long remissions last.","Relapses vary: for about 20% of people they last less than a week; for others 1 to 2 weeks, or 2 weeks to a month.","If relapses get shorter after starting treatment (e.g., from a month down to a week or less), that suggests the medication is working."]},{"s":"Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"clinical-manifestations","k":["Clinical Manifestations▾","No finding is unique to MS, but some are highly characteristic. The big takeaway: symptoms depend on which nerves are affected.","Common initial symptoms","Paresthesias of the face, trunk and limbs; numbness, tingling, weakness, loss of muscle function.","Optic neuritis and unilateral vision loss.","Impaired gait.","Urinary incontinence.","Some develop seizures.","Symptoms to monitor closely","Cognitive \"cog fog\": difficulty thinking clearly and concentrating, not necessarily a memory problem.","Vision problems.","Depression, related to loss of function.","Fatigue: needs more rest periods.","Pain: chronic (continuous) or acute (with exacerbations); burning or stabbing.","Bowel: constipation most common, possibly incontinence. Bladder: incontinence or retention.","Sexual dysfunction; muscle stiffness or spasms.","Walking and balance issues, vertigo: high fall risk; some eventually need a wheelchair.","Clients with MS are at high risk for falls. Most have walking and balance issues, and some have vertigo."]},{"s":"Goals of Drug Therapy▾","a":"goals-of-drug-therapy","k":["Goals of Drug Therapy▾","There is no cure. Goals: slow disability and keep the client functioning at the highest possible capacity.","Decrease relapse frequency, lengthen remissions, and reduce new brain lesions (which lead to more dysfunction and symptoms).","Three jobs: modify the disease process, treat acute exacerbations, and manage symptoms."]},{"s":"Disease-Modifying Drugs▾","a":"disease-modifying-drugs","k":["Disease-Modifying Drugs▾","Injectables","Interferon beta (Avonex) — Interferons occur naturally in the immune system and can be pro- or anti-inflammatory. Here they keep pro-inflammatory T cells from crossing the blood-brain barrier, reducing inflammation. Decreases relapse rates …","Glatiramer acetate (Copaxone) — Increases anti-inflammatory T cells, which cross the blood-brain barrier and suppress inflammation. Efficacy similar to interferons. — Injection site reactions. A reaction right after injection: flushing, pal…","Oral Drugs","Fingolimod — Retains lymphocytes in the lymph nodes so they don't migrate across the blood-brain barrier, decreasing brain inflammation. Commonly used for RRMS.","Dimethyl fumarate — Developed specifically for MS. Thought to inhibit immune cells, decreasing inflammation and preventing nerve damage.","Infusions","Natalizumab — Keeps circulating T cells from leaving the blood vessels, so they can't cross the blood-brain barrier. Very effective: reduces relapses by up to 68%. Also used for Crohn's disease. Monotherapy only; used for RRMS. — Most commo…","Alemtuzumab — Reserved for poor response to two or more MS drugs: a last effort, not first-line. — Not described in the lecture.","Mitoxantrone — For secondary progressive MS, progressive relapsing MS, and worsening RRMS that never gets a complete remission. — Not described in the lecture.","Natalizumab and PML: only through the TOUCH program, only as monotherapy, and the PML risk is significantly higher with other immunosuppressants like steroids."]},{"s":"Treating an Acute Relapse▾","a":"treating-an-acute-relapse","k":["Treating an Acute Relapse▾","High-dose IV glucocorticoids (\"pulse-dose steroids\") — MS is autoimmune, so very high doses calm the immune response: about 500 mg to 1 g daily for 3 to 5 days (vs 40 to 60 mg for asthma). Not for long-term or frequent use; usually reserved…","IV gamma globulin (IVIG) — For clients who can't tolerate high-dose glucocorticoids.","ACTH (H.P. Acthar Gel) — An adrenocorticotropic hormone gel with prolonged ACTH release after injection. Mechanism not clear, but it treats acute relapses. For clients who can't tolerate glucocorticoids, or when steroids aren't (or weren't)…","Plasmapheresis — A dialysis-like process: blood is removed through a catheter, the plasma is separated from the red cells and replaced with donor plasma, and the blood is reinfused."]},{"s":"Managing MS Symptoms▾","a":"managing-ms-symptoms","k":["Managing MS Symptoms▾","Symptoms vary from client to client, so medications are chosen based on the presentation.","Urinary frequency — An anticholinergic.","Urinary retention — A cholinergic.","Constipation — Hydration and fiber first, then a bulk-forming laxative.","Fatigue — Amantadine, plus teaching to plan the day around rest periods.","Muscle spasms — Muscle relaxants.","Severe cognitive dysfunction (cog fog) — Donepezil (Aricept), normally an Alzheimer's medication."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 3","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics","The Four Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It's a map of Week 3's four topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate; it pulls the whole week …"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-the-four-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics▾","Five voiceovers, four pages: the Parkinson's lecture and the Parkinson's pharmacology lecture share one page.","The Episodic Headache — Topic 1, Migraines (all of it) — A neurologic disorder that comes and goes in four phases. Neurotransmitter release triggers vasodilation, so the treatment logic follows: triptans constrict the vessels, which is also…","The Neurotransmitter Imbalance — Topic 2, Parkinson's Disease & Parkinson's Pharmacology (the disease sections first, then the drug sections) — The substantia nigra stops making dopamine, leaving acetylcholine unopposed, so movement loses i…","The Nerve–Muscle Connection — Topic 3, Myasthenia Gravis (all of it) — Acetylcholine again, but this time the problem is the receptor: antibodies destroy the nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction. The fix is more acetylcholine (…","The Nerve Itself — Topic 4, Multiple Sclerosis (all of it) — Autoimmune again, but now the target is CNS myelin. Inflammation slows the signal; axon death blocks it permanently. The drugs mostly keep T cells from crossing the blood-brain ba…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order. Read Parkinson's before myasthenia gravis (MG builds on the acetylcholine review from Parkinson's), and the Parkinson's disease sections before the drug sections."]},{"s":"The Four Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-four-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Four Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Migraines — A 4- to 72-hour headache meeting the diagnostic criteria (at least two pain features plus at least one of nausea and vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia), moving through premonitory, aura, headache and recovery phases. Treat …","2 — Parkinson's Disease & Parkinson's Pharmacology — Dopamine loss with relative acetylcholine excess gives the triad: rest tremor, cogwheel rigidity, and bradykinesia (the major cause of disability), plus shuffling gait and postural instab…","3 — Myasthenia Gravis — Antibodies destroy nicotinic ACh receptors, causing fluctuating weakness that improves with rest, starting in the eyes and face and threatening swallowing and breathing. Steroids and neostigmine treat it. A myastheni…","4 — Multiple Sclerosis — A T-cell and antibody attack on CNS myelin, ending in axon death and permanent loss. Mostly relapsing-remitting; symptoms depend on which nerves are hit (paresthesias, optic neuritis, cog fog, fatigue, bladder probl…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content. Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why. Section names are in quotation marks.","Acetylcholine — Parkinson's: \"Dopamine, Acetylcholine & the Imbalance\" (excitatory; in PD it's in relative excess) — Myasthenia Gravis: there's enough ACh but its receptors are destroyed; neostigmine keeps ACh around longer; a cholinergic c…","Anticholinergics — Myasthenia Gravis: \"Cholinergic vs Anticholinergic Effects\" (atropine: faster heart rate, dried secretions, the crisis antidote) — Parkinson's: anticholinergic drugs as one treatment strategy, and anticholinergics push le…","Cholinergics — Myasthenia Gravis: \"Neostigmine\" and \"Adverse Effects of Neostigmine\" (SLUDGE) — Multiple Sclerosis: a cholinergic for urinary retention.","Autoimmunity and steroids — Myasthenia Gravis: \"Drug Therapy\" (immunosuppressants, particularly steroids) and Multiple Sclerosis: \"Treating an Acute Relapse\" (pulse-dose IV steroids) — Both are autoimmune with unknown triggers and a possibl…","Antiemetics — Migraines: \"Abortive (Symptomatic) Therapy\" (ondansetron, promethazine for severe nausea and vomiting) — Parkinson's: some antiemetics (and antidepressants) are the most common cause of secondary parkinsonism.","Hormones and pregnancy — Migraines: \"Risk Factors & Triggers\" (estrogen and progesterone; fewer migraines in pregnancy and menopause) — Parkinson's: decreased estrogen (e.g., hysterectomy) is a risk factor. Myasthenia Gravis: symptoms often…","Swallowing and aspiration — Parkinson's: \"Bradykinesia\" and \"Complications\" (drooling, aspiration pneumonia) — Myasthenia Gravis: difficulty chewing and swallowing raises aspiration risk and affects nutrition.","Respiratory failure — Myasthenia Gravis: \"Myasthenic Crisis\" and \"Cholinergic Crisis\" — Both crises end in respiratory failure for opposite reasons, and both may need mechanical ventilation.","Orthostatic hypotension — Parkinson's: Sinemet, Duopa, and pramipexole with levodopa (\"profound\") — The same teaching point every time: change positions slowly.","Depression — Parkinson's: a risk factor and a complication — Migraines: the recovery phase. Multiple Sclerosis: common with loss of function, and an interferon side effect.","Fatigue and sleep — Parkinson's: fatigue from sleep disturbance; Duopa and pramipexole sleep attacks — Migraines: fatigue and sleep disturbance as triggers and symptoms. Multiple Sclerosis: fatigue managed with amantadine and rest planning.","Falls and balance — Parkinson's: \"Postural Instability & Gait\" — Multiple Sclerosis: walking and balance issues and vertigo; a high fall risk.","Dementia and cognition — Parkinson's: about 40% develop dementia (Lewy bodies) — Multiple Sclerosis: cog fog, treated with donepezil (an Alzheimer's drug) when severe."]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and safety statements from across the four pages, condensed to a phrase, one row per topic. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation. Click through to a topic for the details behind any …","Migraines — No triptans with ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina (they constrict blood vessels). Rimegepant is a CYP substrate: caution with inhibitors and inducers.","Parkinson's Disease & Parkinson's Pharmacology — Aspiration, UTI, skin breakdown and fall risk as mobility declines. Sinemet: skin assessment before starting (melanoma), avoid high-protein meals, change positions slowly, monitor cardiac fun…","Myasthenia Gravis — Myasthenic crisis threatens the respiratory muscles (risk of respiratory arrest). Neostigmine causes bronchoconstriction: caution in asthma or lung disease. Cholinergic crisis means respiratory arrest risk and possible m…","Multiple Sclerosis — High fall risk. Natalizumab: PML (possibly fatal), TOUCH program only, monotherapy only, higher risk with steroids; monitor LFTs and watch for allergic reactions. Glatiramer: monitor severe laryngeal constriction after …","Patterns You'll Notice Across These Rules","\"Balance is the whole story.\" Parkinson's is too little dopamine for the acetylcholine; myasthenia gravis is too few receptors for the acetylcholine; a cholinergic crisis is too much acetylcholine. Treatment pushes the balance back, and pus…","\"The fix becomes the problem.\" Levodopa loses effect and brings dyskinesias and psychosis; neostigmine overstimulates into cholinergic crisis; triptans constrict vessels a heart patient can't afford to lose; natalizumab suppresses the immun…","\"Autoimmune means steroids, and steroids have limits.\" Myasthenia gravis and MS are both autoimmune with unknown triggers; both use steroids to calm the immune response, and the MS page spells out why they're kept short.","\"Watch the breathing and the swallowing.\" Myasthenia gravis weakens the respiratory and swallowing muscles, both crises end in respiratory failure, and Parkinson's leads to aspiration pneumonia. Airway and nutrition run through all of them.","\"Insidious onset, easy to brush off.\" MG starts as \"a little weakness,\" MS clients look back and realize the weakness started long ago, and Parkinson's takes a while to diagnose because its onset is insidious."]}]},{"f":"week4-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 4","Part 1A — Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)","Part 1B — Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS)","Part 2 — Neuro Infectious Diseases","Part 3A — Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)","Part 3B — Stroke & Ischemic Stroke","Part 3C — Hemorrhagic Stroke & Intracranial Bleeds","Part 3D — Stroke Treatment & Consequences","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 4: the three topic pages that follow, in the order they were taught. Topic 1: ALS and Guillain-Barré syndrome (Parts 1A–1B). Topic 2: neuro infectious diseases (Part 2)…"]},{"s":"Part 1A — Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)▾","a":"part-1a-amyotrophic-lateral-sclerosis-als","k":["Part 1A — Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)▾","Topic 1, first half: what ALS is, its risk factors, the motor neuron pathology and the excitotoxicity (glutamate) hypothesis, the clinical manifestations, and riluzole.","ALS: What It Is","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)","Also called Lou Gehrig's disease (after the Yankees baseball player who had it).","A neurodegenerative, progressive muscle weakness. The cause is unknown.","It is fatal, usually from respiratory failure caused by the progressive muscle weakness. Death usually occurs about 3 years after diagnosis.","Age — Typically diagnosed between 40 and 70; peaks around 60 to 69.","Sex — More diagnoses in males.","Genetics — A familial form with a genetic mutation accounts for about 10% of diagnoses.","Smoking — Some research suggests it is a risk factor.","ALS: Pathology & Cause","The principal feature is degeneration of the upper and lower motor neurons, so the voluntary muscles are affected.","Large motor neurons decrease in the spinal cord, brainstem and cerebral cortex, followed by degeneration of the remaining motor neurons.","Motor neuron death leads to axonal degeneration, demyelination and sclerosis (scarring). Damaged motor neurons stop sending messages, so the muscles can't function.","The excitotoxicity hypothesis (the current working hypothesis): excessive levels of glutamate, an excitatory neurotransmitter, start a series of events that eventually kill the neuron. It came from finding elevated glutamate in the CSF of p…","ALS: Clinical Manifestations","Signs vary greatly from person to person, depending on which neurons are affected. Initial symptoms relate to upper motor neuron dysfunction, lower motor neuron dysfunction, or both.","Spinal form (about half) — Weakness begins in the arms and legs and progresses to muscle atrophy: spasticity, lost dexterity, impaired gait, trouble walking, tripping and falling, hand weakness.","As it progresses — Difficulty chewing, swallowing and speaking; cognitive and behavioral changes in some; constipation (immobility, diet, muscle weakness); sleep problems.","Eventually — Breathing becomes the main issue: ALS paralyzes the breathing muscles, and respiratory failure is why people with ALS die.","ALS: Riluzole","Status — The only FDA-approved drug for ALS.","How it works — A glutamate inhibitor: blocks glutamate receptor sites and helps reduce damage to the motor neurons.","Benefit — Prolongs survival and slows the deterioration of function, but not by long: about 6 months to a year at most.","Side effects — Dizziness, GI upset, nausea, vomiting. Hepatotoxic: watch liver function."]},{"s":"Part 1B — Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS)▾","a":"part-1b-guillain-barr-syndrome-gbs","k":["Part 1B — Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS)▾","Topic 1, second half: what GBS is and what triggers it, the ascending pattern and its timing, the manifestations, and treatment and prognosis.","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: What It Is","Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS), also called acute ascending polyneuritis","A rare autoimmune disorder that causes demyelination: autoantibodies damage the myelin sheath.","Myelin covers the axons and lets impulses travel almost instantaneously. As it is destroyed, impulses are delayed or completely absent.","Viral infection — The usual trigger, a few days to weeks earlier, typically after a respiratory tract or GI infection.","Bacterial infection — Some cases; particularly Campylobacter jejuni, a foodborne illness.","Surgery — Occasionally, days to weeks after surgery.","Flu vaccination — Rarely.","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Pattern & Manifestations","The ascending pattern","Symptoms usually start in the lower extremities (weakness and tingling, pins and needles) and ascend up the body.","Recovery goes the opposite direction: strength returns from the top down (upper body, lungs, then the legs).","Severity ranges from tingling and weakness to full leg paralysis, complete quadriplegia, and respiratory insufficiency.","Timing","Most worsen over hours to weeks.","Some decline rapidly, over 24 hours; these clients may end up intubated on a ventilator.","Recovery is just as variable; some need a tracheostomy after weeks of ventilation.","The concern is the ascending weakness reaching the breathing muscles. Once they're affected, GBS can cause respiratory insufficiency and ultimately respiratory failure needing mechanical ventilation.","Manifestations","Uncoordinated movements, loss of strength, difficulty walking: a high fall risk.","Decreased sensation, so clients may not notice injury.","Loss of bowel and bladder control.","Blurred vision, which doesn't always fit the ascending pattern and can be an early sign.","Problems breathing, swallowing and chewing, so an aspiration risk.","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Treatment & Prognosis","No cure. The goal is to manage severity and speed recovery.","Acute phase: steroids (high dose, titrated down as the client recovers) and IV immunoglobulin (IVIG), which shorten the severity and duration of symptoms; high-dose IVIG can shorten hospital stays.","Most people get GBS only once; some have recurrences.","Survive — 95%","Return to baseline function — 75%","Residual weakness after about 3 years — 25 to 30%; may never return to baseline","Relapses of weakness and tingling many years later — About 3%"]},{"s":"Part 2 — Neuro Infectious Diseases▾","a":"part-2-neuro-infectious-diseases","k":["Part 2 — Neuro Infectious Diseases▾","Topic 2: meningitis (the meninges, causes, who gets it and how it spreads, pathophysiology, signs and the Kernig and Brudzinski tests, bacterial vs viral, treatment and prevention), encephalitis, and brain abscess.","Meningitis: What It Is & What Causes It","The lecture defines meningitis as \"an inflammation of the brain or the spinal cord.\" It is primarily an infection of the pia mater and subarachnoid space, the ventricular system and the CSF.","Infectious meningitis is caused by bacteria: from an infection elsewhere (such as pneumonia), the bloodstream, or a penetrating wound that lets bacteria reach the CSF or brain.","Most common pathogens in adults: Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococci) and Neisseria meningitidis (meningococci).","It may also be caused by viruses (enteroviruses in particular), fungi, parasites or toxins, but bacteria are the most common.","Pia mater — The layer closest to the brain.","Arachnoid mater — The middle layer; the subarachnoid space is filled with cerebrospinal fluid.","Dura mater — The outer layer: really tough and fibrous.","Who Gets Meningitis & How It Spreads","When and who","Typically fall or winter, when people stay inside with the windows closed.","Typically follows otitis or sinusitis (an ear or sinus infection), an immunocompromised state, or pneumonia.","Pneumococcal meningitis mostly affects young people and those older than 40 (as stated in the lecture).","Outbreaks in college dorms, prisons, military bases: anywhere large groups live and sleep together. It is very communicable.","How it spreads","Respiratory droplets, or contact with contaminated saliva or respiratory secretions.","Some carriers of meningococcal bacteria never develop the disease but can still pass it on.","Meningitis: Pathophysiology","1 — Meningococci or pneumococci are inhaled and attach to the epithelial cells of the nasopharynx.","2 — They cross the mucosal barrier and enter the bloodstream.","3 — They travel to the brain, cross the blood-brain barrier and infect the meninges, starting in the arachnoid mater and the CSF of the subarachnoid space.","4 — Large numbers of neutrophils are recruited; the inflammation causes brain edema, and the exudate thickens the CSF and can impair its flow.","5 — Increased intracranial pressure: an adult's skull is a closed system with no room for more.","Meningitis: Clinical Manifestations","The classic triad: Fever (infection), Headache (meningeal irritation; often throbbing), and Stiff neck / nuchal rigidity (tucking the chin to the chest is painful because it stretches the inflamed meninges).","Other manifestations","Change in mental status.","Nausea and vomiting (can happen with increased ICP).","Severe photophobia.","Decreased consciousness, up to coma or death, from increased ICP.","Seizures in about 30% of cases.","Specific to meningococcus: a petechial or purpuric rash covering the skin and mucous membranes.","Tests for Meningeal Irritation","Kernig sign — Client supine; one leg extended, the other flexed 90° at the hip and 90° at the knee. Support the heel and knee of the bent leg and try to straighten it toward the ceiling. — Pain (the client may even flex the neck).","Brudzinski sign — Client supine; place a hand under the base of the skull and flex the neck. — Neck flexion causes the hips and knees to flex, to reduce the pain of meningeal irritation.","Bacterial vs Viral Meningitis","Bacterial","More common, with a higher fatality rate if treatment isn't started quickly: it can kill within hours.","The petechial rash is associated with this form. It is non-blanchable: press a glass over it and a petechial or purpuric rash won't fade.","Can cause septic emboli: tiny microemboli that lodge in capillaries and block blood flow, so adults and children can lose fingers or limbs.","Long-term effects: hearing loss, seizures, brain damage.","Viral (acute viral meningitis)","The milder form.","Often no elevated white cell count in the CSF on lumbar puncture.","Typically no long-term effects.","Bacterial meningitis: give antibiotics as soon as possible and do not hold them for any reason. It can kill within a matter of hours.","Treating & Preventing Meningitis","IV antibiotics — Aggressive therapy with multiple IV antibiotics; two of the most common are ceftriaxone and vancomycin (vancomycin for a MRSA form).","Acyclovir — Given frequently to prevent herpetic encephalitis, which is deadly, in case the cause is viral. Stopped once the CSF results show it isn't viral.","Steroids — To reduce inflammation.","Anti-seizure medications — Often during the acute phase of meningitis and encephalitis, because increased ICP and inflammation raise seizure risk.","Vaccines (the best prophylaxis) — Available for meningococcus, pneumococcus and H. influenzae. The meningitis vaccine is first given around age 11 or 12, with a booster around 16; a different version is given to people who are immunocomprom…","Encephalitis","Inflammation of the brain tissue","An acute condition, usually caused by a virus.","Causes: West Nile virus (from mosquitoes; the lecture mentions a 2016 outbreak), measles, chickenpox, mumps. It can happen very rapidly, and patients can die.","Herpes simplex virus 1 causes herpetic encephalitis: very severe, with rapidly appearing manifestations, unlike viral meningitis, which is relatively mild.","With severe symptoms, clients are often started on both antibiotics and acyclovir until the cause is known.","Onset — Signs typically appear about 2 to 3 days after infection, once enough virus builds up.","Manifestations — From mild up to mental status changes and coma; fever and headache, nausea and vomiting; CNS changes such as seizures (common); others depending on where the infection is. These are vague and nonspecific; you don't need to …","Acyclovir — For the HSV type in particular, but not effective on other viruses. In herpetic encephalitis it reduces mortality but doesn't necessarily reduce neurological complications.","Supportive care — Fluids, acetaminophen, antiemetics for vomiting, and often anti-seizure medications.","Brain Abscess","What it is — An accumulation of pus within the brain tissue, from a localized infection (like encephalitis) or a systemic infection.","Common sources — Ear, tooth, mastoid or sinus infections, because of their proximity to the brain.","Major culprits — Streptococci and Staphylococcus aureus, bacteria found on the skin that typically cause ear, tooth and sinus infections.","Imaging — The abscess walls are visible, and the midline can be shifted by the pressure from pus and inflammation.","Treatment — Drain the abscess to reduce pressure (the brain is a closed system, so this lowers the risk of secondary injury from swelling), then put antibiotics into the area.","Manifestations — Similar to meningitis and encephalitis: headache, fever, nausea, vomiting; signs of increased ICP (drowsiness, confusion, up to seizures). Other symptoms depend on location; for example, a temporal lobe abscess causes visua…"]},{"s":"Part 3A — Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)▾","a":"part-3a-transient-ischemic-attack-tia","k":["Part 3A — Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)▾","Topic 3, first voiceover (TIA): what a TIA is, carotid vs vertebrobasilar presentations, the Bell's palsy mimic, the key terms, and TIA diagnosis and treatment.","Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)","A type of cerebrovascular disease: a focal, acute cerebral insufficiency. Usually only one vessel gets closed off, clotted off or has a spasm, so only that area of the brain is affected.","Typically lasts less than 24 hours: a transient episode of neurologic dysfunction with no acute infarction. Symptoms are often gone by the time the client reaches the ED, with no residual effects.","A stroke, by contrast, causes neurological deficits that last longer than 24 hours.","Fairly common; seen more often in males.","After a TIA, the client is at increased risk for a stroke, especially within the first month. Monitor and teach them to go to the ED if they suspect another TIA, because it might be a full-blown stroke.","Presentation depends on which vessel is involved (a vessel supplying the speech area causes difficulty forming words). Recurrent TIAs usually produce the same symptoms each time. Onset is abrupt, and recovery is abrupt and rapid.","The lecture also says a CT can show blood in the brain tissue with a TIA, though \"not as clear as it is with an ischemic stroke.\" That is kept as stated; check the slide wording.","Carotid area","Weakness or heaviness in the arms, legs or face.","Numbness.","Vision loss.","Vertebrobasilar area (back of the brain)","Blurry vision.","Vertigo.","Ataxia.","Motor or sensory changes.","TIA mimic: Bell's palsy","A problem with the facial nerve causes facial paralysis (drooping mouth, drooping face), so these clients are often evaluated for a stroke or TIA. Bell's palsy recovers, but it takes time and requires treatment; a TIA recovers much more qui…","Key Terms: Dysphagia, Dysphasia, Contralateral & Ipsilateral","Dysphagia (with a G) — Difficulty swallowing. Memory trick: G for gastric. — Keep NPO: high risk for aspiration (a foreign substance entering the lungs from eating and drinking without swallowing properly), which can inflame and damage the …","Dysphasia (with an S) — Difficulty with speech. — A communication problem.","Contralateral — Crossing the midline: the opposite side. — Manifestations appear on the opposite side of the body from the brain damage.","Ipsilateral — Staying on the same side. — Manifestations appear on the same side as the damage.","TIA: Diagnosis & Treatment","History and physical — Is there a pattern of other vascular problems?","Quick CT scan first — Rule out hemorrhage, which is easy to see on CT. Then look for other evidence of CVA, such as lacunar infarcts (tiny infarcts that may cause no symptoms).","MRA (MRI with angiography) — Uses dye to highlight aneurysms.","Carotid Doppler studies — Look for buildup in the carotid arteries reducing blood flow to the brain.","Echocardiogram — Look for a cardiac cause, such as a clot on the left side of the heart that could break off and travel to the brain.","Treatment depends on the cause; the goal is reducing the risk of a full stroke","Small doses of aspirin.","Clopidogrel (Plavix): an antiplatelet that keeps platelets from sticking together.","If the TIA is cardiac-related: heparin or warfarin to prevent coagulation.","If caused by carotid narrowing or stenosis: carotid endarterectomy (opens the artery by removing the plaque), or carotid angioplasty (a balloon catheter fed up through usually the femoral artery and inflated, with a filter to catch plaque p…","Education on lifestyle changes, especially the modifiable risk factors for stroke."]},{"s":"Part 3B — Stroke & Ischemic Stroke▾","a":"part-3b-stroke-ischemic-stroke","k":["Part 3B — Stroke & Ischemic Stroke▾","Topic 3, second voiceover (Stroke and hemorrhage 1): stroke types and statistics, risk factors, signs and the first CT, ischemic stroke (thrombotic and embolic), and the penumbra.","Stroke (CVA): Overview & Statistics","Also called a cerebrovascular accident (CVA), the term used in hospitals, or a \"brain attack\" (a term that didn't stick).","The result of a problem with blood supply to the brain. Two types: ischemic, about 87% of strokes, and hemorrhagic, about 13%.","About 800,000 strokes a year; for over half a million people it's their first stroke. Risk rises with age, but about a third of people with a first stroke are under 65.","A leading cause of disability. Third leading cause of death in women, fifth in men, fourth overall. Someone has a stroke about every 40 seconds and someone dies from one every 4 minutes; about $36.5 billion in healthcare costs a year.","Stroke Risk Factors","Hypertension — Especially uncontrolled or undiagnosed. Commonly leads to hemorrhagic stroke, often by way of an aneurysm that ruptures, or by weakening the arterial wall until it ruptures. More than doubles the risk of intracranial hemorrha…","Atherosclerosis and hyperlipidemia — Vessel damage leads to plaque formation and clots, in brain vessels as in the heart.","Tobacco use — Damages the endothelial lining of blood vessels.","Diabetes — Damages blood vessel walls, which can lead to plaque formation.","Oral contraceptives — Particularly estrogen and progesterone: increase plasma fibrinogen and coagulation factor activity.","Age (starred) — Advanced age: higher risk of stroke but a better chance of recovery, because brain atrophy leaves more room in the closed skull for swelling and bleeding.","Male sex — Males are at higher risk overall.","Sickle cell anemia — In crisis, blood cells clump and can lodge in brain vessels.","Atrial fibrillation — Quivering atria let blood pool and clot; the clot can travel to the brain. Often treated with anticoagulants such as warfarin, which bring their own bleeding risk.","Signs of a Stroke & the First Test","Clinical manifestations","Numbness or weakness of the face, arm or leg, usually on one side (asymmetrical weakness).","Sudden confusion; difficulty speaking or understanding speech; slurred speech (dysarthria).","Vision trouble in one or both eyes.","Trouble walking, balance problems, dizziness, lack of coordination (ataxia).","A sudden, severe headache with no known cause, particularly important in hemorrhagic stroke.","CT without contrast, done very quickly","Part of the stroke alert protocol (with attention to airway and breathing as needed); it drives the rest of the treatment plan.","Blood shows up right away as white; an ischemic stroke shows as a darkened area.","We must know ischemic vs hemorrhagic: never anticoagulate or give a thrombolytic to a hemorrhagic stroke. It makes the bleeding worse and could kill the client.","Ischemic Stroke","An occlusion of a cerebral artery by a thrombus or an embolus; everything distal to the occlusion loses its blood supply.","Both types typically present the same way, based on which part of the brain isn't perfused, but the etiology guides treatment: atherosclerosis? a hypercoagulability problem? a thrombus or embolism elsewhere?","On CT, a darkened area that isn't prominent at first but becomes more noticeable if blood flow isn't restored.","A tiny vessel occlusion may go unnoticed (evidence of an old stroke is sometimes found on an unrelated CT). A larger vessel occlusion produces symptoms based on its location.","Neuro deficits start within about one minute of oxygen deprivation; without reestablished blood flow, the damage becomes irreversible.","Hemorrhagic conversion: after an ischemic stroke, blood from the peripheral circulation extravasates across a disrupted blood-brain barrier into the brain tissue once reperfusion is established, naturally or as a result of therapy. A hemorr…","Thrombotic — Arterial obstruction from thrombus formation. Atherosclerosis: vessel-lining damage leads to plaque that narrows or blocks the vessel; if the plaque ruptures, a clot forms. Hypercoagulable states: cancer, oral contraceptives, a…","Embolic — Look at the heart first: Mural thrombus: a clot in the ventricle (the image shows the left ventricle); a piece breaks off and travels through the aorta and carotids to the brain. Atrial fibrillation: a left atrial thrombus from po…","Mural thrombus: a clot in the ventricle (the image shows the left ventricle); a piece breaks off and travels through the aorta and carotids to the brain.","Atrial fibrillation: a left atrial thrombus from pooled blood. Usually arterial clots, on the left side of the heart.","Patent foramen ovale: a birth-defect hole in the septum between the atria lets a venous clot (such as a DVT piece) pass from the right atrium to the left atrium instead of going to the lungs as a PE; the left ventricle then sends it to the …","Valve infection, particularly the mitral valve: vegetation forms, and a piece breaks off as an embolus.","Mechanical valve replacement (more than a porcine valve): clots can form around the valve.","Carotid artery plaque can rupture, and the clot travels to the brain.","The lecture describes the mitral valve as \"between the right and left ventricle.\" It is kept out of the table as a likely slip; check the slide.","Assessment clues for embolic risk","Carotid bruit: a swishing sound on auscultation from turbulent flow through a vessel narrowed by a growing plaque.","ECG: in A-fib, little quivering lines replace the P waves (the atria aren't contracting properly). These clients are at higher risk for embolic stroke, especially if newly diagnosed and not yet anticoagulated long enough.","Time Is Tissue: The Penumbra","Dead tissue (core)","Injured beyond repair.","Penumbra","The extended area of injured but salvageable tissue around the core.","Goal for ischemic stroke: treatment within 3 hours of symptom onset, to reestablish blood flow and save the penumbra. Once a stroke protocol is activated, the client goes to CT first (often bypassing a room), labs are drawn quickly and the …"]},{"s":"Part 3C — Hemorrhagic Stroke & Intracranial Bleeds▾","a":"part-3c-hemorrhagic-stroke-intracranial-bleeds","k":["Part 3C — Hemorrhagic Stroke & Intracranial Bleeds▾","Topic 3, third voiceover (Stroke and hemorrhage 2): hemorrhagic stroke, epidural, subdural and subarachnoid bleeds, subarachnoid hemorrhage management, berry aneurysms and AVMs, and BE FAST.","Hemorrhagic Stroke","A ruptured vessel with bleeding within the brain tissue (the parenchyma).","Leading cause: untreated hypertension; over time the pressure weakens vessels until they rupture. Clients with aneurysms are also at increased risk.","About 38% mortality, within minutes to hours; in the brainstem, death can occur within seconds. Mortality depends on the size of the area affected.","Blood is very irritating to brain tissue, so inflammation causes edema; blood plus edema in the confined skull sharply increases ICP. The brain tissue can collapse on itself (herniation), which usually results in death.","Usually ages 30 to 60, younger than ischemic stroke clients. High morbidity and mortality, with serious disabilities.","Prognosis depends on","Age: older clients have brain atrophy and more room, so a better prognosis.","Location and size: the brainstem has a very poor prognosis.","How rapid the bleeding is, and whether it causes brain distortion or shift. A midline shift (a bleed pushing one side across the center line, seen on a CT report) means a very poor prognosis.","Presentation","The worst headache of their life.","A rapid change in level of consciousness.","With subdural, epidural or subarachnoid bleeds, the client presents like meningitis from meningeal irritation: nuchal rigidity and photophobia.","Other Intracranial Hemorrhages","Bleeding in the layers covering the brain, named by location. A bleed in the brain tissue itself is a hemorrhagic stroke.","Epidural — Between the skull and the dura mater. — Usually with skull fractures (think trauma). Tend to be arterial, so even a less severe injury can mean a lot of blood loss.","Subdural — Below the dura. — Tend to be venous, e.g., injury to bridging veins. Severe injury can cause rapid decline, but venous blood may accumulate slowly: a client hits their head, seems fine, and symptoms appear later.","Subarachnoid — Between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater, where the CSF lives. — Usually from an aneurysm or an AVM. Causes bloody CSF.","Treatment for epidural and subdural bleeds is to evacuate the collecting blood and relieve pressure on the brain. Blood bleeding into the brain tissue cannot be evacuated.","Subarachnoid Hemorrhage","Diagnosis by lumbar puncture","Normal CSF is clear or slightly yellow. The first tube may have a little blood from needle trauma, but it should clear. With a subarachnoid bleed, the fluid stays bloody. A subarachnoid hemorrhage is identified by lumbar puncture about 12 h…","Bed rest and BP control — Avoid big BP fluctuations: keep it managed to prevent further bleeding, but high enough to perfuse the brain. The nurse is often given very specific BP parameters.","Surgery — If an aneurysm is the culprit, surgical intervention (clipping or coiling), usually done ASAP.","Anticonvulsant — Blood irritates the brain, so seizure risk rises.","Calcium channel blocker — After clipping or coiling, to help treat the symptoms of the subarachnoid hemorrhage.","Triple H — Keeps the brain perfused: pressors to keep BP high enough, colloids and a hypervolemic state, and some expected hemodilution.","The lecture describes the calcium channel blocker as working \"by narrowing and slowing that blood flow.\" It is kept out of the table as stated; check the slide for the intended mechanism.","Berry Aneurysms, AVMs, Clipping & Coiling","Berry aneurysm — The most common aneurysm that ruptures in the brain: little berries hanging off the artery that rupture when big enough.","AVM (arteriovenous malformation) — Normally arteries divide into arterioles, capillaries, venules, then veins. In an AVM the junction is a jumbled clump, so high-pressure arterial blood goes directly into the vein without slowing through ca…","Clipping — A clip is placed around the aneurysm to block blood flow into it.","Coiling (endovascular embolization) — A catheter inserted in the groin is threaded into the aneurysm, and detachable platinum coils reduce blood flow into the sac, lowering the chance of rupture.","The lecture also says \"berry aneurysms are often located in the veins,\" which conflicts with the same slide (\"hanging off the artery\") and with Week 1. It is kept out of the table; check the slide.","BE FAST & Last Known Normal","B: Balance — Headache, dizziness, loss of balance, trouble walking; falls, especially in the elderly.","E: Eyes — Sudden vision loss in one or both eyes; unequal pupils.","F: Face — Facial asymmetry: have them smile. Differentiate from Bell's palsy.","A: Arm — Weakness: both arms held up perpendicular to the body; does one drift?","S: Speech — Difficulty speaking or slurred words.","T: Time — Call 911, get to the hospital, activate a stroke alert.","Find out exactly when symptoms started: the \"last known normal.\" Treatment must happen within a certain time frame."]},{"s":"Part 3D — Stroke Treatment & Consequences▾","a":"part-3d-stroke-treatment-consequences","k":["Part 3D — Stroke Treatment & Consequences▾","Topic 3, fourth voiceover (Stroke treatment): the first steps, hemorrhagic vs ischemic treatment, alteplase, and the consequences of stroke.","Stroke Treatment: First Steps","ABCs: can they maintain their airway, are they breathing, is there a pulse? A GCS below 8 raises concern about airway protection, so the client is intubated. Stroke clients may also have decreased respiratory drive.","Stroke alert / protocol: labs, CT, the neuro team, and a blood sugar check, because hypoglycemia can mimic a stroke.","CT scan without contrast, done first to exclude hemorrhagic stroke: fast, and best in the acute stage. An MRI gives better images later, but it is slower and less available, so it isn't the first choice in an emergency.","Hemorrhagic stroke","Reverse anticoagulation: is there a reversal agent or antidote for the client's medications?","Manage hypertension, the likely cause.","Manage ICP: elevate the head of the bed, give antipyretics for fever, keep the head in a neutral position.","Ischemic stroke","Thrombolytics (clot busters) to open the occlusion and protect and reperfuse the penumbra.","Mechanical thrombectomy: a surgeon removes the clot manually (also called a penumbra procedure).","Alteplase (tPA) for Ischemic Stroke","Heparin and antiplatelets don't dissolve a clot; they only prevent one from forming. Fibrinolytics actually break up clots.","Alteplase (tPA, tissue plasminogen activator), the most commonly used fibrinolytic, converts plasminogen to plasmin; plasmin digests the fibrin.","Used in severe thrombotic events: acute MI, PE, ischemic stroke, occasionally femoral artery clots or a clotted central line. Given IV.","Monitor BP — It can cause hypotension.","Monitor HR and rhythm — Arrhythmias can develop with reperfusion.","Bleeding risk — Increased risk of intracranial bleeding. Don't give it to clients at high risk for bleeding (such as a previous hemorrhagic stroke or a GI bleed).","CT first — Always rule out hemorrhagic stroke before giving it.","With heparin — Often given with heparin to prevent new clots from forming.","Antidote — Aminocaproic acid, an antithrombolytic: blocks plasminogen binding and activation to plasmin, reducing fibrinolysis to stop bleeding. Give it only if the hemorrhage is life-threatening.","Consequences of Stroke","Left side of the brain affected","Difficulty with communication (verbal and written) and with more analytical activities.","Right side of the brain affected","Difficulty with things like imagination, music and art.","Dysphagia — A swallow evaluation comes before giving food or advancing a diet. Speech pathology does a barium swallow study under real-time x-ray with different thicknesses of food and liquid, then recommends a diet: regular, thickened liqu…","Motor and sensory deficits — Neglect of body parts no longer getting sensory input; poor coordination (fall risk); loss of muscle groups, which atrophy over time.","Flaccidity → spasticity → contractures — Flaccid (limp, weak) muscles on the contralateral side become spastic (painful), then contractures (permanently shortened muscles, joints stuck in abnormal positions), typically within the first 6 we…","Homonymous hemianopia — Contralateral field blindness: loss (or blurring) of up to half the vision, on the same side of both visual fields.","Aphasia — The global term for affected language: dysphasia (speech), receptive aphasia (understanding), reading and writing problems; one or all.","Depression — Very common, from significant disabilities and loss of independence.","Memory problems — Even well-known things like family members' names.","Behavior changes — Increased emotional response (upset, angry or crying more easily); slower reaction times; hesitance or caution; confusion or disorientation; apathy.","The lecture also says clients \"can underestimate their own abilities\" and calls it a big safety concern, alongside slower reaction times (fall risk). That is kept as stated; check the slide wording."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One row per part: the points to have cold before an exam. Every detail is taught in full in the parts above.","1A. ALS — Lou Gehrig's disease; unknown cause; fatal from respiratory failure, about 3 years after diagnosis. Ages 40–70 (peak 60–69), males, 10% familial, possibly smoking. Upper and lower motor neuron degeneration → voluntary muscles. Exc…","1B. Guillain-Barré Syndrome — Autoimmune demyelination of peripheral nerves (acute ascending polyneuritis). Trigger: viral respiratory or GI infection; also Campylobacter jejuni, surgery, rarely flu vaccine. Weakness and tingling ascend fro…","2. Neuro Infectious Diseases — Meningitis: S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis; respiratory droplets; fall and winter; dorms, prisons, military. Triad: fever, headache, nuchal rigidity; photophobia; seizures in 30%; non-blanching petechial ra…","3A. TIA — Focal, acute, one vessel; under 24 hours, no infarction, no residual effects. Stroke risk highest in the first month. Carotid: weakness, numbness, vision loss. Vertebrobasilar: blurry vision, vertigo, ataxia. Bell's palsy mimic. D…","3B. Stroke & Ischemic Stroke — 87% ischemic, 13% hemorrhagic; leading cause of disability. Risks: HTN, atherosclerosis, tobacco, diabetes, OCs, age (higher risk, better recovery), males, sickle cell, A-fib. Non-contrast CT first: blood is w…","3C. Hemorrhagic Stroke & Bleeds — Ruptured vessel; untreated HTN; 38% mortality; ages 30–60; edema and ICP → herniation. Prognosis: age, location and size, bleeding speed and midline shift. Worst headache of their life; meningeal signs. Epi…","3D. Stroke Treatment & Consequences — ABCs (GCS below 8 → intubate), blood sugar, non-contrast CT first. Hemorrhagic: reverse anticoagulation, manage BP and ICP (HOB up, antipyretics, neutral head). Ischemic: alteplase (plasminogen → plasmi…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the seven parts above: a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week4-als-gbs.html","t":"ALS & Guillain-Barré Syndrome","secs":[{"s":"ALS & Guillain-Barré Syndrome","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 1","ALS: What It Is","ALS: Pathology & Cause","ALS: Clinical Manifestations","ALS: Riluzole","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: What It Is","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Pattern & Manifestations","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Treatment & Prognosis","Self-Test Flashcards","This page combines two short voiceovers. ALS: what it is, who gets it, the motor neuron pathology, the excitotoxicity hypothesis, clinical manifestations and riluzole. Guillain-Barré syndrome: the autoimmune demyelination, its triggers, the…"]},{"s":"ALS: What It Is▾","a":"als-what-it-is","k":["ALS: What It Is▾","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)","Also called Lou Gehrig's disease (after the Yankees baseball player who had it).","A neurodegenerative, progressive muscle weakness. The cause is unknown.","It is fatal, usually from respiratory failure caused by the progressive muscle weakness. Death usually occurs about 3 years after diagnosis.","Age — Typically diagnosed between 40 and 70; peaks around 60 to 69.","Sex — More diagnoses in males.","Genetics — A familial form with a genetic mutation accounts for about 10% of diagnoses.","Smoking — Some research suggests it is a risk factor."]},{"s":"ALS: Pathology & Cause▾","a":"als-pathology-cause","k":["ALS: Pathology & Cause▾","The principal feature is degeneration of the upper and lower motor neurons, so the voluntary muscles are affected.","Large motor neurons decrease in the spinal cord, brainstem and cerebral cortex, followed by degeneration of the remaining motor neurons.","Motor neuron death leads to axonal degeneration, demyelination and sclerosis (scarring). Damaged motor neurons stop sending messages, so the muscles can't function.","The excitotoxicity hypothesis (the current working hypothesis): excessive levels of glutamate, an excitatory neurotransmitter, start a series of events that eventually kill the neuron. It came from finding elevated glutamate in the CSF of p…"]},{"s":"ALS: Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"als-clinical-manifestations","k":["ALS: Clinical Manifestations▾","Signs vary greatly from person to person, depending on which neurons are affected. Initial symptoms relate to upper motor neuron dysfunction, lower motor neuron dysfunction, or both.","Spinal form (about half) — Weakness begins in the arms and legs and progresses to muscle atrophy: spasticity, lost dexterity, impaired gait, trouble walking, tripping and falling, hand weakness.","As it progresses — Difficulty chewing, swallowing and speaking; cognitive and behavioral changes in some; constipation (immobility, diet, muscle weakness); sleep problems.","Eventually — Breathing becomes the main issue: ALS paralyzes the breathing muscles, and respiratory failure is why people with ALS die."]},{"s":"ALS: Riluzole▾","a":"als-riluzole","k":["ALS: Riluzole▾","Status — The only FDA-approved drug for ALS.","How it works — A glutamate inhibitor: blocks glutamate receptor sites and helps reduce damage to the motor neurons.","Benefit — Prolongs survival and slows the deterioration of function, but not by long: about 6 months to a year at most.","Side effects — Dizziness, GI upset, nausea, vomiting. Hepatotoxic: watch liver function."]},{"s":"Guillain-Barré Syndrome: What It Is▾","a":"guillain-barr-syndrome-what-it-is","k":["Guillain-Barré Syndrome: What It Is▾","Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS), also called acute ascending polyneuritis","A rare autoimmune disorder that causes demyelination: autoantibodies damage the myelin sheath.","Myelin covers the axons and lets impulses travel almost instantaneously. As it is destroyed, impulses are delayed or completely absent.","CNS demyelination (multiple sclerosis) is covered in Multiple Sclerosis; GBS is the peripheral nervous system version","Viral infection — The usual trigger, a few days to weeks earlier, typically after a respiratory tract or GI infection.","Bacterial infection — Some cases; particularly Campylobacter jejuni, a foodborne illness.","Surgery — Occasionally, days to weeks after surgery.","Flu vaccination — Rarely."]},{"s":"Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Pattern & Manifestations▾","a":"guillain-barr-syndrome-pattern-manifestations","k":["Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Pattern & Manifestations▾","The ascending pattern","Symptoms usually start in the lower extremities (weakness and tingling, pins and needles) and ascend up the body.","Recovery goes the opposite direction: strength returns from the top down (upper body, lungs, then the legs).","Severity ranges from tingling and weakness to full leg paralysis, complete quadriplegia, and respiratory insufficiency.","Timing","Most worsen over hours to weeks.","Some decline rapidly, over 24 hours; these clients may end up intubated on a ventilator.","Recovery is just as variable; some need a tracheostomy after weeks of ventilation.","The concern is the ascending weakness reaching the breathing muscles. Once they're affected, GBS can cause respiratory insufficiency and ultimately respiratory failure needing mechanical ventilation.","Manifestations","Uncoordinated movements, loss of strength, difficulty walking: a high fall risk.","Decreased sensation, so clients may not notice injury.","Loss of bowel and bladder control.","Blurred vision, which doesn't always fit the ascending pattern and can be an early sign.","Problems breathing, swallowing and chewing, so an aspiration risk."]},{"s":"Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Treatment & Prognosis▾","a":"guillain-barr-syndrome-treatment-prognosis","k":["Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Treatment & Prognosis▾","No cure. The goal is to manage severity and speed recovery.","Acute phase: steroids (high dose, titrated down as the client recovers) and IV immunoglobulin (IVIG), which shorten the severity and duration of symptoms; high-dose IVIG can shorten hospital stays.","Most people get GBS only once; some have recurrences.","Survive — 95%","Return to baseline function — 75%","Residual weakness after about 3 years — 25 to 30%; may never return to baseline","Relapses of weakness and tingling many years later — About 3%"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week4-neuro-infections.html","t":"Neuro Infectious Diseases","secs":[{"s":"Neuro Infectious Diseases","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 2","Meningitis: What It Is & What Causes It","Who Gets Meningitis & How It Spreads","Meningitis: Pathophysiology","Meningitis: Clinical Manifestations","Bacterial vs Viral Meningitis","Treating & Preventing Meningitis","Encephalitis","Brain Abscess","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the inflammatory and infectious neurological disorders voiceover: meningitis (causes, the meninges, spread, pathophysiology, signs and the Kernig and Brudzinski tests, bacterial vs viral, treatment and prevention), encepha…"]},{"s":"Meningitis: What It Is & What Causes It▾","a":"meningitis-what-it-is-what-causes-it","k":["Meningitis: What It Is & What Causes It▾","The lecture defines meningitis as \"an inflammation of the brain or the spinal cord.\" It is primarily an infection of the pia mater and subarachnoid space, the ventricular system and the CSF.","Infectious meningitis is caused by bacteria: from an infection elsewhere (such as pneumonia), the bloodstream, or a penetrating wound that lets bacteria reach the CSF or brain.","Most common pathogens in adults: Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococci) and Neisseria meningitidis (meningococci).","It may also be caused by viruses (enteroviruses in particular), fungi, parasites or toxins, but bacteria are the most common.","Pia mater — The layer closest to the brain.","Arachnoid mater — The middle layer; the subarachnoid space is filled with cerebrospinal fluid.","Dura mater — The outer layer: really tough and fibrous."]},{"s":"Who Gets Meningitis & How It Spreads▾","a":"who-gets-meningitis-how-it-spreads","k":["Who Gets Meningitis & How It Spreads▾","When and who","Typically fall or winter, when people stay inside with the windows closed.","Typically follows otitis or sinusitis (an ear or sinus infection), an immunocompromised state, or pneumonia.","Pneumococcal meningitis mostly affects young people and those older than 40 (as stated in the lecture).","Outbreaks in college dorms, prisons, military bases: anywhere large groups live and sleep together. It is very communicable.","How it spreads","Respiratory droplets, or contact with contaminated saliva or respiratory secretions.","Some carriers of meningococcal bacteria never develop the disease but can still pass it on."]},{"s":"Meningitis: Pathophysiology▾","a":"meningitis-pathophysiology","k":["Meningitis: Pathophysiology▾","1 — Meningococci or pneumococci are inhaled and attach to the epithelial cells of the nasopharynx.","2 — They cross the mucosal barrier and enter the bloodstream.","3 — They travel to the brain, cross the blood-brain barrier and infect the meninges, starting in the arachnoid mater and the CSF of the subarachnoid space.","4 — Large numbers of neutrophils are recruited; the inflammation causes brain edema, and the exudate thickens the CSF and can impair its flow.","5 — Increased intracranial pressure: an adult's skull is a closed system with no room for more."]},{"s":"Meningitis: Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"meningitis-clinical-manifestations","k":["Meningitis: Clinical Manifestations▾","The classic triad: Fever (infection), Headache (meningeal irritation; often throbbing), and Stiff neck / nuchal rigidity (tucking the chin to the chest is painful because it stretches the inflamed meninges).","Other manifestations","Change in mental status.","Nausea and vomiting (can happen with increased ICP).","Severe photophobia.","Decreased consciousness, up to coma or death, from increased ICP.","Seizures in about 30% of cases.","Specific to meningococcus: a petechial or purpuric rash covering the skin and mucous membranes.","Tests for Meningeal Irritation","Kernig sign — Client supine; one leg extended, the other flexed 90° at the hip and 90° at the knee. Support the heel and knee of the bent leg and try to straighten it toward the ceiling. — Pain (the client may even flex the neck).","Brudzinski sign — Client supine; place a hand under the base of the skull and flex the neck. — Neck flexion causes the hips and knees to flex, to reduce the pain of meningeal irritation."]},{"s":"Bacterial vs Viral Meningitis▾","a":"bacterial-vs-viral-meningitis","k":["Bacterial vs Viral Meningitis▾","Bacterial","More common, with a higher fatality rate if treatment isn't started quickly: it can kill within hours.","The petechial rash is associated with this form. It is non-blanchable: press a glass over it and a petechial or purpuric rash won't fade.","Can cause septic emboli: tiny microemboli that lodge in capillaries and block blood flow, so adults and children can lose fingers or limbs.","Long-term effects: hearing loss, seizures, brain damage.","Viral (acute viral meningitis)","The milder form.","Often no elevated white cell count in the CSF on lumbar puncture.","Typically no long-term effects.","Bacterial meningitis: give antibiotics as soon as possible and do not hold them for any reason. It can kill within a matter of hours."]},{"s":"Treating & Preventing Meningitis▾","a":"treating-preventing-meningitis","k":["Treating & Preventing Meningitis▾","IV antibiotics — Aggressive therapy with multiple IV antibiotics; two of the most common are ceftriaxone and vancomycin (vancomycin for a MRSA form).","Acyclovir — Given frequently to prevent herpetic encephalitis, which is deadly, in case the cause is viral. Stopped once the CSF results show it isn't viral.","Steroids — To reduce inflammation.","Anti-seizure medications — Often during the acute phase of meningitis and encephalitis, because increased ICP and inflammation raise seizure risk.","Vaccines (the best prophylaxis) — Available for meningococcus, pneumococcus and H. influenzae. The meningitis vaccine is first given around age 11 or 12, with a booster around 16; a different version is given to people who are immunocomprom…"]},{"s":"Encephalitis▾","a":"encephalitis","k":["Encephalitis▾","Inflammation of the brain tissue","An acute condition, usually caused by a virus.","Causes: West Nile virus (from mosquitoes; the lecture mentions a 2016 outbreak), measles, chickenpox, mumps. It can happen very rapidly, and patients can die.","Herpes simplex virus 1 causes herpetic encephalitis: very severe, with rapidly appearing manifestations, unlike viral meningitis, which is relatively mild.","With severe symptoms, clients are often started on both antibiotics and acyclovir until the cause is known.","Onset — Signs typically appear about 2 to 3 days after infection, once enough virus builds up.","Manifestations — From mild up to mental status changes and coma; fever and headache, nausea and vomiting; CNS changes such as seizures (common); others depending on where the infection is. These are vague and nonspecific; you don't need to …","Acyclovir — For the HSV type in particular, but not effective on other viruses. In herpetic encephalitis it reduces mortality but doesn't necessarily reduce neurological complications.","Supportive care — Fluids, acetaminophen, antiemetics for vomiting, and often anti-seizure medications."]},{"s":"Brain Abscess▾","a":"brain-abscess","k":["Brain Abscess▾","What it is — An accumulation of pus within the brain tissue, from a localized infection (like encephalitis) or a systemic infection.","Common sources — Ear, tooth, mastoid or sinus infections, because of their proximity to the brain.","Major culprits — Streptococci and Staphylococcus aureus, bacteria found on the skin that typically cause ear, tooth and sinus infections.","Imaging — The abscess walls are visible, and the midline can be shifted by the pressure from pus and inflammation.","Treatment — Drain the abscess to reduce pressure (the brain is a closed system, so this lowers the risk of secondary injury from swelling), then put antibiotics into the area.","Manifestations — Similar to meningitis and encephalitis: headache, fever, nausea, vomiting; signs of increased ICP (drowsiness, confusion, up to seizures). Other symptoms depend on location; for example, a temporal lobe abscess causes visua…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week4-stroke.html","t":"TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage","secs":[{"s":"TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 3","Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)","Key Terms: Dysphagia, Dysphasia, Contralateral & Ipsilateral","TIA: Diagnosis & Treatment","Stroke (CVA): Overview & Statistics","Stroke Risk Factors","Signs of a Stroke & the First Test","Ischemic Stroke","Time Is Tissue: The Penumbra","Hemorrhagic Stroke","Other Intracranial Hemorrhages","Subarachnoid Hemorrhage","Berry Aneurysms, AVMs, Clipping & Coiling","BE FAST & Last Known Normal","Stroke Treatment: First Steps","Alteplase (tPA) for Ischemic Stroke","Consequences of Stroke","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows four voiceovers: transient ischemic attacks; stroke and hemorrhage part 1 (stroke types, statistics, risk factors, signs, CT, ischemic stroke and the penumbra); part 2 (hemorrhagic stroke, other intracranial hemorrhages, a…"]},{"s":"Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)▾","a":"transient-ischemic-attack-tia","k":["Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)▾","A type of cerebrovascular disease: a focal, acute cerebral insufficiency. Usually only one vessel gets closed off, clotted off or has a spasm, so only that area of the brain is affected.","Typically lasts less than 24 hours: a transient episode of neurologic dysfunction with no acute infarction. Symptoms are often gone by the time the client reaches the ED, with no residual effects.","A stroke, by contrast, causes neurological deficits that last longer than 24 hours.","Fairly common; seen more often in males.","After a TIA, the client is at increased risk for a stroke, especially within the first month. Monitor and teach them to go to the ED if they suspect another TIA, because it might be a full-blown stroke.","Presentation depends on which vessel is involved (a vessel supplying the speech area causes difficulty forming words). Recurrent TIAs usually produce the same symptoms each time. Onset is abrupt, and recovery is abrupt and rapid.","The lecture also says a CT can show blood in the brain tissue with a TIA, though \"not as clear as it is with an ischemic stroke.\" That is kept as stated; check the slide wording.","Carotid area","Weakness or heaviness in the arms, legs or face.","Numbness.","Vision loss.","Vertebrobasilar area (back of the brain)","Blurry vision.","Vertigo.","Ataxia.","Motor or sensory changes.","TIA mimic: Bell's palsy","A problem with the facial nerve causes facial paralysis (drooping mouth, drooping face), so these clients are often evaluated for a stroke or TIA. Bell's palsy recovers, but it takes time and requires treatment; a TIA recovers much more qui…"]},{"s":"Key Terms: Dysphagia, Dysphasia, Contralateral & Ipsilateral▾","a":"key-terms-dysphagia-dysphasia-contralateral-ipsilateral","k":["Key Terms: Dysphagia, Dysphasia, Contralateral & Ipsilateral▾","Dysphagia (with a G) — Difficulty swallowing. Memory trick: G for gastric. — Keep NPO: high risk for aspiration (a foreign substance entering the lungs from eating and drinking without swallowing properly), which can inflame and damage the …","Dysphasia (with an S) — Difficulty with speech. — A communication problem.","Contralateral — Crossing the midline: the opposite side. — Manifestations appear on the opposite side of the body from the brain damage.","Ipsilateral — Staying on the same side. — Manifestations appear on the same side as the damage."]},{"s":"TIA: Diagnosis & Treatment▾","a":"tia-diagnosis-treatment","k":["TIA: Diagnosis & Treatment▾","History and physical — Is there a pattern of other vascular problems?","Quick CT scan first — Rule out hemorrhage, which is easy to see on CT. Then look for other evidence of CVA, such as lacunar infarcts (tiny infarcts that may cause no symptoms).","MRA (MRI with angiography) — Uses dye to highlight aneurysms.","Carotid Doppler studies — Look for buildup in the carotid arteries reducing blood flow to the brain.","Echocardiogram — Look for a cardiac cause, such as a clot on the left side of the heart that could break off and travel to the brain.","Treatment depends on the cause; the goal is reducing the risk of a full stroke","Small doses of aspirin.","Clopidogrel (Plavix): an antiplatelet that keeps platelets from sticking together.","If the TIA is cardiac-related: heparin or warfarin to prevent coagulation.","If caused by carotid narrowing or stenosis: carotid endarterectomy (opens the artery by removing the plaque), or carotid angioplasty (a balloon catheter fed up through usually the femoral artery and inflated, with a filter to catch plaque p…","Education on lifestyle changes, especially the modifiable risk factors for stroke."]},{"s":"Stroke (CVA): Overview & Statistics▾","a":"stroke-cva-overview-statistics","k":["Stroke (CVA): Overview & Statistics▾","Also called a cerebrovascular accident (CVA), the term used in hospitals, or a \"brain attack\" (a term that didn't stick).","The result of a problem with blood supply to the brain. Two types: ischemic, about 87% of strokes, and hemorrhagic, about 13%.","About 800,000 strokes a year; for over half a million people it's their first stroke. Risk rises with age, but about a third of people with a first stroke are under 65.","A leading cause of disability. Third leading cause of death in women, fifth in men, fourth overall. Someone has a stroke about every 40 seconds and someone dies from one every 4 minutes; about $36.5 billion in healthcare costs a year."]},{"s":"Stroke Risk Factors▾","a":"stroke-risk-factors","k":["Stroke Risk Factors▾","Hypertension — Especially uncontrolled or undiagnosed. Commonly leads to hemorrhagic stroke, often by way of an aneurysm that ruptures, or by weakening the arterial wall until it ruptures. More than doubles the risk of intracranial hemorrha…","Atherosclerosis and hyperlipidemia — Vessel damage leads to plaque formation and clots, in brain vessels as in the heart.","Tobacco use — Damages the endothelial lining of blood vessels.","Diabetes — Damages blood vessel walls, which can lead to plaque formation.","Oral contraceptives — Particularly estrogen and progesterone: increase plasma fibrinogen and coagulation factor activity.","Age (starred) — Advanced age: higher risk of stroke but a better chance of recovery, because brain atrophy leaves more room in the closed skull for swelling and bleeding.","Male sex — Males are at higher risk overall.","Sickle cell anemia — In crisis, blood cells clump and can lodge in brain vessels.","Atrial fibrillation — Quivering atria let blood pool and clot; the clot can travel to the brain. Often treated with anticoagulants such as warfarin, which bring their own bleeding risk."]},{"s":"Signs of a Stroke & the First Test▾","a":"signs-of-a-stroke-the-first-test","k":["Signs of a Stroke & the First Test▾","Clinical manifestations","Numbness or weakness of the face, arm or leg, usually on one side (asymmetrical weakness).","Sudden confusion; difficulty speaking or understanding speech; slurred speech (dysarthria).","Vision trouble in one or both eyes.","Trouble walking, balance problems, dizziness, lack of coordination (ataxia).","A sudden, severe headache with no known cause, particularly important in hemorrhagic stroke.","CT without contrast, done very quickly","Part of the stroke alert protocol (with attention to airway and breathing as needed); it drives the rest of the treatment plan.","Blood shows up right away as white; an ischemic stroke shows as a darkened area.","We must know ischemic vs hemorrhagic: never anticoagulate or give a thrombolytic to a hemorrhagic stroke. It makes the bleeding worse and could kill the client."]},{"s":"Ischemic Stroke▾","a":"ischemic-stroke","k":["Ischemic Stroke▾","An occlusion of a cerebral artery by a thrombus or an embolus; everything distal to the occlusion loses its blood supply.","Both types typically present the same way, based on which part of the brain isn't perfused, but the etiology guides treatment: atherosclerosis? a hypercoagulability problem? a thrombus or embolism elsewhere?","On CT, a darkened area that isn't prominent at first but becomes more noticeable if blood flow isn't restored.","A tiny vessel occlusion may go unnoticed (evidence of an old stroke is sometimes found on an unrelated CT). A larger vessel occlusion produces symptoms based on its location.","Neuro deficits start within about one minute of oxygen deprivation; without reestablished blood flow, the damage becomes irreversible.","Hemorrhagic conversion: after an ischemic stroke, blood from the peripheral circulation extravasates across a disrupted blood-brain barrier into the brain tissue once reperfusion is established, naturally or as a result of therapy. A hemorr…","Thrombotic — Arterial obstruction from thrombus formation. Atherosclerosis: vessel-lining damage leads to plaque that narrows or blocks the vessel; if the plaque ruptures, a clot forms. Hypercoagulable states: cancer, oral contraceptives, a…","Embolic — Look at the heart first: Mural thrombus: a clot in the ventricle (the image shows the left ventricle); a piece breaks off and travels through the aorta and carotids to the brain. Atrial fibrillation: a left atrial thrombus from po…","Mural thrombus: a clot in the ventricle (the image shows the left ventricle); a piece breaks off and travels through the aorta and carotids to the brain.","Atrial fibrillation: a left atrial thrombus from pooled blood. Usually arterial clots, on the left side of the heart.","Patent foramen ovale: a birth-defect hole in the septum between the atria lets a venous clot (such as a DVT piece) pass from the right atrium to the left atrium instead of going to the lungs as a PE; the left ventricle then sends it to the …","Valve infection, particularly the mitral valve: vegetation forms, and a piece breaks off as an embolus.","Mechanical valve replacement (more than a porcine valve): clots can form around the valve.","Carotid artery plaque can rupture, and the clot travels to the brain.","The lecture describes the mitral valve as \"between the right and left ventricle.\" It is kept out of the table as a likely slip; check the slide.","Assessment clues for embolic risk","Carotid bruit: a swishing sound on auscultation from turbulent flow through a vessel narrowed by a growing plaque.","ECG: in A-fib, little quivering lines replace the P waves (the atria aren't contracting properly). These clients are at higher risk for embolic stroke, especially if newly diagnosed and not yet anticoagulated long enough."]},{"s":"Time Is Tissue: The Penumbra▾","a":"time-is-tissue-the-penumbra","k":["Time Is Tissue: The Penumbra▾","Dead tissue (core)","Injured beyond repair.","Penumbra","The extended area of injured but salvageable tissue around the core.","Goal for ischemic stroke: treatment within 3 hours of symptom onset, to reestablish blood flow and save the penumbra. Once a stroke protocol is activated, the client goes to CT first (often bypassing a room), labs are drawn quickly and the …"]},{"s":"Hemorrhagic Stroke▾","a":"hemorrhagic-stroke","k":["Hemorrhagic Stroke▾","A ruptured vessel with bleeding within the brain tissue (the parenchyma).","Leading cause: untreated hypertension; over time the pressure weakens vessels until they rupture. Clients with aneurysms are also at increased risk.","About 38% mortality, within minutes to hours; in the brainstem, death can occur within seconds. Mortality depends on the size of the area affected.","Blood is very irritating to brain tissue, so inflammation causes edema; blood plus edema in the confined skull sharply increases ICP. The brain tissue can collapse on itself (herniation), which usually results in death.","Usually ages 30 to 60, younger than ischemic stroke clients. High morbidity and mortality, with serious disabilities.","Prognosis depends on","Age: older clients have brain atrophy and more room, so a better prognosis.","Location and size: the brainstem has a very poor prognosis.","How rapid the bleeding is, and whether it causes brain distortion or shift. A midline shift (a bleed pushing one side across the center line, seen on a CT report) means a very poor prognosis.","Presentation","The worst headache of their life.","A rapid change in level of consciousness.","With subdural, epidural or subarachnoid bleeds, the client presents like meningitis from meningeal irritation: nuchal rigidity and photophobia.","Meningeal signs (nuchal rigidity, photophobia) are covered in Neuro Infectious Diseases"]},{"s":"Other Intracranial Hemorrhages▾","a":"other-intracranial-hemorrhages","k":["Other Intracranial Hemorrhages▾","Bleeding in the layers covering the brain, named by location. A bleed in the brain tissue itself is a hemorrhagic stroke.","Epidural — Between the skull and the dura mater. — Usually with skull fractures (think trauma). Tend to be arterial, so even a less severe injury can mean a lot of blood loss.","Subdural — Below the dura. — Tend to be venous, e.g., injury to bridging veins. Severe injury can cause rapid decline, but venous blood may accumulate slowly: a client hits their head, seems fine, and symptoms appear later.","Subarachnoid — Between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater, where the CSF lives. — Usually from an aneurysm or an AVM. Causes bloody CSF.","Treatment for epidural and subdural bleeds is to evacuate the collecting blood and relieve pressure on the brain. Blood bleeding into the brain tissue cannot be evacuated."]},{"s":"Subarachnoid Hemorrhage▾","a":"subarachnoid-hemorrhage","k":["Subarachnoid Hemorrhage▾","Diagnosis by lumbar puncture","Normal CSF is clear or slightly yellow. The first tube may have a little blood from needle trauma, but it should clear. With a subarachnoid bleed, the fluid stays bloody. A subarachnoid hemorrhage is identified by lumbar puncture about 12 h…","Bed rest and BP control — Avoid big BP fluctuations: keep it managed to prevent further bleeding, but high enough to perfuse the brain. The nurse is often given very specific BP parameters.","Surgery — If an aneurysm is the culprit, surgical intervention (clipping or coiling), usually done ASAP.","Anticonvulsant — Blood irritates the brain, so seizure risk rises.","Calcium channel blocker — After clipping or coiling, to help treat the symptoms of the subarachnoid hemorrhage.","Triple H — Keeps the brain perfused: pressors to keep BP high enough, colloids and a hypervolemic state, and some expected hemodilution.","The lecture describes the calcium channel blocker as working \"by narrowing and slowing that blood flow.\" It is kept out of the table as stated; check the slide for the intended mechanism."]},{"s":"Berry Aneurysms, AVMs, Clipping & Coiling▾","a":"berry-aneurysms-avms-clipping-coiling","k":["Berry Aneurysms, AVMs, Clipping & Coiling▾","Berry aneurysm — The most common aneurysm that ruptures in the brain: little berries hanging off the artery that rupture when big enough.","AVM (arteriovenous malformation) — Normally arteries divide into arterioles, capillaries, venules, then veins. In an AVM the junction is a jumbled clump, so high-pressure arterial blood goes directly into the vein without slowing through ca…","Clipping — A clip is placed around the aneurysm to block blood flow into it.","Coiling (endovascular embolization) — A catheter inserted in the groin is threaded into the aneurysm, and detachable platinum coils reduce blood flow into the sac, lowering the chance of rupture.","The lecture also says \"berry aneurysms are often located in the veins,\" which conflicts with the same slide (\"hanging off the artery\") and with Week 1. It is kept out of the table; check the slide.","Berry aneurysms were first covered in Aneurysms (Week 1)"]},{"s":"BE FAST & Last Known Normal▾","a":"be-fast-last-known-normal","k":["BE FAST & Last Known Normal▾","B: Balance — Headache, dizziness, loss of balance, trouble walking; falls, especially in the elderly.","E: Eyes — Sudden vision loss in one or both eyes; unequal pupils.","F: Face — Facial asymmetry: have them smile. Differentiate from Bell's palsy.","A: Arm — Weakness: both arms held up perpendicular to the body; does one drift?","S: Speech — Difficulty speaking or slurred words.","T: Time — Call 911, get to the hospital, activate a stroke alert.","Find out exactly when symptoms started: the \"last known normal.\" Treatment must happen within a certain time frame."]},{"s":"Stroke Treatment: First Steps▾","a":"stroke-treatment-first-steps","k":["Stroke Treatment: First Steps▾","ABCs: can they maintain their airway, are they breathing, is there a pulse? A GCS below 8 raises concern about airway protection, so the client is intubated. Stroke clients may also have decreased respiratory drive.","Stroke alert / protocol: labs, CT, the neuro team, and a blood sugar check, because hypoglycemia can mimic a stroke.","CT scan without contrast, done first to exclude hemorrhagic stroke: fast, and best in the acute stage. An MRI gives better images later, but it is slower and less available, so it isn't the first choice in an emergency.","Hemorrhagic stroke","Reverse anticoagulation: is there a reversal agent or antidote for the client's medications?","Manage hypertension, the likely cause.","Manage ICP: elevate the head of the bed, give antipyretics for fever, keep the head in a neutral position.","Ischemic stroke","Thrombolytics (clot busters) to open the occlusion and protect and reperfuse the penumbra.","Mechanical thrombectomy: a surgeon removes the clot manually (also called a penumbra procedure)."]},{"s":"Alteplase (tPA) for Ischemic Stroke▾","a":"alteplase-tpa-for-ischemic-stroke","k":["Alteplase (tPA) for Ischemic Stroke▾","Heparin and antiplatelets don't dissolve a clot; they only prevent one from forming. Fibrinolytics actually break up clots.","Alteplase (tPA, tissue plasminogen activator), the most commonly used fibrinolytic, converts plasminogen to plasmin; plasmin digests the fibrin.","Used in severe thrombotic events: acute MI, PE, ischemic stroke, occasionally femoral artery clots or a clotted central line. Given IV.","Monitor BP — It can cause hypotension.","Monitor HR and rhythm — Arrhythmias can develop with reperfusion.","Bleeding risk — Increased risk of intracranial bleeding. Don't give it to clients at high risk for bleeding (such as a previous hemorrhagic stroke or a GI bleed).","CT first — Always rule out hemorrhagic stroke before giving it.","With heparin — Often given with heparin to prevent new clots from forming.","Antidote — Aminocaproic acid, an antithrombolytic: blocks plasminogen binding and activation to plasmin, reducing fibrinolysis to stop bleeding. Give it only if the hemorrhage is life-threatening.","Alteplase was first covered for acute MI in Acute MI Pharmacology (Week 1)"]},{"s":"Consequences of Stroke▾","a":"consequences-of-stroke","k":["Consequences of Stroke▾","Left side of the brain affected","Difficulty with communication (verbal and written) and with more analytical activities.","Right side of the brain affected","Difficulty with things like imagination, music and art.","Dysphagia — A swallow evaluation comes before giving food or advancing a diet. Speech pathology does a barium swallow study under real-time x-ray with different thicknesses of food and liquid, then recommends a diet: regular, thickened liqu…","Motor and sensory deficits — Neglect of body parts no longer getting sensory input; poor coordination (fall risk); loss of muscle groups, which atrophy over time.","Flaccidity → spasticity → contractures — Flaccid (limp, weak) muscles on the contralateral side become spastic (painful), then contractures (permanently shortened muscles, joints stuck in abnormal positions), typically within the first 6 we…","Homonymous hemianopia — Contralateral field blindness: loss (or blurring) of up to half the vision, on the same side of both visual fields.","Aphasia — The global term for affected language: dysphasia (speech), receptive aphasia (understanding), reading and writing problems; one or all.","Depression — Very common, from significant disabilities and loss of independence.","Memory problems — Even well-known things like family members' names.","Behavior changes — Increased emotional response (upset, angry or crying more easily); slower reaction times; hesitance or caution; confusion or disorientation; apathy.","The lecture also says clients \"can underestimate their own abilities\" and calls it a big safety concern, alongside slower reaction times (fall risk). That is kept as stated; check the slide wording."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week4-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 4","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Three Topics","The Three Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It's a map of Week 4's three topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate; it pulls the whole week…"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Three Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-the-three-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Three Topics▾","The stroke topic's four voiceovers (TIA, stroke and hemorrhage parts 1 and 2, and stroke treatment) share one page.","The Nerves Break Down — Topic 1, ALS & Guillain-Barré Syndrome (the ALS sections first, then the GBS sections) — Both take away muscle strength until the breathing muscles are in danger. ALS kills the upper and lower motor neurons and doesn…","Infection Around and Inside the Brain — Topic 2, Neuro Infectious Diseases (meningitis, then encephalitis, then brain abscess) — Bacteria or viruses reach the meninges, the brain tissue, or a pocket of pus. Inflammation and swelling in a cl…","The Blood Supply Fails — Topic 3, TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage (TIA, then stroke and ischemic stroke, then hemorrhagic stroke and bleeds, then treatment and consequences) — A blocked vessel (TIA, ischemic stroke) or a ruptured one (hemorrhagic …","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order. Read neuro infections before the hemorrhagic stroke sections (the meningeal signs and meningeal layers are taught there first), and the TIA sections before stroke."]},{"s":"The Three Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-three-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Three Topics at a Glance▾","1 — ALS & Guillain-Barré Syndrome — ALS: progressive, fatal upper and lower motor neuron degeneration, linked to excess glutamate; death from respiratory failure about 3 years after diagnosis; riluzole (hepatotoxic) buys about 6 months to a…","2 — Neuro Infectious Diseases — Meningitis: S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis by droplets; fever, headache, nuchal rigidity, photophobia, Kernig and Brudzinski, a non-blanching rash with meningococcus; bacterial kills in hours, so IV antibi…","3 — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage — TIA: under 24 hours, no infarction, but a warning (highest stroke risk in the first month). Stroke: 87% ischemic (thrombotic or embolic; save the penumbra within 3 hours with alteplase or thrombectomy) and 13%…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content. Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why. Section names are in quotation marks.","The meninges — Neuro Infectious Diseases: \"Meningitis: What It Is & What Causes It\" (pia, arachnoid and dura mater; CSF in the subarachnoid space) — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: \"Other Intracranial Hemorrhages\" names each bleed by which layer …","Meningeal signs — Neuro Infectious Diseases: \"Meningitis: Clinical Manifestations\" (nuchal rigidity, photophobia, Kernig and Brudzinski) — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: subdural, epidural and subarachnoid bleeds present like meningitis.","The closed skull and ICP — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: \"Hemorrhagic Stroke\" (blood plus edema in a confined space → herniation) — Neuro Infectious Diseases: meningitis swelling raises ICP; a brain abscess is drained to lower the pressure. Str…","Headache — Neuro Infectious Diseases: the meningitis triad — Encephalitis and brain abscess; stroke's sudden, severe headache; hemorrhagic stroke's \"worst headache of their life.\"","Seizures and anticonvulsants — Neuro Infectious Diseases: seizures in about 30% of meningitis; anti-seizure drugs in the acute phase of meningitis and encephalitis — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: an anticonvulsant after a subarachnoid hemorrhag…","Breathing failure — ALS & GBS: ALS death from respiratory failure; GBS ascending to the breathing muscles (ventilator, sometimes a tracheostomy) — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: ABCs first, intubate at a GCS below 8, decreased respiratory drive.","Swallowing and aspiration — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: \"Key Terms\" (dysphagia with a G, NPO) and \"Consequences of Stroke\" (the barium swallow study) — ALS & GBS: difficulty chewing and swallowing in ALS; aspiration risk in GBS.","Falls — ALS & GBS: GBS uncoordinated movements and decreased sensation (high fall risk) — ALS: tripping and falling. Stroke: BE FAST balance (falls in the elderly), poor coordination, slower reaction times.","Vision changes — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: carotid and vertebrobasilar TIA signs, BE FAST eyes, homonymous hemianopia — ALS & GBS: blurred vision as an early GBS sign. Neuro Infectious Diseases: photophobia; a temporal lobe abscess causes v…","Steroids — ALS & GBS: \"Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Treatment & Prognosis\" (high dose, titrated down) — Neuro Infectious Diseases: steroids reduce meningitis inflammation.","Demyelination — ALS & GBS: \"Guillain-Barré Syndrome: What It Is\" (peripheral myelin) — ALS: motor neuron death leads to demyelination and sclerosis. Week 3's Multiple Sclerosis is the CNS version.","Aneurysms and alteplase — Week 1: Aneurysms (berry aneurysms) and Acute MI Pharmacology (alteplase) — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: berry aneurysms rupture into subarachnoid bleeds; alteplase busts the clot in ischemic stroke, with the same ble…"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and safety statements from across the three pages, condensed to a phrase, one row per topic. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation. Click through to a topic for the details behind any…","ALS & Guillain-Barré Syndrome — Both: watch the breathing muscles. Riluzole is hepatotoxic: watch liver function. GBS: rapid decline over 24 hours can mean a ventilator; high fall risk; decreased sensation hides injuries; aspiration risk.","Neuro Infectious Diseases — Bacterial meningitis: antibiotics as soon as possible, never held (it kills within hours). A non-blanching petechial rash means meningococcus. Septic emboli can cost fingers or limbs. Very communicable by droplet…","TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage — After a TIA, go to the ED for any new episode. Non-contrast CT before any anticoagulant or thrombolytic: never give them to a hemorrhagic stroke. Treat ischemic stroke within 3 hours; get the last known normal. GC…","Patterns You'll Notice Across These Rules","\"The skull is a closed box.\" Infection swelling, an abscess and a bleed all fight for the same fixed space, so ICP and herniation are the shared danger, and the older, atrophied brain tolerates it best.","\"Time decides the outcome.\" Bacterial meningitis kills in hours, the penumbra dies without reperfusion within 3 hours, and rapid GBS can need a ventilator within a day.","\"Rule out the bleed first.\" The non-contrast CT comes before alteplase, anticoagulants and the rest of the stroke plan, because the right treatment for a clot can kill a client who is bleeding.","\"Watch the airway, the breathing and the swallowing.\" ALS and GBS weaken the breathing and swallowing muscles; stroke clients may lose their airway or their swallow, so they're intubated below GCS 8 and kept NPO until the swallow study.","\"Cover the worst cause until it's ruled out.\" Acyclovir is given with antibiotics until the CSF rules out a viral cause, and every suspected stroke is treated as an emergency even though some turn out to be TIAs or Bell's palsy."]}]},{"f":"week5-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 5","Part 1A — DKA & HHS: Physiology & Labs","Part 1B — DKA & HHS: Presentation, Treatment & Resolution","Part 2A — Thyroid Storm","Part 2B — Myxedema Coma","Part 3A — Addison's Disease & Replacement Therapy","Part 3B — Addisonian (Adrenal) Crisis","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 5: the three topic pages that follow, in the order they were taught. Topic 1: DKA and HHS (Parts 1A–1B). Topic 2: thyroid crisis, thyroid storm and myxedema coma (Parts…"]},{"s":"Part 1A — DKA & HHS: Physiology & Labs▾","a":"part-1a-dka-hhs-physiology-labs","k":["Part 1A — DKA & HHS: Physiology & Labs▾","Topic 1, first half: what DKA and HHS are and who gets them, the insulin and counter-regulatory hormone background, why DKA makes ketones and HHS doesn't, why HHS glucose runs higher, the anion gap, plasma osmolality and sodium, and potassi…","DKA vs HHS: What They Are & Who Gets Them","Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)","A crisis that occurs as an acute metabolic complication of diabetes, with ketosis.","More common in people under 65, and characteristically associated with type 1 diabetes.","Can occur in type 2 under extreme stress: really serious infections, sepsis, trauma, cardiovascular or other emergencies. Type 2 diabetics still make insulin, which is typically enough to prevent fat breakdown into ketones unless the situat…","About 30% of all DKA cases occur in people with type 2 diabetes, particularly those taking SGLT2 inhibitors (the \"-flozins,\" like dapagliflozin).","Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS)","Also called hyperosmolar hyperglycemic syndrome; in the past, hyperglycemic hyperosmolar non-ketotic (HHNK). HHS is the name you'll see most.","Another life-threatening complication of diabetes, but with no ketosis.","Mostly type 2 diabetes, and more common over 65.","Pretty rare.","Background: Insulin, Glucagon & Counter-Regulatory Hormones","Extracellular glucose is primarily regulated by two hormones: insulin and glucagon.","After a meal, glucose enters the pancreatic beta cells, which leads to insulin release. Insulin restores normal blood glucose by reducing hepatic glucose production (less glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis) and by increasing glucose uptake …","Gluconeogenesis turns non-carbohydrate substrates (amino acids, lactate, glycerol) into glucose. Glycogenolysis breaks glycogen down into glucose, which the body can use for energy.","In times of stress, more insulin is needed than usual; in diabetics that insulin isn't available, and DKA or HHS can develop.","In both, the main problem is that insulin isn't working properly: in DKA the body isn't making enough; in HHS the insulin that is available isn't working effectively.","On top of that, the counter-regulatory hormones (glucagon, adrenaline, cortisol, growth hormone) go up. They increase hepatic glucose production and impair the peripheral tissues' ability to use glucose.","Dehydration and electrolyte imbalances occur primarily from the osmotic diuresis caused by glycosuria (glucose in the urine).","The lecture uses the word \"gluconeogenesis\" twice in a row; the second definition (glycogen broken down into glucose) is glycogenolysis, which is how it is listed here.","Why DKA Makes Ketones & HHS Doesn't","DKA is marked by increased gluconeogenesis, lipolysis and ketogenesis, with reduced glycolysis: that is the \"keto\" in ketoacidosis.","The insulin deficiency is absolute in type 1 and relative in type 2, and the excess counter-regulatory hormones add to it.","Suppressing lipolysis and ketogenesis is more sensitive to insulin than suppressing gluconeogenesis. In HHS, the residual insulin is enough to minimize ketoacidosis but not enough to control the hyperglycemia.","1 — Insulin deficiency and resistance mean glucose can't get into the cells, which need it for energy.","2 — Without glucose, the body breaks down fat for energy (lipolysis), releasing free fatty acids and glycerol from peripheral fat stores.","3 — The free fatty acids travel to the liver, are taken up by hepatocytes and activated.","4 — They are converted into acetyl-CoA, enter the ketogenic pathway and form ketone bodies.","Why not in HHS? One theory: there may be enough insulin activity to minimize lipolysis (and so ketone formation), but not enough to block gluconeogenesis, promote glucose use and prevent the hyperglycemia.","Why HHS Glucose Runs Higher","DKA","Clients present earlier, with symptoms of ketoacidosis: shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.","Because they seek treatment earlier, glucose is usually 350 to 450 (can be as high as 800).","Tend to be younger with a higher GFR, so a greater capacity to excrete glucose.","HHS","Clients present later, without those early ketoacidosis symptoms.","Glucose is typically higher: it can exceed 1,000.","Glycosuria first limits the rise in glucose, but the osmotic diuresis causes volume depletion and a lower GFR, which limits glucose excretion. The effect is more pronounced in HHS.","Anion Gap, Plasma Osmolality & Sodium","Anion gap — DKA typically presents as an elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis. The anion gap is the difference between negatively and positively charged electrolytes, calculated from sodium, chloride and bicarb (sodium minus chloride plus …","Beta-hydroxybutyrate — Ketones are no longer really measured directly; in DKA, beta-hydroxybutyrate (one of the main metabolic products in ketoacidosis) is measured instead.","Closing the gap — Ketoacid anion excretion depends on volume status and GFR. Isotonic IV fluids correct the hypovolemia, increase renal excretion of ketoacid anions and reduce the anion gap.","Plasma osmolality — Increased in both, in proportion to the glucose; usually not as high in DKA because the glucose isn't as high.","Sodium — Hyperglycemia pulls water out of the cells and expands the extracellular fluid, so plasma sodium falls: a dilutional hyponatremia. It isn't treated directly, because it corrects as the hyperglycemia is corrected.","Potassium: High on Arrival, Depleted Underneath","Most body potassium is in the cells. Clients with DKA and HHS present with a normal to elevated serum potassium but a significant total body potassium depletion, for three reasons:","Acidosis — In metabolic acidosis, hydrogen ions move into the cells and potassium moves out to balance the charge, raising serum potassium. Working kidneys can offset this by excreting the excess.","Insulin deficiency — Insulin normally moves potassium into the cells; without it, potassium stays in the extracellular fluid, raising serum levels further.","Hyperosmolarity — The osmotic diuresis from high glucose causes massive urinary losses, and potassium is lost continuously in the urine, depleting the total body supply.","Serum potassium can be 5, 6 or beyond on arrival. Don't bring it down unless it's causing problems and the client is symptomatic: it returns toward normal as the pH is corrected.","Once treatment starts, insulin drives potassium back into the cells and the serum level falls quickly, which can cause severe hypokalemia. If the kidneys have already excreted potassium, there is even less left to move back in.","As soon as the potassium reaches a normal level, replacement typically starts, because it will keep moving into the cells and the serum still needs a normal level to prevent cardiac problems.","Watch the client: are they symptomatic, and are there EKG changes of hyper- or hypokalemia (including dysrhythmias)?"]},{"s":"Part 1B — DKA & HHS: Presentation, Treatment & Resolution▾","a":"part-1b-dka-hhs-presentation-treatment-resolution","k":["Part 1B — DKA & HHS: Presentation, Treatment & Resolution▾","Topic 1, second half: precipitating factors, how each presents, the treatment steps, how you know each has resolved, and why hydration matters so much in HHS.","Precipitating Factors","A precipitating factor can often be identified.","Infection is the most common: severe pneumonia or a UTI without an insulin adjustment.","Inflammatory events: MI, CVA, sepsis, pancreatitis, without an insulin adjustment.","Poor compliance with insulin, or increased insulin needs that aren't met. Compliance can be checked with a hemoglobin A1c.","Clinical Presentation","DKA: evolves rapidly, over about 24 hours","GI: nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, from delayed gastric emptying or an ileus induced by the metabolic acidosis.","Neurologic: slight confusion up to obtunded, with severe acidosis.","Volume depletion: decreased skin turgor, decreased urine output, dry oral mucosa, tachycardia, hypotension.","Fruity breath (some say it smells like nail polish remover) from exhaled acetone.","Kussmaul respirations: really deep respirations and hyperventilation, compensating for the metabolic acidosis.","HHS: begins several days before admission","Marked hyperglycemia with the typical signs: polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, plus weakness.","As it progresses: lethargy, obtunded, up to coma. Neurologic symptoms are most common in HHS because of the hyperosmolarity.","Extracellular volume depletion: dry mucous membranes, poor skin turgor, sunken eyes, hypotension, tachycardia.","Symptoms of a coexisting illness that may have precipitated it (pneumonia, UTI) are usually present.","Treatment","Hospitals usually have a protocol and algorithm; follow your hospital's protocol. The lecturer cites the University of Kentucky (UK) protocol for the potassium numbers.","1. Fluid replacement — Usually first: expands extracellular volume and stabilizes the client hemodynamically. Treat volume depletion rapidly (normal saline); fluid resuscitation in HHS should be pretty aggressive.","2. Correct the potassium deficit — Per the UK protocol, a DKA client receives potassium replacement if potassium is less than 5 before insulin is started, to prevent the hypokalemia that follows. If potassium is less than 3.3, replace it fi…","Sodium bicarb — May be given if the pH is low (around 7.1 to 7.2), depending on how the client presents.","3. IV insulin — An IV insulin infusion, with blood sugar checked regularly (typically once an hour at first) and the rate adjusted to the glucose. Continue until the anion gap is closed and the condition is resolving.","Add dextrose — When glucose reaches about 200 to 250, fluids change from no glucose to D5W/normal saline (dextrose added to the saline) while the insulin infusion continues, to prevent hypoglycemia if the gap is still open.","Monitor — Glucose very closely and frequently (it shouldn't fall too rapidly), electrolytes and the anion gap.","Insulin drives potassium into the cells. Check potassium before insulin: below 3.3, replace potassium first. Watch for EKG changes of hyper- and hypokalemia throughout treatment.","How You Know It's Resolved","DKA: look at the labs","Make sure the ketoacidosis has resolved: venous pH or the anion gap, and the level of ketoacidosis by beta-hydroxybutyrate, which falls as the acidosis is corrected.","HHS: look at the patient","Are they mentally alert and back to baseline? Also check the plasma osmolality: the goal is about 315, which the lecture calls a normal level.","Once the client can eat regularly, IV insulin is changed to subQ insulin.","HHS: Why Hydration Matters So Much","If adequate hydration isn't maintained, dehydration and worsening renal function follow.","Clients with poor fluid intake from altered thirst mechanisms or inability to access fluids (as may be seen in the elderly) are at increased risk of severe dehydration.","Diminished renal filtration further impairs glucose excretion, which exacerbates the hyperglycemia."]},{"s":"Part 2A — Thyroid Storm▾","a":"part-2a-thyroid-storm","k":["Part 2A — Thyroid Storm▾","Topic 2, first half: the two extremes of the thyroid spectrum, then thyroid storm: what it is, risk factors, clinical manifestations and treatment.","The Two Extremes of the Thyroid Spectrum","Thyroid storm (thyrotoxic crisis)","The hyperthyroid extreme: an overwhelming release of thyroid hormones.","Myxedema coma","The hypothyroid extreme: severe hypothyroidism.","Many manifestations are the same as ordinary hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism, just more dramatic or severe.","Thyroid Storm: What It Is","An overwhelming release of thyroid hormones that puts an intense stimulus on the body's metabolism.","Typically a reaction to a major stressor (a big infection, major surgery, a car wreck). The stressor releases stress hormones such as adrenaline, everything in the body is \"revved up,\" and the body can't tolerate this for long.","Life-threatening: untreated, it can cause death within about 48 hours.","Long-standing untreated hyperthyroidism — The lecture calls this Graves' disease.","A precipitating acute event — Surgery, trauma or infection, which may or may not involve the thyroid.","Irregular use or stopping antithyroid drugs — Commonly reported before a thyroid storm.","Thyroid Storm: Clinical Manifestations","Fever (the most notable) — Body temperature rises greatly, to 104 or 105; excessive sweating; the client is hot and can't tolerate heat.","Tachycardia — Often above 140.","Blood pressure — Hypertension at first. With sustained tachycardia the heart can't fill properly, so BP may start to drop. Untreated: hypotension, heart failure and pulmonary edema that can lead to death.","Dysrhythmias — A-fib and atrial flutter are the most common.","Other — Nausea, vomiting, agitation, tremors, psychosis, up to stupor or coma.","The transcript says \"heat tolerance\"; with fever and excessive sweating, the page reads it as not tolerating heat.","Thyroid Storm: Treatment","Treatment hits several points where thyroid hormone acts. These clients are in the ICU and very closely monitored.","Beta blocker — Controls the symptoms and signs of the increased adrenergic tone; adjusted as needed to keep a proper heart rate and blood pressure.","Thionamide (PTU) — Blocks new hormone synthesis and suppresses conversion of T4 to T3. Synthesis is blocked within about 1 to 2 hours; within 24 hours, T3 levels can drop up to about 45%.","Iodine solution — Blocks the release of thyroid hormone. Given about 1 hour after the first dose of PTU.","Glucocorticoids — Reduce T4 to T3 conversion. Evidence is conflicting, so they're given when there is clear evidence of thyroid storm, because they rapidly reduce active thyroid hormone and its effects.","Bile acid sequestrants — Thyroid hormones are metabolized in the liver and excreted in bile; free hormone released into the intestines is reabsorbed. Sequestrants decrease enterohepatic circulation and recycling of thyroid hormone.","Enterohepatic circulation is the circulation of biliary acids, bilirubin and other substances from the liver to the bile, stored in the gallbladder before reaching the small intestine.","The lecture says \"thionamide, which is also called PTU.\" PTU is the thionamide named here."]},{"s":"Part 2B — Myxedema Coma▾","a":"part-2b-myxedema-coma","k":["Part 2B — Myxedema Coma▾","Topic 2, second half: myxedema coma's risk factors, clinical manifestations and treatment, then thyroid storm vs myxedema coma side by side.","Myxedema Coma: What It Is","Severe hypothyroidism leading to decreased mental state, hypothermia and slowed function of other organs.","A medical emergency with a high mortality rate, but uncommon, because hypothyroidism can be diagnosed earlier and kept from getting worse.","Severe, long-standing or poorly controlled hypothyroidism — The baseline risk, as with thyroid storm on the other end.","A precipitating acute event — Infection, MI or major surgery on top of poorly controlled hypothyroidism.","Sedative drugs — Especially opioids, which can trigger myxedema coma in someone who already has hypothyroidism.","Myxedema Coma: Clinical Manifestations","Altered consciousness — Despite the name, clients don't often present in coma: confusion, lethargy, obtunded, some with psychosis. Untreated, it can progress to coma.","Hypothermia — Decreased thermogenesis from decreased metabolism. The lower the temperature, the higher the mortality.","Hypotension and bradycardia — Thyroid hormone helps maintain BP homeostasis; severe hypothyroidism causes bradycardia, decreased contractility, low cardiac output and hypotension.","Hyponatremia — About 50% of the time. When present it is severe and adds to the decreased mental status. Impaired free water excretion causes a dilutional hyponatremia.","Hypoglycemia — Can be caused by hypothyroidism alone and may be worse in myxedema coma.","Hypoventilation — Overall depression of the ventilatory drive and decreased responsiveness, leading to hypoxia and hypercapnia (respiratory acidosis). Some clients need a mechanical ventilator.","The lecture words it as hypoventilation \"results from a respiratory acidosis\"; the page follows the rest of that sentence (depressed ventilatory drive leading to hypoxia and hypercapnia).","Myxedema Coma: Treatment","If myxedema coma is suspected, treat before lab confirmation.","Thyroid hormones (combination) — Levothyroxine (T4) plus liothyronine (T3): the combination works better than T4 alone. Given IV until the client can take T4 orally. Thyroid levels are monitored daily to confirm the therapy is working.","Glucocorticoids — Clients often have a coexisting adrenal insufficiency (decreased pituitary function, secondary adrenal insufficiency, or ACTH secretion blunted by severe hypothyroidism). IV hydrocortisone is given until adrenal insufficie…","Supportive measures — IV fluids, electrolyte replacement as needed, mechanical ventilation for hypoventilation, glucose monitoring, and treating any underlying infection.","Thyroid Storm vs Myxedema Coma","End of the spectrum — Hyperthyroid — Hypothyroid","Underlying risk — Long-standing untreated hyperthyroidism; irregular use or stopping antithyroid drugs — Severe, long-standing or poorly controlled hypothyroidism; sedatives, especially opioids","Trigger — Infection, surgery, trauma — Infection, MI, surgery","Temperature — Fever, 104 to 105 — Hypothermia","Heart rate and BP — Tachycardia above 140; hypertension, then hypotension — Bradycardia and hypotension","Mental status — Agitation, tremors, psychosis, up to stupor or coma — Confusion, lethargy, obtunded, psychosis, up to coma","Key drugs — Beta blocker, PTU, iodine, glucocorticoids, bile acid sequestrants — IV levothyroxine plus liothyronine, IV hydrocortisone"]},{"s":"Part 3A — Addison's Disease & Replacement Therapy▾","a":"part-3a-addison-s-disease-replacement-therapy","k":["Part 3A — Addison's Disease & Replacement Therapy▾","Topic 3, first half: the three S's, causes and the ACTH feedback loop, aldosterone vs cortisol deficiency, lifelong replacement therapy, and stress dosing and teaching.","Addison's Disease: The Three S's","Addison's disease is a disease of the adrenal cortex that causes hyposecretion of all three adrenocortical hormones.","Sugar — Cortisol — Stimulates gluconeogenesis and raises blood sugar; mobilizes fat stores; helps break down proteins.","Salt — Aldosterone — Assists in fluid and electrolyte balance.","Sex — Androgens — Male sex hormones.","The most severe effects come from the lack of cortisol.","Long-term effects: immune suppression and bone breakdown (bone density problems).","Causes & the Feedback Loop","It can be idiopathic, but the most common cause is autoimmune. The adrenal gland can also be damaged or destroyed by disease or trauma.","In primary adrenocortical insufficiency (primary Addison's), the adrenal gland stops secreting cortisol, so there is no negative feedback. The anterior pituitary increases MSH and ACTH to try to stimulate cortisol, but despite very high lev…","Manifestations don't become evident until about 90% of the adrenal cortex is destroyed, so Addison's is usually advanced at diagnosis, or diagnosed during an Addisonian crisis.","Aldosterone Deficiency vs Cortisol Deficiency","Low aldosterone (hypoaldosteronism)","Aldosterone regulates sodium, and so water retention. Low aldosterone means loss of water and sodium, with potassium retention.","Hyponatremia, hyperkalemia and dehydration, with the signs of all three.","Hypotension from decreased vascular tone, cardiac output and circulating blood volume.","Salt craving.","The lecture adds that hypoaldosteronism signals more ADH, which stimulates water reabsorption and could lead to fluid volume excess in some cases.","Low cortisol (hypocortisolism)","Low energy: a lack of stress hormones.","Hypoglycemia, since cortisol normally raises blood glucose.","Hyperpigmentation: the pituitary increases ACTH to raise cortisol, and ACTH also stimulates melanin secretion by melanocytes, so unsuppressed ACTH darkens the skin.","Lifelong Replacement Therapy","Adrenal insufficiency requires lifelong replacement with the appropriate corticosteroids.","Hydrocortisone (Cortef) — The drug of choice: has both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid activity. IM, IV and PO.","Prednisone — Glucocorticoid, PO.","Dexamethasone — Glucocorticoid, IV or PO.","Fludrocortisone — Added when extra mineralocorticoid activity is needed; the only mineralocorticoid available. Works like aldosterone, so expect an impact on sodium and water balance. PO.","Dosing should mimic the body's normal pattern of corticosteroid secretion. Mineralocorticoids can be given once a day; glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid doses should approximate what the adrenal glands normally secrete.","Never stop therapy abruptly.","Stress Dosing & Teaching","Healthy adrenal glands increase glucocorticoid output in response to stress. These clients don't get that boost, so it has to be given exogenously: increase the dose in times of stress.","Supplemental dosing follows guidelines for specific medical conditions and surgical procedures, so it varies. Example: for a mild or febrile illness, the 3 x 3 rule (loosely, three times the usual dose for three days).","Keep an emergency supply: an injectable preparation plus an oral preparation.","Wear a medical alert bracelet so emergency personnel know about the glucocorticoid needs.","Not following the regimen (stopping abruptly, or not increasing the dose under stress) can cause an Addisonian crisis, which can be fatal."]},{"s":"Part 3B — Addisonian (Adrenal) Crisis▾","a":"part-3b-addisonian-adrenal-crisis","k":["Part 3B — Addisonian (Adrenal) Crisis▾","Topic 3, second half: what an Addisonian crisis is and what causes it, its symptoms, and its treatment.","Addisonian (Adrenal) Crisis: What It Is & What Causes It","A complication of Addison's disease: acute adrenal insufficiency, a sudden insufficiency of serum corticosteroids. A severe, life-threatening endocrine emergency.","More common with primary adrenal insufficiency, but can occur with secondary and tertiary.","Primary — The adrenal gland itself stops secreting cortisol (Addison's disease).","Secondary — ACTH deficiency, usually from conditions affecting the hypothalamus or pituitary.","Tertiary — Typically excessive glucocorticoid exposure.","Triggers","A sudden loss of the adrenal glands.","Increased stress in someone with underlying adrenal problems: surgery, infection, trauma, severe illness.","Sudden cessation of long-term steroid therapy, which is why clients stopping steroids are tapered.","Addisonian Crisis: Symptoms","Symptoms stem mainly from the lack of cortisol and aldosterone.","Sudden pain — Lower back, legs and abdomen; the lecture attributes it to hyponatremia from the lack of mineralocorticoids.","Severe vomiting and diarrhea — Lead to dehydration and hypotension; also tied to the mineralocorticoid deficiency.","CNS changes — Confusion, slurred speech, up to loss of consciousness.","Electrolyte imbalances — Hyperkalemia and hyponatremia, with their signs and symptoms. Put the client on an EKG and watch for dysrhythmias.","The lecture's video example shows shaking and decreased level of consciousness in a girl with no prior diagnosis: Addison's is often first diagnosed when the person has an Addisonian crisis, and family may not recognize it as an emergency.","Addisonian Crisis: Treatment","Untreated, it can be fatal. Rapidly replace fluid, salt and glucocorticoids, and give glucose.","Hydrocortisone IV — About a 100 mg initial bolus, then about 50 mg every 6 hours IV, until vital signs stabilize and the client can take food and medications orally. Hydrocortisone has both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid actions.","Normal saline — Replaces lost sodium: about 1 to 3 liters IV, or 5% dextrose in normal saline, based on volume status and urine output.","Once stable — Switch to PO medications, continue fluids, and taper to maintenance doses as the condition resolves."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One row per part: the points to have cold before an exam. Every detail is taught in full in the parts above.","1A. DKA & HHS: Physiology & Labs — DKA: type 1, under 65, ketosis (30% of cases in type 2, especially SGLT2 inhibitors). HHS: type 2, over 65, no ketosis, rare. Insulin isn't working; counter-regulatory hormones rise; osmotic diuresis from …","1B. Presentation, Treatment & Resolution — Triggers: infection (most common), MI, CVA, sepsis, pancreatitis, poor insulin compliance (check A1c). DKA evolves over about 24 hours: N/V and abdominal pain, confusion, volume depletion, fruity b…","2A. Thyroid Storm — Overwhelming thyroid hormone release after a major stressor; death within about 48 hours untreated. Risks: long-standing untreated hyperthyroidism, acute event, irregular use or stopping antithyroid drugs. Fever 104–105,…","2B. Myxedema Coma — Severe hypothyroidism; high mortality, uncommon. Risks: poorly controlled hypothyroidism plus infection, MI, surgery; sedatives, especially opioids. Usually confusion or lethargy, not coma; hypothermia (lower temp, highe…","3A. Addison's Disease & Replacement — Three S's: sugar (cortisol), salt (aldosterone), sex (androgens); cortisol loss is the most severe. Mostly autoimmune; ACTH and MSH rise without feedback; symptoms at 90% destruction. Low aldosterone: h…","3B. Addisonian Crisis — Sudden corticosteroid insufficiency; life-threatening. Triggers: surgery, infection, trauma, severe illness, sudden stop of long-term steroids (taper). Sudden back, leg and abdominal pain; severe vomiting and diarrhe…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the six parts above: a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week5-dka-hhs.html","t":"DKA & HHS","secs":[{"s":"DKA & HHS","a":"","k":["Week 5 · Topic 1","DKA vs HHS: What They Are & Who Gets Them","Background: Insulin, Glucagon & Counter-Regulatory Hormones","Why DKA Makes Ketones & HHS Doesn't","Why HHS Glucose Runs Higher","Anion Gap, Plasma Osmolality & Sodium","Potassium: High on Arrival, Depleted Underneath","Precipitating Factors","Clinical Presentation","Treatment","How You Know It's Resolved","HHS: Why Hydration Matters So Much","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the DKA and HHS voiceover, which compares and contrasts the two throughout: what each is and who gets it, the insulin and counter-regulatory hormone background, why DKA makes ketones and HHS doesn't, the anion gap, plasma …"]},{"s":"DKA vs HHS: What They Are & Who Gets Them▾","a":"dka-vs-hhs-what-they-are-who-gets-them","k":["DKA vs HHS: What They Are & Who Gets Them▾","Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)","A crisis that occurs as an acute metabolic complication of diabetes, with ketosis.","More common in people under 65, and characteristically associated with type 1 diabetes.","Can occur in type 2 under extreme stress: really serious infections, sepsis, trauma, cardiovascular or other emergencies. Type 2 diabetics still make insulin, which is typically enough to prevent fat breakdown into ketones unless the situat…","About 30% of all DKA cases occur in people with type 2 diabetes, particularly those taking SGLT2 inhibitors (the \"-flozins,\" like dapagliflozin).","Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS)","Also called hyperosmolar hyperglycemic syndrome; in the past, hyperglycemic hyperosmolar non-ketotic (HHNK). HHS is the name you'll see most.","Another life-threatening complication of diabetes, but with no ketosis.","Mostly type 2 diabetes, and more common over 65.","Pretty rare."]},{"s":"Background: Insulin, Glucagon & Counter-Regulatory Hormones▾","a":"background-insulin-glucagon-counter-regulatory-hormones","k":["Background: Insulin, Glucagon & Counter-Regulatory Hormones▾","Extracellular glucose is primarily regulated by two hormones: insulin and glucagon.","After a meal, glucose enters the pancreatic beta cells, which leads to insulin release. Insulin restores normal blood glucose by reducing hepatic glucose production (less glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis) and by increasing glucose uptake …","Gluconeogenesis turns non-carbohydrate substrates (amino acids, lactate, glycerol) into glucose. Glycogenolysis breaks glycogen down into glucose, which the body can use for energy.","In times of stress, more insulin is needed than usual; in diabetics that insulin isn't available, and DKA or HHS can develop.","In both, the main problem is that insulin isn't working properly: in DKA the body isn't making enough; in HHS the insulin that is available isn't working effectively.","On top of that, the counter-regulatory hormones (glucagon, adrenaline, cortisol, growth hormone) go up. They increase hepatic glucose production and impair the peripheral tissues' ability to use glucose.","Dehydration and electrolyte imbalances occur primarily from the osmotic diuresis caused by glycosuria (glucose in the urine).","The lecture uses the word \"gluconeogenesis\" twice in a row; the second definition (glycogen broken down into glucose) is glycogenolysis, which is how it is listed here."]},{"s":"Why DKA Makes Ketones & HHS Doesn't▾","a":"why-dka-makes-ketones-hhs-doesn-t","k":["Why DKA Makes Ketones & HHS Doesn't▾","DKA is marked by increased gluconeogenesis, lipolysis and ketogenesis, with reduced glycolysis: that is the \"keto\" in ketoacidosis.","The insulin deficiency is absolute in type 1 and relative in type 2, and the excess counter-regulatory hormones add to it.","Suppressing lipolysis and ketogenesis is more sensitive to insulin than suppressing gluconeogenesis. In HHS, the residual insulin is enough to minimize ketoacidosis but not enough to control the hyperglycemia.","1 — Insulin deficiency and resistance mean glucose can't get into the cells, which need it for energy.","2 — Without glucose, the body breaks down fat for energy (lipolysis), releasing free fatty acids and glycerol from peripheral fat stores.","3 — The free fatty acids travel to the liver, are taken up by hepatocytes and activated.","4 — They are converted into acetyl-CoA, enter the ketogenic pathway and form ketone bodies.","Why not in HHS? One theory: there may be enough insulin activity to minimize lipolysis (and so ketone formation), but not enough to block gluconeogenesis, promote glucose use and prevent the hyperglycemia."]},{"s":"Why HHS Glucose Runs Higher▾","a":"why-hhs-glucose-runs-higher","k":["Why HHS Glucose Runs Higher▾","DKA","Clients present earlier, with symptoms of ketoacidosis: shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.","Because they seek treatment earlier, glucose is usually 350 to 450 (can be as high as 800).","Tend to be younger with a higher GFR, so a greater capacity to excrete glucose.","HHS","Clients present later, without those early ketoacidosis symptoms.","Glucose is typically higher: it can exceed 1,000.","Glycosuria first limits the rise in glucose, but the osmotic diuresis causes volume depletion and a lower GFR, which limits glucose excretion. The effect is more pronounced in HHS."]},{"s":"Anion Gap, Plasma Osmolality & Sodium▾","a":"anion-gap-plasma-osmolality-sodium","k":["Anion Gap, Plasma Osmolality & Sodium▾","Anion gap — DKA typically presents as an elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis. The anion gap is the difference between negatively and positively charged electrolytes, calculated from sodium, chloride and bicarb (sodium minus chloride plus …","Beta-hydroxybutyrate — Ketones are no longer really measured directly; in DKA, beta-hydroxybutyrate (one of the main metabolic products in ketoacidosis) is measured instead.","Closing the gap — Ketoacid anion excretion depends on volume status and GFR. Isotonic IV fluids correct the hypovolemia, increase renal excretion of ketoacid anions and reduce the anion gap.","Plasma osmolality — Increased in both, in proportion to the glucose; usually not as high in DKA because the glucose isn't as high.","Sodium — Hyperglycemia pulls water out of the cells and expands the extracellular fluid, so plasma sodium falls: a dilutional hyponatremia. It isn't treated directly, because it corrects as the hyperglycemia is corrected."]},{"s":"Potassium: High on Arrival, Depleted Underneath▾","a":"potassium-high-on-arrival-depleted-underneath","k":["Potassium: High on Arrival, Depleted Underneath▾","Most body potassium is in the cells. Clients with DKA and HHS present with a normal to elevated serum potassium but a significant total body potassium depletion, for three reasons:","Acidosis — In metabolic acidosis, hydrogen ions move into the cells and potassium moves out to balance the charge, raising serum potassium. Working kidneys can offset this by excreting the excess.","Insulin deficiency — Insulin normally moves potassium into the cells; without it, potassium stays in the extracellular fluid, raising serum levels further.","Hyperosmolarity — The osmotic diuresis from high glucose causes massive urinary losses, and potassium is lost continuously in the urine, depleting the total body supply.","Serum potassium can be 5, 6 or beyond on arrival. Don't bring it down unless it's causing problems and the client is symptomatic: it returns toward normal as the pH is corrected.","Once treatment starts, insulin drives potassium back into the cells and the serum level falls quickly, which can cause severe hypokalemia. If the kidneys have already excreted potassium, there is even less left to move back in.","As soon as the potassium reaches a normal level, replacement typically starts, because it will keep moving into the cells and the serum still needs a normal level to prevent cardiac problems.","Watch the client: are they symptomatic, and are there EKG changes of hyper- or hypokalemia (including dysrhythmias)?","The hydrogen-potassium shift was first covered in Acid-Base Balance (Week 2)"]},{"s":"Precipitating Factors▾","a":"precipitating-factors","k":["Precipitating Factors▾","A precipitating factor can often be identified.","Infection is the most common: severe pneumonia or a UTI without an insulin adjustment.","Inflammatory events: MI, CVA, sepsis, pancreatitis, without an insulin adjustment.","Poor compliance with insulin, or increased insulin needs that aren't met. Compliance can be checked with a hemoglobin A1c."]},{"s":"Clinical Presentation▾","a":"clinical-presentation","k":["Clinical Presentation▾","DKA: evolves rapidly, over about 24 hours","GI: nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, from delayed gastric emptying or an ileus induced by the metabolic acidosis.","Neurologic: slight confusion up to obtunded, with severe acidosis.","Volume depletion: decreased skin turgor, decreased urine output, dry oral mucosa, tachycardia, hypotension.","Fruity breath (some say it smells like nail polish remover) from exhaled acetone.","Kussmaul respirations: really deep respirations and hyperventilation, compensating for the metabolic acidosis.","HHS: begins several days before admission","Marked hyperglycemia with the typical signs: polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, plus weakness.","As it progresses: lethargy, obtunded, up to coma. Neurologic symptoms are most common in HHS because of the hyperosmolarity.","Extracellular volume depletion: dry mucous membranes, poor skin turgor, sunken eyes, hypotension, tachycardia.","Symptoms of a coexisting illness that may have precipitated it (pneumonia, UTI) are usually present."]},{"s":"Treatment▾","a":"treatment","k":["Treatment▾","Hospitals usually have a protocol and algorithm; follow your hospital's protocol. The lecturer cites the University of Kentucky (UK) protocol for the potassium numbers.","1. Fluid replacement — Usually first: expands extracellular volume and stabilizes the client hemodynamically. Treat volume depletion rapidly (normal saline); fluid resuscitation in HHS should be pretty aggressive.","2. Correct the potassium deficit — Per the UK protocol, a DKA client receives potassium replacement if potassium is less than 5 before insulin is started, to prevent the hypokalemia that follows. If potassium is less than 3.3, replace it fi…","Sodium bicarb — May be given if the pH is low (around 7.1 to 7.2), depending on how the client presents.","3. IV insulin — An IV insulin infusion, with blood sugar checked regularly (typically once an hour at first) and the rate adjusted to the glucose. Continue until the anion gap is closed and the condition is resolving.","Add dextrose — When glucose reaches about 200 to 250, fluids change from no glucose to D5W/normal saline (dextrose added to the saline) while the insulin infusion continues, to prevent hypoglycemia if the gap is still open.","Monitor — Glucose very closely and frequently (it shouldn't fall too rapidly), electrolytes and the anion gap.","Insulin drives potassium into the cells. Check potassium before insulin: below 3.3, replace potassium first. Watch for EKG changes of hyper- and hypokalemia throughout treatment."]},{"s":"How You Know It's Resolved▾","a":"how-you-know-it-s-resolved","k":["How You Know It's Resolved▾","DKA: look at the labs","Make sure the ketoacidosis has resolved: venous pH or the anion gap, and the level of ketoacidosis by beta-hydroxybutyrate, which falls as the acidosis is corrected.","HHS: look at the patient","Are they mentally alert and back to baseline? Also check the plasma osmolality: the goal is about 315, which the lecture calls a normal level.","Once the client can eat regularly, IV insulin is changed to subQ insulin."]},{"s":"HHS: Why Hydration Matters So Much▾","a":"hhs-why-hydration-matters-so-much","k":["HHS: Why Hydration Matters So Much▾","If adequate hydration isn't maintained, dehydration and worsening renal function follow.","Clients with poor fluid intake from altered thirst mechanisms or inability to access fluids (as may be seen in the elderly) are at increased risk of severe dehydration.","Diminished renal filtration further impairs glucose excretion, which exacerbates the hyperglycemia."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week5-thyroid-crisis.html","t":"Thyroid Crisis: Thyroid Storm & Myxedema Coma","secs":[{"s":"Thyroid Crisis: Thyroid Storm & Myxedema Coma","a":"","k":["Week 5 · Topic 2","The Two Extremes of the Thyroid Spectrum","Thyroid Storm: What It Is","Thyroid Storm: Clinical Manifestations","Thyroid Storm: Treatment","Myxedema Coma: What It Is","Myxedema Coma: Clinical Manifestations","Myxedema Coma: Treatment","Thyroid Storm vs Myxedema Coma","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the thyroid emergencies voiceover: thyroid storm (thyrotoxic crisis) on the hyperthyroid end of the spectrum and myxedema coma on the hypothyroid end, covering what each is, risk factors, clinical manifestations and treatm…"]},{"s":"The Two Extremes of the Thyroid Spectrum▾","a":"the-two-extremes-of-the-thyroid-spectrum","k":["The Two Extremes of the Thyroid Spectrum▾","Thyroid storm (thyrotoxic crisis)","The hyperthyroid extreme: an overwhelming release of thyroid hormones.","Myxedema coma","The hypothyroid extreme: severe hypothyroidism.","Many manifestations are the same as ordinary hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism, just more dramatic or severe."]},{"s":"Thyroid Storm: What It Is▾","a":"thyroid-storm-what-it-is","k":["Thyroid Storm: What It Is▾","An overwhelming release of thyroid hormones that puts an intense stimulus on the body's metabolism.","Typically a reaction to a major stressor (a big infection, major surgery, a car wreck). The stressor releases stress hormones such as adrenaline, everything in the body is \"revved up,\" and the body can't tolerate this for long.","Life-threatening: untreated, it can cause death within about 48 hours.","Long-standing untreated hyperthyroidism — The lecture calls this Graves' disease.","A precipitating acute event — Surgery, trauma or infection, which may or may not involve the thyroid.","Irregular use or stopping antithyroid drugs — Commonly reported before a thyroid storm."]},{"s":"Thyroid Storm: Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"thyroid-storm-clinical-manifestations","k":["Thyroid Storm: Clinical Manifestations▾","Fever (the most notable) — Body temperature rises greatly, to 104 or 105; excessive sweating; the client is hot and can't tolerate heat.","Tachycardia — Often above 140.","Blood pressure — Hypertension at first. With sustained tachycardia the heart can't fill properly, so BP may start to drop. Untreated: hypotension, heart failure and pulmonary edema that can lead to death.","Dysrhythmias — A-fib and atrial flutter are the most common.","Other — Nausea, vomiting, agitation, tremors, psychosis, up to stupor or coma.","The transcript says \"heat tolerance\"; with fever and excessive sweating, the page reads it as not tolerating heat."]},{"s":"Thyroid Storm: Treatment▾","a":"thyroid-storm-treatment","k":["Thyroid Storm: Treatment▾","Treatment hits several points where thyroid hormone acts. These clients are in the ICU and very closely monitored.","Beta blocker — Controls the symptoms and signs of the increased adrenergic tone; adjusted as needed to keep a proper heart rate and blood pressure.","Thionamide (PTU) — Blocks new hormone synthesis and suppresses conversion of T4 to T3. Synthesis is blocked within about 1 to 2 hours; within 24 hours, T3 levels can drop up to about 45%.","Iodine solution — Blocks the release of thyroid hormone. Given about 1 hour after the first dose of PTU.","Glucocorticoids — Reduce T4 to T3 conversion. Evidence is conflicting, so they're given when there is clear evidence of thyroid storm, because they rapidly reduce active thyroid hormone and its effects.","Bile acid sequestrants — Thyroid hormones are metabolized in the liver and excreted in bile; free hormone released into the intestines is reabsorbed. Sequestrants decrease enterohepatic circulation and recycling of thyroid hormone.","Enterohepatic circulation is the circulation of biliary acids, bilirubin and other substances from the liver to the bile, stored in the gallbladder before reaching the small intestine.","The lecture says \"thionamide, which is also called PTU.\" PTU is the thionamide named here."]},{"s":"Myxedema Coma: What It Is▾","a":"myxedema-coma-what-it-is","k":["Myxedema Coma: What It Is▾","Severe hypothyroidism leading to decreased mental state, hypothermia and slowed function of other organs.","A medical emergency with a high mortality rate, but uncommon, because hypothyroidism can be diagnosed earlier and kept from getting worse.","Severe, long-standing or poorly controlled hypothyroidism — The baseline risk, as with thyroid storm on the other end.","A precipitating acute event — Infection, MI or major surgery on top of poorly controlled hypothyroidism.","Sedative drugs — Especially opioids, which can trigger myxedema coma in someone who already has hypothyroidism."]},{"s":"Myxedema Coma: Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"myxedema-coma-clinical-manifestations","k":["Myxedema Coma: Clinical Manifestations▾","Altered consciousness — Despite the name, clients don't often present in coma: confusion, lethargy, obtunded, some with psychosis. Untreated, it can progress to coma.","Hypothermia — Decreased thermogenesis from decreased metabolism. The lower the temperature, the higher the mortality.","Hypotension and bradycardia — Thyroid hormone helps maintain BP homeostasis; severe hypothyroidism causes bradycardia, decreased contractility, low cardiac output and hypotension.","Hyponatremia — About 50% of the time. When present it is severe and adds to the decreased mental status. Impaired free water excretion causes a dilutional hyponatremia.","Hypoglycemia — Can be caused by hypothyroidism alone and may be worse in myxedema coma.","Hypoventilation — Overall depression of the ventilatory drive and decreased responsiveness, leading to hypoxia and hypercapnia (respiratory acidosis). Some clients need a mechanical ventilator.","The lecture words it as hypoventilation \"results from a respiratory acidosis\"; the page follows the rest of that sentence (depressed ventilatory drive leading to hypoxia and hypercapnia)."]},{"s":"Myxedema Coma: Treatment▾","a":"myxedema-coma-treatment","k":["Myxedema Coma: Treatment▾","If myxedema coma is suspected, treat before lab confirmation.","Thyroid hormones (combination) — Levothyroxine (T4) plus liothyronine (T3): the combination works better than T4 alone. Given IV until the client can take T4 orally. Thyroid levels are monitored daily to confirm the therapy is working.","Glucocorticoids — Clients often have a coexisting adrenal insufficiency (decreased pituitary function, secondary adrenal insufficiency, or ACTH secretion blunted by severe hypothyroidism). IV hydrocortisone is given until adrenal insufficie…","Supportive measures — IV fluids, electrolyte replacement as needed, mechanical ventilation for hypoventilation, glucose monitoring, and treating any underlying infection.","Adrenal insufficiency and hydrocortisone are covered in Addisonian Crisis"]},{"s":"Thyroid Storm vs Myxedema Coma▾","a":"thyroid-storm-vs-myxedema-coma","k":["Thyroid Storm vs Myxedema Coma▾","End of the spectrum — Hyperthyroid — Hypothyroid","Underlying risk — Long-standing untreated hyperthyroidism; irregular use or stopping antithyroid drugs — Severe, long-standing or poorly controlled hypothyroidism; sedatives, especially opioids","Trigger — Infection, surgery, trauma — Infection, MI, surgery","Temperature — Fever, 104 to 105 — Hypothermia","Heart rate and BP — Tachycardia above 140; hypertension, then hypotension — Bradycardia and hypotension","Mental status — Agitation, tremors, psychosis, up to stupor or coma — Confusion, lethargy, obtunded, psychosis, up to coma","Key drugs — Beta blocker, PTU, iodine, glucocorticoids, bile acid sequestrants — IV levothyroxine plus liothyronine, IV hydrocortisone"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week5-addisonian-crisis.html","t":"Addisonian Crisis","secs":[{"s":"Addisonian Crisis","a":"","k":["Week 5 · Topic 3","Addison's Disease: The Three S's","Causes & the Feedback Loop","Aldosterone Deficiency vs Cortisol Deficiency","Lifelong Replacement Therapy","Stress Dosing & Teaching","Addisonian (Adrenal) Crisis: What It Is & What Causes It","Addisonian Crisis: Symptoms","Addisonian Crisis: Treatment","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the Addisonian crisis voiceover, the last new content of the course: a review of Addison's disease (the three adrenocortical hormones, causes, the ACTH feedback loop, aldosterone and cortisol deficiency), lifelong steroid …"]},{"s":"Addison's Disease: The Three S's▾","a":"addison-s-disease-the-three-s-s","k":["Addison's Disease: The Three S's▾","Addison's disease is a disease of the adrenal cortex that causes hyposecretion of all three adrenocortical hormones.","Sugar — Cortisol — Stimulates gluconeogenesis and raises blood sugar; mobilizes fat stores; helps break down proteins.","Salt — Aldosterone — Assists in fluid and electrolyte balance.","Sex — Androgens — Male sex hormones.","The most severe effects come from the lack of cortisol.","Long-term effects: immune suppression and bone breakdown (bone density problems)."]},{"s":"Causes & the Feedback Loop▾","a":"causes-the-feedback-loop","k":["Causes & the Feedback Loop▾","It can be idiopathic, but the most common cause is autoimmune. The adrenal gland can also be damaged or destroyed by disease or trauma.","In primary adrenocortical insufficiency (primary Addison's), the adrenal gland stops secreting cortisol, so there is no negative feedback. The anterior pituitary increases MSH and ACTH to try to stimulate cortisol, but despite very high lev…","Manifestations don't become evident until about 90% of the adrenal cortex is destroyed, so Addison's is usually advanced at diagnosis, or diagnosed during an Addisonian crisis."]},{"s":"Aldosterone Deficiency vs Cortisol Deficiency▾","a":"aldosterone-deficiency-vs-cortisol-deficiency","k":["Aldosterone Deficiency vs Cortisol Deficiency▾","Low aldosterone (hypoaldosteronism)","Aldosterone regulates sodium, and so water retention. Low aldosterone means loss of water and sodium, with potassium retention.","Hyponatremia, hyperkalemia and dehydration, with the signs of all three.","Hypotension from decreased vascular tone, cardiac output and circulating blood volume.","Salt craving.","The lecture adds that hypoaldosteronism signals more ADH, which stimulates water reabsorption and could lead to fluid volume excess in some cases.","Low cortisol (hypocortisolism)","Low energy: a lack of stress hormones.","Hypoglycemia, since cortisol normally raises blood glucose.","Hyperpigmentation: the pituitary increases ACTH to raise cortisol, and ACTH also stimulates melanin secretion by melanocytes, so unsuppressed ACTH darkens the skin."]},{"s":"Lifelong Replacement Therapy▾","a":"lifelong-replacement-therapy","k":["Lifelong Replacement Therapy▾","Adrenal insufficiency requires lifelong replacement with the appropriate corticosteroids.","Hydrocortisone (Cortef) — The drug of choice: has both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid activity. IM, IV and PO.","Prednisone — Glucocorticoid, PO.","Dexamethasone — Glucocorticoid, IV or PO.","Fludrocortisone — Added when extra mineralocorticoid activity is needed; the only mineralocorticoid available. Works like aldosterone, so expect an impact on sodium and water balance. PO.","Dosing should mimic the body's normal pattern of corticosteroid secretion. Mineralocorticoids can be given once a day; glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid doses should approximate what the adrenal glands normally secrete.","Never stop therapy abruptly."]},{"s":"Stress Dosing & Teaching▾","a":"stress-dosing-teaching","k":["Stress Dosing & Teaching▾","Healthy adrenal glands increase glucocorticoid output in response to stress. These clients don't get that boost, so it has to be given exogenously: increase the dose in times of stress.","Supplemental dosing follows guidelines for specific medical conditions and surgical procedures, so it varies. Example: for a mild or febrile illness, the 3 x 3 rule (loosely, three times the usual dose for three days).","Keep an emergency supply: an injectable preparation plus an oral preparation.","Wear a medical alert bracelet so emergency personnel know about the glucocorticoid needs.","Not following the regimen (stopping abruptly, or not increasing the dose under stress) can cause an Addisonian crisis, which can be fatal."]},{"s":"Addisonian (Adrenal) Crisis: What It Is & What Causes It▾","a":"addisonian-adrenal-crisis-what-it-is-what-causes-it","k":["Addisonian (Adrenal) Crisis: What It Is & What Causes It▾","A complication of Addison's disease: acute adrenal insufficiency, a sudden insufficiency of serum corticosteroids. A severe, life-threatening endocrine emergency.","More common with primary adrenal insufficiency, but can occur with secondary and tertiary.","Primary — The adrenal gland itself stops secreting cortisol (Addison's disease).","Secondary — ACTH deficiency, usually from conditions affecting the hypothalamus or pituitary.","Tertiary — Typically excessive glucocorticoid exposure.","Triggers","A sudden loss of the adrenal glands.","Increased stress in someone with underlying adrenal problems: surgery, infection, trauma, severe illness.","Sudden cessation of long-term steroid therapy, which is why clients stopping steroids are tapered."]},{"s":"Addisonian Crisis: Symptoms▾","a":"addisonian-crisis-symptoms","k":["Addisonian Crisis: Symptoms▾","Symptoms stem mainly from the lack of cortisol and aldosterone.","Sudden pain — Lower back, legs and abdomen; the lecture attributes it to hyponatremia from the lack of mineralocorticoids.","Severe vomiting and diarrhea — Lead to dehydration and hypotension; also tied to the mineralocorticoid deficiency.","CNS changes — Confusion, slurred speech, up to loss of consciousness.","Electrolyte imbalances — Hyperkalemia and hyponatremia, with their signs and symptoms. Put the client on an EKG and watch for dysrhythmias.","The lecture's video example shows shaking and decreased level of consciousness in a girl with no prior diagnosis: Addison's is often first diagnosed when the person has an Addisonian crisis, and family may not recognize it as an emergency."]},{"s":"Addisonian Crisis: Treatment▾","a":"addisonian-crisis-treatment","k":["Addisonian Crisis: Treatment▾","Untreated, it can be fatal. Rapidly replace fluid, salt and glucocorticoids, and give glucose.","Hydrocortisone IV — About a 100 mg initial bolus, then about 50 mg every 6 hours IV, until vital signs stabilize and the client can take food and medications orally. Hydrocortisone has both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid actions.","Normal saline — Replaces lost sodium: about 1 to 3 liters IV, or 5% dextrose in normal saline, based on volume status and urine output.","Once stable — Switch to PO medications, continue fluids, and taper to maintenance doses as the condition resolves.","IV hydrocortisone for coexisting adrenal insufficiency also comes up in Myxedema Coma"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week5-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 5","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Three Topics","The Three Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It's a map of Week 5's three topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate; it pulls the whole week…"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Three Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-the-three-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Three Topics▾","When Insulin Fails — Topic 1, DKA & HHS (physiology and labs first, then presentation, treatment and resolution) — Not enough working insulin plus rising counter-regulatory hormones. No insulin at all makes ketones (DKA); a little insulin s…","When the Thyroid Swings Too Far — Topic 2, Thyroid Crisis (thyroid storm, then myxedema coma) — The two ends of one spectrum: everything revved up (fever, tachycardia) or everything slowed down (hypothermia, bradycardia). The drugs either s…","When the Adrenal Cortex Can't Respond — Topic 3, Addisonian Crisis (Addison's disease and replacement first, then the crisis) — No cortisol and no aldosterone, and no stress boost. Replacement has to be raised under stress and never stopped…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order. Read Addisonian crisis before the myxedema coma treatment if the hydrocortisone there seems to come out of nowhere: the coexisting adrenal insufficiency is the reaso…"]},{"s":"The Three Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-three-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Three Topics at a Glance▾","1 — DKA & HHS — DKA: type 1, under 65, fast (about 24 hours), ketones, elevated anion gap acidosis, fruity breath, Kussmaul respirations, glucose about 350–450. HHS: type 2, over 65, slow (days), no ketones, glucose over 1,000, hyperosmolar…","2 — Thyroid Crisis — Thyroid storm: fever 104–105, tachycardia above 140, hypertension then hypotension, death within 48 hours untreated; beta blocker, PTU, iodine an hour later, glucocorticoids, bile acid sequestrants. Myxedema coma: confu…","3 — Addisonian Crisis — Addison's: sugar, salt and sex hormones all low; hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypotension, hypoglycemia, hyperpigmentation; lifelong hydrocortisone (plus fludrocortisone), stress dosing (3 x 3), never stop abruptly. C…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content. Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why. Section names are in quotation marks.","Stress as the trigger — DKA & HHS: \"Precipitating Factors\" (infection, MI, CVA, sepsis, pancreatitis) — Thyroid storm (infection, surgery, trauma); myxedema coma (infection, MI, surgery); Addisonian crisis (surgery, infection, trauma, sever…","Missed or stopped medication — DKA & HHS: poor insulin compliance (checked with an A1c) — Thyroid storm: irregular use or stopping antithyroid drugs. Addisonian crisis: sudden cessation of long-term steroids.","Hydrocortisone — Addisonian Crisis: \"Lifelong Replacement Therapy\" and \"Addisonian Crisis: Treatment\" — Myxedema coma: IV hydrocortisone for coexisting adrenal insufficiency. Thyroid storm: glucocorticoids reduce T4-to-T3 conversion.","Potassium — DKA & HHS: \"Potassium: High on Arrival, Depleted Underneath\" — Addisonian crisis: hyperkalemia from low aldosterone. Both call for EKG monitoring and watching for dysrhythmias.","Dilutional hyponatremia — DKA & HHS: \"Anion Gap, Plasma Osmolality & Sodium\" (glucose pulls water out of the cells) — Myxedema coma: impaired free water excretion. Addisonian crisis: hyponatremia from low aldosterone.","Glucose swings — DKA & HHS (hyperglycemia; dextrose added to avoid hypoglycemia during treatment) — Myxedema coma: hypoglycemia and glucose monitoring. Addison's: hypoglycemia from low cortisol; glucose given in the crisis.","Dehydration and hypotension — DKA & HHS: osmotic diuresis and volume depletion; fluids first — Addisonian crisis: vomiting, diarrhea and low aldosterone; normal saline 1–3 L. Thyroid storm and myxedema coma: hypotension late or from low car…","Mental status changes — DKA & HHS: neuro symptoms most common in HHS (hyperosmolarity) — Thyroid storm (agitation, psychosis, up to coma); myxedema coma (confusion, lethargy, up to coma); Addisonian crisis (confusion, slurred speech, loss o…","Acid-base — Week 2's Acid-Base Balance and ABGs (metabolic acidosis, the hydrogen-potassium shift, Kussmaul breathing) — DKA's anion gap metabolic acidosis; myxedema coma's hypoventilation and hypercapnia (respiratory acidosis).","Medical alert bracelet — Addisonian Crisis: \"Stress Dosing & Teaching\" — Week 3's Myasthenia Gravis teaches the same thing."]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and safety statements from across the three pages, condensed to a phrase, one row per topic. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation. Click through to a topic for the details behind any…","DKA & HHS — Fluids first. Check potassium before insulin: below 3.3, replace first (UK protocol: replace if below 5 before insulin). Insulin drives potassium into the cells, so watch for severe hypokalemia and EKG changes. Don't treat the e…","Thyroid Crisis — Thyroid storm: death within about 48 hours untreated; ICU; iodine only after the first PTU dose (about 1 hour). Myxedema coma: treat before lab confirmation; opioids and sedatives can trigger it; hypoventilation may need a …","Addisonian Crisis — Never stop steroids abruptly (taper); increase the dose in stress (3 x 3 rule); keep injectable plus oral emergency supply; medical alert bracelet. In crisis: rapid hydrocortisone, saline and glucose; EKG for hyperkalemi…","Patterns You'll Notice Across These Rules","\"Stress uncovers the weakness.\" Each crisis starts with a chronic disorder that was holding steady until infection, surgery, trauma or illness asked the body for more than it could give: more insulin, more thyroid control, more cortisol.","\"Missed medication is a trigger too.\" Skipped insulin, irregular antithyroid drugs and abruptly stopped steroids each set off their own crisis.","\"The treatment can create the next problem.\" Insulin drops the potassium, and dropping the glucose too fast causes hypoglycemia; that is why potassium comes before insulin and dextrose joins the drip.","\"Opposite ends, mirror-image signs.\" Thyroid storm and myxedema coma flip almost every vital sign: fever vs hypothermia, tachycardia vs bradycardia.","\"Fluids, electrolytes, glucose, monitor.\" Every crisis this week gets IV fluids, and close electrolyte, glucose and vital sign monitoring."]}]},{"f":"final-exam-study-guide.html","t":"Final Exam Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Final Exam Study Guide","a":"","k":["Final Exam Review · High-Yield","Must-Know Antidotes & Rescue Drugs","Must-Know Numbers","Cardiovascular","Respiratory & Acid-Base","Fluids & Electrolytes","Neurologic","Endocrine","Infection, Antimicrobials & Immunity","Hematology","Renal, GI & Liver","Cancer, Chemotherapy & Musculoskeletal","Mental Health & Substance Use","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is the short version of your final exam review. It pulls the highest-yield points from the three course reviews, the ones that show up most across your lectures and that your lecturers flagged as NCLEX favorites, and groups them b…","How to use this page","Start with the two must-know tables (antidotes and numbers).","Then work through one body system at a time; hide the right-hand column of any table to quiz yourself.","Finish with the flashcards at the bottom.","The three full reviews","NUR 325 Review: Pathopharm I (Modules 1–7)","NUR 326 Review: Pathopharm II (Weeks 1–7)","NUR 406 Review: this course (Weeks 1–5)"]},{"s":"Must-Know Antidotes & Rescue Drugs▾","a":"must-know-antidotes-rescue-drugs","k":["Must-Know Antidotes & Rescue Drugs▾","Opioid overdose, respiratory depression — Naloxone (Narcan) — 325","Benzodiazepines — Flumazenil — 325","Heparin, enoxaparin — Protamine sulfate, IV slowly (fast push = profound hypotension) — 325","Warfarin — Vitamin K; fresh frozen plasma — 325","HIT (platelets below 100,000 on heparin, new clots) — Stop heparin; switch to argatroban — 325","Hypoglycemia — Awake and can swallow: carbs. Can't: glucagon IM or IV dextrose — 325","Anaphylaxis — Epinephrine 1:1,000 — 325","Acetaminophen overdose — Acetylcysteine — 326","Iron overdose (common childhood poisoning) — Deferoxamine — 326","Digoxin toxicity — Digoxin immune Fab — 326","Hepatic encephalopathy (high ammonia) — Lactulose: 2–3 soft stools a day — 326","Cholinergic crisis (neostigmine toxicity, SLUDGE) — Atropine — 406","Life-threatening bleeding after alteplase — Aminocaproic acid — 406","Addisonian crisis — Hydrocortisone about 100 mg IV, then 50 mg every 6 h; NS 1–3 L or D5NS — 406"]},{"s":"Must-Know Numbers▾","a":"must-know-numbers","k":["Must-Know Numbers▾","Sodium / potassium — 136–145 / 3.5–5.0 — 325","Calcium / magnesium — 9.0–10.5 / 1.3–2.1 — 325","WBC / platelets — 5,000–10,000 / 150,000–400,000 — 325","Neutropenia (precautions) — ANC below 1,000 — 325","INR on warfarin — Therapeutic about 2–3.5 — 325","Phenytoin level — 10–20 mcg/mL — 325","Acetaminophen limit — 4 g/24 h (2 g with liver disease or chronic alcohol use) — 325","ABG — pH 7.35–7.45; PaCO2 35–45; HCO3 24–29 — 406","AIDS — CD4 below 200 (or an AIDS-defining condition) — 326","Epoetin alfa — Only if Hgb below 10 (black box) — 326","GFR / oliguria — Normal above 90 / below 400 mL a day — 326","Digoxin — Apical pulse a full minute; hold if below 60 — 326","Sublingual nitroglycerin — Every 5 min, up to 3; no relief 5 min after the first: call 911 — 326","Ejection fraction — Normal 55–65%; HFrEF below 40% — 326","Ankle-brachial index — Normal about 1–1.4; 0.5 or less = severe — 326","Chemo nadir — About 7–14 days after a dose — 326","DEXA T-score — −1 or higher normal; −1 to −2.5 osteopenia; −2.5 or lower osteoporosis — 326","MI reperfusion — Within 4 h (ideally 30 min); irreversible injury from 30 min — 406","Ischemic stroke — Save the penumbra within 3 h; GCS below 8 = intubate — 406","DKA treatment — Potassium below 3.3: replace before insulin; add dextrose at glucose 200–250 — 406"]},{"s":"Cardiovascular▾","a":"cardiovascular","k":["Cardiovascular▾","Covered in all three courses, so it's the heaviest-weighted system. Dive deeper: NUR 325 HLD & HTN · NUR 325 coagulation · NUR 326 cardiovascular · NUR 406 Week 1","RAAS & BP drugs — Renin → angiotensin I → ACE (lungs) → angiotensin II (vasoconstriction + aldosterone: sodium and water retained). ACE inhibitors: dry cough (top reason to switch), angioedema, hyperkalemia, not in pregnancy. ARBs (\"-sartan…","Lipids & statins — LDL goal below 130 (below 70 if high risk). Statins: myopathy, rhabdomyolysis (AKI), hepatotoxicity (AST/ALT).","Stable angina vs ACS — Stable: exertion, relieved by rest, 2–5 min. ACS = unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI (\"time is tissue\"); pain lasts longer, not relieved by nitroglycerin; women may report heartburn. First-time chest pain: call 911, neve…","Acute MI drugs — VS before every drug. Aspirin: chew the first dose (lowers mortality). Morphine: respiratory depression, hypotension. Nitroglycerin: lowers preload and afterload, doesn't lower mortality, never with sildenafil. Beta blocker…","Pericarditis & tamponade — Pericarditis: sharp pain worse with deep breaths, fever, friction rub; NSAIDs (colchicine if not working). Tamponade (emergency): hypotension, JVD, muffled heart sounds, pulsus paradoxus: pericardiocentesis.","Heart failure — Left = lungs (crackles, frothy sputum, orthopnea; usually from HTN). Right = rest of the body (JVD, dependent edema, weight gain, ascites; often from COPD). HF drugs: ACE/ARB/ARNI + beta blocker + spironolactone + SGLT2 inhi…","Digoxin — Apical pulse 1 full minute, hold below 60. Toxicity risk: older age, women, diuretics, low potassium (the NCLEX favorite). Signs: bradycardia, confusion, nausea, visual changes. Antidote: digoxin immune Fab.","Dysrhythmias — A-fib: irregularly irregular, no P waves; stroke risk (often anticoagulated). New onset: check electrolytes and O2 first. Adenosine for SVT: 6 mg rapid IV push then 12, then 12, with an immediate rapid flush; brief asystole e…","Aneurysm & dissection — Mostly from hypertension. Suspected AAA: light palpation only. Dissection: sudden severe tearing pain; surgical emergency; beta blockers slow tearing.","PAD vs VTE — PAD: 5 P's (pain, pulselessness, palpable coolness, paresthesias, paresis); smoking cessation is the biggest treatment. Virchow's triad: venous stasis, endothelial damage, hypercoagulability.","Anticoagulants — Heparin → protamine. Warfarin → vitamin K; keep vitamin K foods consistent, not avoided. Platelets below 100,000 on heparin with new clots = HIT: stop heparin."]},{"s":"Respiratory & Acid-Base▾","a":"respiratory-acid-base","k":["Respiratory & Acid-Base▾","Dive deeper: NUR 326 respiratory · NUR 406 acid-base & ABGs","ABG steps — pH (7.40 midpoint) → PaCO2 → HCO3 → compensation. The value that matches the pH is the problem. ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Uncompensated = one abnormal value; partial = both abnormal and pH abnormal; compensate…","The four disorders — Resp acidosis: hypoventilation, overdose, sedation, COPD. Resp alkalosis: hyperventilation, panic, fever. Metabolic acidosis: renal failure, DKA, diarrhea, lactic acid (Kussmaul breathing, hyperkalemia). Metabolic alkal…","Acidosis & potassium — In acidosis, hydrogen moves into cells and potassium moves out (hyperkalemia). Buffers act in seconds, lungs in minutes to hours, kidneys in hours to days.","Asthma drugs — The only rescue drug is the SABA (albuterol); everything else is long-term control. Give the bronchodilator before the inhaled steroid, and rinse the mouth after inhaled steroids.","Tuberculosis — Airborne; latent (no symptoms) vs active (night sweats most suggestive, low-grade afternoon fever, weight loss, hemoptysis later). Contagious until sputum stops growing mycobacteria. Isoniazid: peripheral neuropathy (give B6)…"]},{"s":"Fluids & Electrolytes▾","a":"fluids-electrolytes","k":["Fluids & Electrolytes▾","Dive deeper: NUR 325 fluids & electrolytes","Hypokalemia (below 3.5) — GI or renal losses (vomiting, diarrhea, furosemide), alkalosis. Dysrhythmias (can be lethal), weakness, leg cramps, slowed bowel. Raises digoxin toxicity risk.","Hyperkalemia (above 5.0) — Renal failure, cell injury (burns, crush, sepsis), potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, NSAIDs, acidosis, Addison's. Peaked T waves, weakness, cramps, diarrhea.","Hyponatremia (below 136) — GI losses, diuretics, adrenal insufficiency, water intoxication. Brain cells swell: confusion.","Hypocalcemia (below 9.0) — Hypoparathyroidism, pancreatitis, thyroid or parathyroid surgery (sudden drop: monitor closely). Increased neuromuscular excitability."]},{"s":"Neurologic▾","a":"neurologic","k":["Neurologic▾","Dive deeper: NUR 325 dementia, pain & seizures · NUR 406 Neuro I · NUR 406 Neuro II","Stroke — 87% ischemic, 13% hemorrhagic. Non-contrast CT first (blood is white): never anticoagulate a bleed. BE FAST; last known normal. Ischemic: alteplase (watch BP, HR, rhythm, intracranial bleeding) or thrombectomy. Hemorrhagic: reverse…","TIA — Under 24 h, no infarction, no residual effects; stroke risk highest in the first month. Dysphagia (swallowing: NPO) vs dysphasia (speech).","Seizures — Phenytoin: level 10–20; gingival hyperplasia (very good dental care). Valproic acid: hepatotoxic; works for every seizure type.","Parkinson's — Low dopamine, relative excess acetylcholine. Rest (pill-rolling) tremor, cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia (most disabling), shuffling gait. Sinemet (carbidopa-levodopa) = gold standard: avoid high protein, orthostatic hypotensi…","Myasthenia gravis — Autoimmune attack on ACh receptors; weakness that improves with rest; eyes and face first; aspiration and respiratory risk. Myasthenic crisis = too little drug (improves with edrophonium). Cholinergic crisis = too much (…","Multiple sclerosis — T cells attack CNS myelin; women 20–40; relapsing-remitting most common. Paresthesias, optic neuritis, fatigue, bladder problems. Relapse: pulse IV steroids (watch glucose). Natalizumab: PML risk.","GBS & ALS — GBS: ascending weakness after a viral infection; watch the breathing muscles (ventilator); 75% return to baseline. ALS: upper and lower motor neurons; death from respiratory failure; riluzole (hepatotoxic).","Meningitis — Triad: fever, headache, nuchal rigidity; photophobia; petechial rash (meningococcal); Kernig and Brudzinski. Bacterial kills in hours: antibiotics ASAP.","Pain & opioids — Acetaminophen limit 4 g a day (counting combination products). Opioids: respiratory and CNS depression, constipation; itching is histamine, not allergy. Reversal: naloxone.","Dementia — Donepezil (cholinesterase inhibitor) for mild to moderate Alzheimer's; memantine (NMDA blocker) for moderate to severe."]},{"s":"Endocrine▾","a":"endocrine","k":["Endocrine▾","Dive deeper: NUR 325 diabetes · NUR 326 endocrine · NUR 406 endocrine emergencies","Insulin — Rapid-acting (lispro): give with the meal tray. Regular is the only IV insulin (DKA, pumps). NPH is cloudy: draw clear before cloudy. Glargine: long-acting.","DKA vs HHS — DKA: type 1, ketosis, Kussmaul breathing, fruity breath, anion gap acidosis; over about 24 h. HHS: type 2, older, no ketosis, glucose can exceed 1,000, builds over days, neuro changes. Treat: fluids first, potassium (serum norm…","Thyroid — Levothyroxine on an empty stomach about 30 min before breakfast. Thyroid storm: fever 104–105, HR above 140, A-fib; beta blocker, PTU, then iodine 1 h later, steroids. Myxedema coma: hypothermia, bradycardia, hyponatremia, hypoven…","Addison's & crisis — Three S's: sugar, salt, sex. Low aldosterone: hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypotension, salt craving; low cortisol: hypoglycemia, hyperpigmentation. Lifelong hydrocortisone; never stop abruptly; 3 × 3 rule in stress; med…","Steroids (long term) — The adrenal gland can't respond to stress, so the dose must increase when sick; taper, never stop abruptly.","SIADH vs DI — SIADH: too much ADH, water retained, dilutional hyponatremia. DI: too little ADH, large dilute urine output, hypernatremia."]},{"s":"Infection, Antimicrobials & Immunity▾","a":"infection-antimicrobials-immunity","k":["Infection, Antimicrobials & Immunity▾","Dive deeper: NUR 325 immunity & infection · NUR 326 antimicrobials & HIV","Cephalosporins — Cross-sensitivity with penicillin is rare (1–4%); avoid if the penicillin reaction was anaphylaxis. Ceftriaxone: once daily, crosses into the CSF.","Vancomycin — MRSA. IV: nephrotoxic (watch renal labs). PO: only for C. diff (stays in the gut).","Other antibiotics — Fluoroquinolones: CNS effects, seizures, prolonged QT, photosensitivity. Metronidazole: no alcohol 24 h before and at least 36 h after. Bactrim: first-line uncomplicated UTI; not with a sulfa allergy.","C. diff — Frequent, very watery diarrhea after antibiotics (normal flora killed); diagnosed by stool toxin.","HIV — Destroys CD4 cells; AIDS = CD4 below 200 or an AIDS-defining condition. Goal: undetectable viral load. PEP: ART for 28 days, then tests at 6 and 12 weeks.","CBC & neutropenia — Bands up = shift to the left (acute bacterial infection). ANC below 1,000: neutropenic precautions (hand hygiene, no sick contacts, no raw produce or fresh flowers).","Hypersensitivity — Type I: IgE and mast cells, about 15–20 min (anaphylaxis: epinephrine). Type IV: T cells, 48–72 h (TB skin test)."]},{"s":"Hematology▾","a":"hematology","k":["Hematology▾","Dive deeper: NUR 326 anemia · NUR 325 coagulation","Anemia types — Microcytic (small, pale): iron deficiency. Macrocytic (large): B12 or folate deficiency.","Iron — Black or dark green stools are expected; liquid stains teeth (straw); don't crush coated tablets. IV iron dextran: test dose first (anaphylaxis).","Sickle cell — Cells sickle when oxygen is low; triggers include exertion and dehydration; painful crisis. Oxygen, hydration, pain control.","Epoetin — For anemia of CKD; only if Hgb below 10; check Hgb before each dose."]},{"s":"Renal, GI & Liver▾","a":"renal-gi-liver","k":["Renal, GI & Liver▾","Dive deeper: NUR 326 renal · NUR 326 liver & pancreas · NUR 326 GI · NUR 325 GI & urologic","AKI & CKD — AKI: pre-renal most common (blood loss, dehydration, sepsis). CKD: hyperkalemia (the big one), fluid overload, metabolic acidosis, anemia, uremia; GFR above 90 is normal.","Pancreatitis — Sudden severe epigastric pain radiating to the back; amylase and lipase up; glucose up or down. Grey Turner (flank) and Cullen (umbilical) bruising = hemorrhagic.","Liver — Hepatic encephalopathy: confusion to coma. Lactulose traps ammonia in the gut (2–3 soft stools a day). Acetaminophen overdose: acetylcysteine.","Ulcers & acid drugs — Gastric pain 1–2 h after eating; duodenal 2–4 h. H. pylori is the most common infectious cause of gastritis. PPIs (\"-prazole\") are for short-term use: long-term risk of pneumonia, bone loss and hip fracture.","Laxatives — Bulk-forming (psyllium) is OK long term: take with plenty of water."]},{"s":"Cancer, Chemotherapy & Musculoskeletal▾","a":"cancer-chemotherapy-musculoskeletal","k":["Cancer, Chemotherapy & Musculoskeletal▾","Dive deeper: NUR 326 cancer, chemo & MSK","Chemo toxicities — Rapidly dividing cells: hair, GI (N/V, stomatitis), bone marrow. Highest infection and bleeding risk at the nadir (about 7–14 days). Extravasation: severe tissue damage.","Drug classes — Doxorubicin: cardiotoxic (lifetime max dose). Vincristine: peripheral neuropathy; IV only (intrathecal is fatal). Cyclophosphamide: hemorrhagic cystitis. Methotrexate: nephrotoxic, hepatotoxic, category X.","Osteoporosis — Alendronate: morning, sip of plain water, empty stomach, stay upright (esophageal ulcers). Raloxifene: clot risk, stop 72 h before a procedure.","Fractures — Compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion, weak pulses: fasciotomy. Fat embolism after long-bone fracture: hypoxemia, altered LOC, petechial rash.","Arthritis & gout — RA: symmetric, morning stiffness, systemic; methotrexate once weekly with folic acid. Gout: big toe; allopurinol prevents (no help in an attack); colchicine: vomiting = toxicity, stop it."]},{"s":"Mental Health & Substance Use▾","a":"mental-health-substance-use","k":["Mental Health & Substance Use▾","Dive deeper: NUR 325 mental health & SUD","Antidepressants — Onset 4–8 weeks (SSRIs about 3–4 weeks; bupropion 1–3 weeks).","Withdrawal — Opioids start in 12–24 h (peak 72 h); alcohol about 8 h (peak 1–3 days). Alcohol and benzo withdrawal: benzodiazepine taper (prevents seizures) plus thiamine for alcohol."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"final-nur325-review.html","t":"NUR 325 Review","secs":[{"s":"NUR 325 Review","a":"","k":["Final Exam Review · NUR 325","Whole-Course Quick Reference","Module 1: Foundations of Pathophysiology","Module 1: Introduction to Pharmacology","Module 2: Hyperlipidemia & Hypertension","Module 3: Diabetes, Obesity & Metabolic Syndrome","Module 4: Dementia, Pain & Seizures","Module 5: Fluids, Skin & Blood Cells","Module 6: Coagulation, Stress & Steroids","Module 7: GI, Hypersensitivity, Urologic & Mental Health","Self-Test Flashcards","This review is built only from your NUR 325 lectures (Modules 1–7). Grey notes flag spots where a voiceover misspoke: check those against your slides. Use the hide toggles on any table to quiz yourself."]},{"s":"Whole-Course Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-course-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Course Quick Reference▾","Antidotes & Reversal Agents","Opioids (respiratory depression, overdose, heroin) — Naloxone (Narcan) — 4","Benzodiazepines — Flumazenil — 7","Hypoglycemia (insulin) — Carbohydrates if awake and able to swallow; otherwise glucagon IM or IV dextrose — 3","Heparin, enoxaparin — Protamine sulfate (IV slowly, or profound hypotension) — 6","Warfarin — Vitamin K IV; fresh frozen plasma or whole blood — 6","Apixaban, rivaroxaban — Andexxa (recombinant factor Xa) — 6","Aspirin — DDAVP (desmopressin) — 6","Clopidogrel, ticagrelor — DDAVP or a platelet transfusion — 6","HIT (heparin-induced thrombocytopenia) — Stop heparin; switch to argatroban IV — 6","Anaphylaxis — Epinephrine 1:1,000 (adult 0.2–1 mg; peds 0.01 mg/kg) — 7","Numbers to Know","Sodium / potassium — 136–145 / 3.5–5.0 — 5","Magnesium / calcium / phosphorus — 1.3–2.1 / 9.0–10.5 / 3.0–4.5 — 5","WBC / platelets — 5,000–10,000 / 150,000–400,000 (below about 100,000 = thrombocytopenia) — 5, 6","Hemoglobin / hematocrit — M 14–18, F 12–16 / M 40–52%, F 37–47% — 5","Neutropenia — ANC below 1,000 — 5","INR on warfarin — Normal about 1; therapeutic about 2–3.5 — 6","Phenytoin level — 10–20 mcg/mL — 4","Acetaminophen daily limit — 4 g/24 h (2 g with liver disease or chronic alcohol use), counting combination products — 4","A1c / fasting glucose (normal) — About 5–5.5% / 99 or below — 3","Total cholesterol / LDL / triglycerides — Below 200 / below 130 (below 70 if high risk) / below 160 — 2","Antidepressant onset — 4–8 weeks (SSRI about 3–4 weeks; bupropion 1–3 weeks) — 7","Withdrawal onset — Opioids 12–24 h (peak 72 h); alcohol about 8 h (peak 1–3 days); benzos peak about week 2 — 7"]},{"s":"Module 1: Foundations of Pathophysiology▾","a":"module-1-foundations-of-pathophysiology","k":["Module 1: Foundations of Pathophysiology▾","Intro to patho, cellular adaptation, immunity and infection.","Intro to Pathophysiology","Physiology = how the body normally works; pathophysiology = the study of disease and injury and how they affect the body: the abnormal processes that disrupt normal functioning and cause signs and symptoms.","Disease = a disruption in homeostasis (homeo = same, stasis = balance); it can be physical (heart failure), mental (chronic stress) or social (chronic social isolation).","Homeostasis keeps us within a small normal window (heart rate 60–100; temperature around 98.5). Too hot: vessels dilate, sweat glands secrete. Too cold: vessels constrict, no sweating, shivering generates heat.","Intrinsic (inside the body)","Over- or underproduction of a hormone (type 1 diabetes, thyroid), autoimmune disorders, genes, immunity, age, sex.","Extrinsic (outside the body)","Toxins, pollutants, bacteria, viruses; lifestyle and behaviors (diet, exercise, smoking, alcohol); stressors.","Epidemiologic triangle (epi-triad): pathogen (the causative agent), host (susceptibility: genetics, age, immune status) and environment (external conditions, e.g., some bacteria can't survive heat, which is why meat is cooked to set tempera…","Signs vs symptoms — Signs = objective (what you see, hear, touch: wheezes, pulse). Symptoms = subjective (what the patient tells you: pain, nausea, dizziness).","Diagnosis / etiology / prognosis — Naming the disease / the cause / the likely course and chance of recovery.","Exposure — Contact with an agent (chemical, temperature, organism) that can start the disease process.","Sudden vs insidious onset — Sudden: rapid (MI, asthma attack). Insidious: slow and gradual, people adapt until they can't function (a slow GI bleed).","Latent — Lying in wait: chickenpox virus stays dormant and can reactivate as shingles.","Prodromal — Early signs that the disease is starting (chills, headache before a cold or flu).","Manifestations / remission / convalescence — Signs and symptoms / disease no longer active / recovering.","Idiopathic — Cause unknown (idiopathic cardiomyopathy).","Iatrogenic — Caused by healthcare: pressure ulcers, hospital-acquired infections, medication errors, a colon perforated during colonoscopy.","Exacerbation — Worsening, often an acute decline in a chronic disease (an asthma attack after an allergen).","Prefixes and suffixes: hypo- (below), hyper- (above), -penia (deficiency: leukopenia), -cytosis (increase in cells: leukocytosis), -osis (process or condition), -itis (inflammation: hepatitis), -pathy (disease: myopathy).","Investigate every sign or symptom in detail: for a cough, is it productive (color, thickness, blood), when does it occur, acute or chronic, allergens, smoking, medications, other symptoms? Weight loss: how fast, and is it intentional?","Cellular Adaptation","Cells change size or form to survive and keep functioning. This can be normal (muscles growing with exercise, uterine cells in pregnancy) or abnormal, usually after something like radiation, a medication or lack of oxygen.","Atrophy — Cells shrink — Physiologic: developmental (e.g., thymus). Pathologic: decreased workload or changed environment (unused broken leg), nutritional deficiency, decreased blood supply, hormonal problems, prolonged immobility, aging. D…","Hypertrophy — Cells get bigger (can increase function) — Response to mechanical stimuli (stretching, chronic pressure, volume overload). Normal in exercised muscle; heart and kidney are most prone to harmful hypertrophy, usually from hypert…","Hyperplasia — More cells, same size — Only cells that can divide (skin, intestinal epithelium, glands). Normal in pregnancy/lactation and wound healing; otherwise usually negative: cancers, abnormal hormone stimulation, endometrial hyperpla…","Dysplasia — Abnormal size, shape or organization of mature cells — Often from atypical hyperplasia, chronic irritation or inflammation. Mild, moderate or severe; reversible (tobacco-related mouth changes can reverse after quitting). Not can…","Metaplasia — Reversible replacement of one mature cell type with another — Meta = change. The new cell is less differentiated and survives a hostile environment better. From chronic irritation or inflammation; more associated with cancer th…","Neoplasia / anaplasia — Growth no longer responding to normal regulation — Usually a gene mutation. Anaplastic cells are immature, embryonic-like cancer cells: think neoplasm and malignant tumor.","Benign neoplasm","More differentiated (less anaplastic) cells; grows slowly; usually encapsulated; doesn't metastasize. Usually harmless unless it compresses a sensitive area like the brain.","Malignant neoplasm","Very anaplastic, immature cells; reproduces rapidly; no capsule; metastasizes near or far, depending on closeness to lymph or blood systems.","Necrosis — Cell death from disease, injury or failed blood supply; cells burst with inflammation, swelling and redness; irreversible. Ischemic necrosis follows infarction or prolonged ischemia.","Liquefactive necrosis — Cells digested by enzymes into liquid (e.g., lung abscesses); typical in lipid-rich organs (lungs, brain) or abscesses.","Gangrene — Necrotic tissue plus bacterial invasion, usually from loss of major blood supply; most often toes and feet of patients with poor circulation (PVD, diabetes).","Dry gangrene — Minimal bacteria; black, dry, wrinkled; spreads slowly (weeks to months); line of demarcation.","Wet gangrene — Liquefactive necrosis; moist, dark, swollen, cold, pulseless, blisters, foul odor; no demarcation line; spreads rapidly; systemic signs (tachycardia, fever, confusion, decreased kidney function) can lead to death.","Gas gangrene — A wet gangrene from Clostridium perfringens, an anaerobic spore-former in soil; after severe trauma or compound fractures (the ATV accident); gas bubbles; very serious.","Intro to Immunity: Inflammation","Inflammation happens when cells are injured, whatever the cause. At first it is protective: it destroys invading agents, limits their spread and prepares damaged tissue for repair. Left unchecked, it becomes the problem. The suffix -itis me…","Five cardinal signs: redness, swelling, heat, pain, loss of function.","Causes: exogenous (surgery, trauma, falls, burns) or endogenous (internal cell damage, such as tissue ischemia).","Acute inflammation lasts less than 2 weeks and can be helpful. Chronic inflammation lasts longer, can form scar tissue and is no longer helpful.","1 — Tissue injury or a bacterial antigen triggers release of chemical mediators.","2 — Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability around the injury.","3 — Leukocytes (mostly neutrophils and monocytes) attach to the endothelium, move through the capillary wall and migrate into the tissue. Recruiting them is chemotaxis.","4 — Phagocytosis: the white blood cells engulf and break down bacteria, antigens and debris.","5 — Inflammatory exudate: fluid that leaks from blood vessels, containing neutrophils and cellular debris.","Serous — Clear, watery, low protein — Mild inflammation; normal healing.","Serosanguineous — Clear with a pink tinge (a few RBCs) — Normal right after surgery.","Purulent (fibrinous) — Thick, sticky, green or yellow, may smell; lots of neutrophils, protein, debris — Severe inflammation; think bacterial infection; can form an abscess that must be removed.","Hemorrhagic — Large amounts of RBCs — Most severe inflammation, from severely injured or broken-down vessels; think the patient is bleeding from somewhere.","Systemic inflammation is driven by cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha; the names aren't needed): fever, increased WBC count, fatigue, muscle catabolism. It looks like infection, so look for a source of infection to tell them apart.","Intro to Immunity: Specific Adaptive Immunity","The major histocompatibility complex (MHC), called HLA in humans, is a gene cluster on chromosome 6 whose proteins sit on almost every cell surface as a \"self\" name tag. Two classes: MHC class 1 and 2.","Specific adaptive immunity recognizes foreign invaders, destroys them and remembers them for a faster response next time. It depends on lymphocytes (B and T cells) that react to foreign antigens while staying tolerant to self.","B cells: humoral (antibody-mediated) immunity, through antibodies in body fluids. Memory B cells remember an antigen and respond rapidly on re-exposure; plasma cells are short-lived and secrete antibodies.","T cells: cell-mediated immunity, recognizing antigens presented on cell surfaces.","An antigen is the \"name tag\" on a foreign or harmful cell (cancer, bacterial, fungal); antibodies (immunoglobulins) are what our own body makes against it.","IgG — Most common (75–80%). Moves easily into tissues. Produced once an infection has been going on a while or resolved, or after a vaccine. Protects against bacteria and viruses. Crosses the placenta.","IgM — About 10%. First produced on exposure or after immunization; activates complement. IgM means something new or acute.","IgA — In secretions: saliva, tears, breast milk, GI and GU.","IgD — Trace amounts on B cells; stimulates B cells to multiply, differentiate and secrete other immunoglobulins.","IgE — Parasites and allergic reactions; works with mast cells.","Passive immunity","Antibodies transferred from a protected person: mother to fetus (IgG across the placenta, IgA in breast milk), or injected antibodies (IgG, hepatitis B immune globulin, COVID convalescent plasma). Not vaccines.","Active immunity","From an active infection or a vaccine: the body makes its own antibodies, mostly through memory B cells, and responds rapidly on the next exposure.","Immunity is resistance to a particular pathogen, provided mostly by circulating antibodies; a titer blood test measures them and can confirm immunity.","Traditional (inactivated) — An inactivated or killed organism or part of one (the flu shot).","Attenuated (live) — A weakened live version (the nasal flu vaccine). Avoid in a weakened immune system or conditions that raise complication risk (lung, heart, kidney or metabolic disease); use the inactivated version instead.","Toxoid — An inactivated toxin that stimulates antitoxin (tetanus).","Conjugate — Protein or toxoid from one organism attached to a disease-causing one (H. influenzae type B).","mRNA — A snip of a virus's genetic code teaches the body to respond.","Intro to Infection: Microorganisms & Transmission","Infection = a host colonized or taken over by a microbial species, either localized or systemic (spread to several body regions).","Virus — Only DNA or only RNA in a protein shell; needs a host cell to replicate. COVID-19, SARS, AIDS, chickenpox, influenza.","Bacteria — Larger, single-celled, usually one DNA strand; reproduce inside or outside cells; can adapt and suppress defenses. Strep throat, tuberculosis, UTIs.","Fungi — Spore-forming (yeast infections).","Protozoa — Live in water; malaria is the most common.","Helminths — Parasitic worms (roundworms, hookworms).","Prions — Misfolded proteins that attach to other proteins; devastating disease such as Creutzfeldt-Jakob (mad cow) disease.","Microorganisms live in a reservoir (humans, water, animals, insects).","Direct transmission","Physical contact (kissing, sex, contaminated soil: STDs, hookworm) and droplets, short-range aerosols from sneezing or coughing (pertussis, meningitis).","Indirect transmission","Airborne (droplet nuclei stay suspended: measles); vehicle-borne (food, water, blood: hepatitis A in contaminated food); vector-borne (mosquitoes, fleas, ticks: malaria, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, Lyme disease).","Portals of entry: airways, GI tract, genitourinary tract (STDs, catheters), blood (transfusion, needle sticks), maternal-fetal (Zika, Listeria; across the placenta or during birth).","The skin, mucous membranes and endothelium are the main physical barrier (tight junctions). The skin is the biggest barrier, so a break in skin integrity is the biggest vulnerability.","Translocation: bacteria move across the intestinal lining (a leaky gut, into the peritoneal cavity), or an infection in the blood lodges in another organ such as bone.","Incubation — Entry to first symptoms; no symptoms while the pathogen multiplies.","Prodromal — Nonspecific symptoms: extra tired, small fever.","Acute — Full-blown, specific signs and symptoms.","Convalescence — Symptoms improving, illness disappearing.","Resolution — Pathogen eliminated. Some never reach it (chickenpox stays dormant).","1. Injury — A brief vasoconstriction stops bleeding and slows invaders, then vasodilation brings blood flow and white blood cells (and the five cardinal signs).","2. Increased permeability — Vessels leak fluid into the tissue, letting white blood cells out.","3. Emigration of leukocytes — Mostly neutrophils (also eosinophils, NK cells, monocytes) attach to the endothelium and move to the injury.","4. Phagocytosis — Neutrophils and monocytes engulf and destroy invaders.","5. Exudate — Transports white blood cells, dilutes toxins, carries nutrients.","6. Systemic symptoms — If not kept local: the hypothalamic fever set point rises. Fever is protective: it conserves heat, some bacteria divide more slowly, and neutrophils, macrophages and B/T-cell activation work better.","Intro to Infection: Diagnosis & Healthcare-Acquired Infections","Colonization: a pathogen lives at a body site without signs of infection (e.g., MRSA or VRE found on admission swabs). It can become active infection if the immune system is compromised or tissue is disrupted.","Signs of infection: vital sign changes (fever, increased heart rate and respiratory rate from a higher metabolic rate).","Culture: a Gram stain first (gram-positive vs gram-negative, shape and arrangement), then sensitivity (at least 24 hours, up to 72 for full identification): the exact bacteria and which antibiotics it's sensitive or resistant to. Gram-negat…","Culture the likely source: urine (catheter), sputum (cough), blood if concerned about sepsis (\"septic\" = infection in the blood). Blood cultures: aerobic and anaerobic bottles, two sets from two sticks, at least one peripheral; high risk of…","pH — 5 to 9 (as stated in the lecture)","Nitrites — None — Bacterial infection","Leukocyte esterase — Negative — Infection","RBCs — Fewer than 5 — More can indicate infection","Epithelial cells — Not infection: a contaminated sample, or sometimes cancer","The lecture says \"nitrates\" in the urinalysis; the urine test for bacteria is nitrites, which is how it's listed here.","Nosocomial infection: acquired in the hospital; more virulent and more likely drug-resistant. MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staph aureus), VRE (vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus), CRE (carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae: resistant to a w…","Superinfection: a new infection during treatment for another. Antibiotics kill normal flora that kept opportunistic pathogens in check, so resistant organisms proliferate, mainly in the GI tract, skin and mucosa.","C. difficile (C. diff)","Frequent (as often as hourly), very watery diarrhea (can be orange-tinged), abdominal cramping and tenderness; can appear months after antibiotics.","Diagnosed by stool C. diff toxin PCR, not culture.","Contact isolation (gown and gloves) and soap and water: spores resist hand sanitizer.","Treat with PO or IV metronidazole (Flagyl) or PO vancomycin. No antidiarrheals.","Complication: pseudomembranous colitis, life-threatening colon dilation needing decompression or partial removal.","Candidiasis","Fungal (yeast) overgrowth after antibiotics kill normal flora: oral (thrush), vaginal, and moist skin folds (under the breasts, fat folds).","Untreated thrush can descend into the esophagus.","Prevention is key: nystatin (antifungal), vaginal suppositories, antifungal powders or sprays."]},{"s":"Module 1: Introduction to Pharmacology▾","a":"module-1-introduction-to-pharmacology","k":["Module 1: Introduction to Pharmacology▾","Drug development, schedules, the three phases, CYP450 and drug safety.","Intro to Pharmacology","Pharmacology = the study of drugs (chemicals, medications) put into the body that cause a change. Pharmacokinetics = what the body does to the drug. Pharmacodynamics = what the drug does to the body (mechanism of action and effects).","Three names: chemical (long, used in research: acetylparaminophenol), generic (the one official name, lowercase: acetaminophen; what exams and NCLEX use), trade (brand name, capitalized: Tylenol). When the patent expires, any company can ma…","Prototype: the first drug in a class; new drugs in the class are compared to it for effectiveness and side effects (ibuprofen/Advil for NSAIDs).","Therapeutic effect = what we intend the drug to do. Side effects = unintended effects of a normal dose (used interchangeably with adverse effects in this class). Toxicity = harmful effects. Adverse effects = unexpected and dangerous. Allerg…","For every drug, know: generic name, class, mechanism of action, indications, common and serious adverse effects, contraindications, nursing considerations (e.g., check blood sugar before insulin, BP before a BP medication) and serious inter…","Preclinical — Lab animals: therapeutic and adverse effects. Only about 5 in 100,000 chemicals ever reach market.","Phase 1 — Healthy human volunteers: any negative reaction?","Phase 2 — Patients who have the disease; identify rarer side effects; may be pulled if too dangerous.","Phase 3 — Wide clinical use; prescribers monitor closely; may be withdrawn.","Phase 4 — On the market; the FDA keeps evaluating. A pattern of severe reactions can lead to a black box warning, the FDA's strictest warning.","Schedule I — No accepted medical use, very high abuse potential: heroin, LSD, ecstasy.","Schedule II — High abuse potential, severe dependence, but medical use: opioids, narcotics, amphetamines (hydromorphone/Dilaudid, oxycodone). No refills: a new prescription each time.","Schedule III — Moderate to low dependence: non-barbiturate sedatives, non-amphetamine stimulants, ketamine, testosterone and anabolic steroids.","Schedule IV — Low abuse and dependence: Xanax, Valium, Ambien (anti-anxiety and sedatives).","Schedule V — Very low: small amounts of narcotics or stimulants (cough suppressants with a little codeine, ephedrine products).","Over the counter (OTC): no prescription; the consumer must be able to diagnose the condition and monitor effectiveness easily (Benadryl for a known poison ivy reaction), with low side-effect and abuse risk. Some stay behind the counter: pse…","Dietary and herbal supplements (1994 Dietary Supplement Health and Education Act set purity standards) may claim only an effect on a body structure or function (\"emotional balance\" for St. John's wort, never \"treats depression\"); not evalua…","Ginkgo biloba suppresses platelet aggregation: increased bleeding risk with antiplatelets or anticoagulants.","Teratogens cause congenital malformations (alcohol, marijuana, nicotine).","The lecture calls ginkgo biloba \"a derivative of garlic\"; that detail is left out here.","A — No risk in human studies (first trimester).","B — No risk in animal studies; no adequate human studies.","C — Risk can't be ruled out; benefit may outweigh risk.","D — Human studies show some fetal risk; benefit may still outweigh risk.","X — Known significant risk; contraindicated in pregnancy.","Since 2015 the Pregnancy and Lactation Labeling Rule (PLLR) replaces letters with narrative sections (pregnancy incl. labor and delivery; lactation; females and males of reproductive potential), each with a risk summary, clinical considerat…","Pharmacogenomics: how genes affect drug response, to tailor regimens (good responders, poor responders, those with bad side effects). Already used for pain control, cancer (genotyping tumors) and psychiatric medications.","Pharm Principles: Routes & the Three Phases","Enteral = through the GI tract (PO, enteric-coated, sublingual, buccal, rectal). Parenteral = outside the GI tract, usually injection (intradermal like a TB skin test, subQ, IM, intra-arterial, intrathecal/epidural, intra-articular, IV). To…","Pharmaceutical phase (oral drugs only): disintegration (tablet breaks into particles) then dissolution (particles dissolve in GI fluid). Liquids are already dissolved and absorb fastest; enteric-coated tablets resist stomach acid and dissol…","Pharmacokinetic phase = what the body does to the drug: absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion.","Pharmacodynamic phase = what the drug does to the body (mechanism of action, therapeutic effect).","Pharm Principles: Absorption & Distribution","Cell membranes have a phospholipid (fat) bilayer: most drugs are lipid-soluble and cross easily; water-soluble drugs need a channel or transport mechanism.","IV is fastest (no barriers) and essentially irreversible once given.","Bioavailability = the percentage of a dose reaching systemic circulation in active form. IV = 100%. Oral is less because of the first-pass effect: drug absorbed from the intestine passes through the liver first, which inactivates a portion …","Distribution depends on blood flow: best to the heart, liver, kidneys and brain; slower to muscle, skin and fat. Poor circulation (peripheral vascular disease) or an abscess reduces drug delivery; draining pus improves antibiotic distributi…","Blood-brain barrier: tight capillary junctions; only lipid-soluble drugs or those with a transport system cross (alcohol, glucose). It protects the CNS and is not fully developed in infants.","Protein binding: drugs travel partly bound to plasma proteins (mainly albumin). Only free (unbound) drug acts; bound drug is a reversible store, so highly bound drugs last longer. Low albumin (malnutrition, liver disease, alcoholism) means …","Pharm Principles: Metabolism, Excretion & Half-Life","Metabolism (biotransformation), mainly in the liver: active lipid-soluble drug becomes an inactive water-soluble metabolite ready for excretion. Poor liver function means toxicity risk, so monitor liver function tests.","Excretion, mainly by the kidneys (glomerular filtration, tubular secretion, reabsorption). Penicillin G is about 90% excreted, so it must be given often. Monitor BUN, creatinine and GFR (the best measure of kidney function); rising values m…","Substrate — A drug metabolized by a CYP450 enzyme — Affected by inducers and inhibitors","Prodrug — A substrate converted from inactive to active by CYP450","Inducer (\"gas pedal\") — Increases enzyme activity — Faster metabolism: less drug, lower effect","Inhibitor — Decreases enzyme activity — Slower metabolism: drug builds up, toxicity risk","Grapefruit juice is a CYP450 inhibitor: don't take CYP450 substrates with it; avoid grapefruit for 2–4 hours after the dose. Some drugs are both inducers and inhibitors.","Half-life (t½) = time for serum concentration to drop 50% (100 mg/L to 50 mg/L in 8 hours = 8-hour half-life). About 5 half-lives eliminate about 97% (a 1-week half-life takes about 5 weeks to clear).","Steady state (amount eliminated = amount absorbed each dose) takes about 4–5 half-lives; it gives maximum therapeutic benefit. Half-life guides dosing intervals (QD, QID).","Around-the-clock dosing (e.g., morphine 10 mg every 3 hours with a 3-hour half-life) keeps about 50% concentration on board for chronic pain, with a PRN for breakthrough pain.","Onset = time to reach the minimum effective concentration; peak = time to maximum effect; duration = how long it works (determines how often to dose).","Pharm Principles: Pharmacodynamics & Drug Safety","Drugs can increase, decrease, replace, inhibit, destroy, protect or irritate. A bronchodilator's therapeutic effect is opening the airways; tachycardia and palpitations are side effects.","Receptors: reactive sites (usually proteins) on or in cells; a drug-receptor complex stimulates or inhibits.","Agonist: binds a receptor and initiates the effect. Antagonist: blocks natural substances from binding (diphenhydramine/Benadryl is an H1 antagonist that blocks histamine).","Receptor-less actions: antacids neutralize acid chemically; magnesium sulfate is an osmotic laxative.","Therapeutic index: ratio of toxic level to therapeutic level. Narrow (small window; warfarin, digoxin): more adverse reactions, monitor closely. Wide (amoxicillin): rarely toxic.","Black box warning: the FDA's strongest warning for a marketed drug, prominent on labels, ads and inserts (Celebrex: MI and thrombotic events in cardiovascular disease).","Medication errors cause about 1.3 million ED visits and about $3.5 billion in excess costs a year; the lecture calls them the third leading cause of death (CDC 2018). Safeguards: restrict high-alert drugs, tall man lettering (heParin), barc…","No trailing zeros (write 1 mg, not 1.0 mg); always use a leading zero (0.25 mg, not .25 mg).","High-alert medications (ISMP list, 1995): most likely to cause serious harm or death; extra precautions when prescribing, dispensing and administering.","Additive — Two drugs with similar mechanisms: 1 + 1 = 2 (two antibiotics for a complicated infection).","Synergism / potentiation — Different mechanisms, combined effect greater than either alone (Coumadin and aspirin).","Displacement — A second drug pushes the first off albumin, increasing the free drug's effect.","Antidote — Antagonizes a toxic drug (naloxone/Narcan for opioid overdose).","Decreased absorption — Bowel or stomach removed; activated charcoal given on purpose.","Enzyme induction — CYP450 inducers speed metabolism and clearance.","Drug-food / herb / disease — Grapefruit (CYP450 inhibitor); St. John's wort with Coumadin and oral contraceptives; liver or kidney disease alters processing.","Minimize interactions: limit the number of drugs (polypharmacy), take a thorough drug history (prescription, OTC, herbals), and monitor narrow-therapeutic-index drugs closely.","Older adults: slower hepatic metabolism, reduced circulation and distribution, decreased GI absorption, less complete renal excretion, so doses may need lowering or rescheduling. Common adverse reactions: depression, constipation, falls, im…"]},{"s":"Module 2: Hyperlipidemia & Hypertension▾","a":"module-2-hyperlipidemia-hypertension","k":["Module 2: Hyperlipidemia & Hypertension▾","Cholesterol, atherosclerosis, lipid drugs, RAAS and antihypertensives.","Hyperlipidemia: Cholesterol & Lipoproteins","We need cholesterol: it's part of every cell membrane and builds estrogen, testosterone, vitamin D, cortisol and most bile acid. It is highly insoluble, so it enters cells by binding surface receptors.","Only about a quarter comes from diet (exogenous); the 2015 USDA report says dietary cholesterol is not a nutrient of concern. About three quarters is made in the liver (endogenous) through the HMG-CoA reductase pathway.","More dietary cholesterol makes the liver produce less; more saturated fat lets the liver make more, raising cholesterol about 15–25%. So education targets unhealthy saturated and trans fats, not cholesterol itself.","Liver cells have LDL receptors; a negative feedback loop: when the liver isn't making cholesterol, it makes more LDL receptors and pulls more LDL from the blood.","HDL (high density) — Good: want it higher — More protein; lowers cardiovascular risk.","LDL (low density) — Bad: \"L for lousy, want it lower\" — Less protein, more cholesterol; raises atherosclerosis risk.","VLDL — Bad — Mostly triglycerides; high triglycerides suggest high VLDL and heart disease risk.","Total cholesterol — Below 200","HDL — Females above 55, males above 45; over 60 optimal","LDL — Below 130 (often below 100); below 70 for high-risk patients","Triglycerides — Less than 160","Hypercholesterolemia (hyperlipidemia, dyslipidemia) = too much cholesterol in the blood. High-lipid serum looks pinkish, opaque and cloudy. Draw the panel fasting overnight; screen routinely because there are few symptoms until it's severe.","Familial hypercholesterolemia: genetic defect in liver LDL receptors, so LDL stays high despite diet and exercise. Suspect it in young people with high cholesterol; inheriting it from both parents can cause heart disease and heart attacks i…","Risk factors: age and family history (can't change); tobacco, hypertension, diabetes, inactivity, poor diet (can change).","Hyperlipidemia: Atherosclerosis","Atherosclerosis = cholesterol buildup in vessels; arteriosclerosis = thickening and hardening of artery walls (often used interchangeably). Arteries matter most because they carry oxygenated blood; the coronary and peripheral arteries are t…","1. Endothelial injury — From smoking, chronic hemodynamic wall stress (hypertension) or hyperglycemia.","2. Increased permeability — LDL slips under the injured lining and triggers inflammation.","3. Foam cells — Macrophages engulf the lipids, becoming foam cells that form fatty streaks.","4. Plaque — A lipid core grows into the lumen and impedes flow. A large lipid core is prone to rupture; a clot then blocks the vessel (MI in the heart, stroke in the brain). Older plaques have more collagen and a stable cap.","Narrowed vessels can't dilate for more flow, so exercise brings chest pain. A blockage can reach 90% without symptoms (inactive person, small artery); at 99%, angina comes even walking from bed to chair: needs immediate intervention.","Only an invasive dye study under fluoroscopy shows the lumen. C-reactive protein (CRP) is a nonspecific blood marker of systemic inflammation released by the liver: elevated means higher risk, not a diagnosis of atherosclerosis.","CHD, CAD, ischemic heart disease and ASCVD all describe plaque in the coronary arteries limiting oxygen to the heart.","Lower the risk: decrease LDL, increase HDL, medication, the DASH diet (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, low-fat dairy; limit sugar-sweetened foods and drinks, red meat, added fats), weight control, exercise, and stop smoking (strongly tied…","Hyperlipidemia: Drug Therapy","Try diet and activity changes for 6 months first (unless there's already a heart attack, stroke or other organ damage); then medication.","Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors): atorvastatin (prototype), simvastatin, rosuvastatin — First line. Block liver cholesterol production, so the liver makes more LDL receptors and pulls LDL from the blood. Also reduce plaque inflammatio…","Statin adverse effects — Myopathy (ask about muscle weakness), rhabdomyolysis (muscle breakdown products can cause acute kidney failure), hepatotoxicity (monitor AST and ALT).","Statin teaching — About 2 weeks to see effect. Take with food if GI upset. Take at night (the liver makes more cholesterol at night): required for simvastatin and rosuvastatin (short half-lives); atorvastatin's longer half-life allows any t…","Ezetimibe (cholesterol absorption inhibitor) — The only one in its class; second line. Blocks the jejunum from absorbing dietary and bile cholesterol. Sometimes combined with a statin in one (expensive) pill for a greater LDL drop.","Before starting — Fasting lipid panel, AST/ALT baseline, CK (creatine kinase, muscle breakdown), and rule out secondary causes.","Other second-line drugs — Bile acid sequestrants, niacin, fibric acid derivatives, fish oil (OTC: ask about it). Not on the drug matrix.","Statins benefit: clinical ASCVD; LDL above 190; ages 40–75 with diabetes and LDL 70–189; ages 40–75 with LDL 70–189 and a 10-year ASCVD risk of 7.5% or higher (numbers not required).","Clinical ASCVD: acute coronary syndrome (unstable angina and MI: plaque rupture plus clot; MI = complete blockage with possible irreversible damage), history of MI, stable or unstable angina, coronary revascularization, stroke or TIA, perip…","Secondary causes of hyperlipidemia: diet; drugs (diuretics, glucocorticoids, cyclosporine, amiodarone); bile duct obstruction; hypothyroidism, obesity, pregnancy.","Hypertension: Pathophysiology","Trigger — Low blood volume, a drop in BP, or decreased renal perfusion.","1 — The kidney releases renin.","2 — Renin converts angiotensinogen (from the liver; \"-ogen\" = precursor) to angiotensin I.","3 — In the lungs, ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) converts it to angiotensin II, a potent vasoconstrictor.","4 — Angiotensin II makes the adrenal glands release aldosterone: the kidney reabsorbs sodium, and water follows (\"salt sucks\").","Endpoints — Sodium and water retention plus vasoconstriction = higher BP. RAAS regulates long-term BP and extracellular volume.","RAAS is normally appropriate, but it can be pathologic (hypersensitivity to angiotensin II, high renin secretors). Chronic stress stimulates renin and raises angiotensin II.","Arterial baroreceptors (carotid sinus, aorta, left ventricle) sense BP and change heart rate and vessel tone.","Vascular autoregulation keeps tissue perfusion steady by changing arteriole diameter (resistance) based on the mean arterial pressure (MAP), the number in parentheses on a BP reading.","Normal — Less than 120 — and — Less than 80","Elevated — 120–129 — and — Less than 80","Stage 1 hypertension — 130–139 — or — 80–89","Stage 2 hypertension — 140 or higher — or — 90 or higher","Hypertensive crisis — Higher than 180 — and/or — Higher than 120","Watch the and/or: 120/85 is stage 1, because stage 1 needs only one number. About 1 in 3 people in the US have hypertension; exams use these newer, more conservative guidelines.","Primary (essential) hypertension","90–95% of cases; no known underlying cause (often idiopathic): genetics interacting with environment.","Mechanisms: overactive SNS (higher heart rate, vasoconstriction), overactive RAAS (vessel remodeling and permanently higher peripheral vascular resistance, the target of most BP drugs), natriuretic peptide disruption; systemic inflammation …","Risk factors: smoking, excess sodium, sedentary lifestyle, hyperlipidemia, stress, family history, obesity, age (males more before 55, females more after 60), insulin resistance, high alcohol intake.","Secondary hypertension","A known cause: treat the underlying cause.","Renal disease, adrenal cortical tumors, adrenal medullary tumors (pheochromocytoma), pregnancy, hormonal therapy.","Drugs: oral contraceptives, corticosteroids, antihistamines, cocaine, amphetamines.","Usually no symptoms, the \"silent killer\": symptoms mean end organ damage (chest pain, headache, visual changes, extremity weakness or pain), so screen early.","Long-term: more left ventricle workload, left ventricular hypertrophy, higher myocardial oxygen demand; accelerated atherosclerosis; aortic aneurysm; a primary cause of end-stage renal disease; stroke, aneurysm and hemorrhage; retinopathy a…","Hypertensive urgency","BP 180 or above and/or 120 or above, no end organ damage. Treat with oral drugs, lowering BP gradually over hours to days. Causes: anxiety, pain, abrupt withdrawal of illicit drugs or certain antihypertensives.","Hypertensive emergency","Same BP plus end organ damage (headache, blurry vision, then heart dysfunction, stroke, brain hemorrhage, chest pain, ACS, unresponsiveness, seizures). IV drugs (usually labetalol) lower BP aggressively over minutes to hours: the organ dama…","Hypertension: Diuretics","Diuretics (\"water pills\") increase urine output, reduce circulating volume and cardiac output (heart rate x stroke volume) by blocking sodium and chloride reabsorption. Cheap, enhance other antihypertensives, often first line.","Thiazide: hydrochlorothiazide (HCTZ) — First line for mild hypertension. Distal convoluted tubule: blocks sodium, potassium and chloride reabsorption; also relaxes arterioles (lower PVR). PO; alone or combined. — Hypokalemia, orthostatic hy…","Loop: furosemide (Lasix) — Blocks sodium reabsorption in the loop of Henle: profound diuresis. PO or IV. Also for fluid volume overload (ICU); works even with renal disease. — Hypokalemia, low magnesium, sodium and chloride, dehydration, hy…","Potassium-sparing (aldosterone antagonist): spironolactone (Aldactone) — Blocks aldosterone: excretes sodium and water but keeps potassium. Mild; PO; added to other diuretics for more effect without hypokalemia. — Hyperkalemia; endocrine ef…","The lecture says thiazides cause \"orthostatic hypertension\" but describes a drop in BP on position change; the page lists it as orthostatic hypotension.","Hypertension: Sympatholytics","Blocking the sympathetic (\"fight or flight\") system reduces vasoconstriction and peripheral vascular resistance. Beta 1 = heart (\"one heart\"); beta 2 = lungs (\"two lungs\").","Beta blockers (\"-olol\"): metoprolol (selective beta 1), propranolol (nonselective beta 1 and 2), carvedilol (alpha and beta) — Decrease heart rate, force of contraction and some renin secretion (the lecture also says they increase nitric ox…","Beta blocker effects & nursing — Fatigue, lethargy, bradycardia, hypotension; mask hypoglycemia (no tachycardia), so caution in diabetes. Never stop abruptly (rebound hypertension, stroke, death): wean. Check BP and HR right before every do…","Centrally acting alpha-2 agonist: clonidine — Decreases sympathetic outflow: bradycardia, lower cardiac output, vasodilation. Not first line (many side effects); usually combined. Also for opioid withdrawal and ADHD. PO or transdermal patch…","Selective alpha-1 blocker: doxazosin — Blocks alpha-1: venous and arterial dilation, lower PVR. Not first line. Hypotension, dizziness. (Tamsulosin/Flomax is an alpha-1 blocker used for BPH, not BP.)","Hypertension: RAAS Blockers, Calcium Channel Blockers & Vasodilators","ACE inhibitors (\"-pril\"): captopril, lisinopril — Block ACE, so less angiotensin II (less vasoconstriction) and less aldosterone (less water retention). Safe, effective, first line; also heart failure. Slow left ventricular hypertrophy; ren…","ACE inhibitor adverse effects — First-dose hypotension (15–20% drop within 6–8 hours: take at night, not before driving). Dry, persistent, nonproductive cough (the most common reason to switch). Dizziness, rash. Angioedema (laryngeal swelli…","ARBs (\"-sartan\"): losartan — Block angiotensin II's action after it forms: vasodilation, more sodium and water excretion. Doesn't affect heart rate. Many uses (HTN, heart failure, diabetic neuropathy and retinopathy, after MI or stroke): as…","Renin inhibitor: aliskiren — Directly inhibits renin: vasodilation, less volume and SNS activity, inhibits cardiac and vascular hypertrophy. PO, well tolerated (some GI upset), no ACE cough, less angioedema. Watch hyperkalemia with an ACE i…","Calcium channel blockers: nifedipine, amlodipine, nicardipine, verapamil, diltiazem — Block calcium channels: dilate arterioles (arteries, not veins). Verapamil and diltiazem also slow heart rate and conduction, so they're used more for rhy…","Vasodilator: hydralazine — Relaxes arterial and venous smooth muscle: lower PVR. PO (often with other drugs) or IV (emergencies, or when PO isn't tolerated). Hypotension, dizziness, headache, tachycardia, edema, dyspnea, GI upset. With isos…"]},{"s":"Module 3: Diabetes, Obesity & Metabolic Syndrome▾","a":"module-3-diabetes-obesity-metabolic-syndrome","k":["Module 3: Diabetes, Obesity & Metabolic Syndrome▾","Diabetes patho, insulin, non-insulin drugs, obesity hormones.","Diabetes Pathophysiology","Diabetes affects about 12% of the US (2023), with about 11 million undiagnosed and about 115 million with prediabetes; the 7th leading cause of death. A metabolic disorder characterized by hyperglycemia from a problem with insulin secretion…","The liver stores glucose as glycogen (glycogenesis) and breaks it back down when glucose is low (glycogenolysis).","The pancreas is exocrine (amylase, lipase into the pancreatic duct) and endocrine. The islets of Langerhans are 1–2% of the pancreas but get about 20% of its blood flow. Alpha cells secrete glucagon (raises glucose via glycogenolysis); beta…","Counter-regulatory hormones raise glucose and defend against hypoglycemia: glucagon, epinephrine, glucocorticoids, growth hormone.","Insulin also inhibits fat breakdown, so insulin problems disturb both carbohydrate and fat metabolism.","Hyperglycemia — Glucose can't enter cells without insulin, so it stays in the blood and the cells starve.","Lipolysis (fat breakdown) — Free fatty acids for energy; the excess becomes cholesterol and phospholipids (atherosclerosis risk). Fatty acids → acetyl-CoA → acetoacetic acid in the liver → ketone bodies → ketosis, which can progress to seve…","Proteolysis (protein breakdown) — Protein synthesis stops; amino acids are used for energy: muscle wasting, amino acidemia, increased BUN, organ dysfunction. More typical of type 1.","Osmotic diuresis & dehydration — High glucose raises serum osmolality, pulling water out of cells (intracellular dehydration). Above the renal threshold (about 200 mg/dL) glucose spills into urine (glycosuria), dragging water and electrolyt…","The three P's: polyphagia (hunger from cellular starvation and fat/protein breakdown), polydipsia (thirst from dehydration and high osmolality), polyuria (osmotic diuresis).","Hemoglobin A1c (% of hemoglobin with glucose attached; 2–3 month average) — About 5–5.5% — 5.7–6.4% — 6.5% or above","Fasting plasma glucose (about 8 hours fasting) — 99 or below — 100–125 — 126 or above","Oral glucose tolerance test — Drink a glucose load, test at set times; mostly used for gestational diabetes.","Type 1","Most common pediatric chronic disease; diagnosed around age 12 (adult onset is often misdiagnosed as type 2).","Usually autoimmune (genetics plus environment): T cells destroy beta cells, which can't regenerate. 80–90% must be destroyed before insulin secretion falls.","Absolute insulin deficiency: no endogenous insulin. 5–10% of cases (formerly insulin-dependent or juvenile-onset).","Long subclinical period; then the three P's, more often weight loss.","Type 2","Genetic and environmental. Risk: age, obesity, high BP, poor diet, inactivity, family history, high cholesterol.","Insulin resistance (receptors lose sensitivity) plus some decreased secretion from exhausted beta cells; linked to long-term obesity.","Vague symptoms: recurrent infections, visual changes, slow wound healing, fatigue; the three P's are less common.","Compensatory hyperinsulinemia stimulates fat creation and blocks fat breakdown; the liver releases more glucose.","ADA: screen all adults 35 or older; earlier with risk factors.","DKA — Most common acute complication of type 1: severe hyperglycemia (400s–600s in this lecture), acidosis from ketones, ketonuria, can cause coma and death.","HHNS (hyperosmolar hyperglycemic syndrome) — Mainly type 2: extreme hyperglycemia (600s–800s), severe osmolality and fluid shifts, from a less profound insulin deficiency.","Hypoglycemia — Both types; usually below about 55–60, mostly from medications (too much insulin) or not eating. A person who usually runs about 250 may feel it at 120. Cold sweats, pallor, racing heart, hunger, restlessness, anxiety, up to …","Chronic complications come from poorly controlled diabetes (chronic hyperglycemia) and advanced glycation end products.","Microvascular (capillaries: eyes, kidneys, nerves) — Capillary membrane thickening, tissue hypoxemia and ischemia.","Diabetic neuropathy — The most common microvascular complication: ischemia, inflammation, demyelination; pain, paresthesia, loss of pain, temperature and vibration sense, weakness in the feet and legs. Unnoticed wounds plus poor healing lea…","Diabetic retinopathy — Leading cause of blindness worldwide; both types; more cataracts and glaucoma. Intensive glucose control delays it. Teach regular eye screening.","Diabetic nephropathy — Diabetes is the most common cause of CKD and end-stage renal disease. Glomerular basement membranes thicken; microalbuminuria is the first sign; then low protein, fluid overload, edema, hypertension. Control BP and gl…","Macrovascular (large and medium arteries) — Especially type 2: hypertension, accelerated atherosclerosis, CAD, MI, stroke, heart failure, peripheral artery disease; about a third of type 2 patients worldwide.","Infection — More common and more serious: immune dysfunction, decreased blood supply, more nutrients for pathogens, impaired senses (cystitis, skin and soft tissue, pneumonia, ear infections).","Diabetes Pharmacology: Insulin","Goal: prevent hyperglycemia and long-term micro- and macrovascular complications through tight glycemic control (glucose about 80–140, A1c below 6.5) and tight lipid control.","History (background only): before insulin, type 1 patients died within days to weeks or were put on starvation diets; Frederick Banting isolated \"isletin\" (insulin) from dogs; first human injection 1922; synthetic insulin 1963; first pen 19…","All type 1 diabetics need insulin; some type 2 can be managed with oral drugs. Insulin is all about timing.","Given subQ (back of arms, abdomen, thighs) with an insulin syringe or pen; ordered in units. Using a regular syringe is dangerous: 1 mL = 100 units. Only regular insulin can go IV (or IM).","Rapid-acting: lispro — 15 min — About 1 hr — 2–4 hr — Give with meals (when the tray is in front of them); given before a meal they don't eat, it causes hypoglycemia. Paired with intermediate or long-acting insulin.","Short-acting: regular — 30–60 min — 2–6 hr — 3–8 hr — Before meals; tube feedings; the only IV insulin (bolus or infusion, DKA, coma) and the insulin in pumps.","Intermediate: NPH (isophane suspension) — 2–4 hr — 4–10 hr — 10–20 hr — Cloudy: roll or agitate before drawing up. Often twice a day and mixed with regular to reduce injections. Draw clear before cloudy so the short-acting vial isn't contam…","Long-acting: glargine (Lantus) — About 70 min — No peak — 24 hr — Once a day, often at night; don't mix with other insulins.","Get a current blood sugar right before giving any insulin (fingerstick, FSBG), not one from an hour ago.","Insulin pumps: a basal infusion of regular insulin plus patient-calculated boluses for carbohydrates eaten; often paired with continuous glucose monitors.","Sliding scale: regular insulin before meals and at bedtime (AC and HS) dosed by the current glucose (e.g., 190 falls in the 150–200 row: 2 units), often with a basal long-acting dose. Use certified diabetes educators.","Hypoglycemia is the main complication: too much insulin, not eating, vomiting or diarrhea, alcohol (raises then drops glucose), new exercise, childbirth and postpartum. Signs: tachycardia, sweating, nervousness, headache, drowsiness, shakin…","Allergic reactions are rare. Lipodystrophy/lipoatrophy: hard, discolored, dimpled skin from repeated use of the same site.","Somogyi effect","Too much insulin at bedtime causes nocturnal hypoglycemia; counter-regulatory hormones and the liver's stored glucose cause rebound morning hyperglycemia (can reach ketosis). \"I wake up at 320 and haven't eaten.\" Review the night dose, basa…","Dawn phenomenon","Morning hyperglycemia from the natural early-morning release of cortisol and growth hormone; usually milder than Somogyi.","Teaching: monitor glucose as prescribed; carry 15–20 g of simple carbohydrate (4–6 oz fruit juice, a little regular soda, glucose tablets), not candy; rotate sites and avoid red or irritated ones; vials last about 28–30 days at room tempera…","Diabetes Pharmacology: Non-Insulin Drugs (Type 2)","Sulfonylureas: glipizide, glyburide (2nd generation) — Close potassium-ATP channels in beta cells, stimulating insulin secretion; increase insulin sensitivity; reduce liver glucose release. — The lecture calls them the first-line oral drugs…","Biguanide: metformin — Stops the liver converting glycogen to glucose; enhances muscle glucose uptake; doesn't release insulin, so no hypoglycemia in the traditional sense. — Often first line for type 2; also PCOS. Peaks in 2–4 weeks. GI ef…","DPP-4 inhibitors (\"-gliptin\"): linagliptin, saxagliptin, sitagliptin — Block DPP-4, which breaks down incretins (GLP-1, gastric inhibitory peptide), so incretins last longer: more insulin, less glucagon, less liver glucose, slower digestion…","GLP-1 receptor agonists (\"-tide\"): dulaglutide, exenatide, semaglutide — Stimulate GLP-1 (incretin) receptors: more insulin, less post-meal glucagon, slower gastric emptying, more fullness. Derived from Gila monster saliva. — Usually adjunc…","SGLT2 inhibitors (\"-flozin\"): dapagliflozin — Block kidney glucose reabsorption (SGLT2 handles about 90% of it), so glucose leaves in the urine. — UTIs and mycotic (yeast) infections, especially in females; hypotension, fainting, dizziness,…","Glucagon (hypoglycemia antidote) — Activates liver glucagon receptors: glycogenolysis releases glucose. — Oral option for an awake patient who can swallow (the lecture lists an oral tablet); IM, IV or subQ (and a new inhaled form) for decre…","The lecture says sulfonylureas stop liver gluconeogenesis, then explains they stimulate insulin release by closing beta-cell potassium-ATP channels; both are listed as stated. It also describes glucagon as an oral tablet, which may refer to…","Obesity & Metabolic Syndrome","74% of US adults (20+) are overweight or obese; 42% obese, 10% severely obese; highest prevalence at ages 40–59. Obesity is the 5th leading cause of death in the US and a risk factor for type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, cancer, hype…","Adipose tissue insulates, supports, stores energy as triglycerides in adipocytes (which can increase in number and hypertrophy) and acts as an endocrine organ secreting adipokines.","Subcutaneous (peripheral) fat","Just under the skin (pinchable); less inflammatory, the healthier storage.","Visceral fat","Around abdominal organs (e.g., the omentum); more hormonally active: impaired lipid and glucose metabolism, insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome, cancer and cardiovascular risk.","Android (apple) obesity, more common in males, means more visceral fat: higher risk of CAD, hypertension, stroke and diabetes. Gynoid (pear), more common in females, carries less of that risk.","Waist circumference is a cardiovascular risk factor: women over 35 inches, men over 40 inches.","Adiponectin (\"good\") — Inverse relationship with fat: falls as fat increases (especially visceral), with eventual resistance. Increases energy expenditure, insulin-sensitizing, anti-inflammatory, protects against arteriosclerosis. Low level…","Leptin (\"good\") — Made by adipocytes; rises with fat. Signals satiety: suppresses appetite and raises energy expenditure after eating. High levels in obesity cause leptin resistance: overeating, weight gain, hyperglycemia, hyperinsulinemia,…","Others — Angiopoietin-related protein (inflammation, insulin resistance), angiotensinogen (BP, inflammation, insulin resistance, lipogenesis), retinol binding protein (insulin resistance), IL-6 and TNF-alpha (systemic inflammation). Adipoki…","Ghrelin — From the stomach; rises with fasting. The lecture says it promotes satiety and is cardioprotective, and that it is decreased in obesity with ghrelin resistance.","GLP-1 — From intestinal endocrine cells; involved in glucose and glucagon; decreased in obesity; GLP-1 agonists are used for weight loss.","Peptide YY — From the intestine; decreases appetite and increases gastric motility; decreased in obesity.","Cholecystokinin (CCK) — Gallbladder contraction, pancreatic enzymes, satiety, slower gastric emptying; usually decreased in obesity (some newer studies say increased).","Obesity is a chronic low-grade inflammatory state: immune cells infiltrate adipocytes and release cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-6), leading to insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome.","Polygenic plus environmental (nature and nurture). Leptin gene mutations; secondary causes: Cushing syndrome, PCOS, hypothyroidism; obesogens (endocrine-disrupting chemicals).","Risk factors: calories in exceeding calories out, sedentary lifestyle, age, low socioeconomic status, culture, obesogens, genetics, secondary disorders, smoking cessation (weight gain).","Obesity = BMI over 30; in children, at or above the 95th percentile for age and sex. BMI = kg / m², or lb / in² × 703 (know both; a calculator is allowed). BMI misleads in muscular people; ideal body fat: women 30% or less, men 25% or less.","Orlistat (OTC) — Binds gastric and pancreatic enzymes (lipases), cutting fat absorption up to 30%. Black box: liver injury. GI effects: oily spotting, flatulence, fecal incontinence (keep dietary fat below 30% of intake). Lowers vitamins A,…","All weight-loss drugs — Minimal effect without diet and exercise. Reserved for BMI over 30, or over 27 with risk factors, usually after 6 months of failed diet and exercise. Long-term treatment: weight returns when stopped. Other FDA-approv…","Bariatric surgery — Liposuction, gastric bypass, gastric banding: serious and permanent; weigh risks and benefits.","Metabolic syndrome = a cluster of risk factors that greatly raises stroke and cardiovascular risk; diagnosed with 3 or more criteria (e.g., a man with waist over 40 inches, high triglycerides and hypertension). Exam values will be obviously…","Treat each condition (BP drugs, antilipemics, diabetes management) and counsel on weight loss, exercise, healthy diet and smoking cessation, in small incremental steps. Remember the social determinants of health; avoid snap judgments."]},{"s":"Module 4: Dementia, Pain & Seizures▾","a":"module-4-dementia-pain-seizures","k":["Module 4: Dementia, Pain & Seizures▾","Delirium and dementia, pain patho, non-opioids, opioids, seizures and AEDs.","Delirium & Dementia","Delirium (acute confusional state, organic brain syndrome) is a transient disorder of cognition, consciousness or perception, with sudden or gradual onset, usually tied to another condition: drug or alcohol intoxication or withdrawal, hypog…","Hyperactive delirium","Develops over 2–3 days; ICU, post-surgery, withdrawal, hospitalized elderly; autonomic nervous system overactivity.","Risk: benzodiazepines, narcotics, infection or sepsis, surgery, hypoxia, electrolyte or metabolic dysfunction, insomnia.","Restless, irritable, poor concentration, insomnia, shaking, poor appetite; then hallucinations, inattention, altered perception; dilated pupils, high heart rate, diaphoresis, fever.","Unresolved, it can become excited delirium syndrome (combative, aggressive, rapid breathing): seizures and death.","Usually resolves in 2–3 days; can persist weeks if risk factors remain.","Hypoactive delirium","Linked to right-sided frontal basal ganglia disruption (coordinated movement and alertness).","More common with metabolic disorders (liver or kidney failure: metabolite buildup).","Decreased alertness and attention, can't perceive or interpret surroundings, forgetful, apathetic, slow speech, falls asleep often.","Treatment: identify and remove the cause and risk factors (lowest opioid dose for the shortest time, especially in the elderly; dialysis for kidney failure; stop offending drugs). Prevent it: avoid CNS-altering drugs, let patients sleep (da…","Antipsychotics (haloperidol/Haldol, quetiapine/Seroquel; IV or PO) only short term, when there's danger to the patient or staff. Symptoms that persist after the acute situation resolves need evaluation for another condition.","People with dementia are at higher risk of delirium, but they're different conditions: delirium is acute and transient; dementia is chronic and irreversible. Routines are crucial for dementia patients.","Dementia: acquired, progressive failure of cerebral function (memory, language, judgment, orientation, decision-making). Patients usually aren't aware; family notices. Patients show agitation, wandering, frustration, aggression. Causes: neu…","Alzheimer's disease — Leading cause of severe cognitive dysfunction in the elderly; about 6 million in the US, two-thirds female. Early-onset has a genetic link; sporadic late-onset is more common. Diagnosed by ruling out other causes (tumo…","Alzheimer's pathophysiology — Neuritic (amyloid) plaques and neurofibrillary tangles of tau protein in the cerebral cortex and hippocampus (memory); loss of synapses, acetylcholine and other neurotransmitters; brain atrophy. Changes start d…","Vascular dementia — Second most common; cerebrovascular disease (large artery disease, cardioembolism, small vessel disease, stroke) causing brain hypoperfusion. Risk: diabetes, hyperlipidemia, hypertension, smoking; treat by preventing ris…","Frontotemporal dementia (Pick disease) — Rare; familial when onset is before 60; mutations in genes encoding tau. Behavioral variant (personality, judgment), progressive nonfluent (language, writing), semantic (forming words and sentences).…","Lewy body dementia — Covered in a later course.","Donepezil (cholinesterase inhibitor) — Inhibits acetylcholinesterase to raise acetylcholine and improve cognition; mild to moderate Alzheimer's. Usually mild side effects: GI (ulcer and GI bleed risk from more secretions: avoid NSAIDs), bra…","Memantine (NMDA receptor blocker) — Blocks excess glutamate at NMDA receptors, reducing calcium influx and neuronal damage; moderate to severe Alzheimer's; can be combined with donepezil; the only NMDA blocker. Confusion, hypotension, heada…","Neither drug cures Alzheimer's; they slow progression for a time and are expensive.","The lecture calls memantine an NMDA receptor \"agonist\" but describes it blocking the receptor; the page follows the description.","Pain Pathophysiology","Pain is whatever the patient says it is: shaped by physical, cognitive, spiritual, emotional, environmental factors, past experiences and genetics.","Acute pain is protective (pulling away from a hot stove; resting a hurt ankle). Chronic pain lasts months to years and isn't protective.","Neuroanatomy: afferent pathways (peripheral nerves to the spinal cord and up to the cortex), interpretive centers (brainstem, midbrain, cerebral cortex recognize \"pain\"), efferent pathways (messages back down that produce the response). The…","Nociceptors = free nerve endings (pain receptors) activated above a threshold by mechanical, thermal (heat, cold) or chemical (acids, inflammatory chemicals) stimuli. Many in skin, dental pulp, meninges and some organs; few in the brain, al…","Neurotransmitters are excitatory (enhance pain, sensitize nociceptors) or inhibitory (GABA is the primary inhibitor). Endorphins (\"endogenous morphine\") are natural opioids that inhibit pain; morphine mimics them.","1. Transduction — A painful stimulus becomes an action potential at the receptor. Injury releases chemical mediators; prostaglandins lower the pain threshold.","2. Transmission — The impulse travels along A-delta and C fibers to the dorsal horn of the spinal cord and up to the thalamus.","3. Perception — The somatosensory cortex interprets location and intensity; conscious awareness in the limbic and reticular systems, shaped by attention, distraction, anxiety, fear, fatigue, past experience, genetics, age, culture.","4. Modulation — Transmission is amplified or dampened before, during and after perception; endorphins bind opioid receptors and close spinal cord \"gates\".","A-delta fibers","Small (not the smallest); sharp, well-localized, \"fast\" pain (stinging, cutting, pinching); can trigger a spinal withdrawal reflex before pain is perceived (curling iron burn, needle stick).","C fibers","Smallest and most numerous (muscles, tendons, organs, skin); dull, aching, burning, poorly localized, constant pain.","Pain threshold = the lowest intensity recognized as pain. Perceptual dominance: intense pain in one spot raises the threshold elsewhere (an acute knee masks back pain). Stress, exertion, acupuncture and sexual activity raise the threshold.","Pain tolerance = the most pain a person can handle; it varies. Decreased by repeated pain, fatigue, anger, boredom, apprehension, sleep deprivation; increased by alcohol, persistent opioid use, hypnosis, distraction, strong faith.","Opioid tolerance: receptor changes after ongoing opioid exposure; doesn't necessarily mean addiction.","Gate control theory: stimulating large A-alpha and A-beta fibers (touch, massage, distraction, activity) closes the gate to small-fiber pain signals (why you grab a stubbed toe).","Inflammation: injury activates histamine and arachidonic acid metabolites (prostaglandins, leukotrienes). Prostaglandins promote inflammation, pain and fever, but also protect the stomach lining, activate platelets (clotting) and dilate ren…","Acute (nociceptive) — Sudden, protective, resolves with healing; seconds up to 3 months (a pulled tooth). Autonomic signs: increased HR and BP, sweating, anxiety, dilated pupils.","Chronic — Lasts beyond 3–6 months, serves no purpose, often out of proportion to visible damage; ongoing or intermittent (migraines); from nervous system sensitization. No autonomic vital-sign changes, but major psychosocial and emotional p…","Cutaneous/somatic (nociceptive) — Skin, joints, tendons, musculoskeletal: constant aches; A-delta (sharp, localized) or C fibers (dull, as with a broken bone).","Visceral (nociceptive) — Organs; C fibers, poorly localized, deep and diffuse; cramping, splitting, nausea, vomiting, diaphoresis; radiating or referred.","Neuropathic — Lesion or injury of the nerves (peripheral, spinal cord, brain); more often chronic; shooting, burning, electric-shock, numbness, motor weakness; often doesn't respond to usual pain meds. Diabetic neuropathy, postherpetic neur…","Referred — Felt away from the source because the brain can't distinguish sources: neck, jaw, shoulder or arm pain with an MI.","Phantom — From an amputated part; most intense right after amputation, can continue but usually resolves; real abnormal signals in the spinal cord and brain, not psychological.","Non-Opioids & Adjuvants","Tramadol (Ultram), miscellaneous analgesic — Binds weakly to mu opioid receptors and inhibits norepinephrine and serotonin reuptake; moderate to severe pain; PO only (also combined with Tylenol or extended release). Mild side effects: drows…","Gabapentin (Neurontin), pregabalin (Lyrica) — Anticonvulsants used for neuropathic pain; thought to suppress neuronal firing. Enhance opioid analgesia, manage symptoms that worsen pain, treat some opioid side effects. Drowsiness, dizziness,…","Non-opioid analgesics are the most prescribed (many OTC): analgesic, anti-inflammatory and antipyretic. NSAIDs treat mild to moderate pain (headache, myalgia, arthritis, post-op).","NSAIDs are anti-prostaglandins: they block cyclooxygenase (COX). COX-1 makes protective prostaglandins (intact GI mucosa) and thromboxane (platelet aggregation). COX-2 makes prostaglandins for inflammation and fever.","Aspirin is unique: an irreversible inhibitor of platelet COX-1, cutting thromboxane, so it's antiplatelet; other NSAIDs lack this and are reversible.","Aspirin — Non-selective (irreversible) — With caffeine = Excedrin; enteric-coated: don't crush. Most antithrombotic. Exempt from the cardiovascular black box.","Ibuprofen — Non-selective — Easiest on the stomach; most commonly used.","Naproxen — Non-selective — Hardest on the kidneys; lasts longer (Aleve once a day).","Ketorolac (Toradol) — Non-selective — Most potent NSAID; IV or IM (rarely PO); acute moderate to severe (post-op) pain, analgesia comparable to morphine without addiction or respiratory depression (good for opioid-addicted patients or those…","Celecoxib (Celebrex) — COX-2 selective — Little COX-1 effect: GI mucosa and platelets spared (about 87% less ulcer risk long term, but not zero). Downside: more cardiovascular thrombotic events (MI, stroke).","Non-selective NSAID effects: analgesia, antipyretic, antithrombotic, but also GI upset, ulcers, GI bleeding (COX-1) and kidney failure (renal function depends on prostaglandins; high risk with renal disease, acute illness, high doses).","Since 2006, two black box warnings on NSAIDs: cardiovascular (not aspirin; avoid NSAIDs after stroke or MI) and GI bleeding/ulceration (can be fatal; especially elderly and long-term users).","Salicylate toxicity (aspirin): acute = nausea, vomiting, seizures, cerebral edema; chronic = nausea, vomiting, tinnitus, hearing loss.","Reye's syndrome: no aspirin for children, especially under 15 with a viral (flu-like) illness: severe liver and brain swelling, high mortality. Use Tylenol or ibuprofen.","Action — Not an NSAID; mechanism largely unknown (thought to decrease CNS prostaglandins). Analgesic and antipyretic (acts on the hypothalamus), no anti-inflammatory effect. Ceiling effect about 1,000 mg.","Good choice when — On anticoagulants, GI upset with NSAIDs, children with viral illness (no Reye's).","Toxicity — Large amounts: hepatic necrosis (acute), liver failure (chronic), nephropathy. Watch jaundice, elevated LFTs, creatinine.","Daily limit — 4 g per 24 hours in adults, counting combination products (Lortab, Norco = hydrocodone; Percocet = oxycodone). 2 g/day with liver disease or chronic alcohol use. Expect a calculation question.","Overdose — A toxic metabolite is normally detoxified by liver glutathione; overdose depletes it, causing liver necrosis. Antidote acetylcysteine (Mucomyst) for acute overdose, PO (terrible odor) or IV (better tolerated). Chronic damage can'…","IV acetaminophen — Acute and post-op pain with less opioid use and fewer side effects (paralytic ileus, respiratory depression); often first dose at incision or pre-op; useful in substance use disorder. Counts toward the daily total.","Pearls: alternate NSAIDs and acetaminophen (different mechanisms); combine with opioids for moderate to severe pain, but don't add Tylenol to Percocet without counting; if inflammation causes the pain, choose an NSAID.","Opioids","Pain ladder: non-opioid ± adjuvant → add an opioid as pain persists → opioid ± non-opioid ± adjuvant for moderate to severe pain.","All opioids are high-alert drugs. Originally from the opium poppy; now mostly synthetic. Three chemical classes: morphine-like, meperidine-like, methadone-like: an allergy to one class may not apply to another.","Assess LOC, BP, pulse and respirations before and periodically after every dose.","Respiratory depression is the biggest concern (the cause of overdose death). If RR is below 10, assess sedation and use physical stimulation if needed; if the patient is depressed or needs significant stimulation to rouse, reduce the next d…","Morphine (MS Contin) — Prototype; mu agonist mimicking endorphins; Schedule II; PO, IV, epidural, rectal; moderate to severe pain. MS Contin = extended release (12 or 24 hr) for chronic pain, not for opioid-naive patients. Interacts with al…","Hydromorphone (Dilaudid) — Semi-synthetic; Schedule II; IV or PO; severe pain. 7 times more potent than morphine (1 mg IV = 7 mg IV morphine); doses about 0.2–1 mg; patients often ask for it.","Fentanyl — Fully synthetic, extremely potent (0.1 mg IV = 10 mg IV morphine; dosed in micrograms, e.g., 25 mcg). Severe pain, surgical induction, chronic pain. PO, IV, IM, transdermal patch (chronic and cancer pain; not opioid-naive; change…","Meperidine (Demerol) — Synthetic; Schedule II; rarely used (ED migraine, post-op shivering). Weaker and shorter than morphine; less respiratory depression; PO less effective than IV; many interactions. A toxic metabolite accumulates with re…","Codeine — Schedule II alone; Schedule III with acetaminophen (Tylenol #3). Mild to moderate pain but mostly an antitussive; PO only, usually combined. Not under 18 (life-threatening breathing problems). Biggest effect: nausea/GI upset (expe…","Oxycodone — Schedule II; semi-synthetic codeine derivative; PO only; moderate to severe pain; the most used opioid on UK med-surg floors. 10 times more potent than codeine; high abuse potential (often the first opioid in opioid use disorder…","Hydrocodone — Mild to moderate pain and cough; about 6 times more potent than codeine (less than oxycodone); usually PO and combined: with acetaminophen = Lortab, Norco, Vicodin.","Methadone — Fully synthetic; Schedule II; opioid of choice for detox and opioid addiction. Long half-life (every 12–24 hours). Less euphoria, still addictive; PO.","Naloxone (Narcan) — Opioid antagonist: occupies mu receptors without activating them. No analgesia, no respiratory depression. For opioid-induced respiratory depression and suspected overdose (including heroin). Onset under 2 minutes; IV, I…","Opioid-naive patients (never or rarely used opioids; often the elderly and young) have exaggerated responses: know their history and baseline neuro status, start with the lowest dose (unless clearly severe pain; sliding-scale order sets), a…","Treat side effects proactively: constipation (stool softeners, hydration, nutrition; untreated can become paralytic ileus, especially post-op); nausea and vomiting (antiemetics).","Decide collaboratively: respect patients who decline opioids (history or family history of substance use disorder, fear, dislike), educate, and use adjuvants.","Seizures","Seizure = brief abnormal electrical activity in brain neurons (motor, sensory or cognitive). Convulsion = a more severe seizure with involuntary spasmodic muscle contractions. Epilepsy = chronic recurrent seizures. Myoclonic = brief shock-l…","The seizure focus is a group of neurons firing abnormally and autonomously (often with scar tissue, gliosis); it can recruit nearby neurons so the seizure spreads locally or globally.","Primary (idiopathic, about 50%, genetic predisposition) = epilepsy. Secondary = a known cause: blood sugar and chemical imbalances, drugs, fever (febrile seizures in children), TBI, stroke, meningitis, tumors.","Epilepsy is a disease of recurrent seizures with no reversible metabolic cause, requiring more than one seizure (\"an electrical storm\"). EEG shows abnormal activity (nurses needn't read it).","Seizure threshold = likelihood of seizing; higher is safer. Raised by sleep and taking AEDs as prescribed. Lowered by bupropion, some antibiotics, tramadol, some anesthetic gases, alcohol, menstruation, missed medication, stress, illness.","Onset in one area — Partial — Focal","Onset across both hemispheres — Generalized (grand mal) — Generalized","Awake during the seizure — Simple — Aware","Awareness impaired — Complex — Impaired awareness","Spreads to both sides — Secondary generalization — Focal to bilateral tonic-clonic","Generalized onset: tonic-clonic (tonic = prolonged contraction, often a cry; clonic = alternating contraction and relaxation, jerking limbs) and absence (brief loss of awareness with repetitive eye blinking, about 30 seconds, usually childh…","Phases: prodromal and aura (a sense of an impending seizure: jerking, headache, lethargy, palpitations, mood change, odors, tastes, sounds; helps locate the focus), ictal (the seizure), postictal (recovery).","Status epilepticus: repeated seizures without postictal recovery for 30 minutes or more, especially generalized tonic-clonic: respiratory arrest, hypoxia, brain damage, death. Focal seizures can progress to generalized. Time every seizure.","Antiepileptic Drugs (AEDs)","Goal: control or prevent seizures while maintaining quality of life (risk vs benefit). 70% become seizure-free on one drug; 30% need several.","Usually lifelong; may be tapered only under medical supervision after 1–2 seizure-free years. Never stop abruptly: it causes seizures.","Not usually started after one seizure (about a 40% chance it won't recur). Chosen by efficacy, side effects, interactions, cost and access (social determinants: stopping because of cost causes seizures).","Monotherapy first, slowly titrated; switch drugs rather than pile them on. Almost all need therapeutic drug monitoring (serum levels guide dosing) and frank conversations about taking them every day.","Traditional: barbiturates, hydantoins, iminostilbenes, valproic acid. Second and third generation: fewer side effects and interactions (good for older adults on many drugs), but many neurologists still use traditional drugs.","AEDs raise the seizure threshold, limit spread and slow nerve impulses; many raise GABA (low GABA is associated with seizures).","Black box for all AEDs: suicidal ideation, worsening depression, unusual mood changes: educate and give follow-up steps. AEDs are teratogenic: pregnant patients need close OB and neurology care. Common: dizziness, drowsiness (CNS depression…","Phenytoin (Dilantin), hydantoin — First line for tonic-clonic and focal seizures; PO (IV emergently). Lethargy, abnormal movements, confusion, cognitive changes; gingival hyperplasia (prevent with very good dental care: a favorite NCLEX que…","Valproic acid — Works for every seizure type (generalized, absence, myoclonic, tonic-clonic, partial); also bipolar disorder. PO or IV; delayed-release sprinkles for kids. Highly protein bound. Contraindicated in liver disease and urea cycl…","Topiramate (Topamax) — Adjunct for partial and secondary generalized/tonic-clonic seizures; mechanism unclear (structurally like fructose); PO. CNS depression, GI upset; monitor vision (angle-closure glaucoma); interacts with contraceptives…","Levetiracetam (Keppra) — Adjunct for partial seizures with or without generalization; mechanism unknown; well tolerated; PO or IV; used mostly in the hospital.","Most seizures stop on their own in about 2 minutes. Hospitalized patients with seizures need IV access. Gold standard: push IV benzodiazepines (rectal diazepam outside the hospital), then perhaps a Keppra load.","Airway is the priority; keep the head away from side rails; check blood sugar (hypoglycemia is a common cause)."]},{"s":"Module 5: Fluids, Skin & Blood Cells▾","a":"module-5-fluids-skin-blood-cells","k":["Module 5: Fluids, Skin & Blood Cells▾","Fluids and electrolytes, skin disorders, antifungals, CBC and WBC disorders.","Fluids","Body fluid (water with glucose, electrolytes, proteins) transports nutrients and wastes, dissolves electrolytes, and supports temperature, digestion, elimination, acid-base balance and lubrication. About 50–60% of adult body weight is water…","Osmosis: water moves across a semipermeable membrane from low to high solute concentration until balanced (or hydrostatic pressure opposes it). Diffusion: solute molecules move from high to low concentration; volume stays the same.","Colloids (mainly albumin, also globulin, fibrinogen) create oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure, pulling fluid from the interstitial space into the blood. Total protein measures it (not protein nutrition); it falls with age and malnutrition.","Hydrostatic pressure (from blood pressure, at the capillary) pushes fluid out of vessels into tissue, delivering nutrients. Oncotic pressure (from plasma proteins) holds fluid in and pulls water back, removing wastes.","Electrolytes","Electrolytes are charged in solution; they affect fluid and acid-base balance, nerve impulses, muscle contraction and heart rhythm, and they influence each other (electroneutrality). Levels depend on intake, absorption, distribution and exc…","Intracellular: potassium, magnesium, phosphorus. Extracellular: sodium, chloride, bicarbonate, calcium. Cations (+): sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium. Anions (−): phosphorus, chloride, bicarbonate. Replace losses with similar fluids an…","Reference ranges are provided on exams (values given will be extreme), but know them well.","Sodium — 136–145 — Brain (water follows sodium)","Potassium — 3.5–5.0 — Heart","Magnesium — 1.3–2.1 — Muscle and movement","Calcium — 9.0–10.5 — Neuromuscular (and bone)","Phosphorus — 3.0–4.5 — Inverse of calcium","Hyponatremia (below 136) — GI losses (diarrhea, vomiting, fistulas, NG suction), renal losses (adrenal insufficiency, diuretics), skin losses (burns, wounds), fasting, polydipsia (water intoxication), excess hypotonic fluid. — Water enters …","Hypernatremia (above 145) — Not diet: sodium-containing IV fluids, high-sodium tube feeds, saltwater near-drowning; too little water or too much water loss (cognitively impaired, severe diarrhea, high fever, heat stroke, profound diuresis).…","Hypokalemia (below 3.5) — Renal or GI losses (diarrhea, vomiting, ileostomy drainage, diuresis/furosemide), alkalosis shifts; rarely diet. — Cardiac rhythm disturbances (can be lethal), weakness, leg cramps, decreased bowel motility (consti…","Hyperkalemia (above 5.0) — Decreased output (renal failure, no urine); massive cell injury (burns, crush, sepsis); drugs: potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, NSAIDs. — Peaked T waves (not required), weakness, cramps, abdomina…","Hypomagnesemia (below 1.3) — Diuresis, GI and renal losses, limited intake (starving, fasting), alcohol abuse, pancreatitis, hyperglycemia. — Overactive muscles: hyperactive reflexes, confusion, cramps, tremors, seizures, nystagmus. — Magne…","Hypermagnesemia (above 2.1) — Increased intake with renal failure (milk of magnesia with chronic renal failure in the elderly); OB magnesium for preeclampsia. — Lethargy, floppiness, weakness, decreased reflexes, flushing; very high: low pu…","Hypocalcemia (below 9.0) — Can't mobilize bone calcium (hypoparathyroidism, hypomagnesemia), renal loss, increased binding, low intake or vitamin D, acute pancreatitis, thyroid or parathyroid surgery (sudden drop: monitor closely). — Increa…","Hypercalcemia (above 10.5) — Hyperparathyroidism, cancers (lung, breast, hematologic), immobility, antacid (Tums) overdose. — Calcium acts like a sedative: fatigue, lethargy, confusion, weakness, seizures, coma; chronic: kidney stones. — Hy…","Hypophosphatemia (below 3.0) — Decreased absorption, antacid overdose, severe diarrhea, increased renal loss, malnutrition (alcoholism, TPN), refeeding syndrome (reintroduce nutrition slowly; critically low phosphorus is an early sign). — M…","Hyperphosphatemia (above 4.5) — Kidney failure, phosphate laxatives or enemas, massive trauma or heat stroke (shift out of cells).","Potassium: 98% intracellular, held by an ATP-driven active transport pump; mostly from diet, mostly lost through the kidneys; regulates cardiac and muscle cell excitability. Sodium governs osmolality and water distribution, aids acid-base b…","Sodium bicarbonate — Provides bicarbonate (raises blood and urine pH) and sodium; for metabolic acidosis and long-term sodium deficiency (IV form not used for hyponatremia). Vesicant at high concentration (central line). Edema, cerebral hem…","Potassium chloride (KCl) — Replaces potassium when diet isn't enough. PO: dilute in water or juice (tastes awful; GI distress, ulcers, bleeding); large pills. IV: always diluted, NEVER IV push, never faster even in emergencies: max about 10…","Sodium polystyrene sulfonate (Kayexalate) — Cation exchange resin: swaps sodium for potassium in the GI tract; lowers potassium about 0.5–1 mEq/L in 4–6 hours. PO or rectal. Only with normal bowel function (not constipation or ileus). Const…","D50 + regular insulin — Urgent hyperkalemia (dysrhythmias): insulin shifts potassium into cells (temporary); D50 prevents hypoglycemia. Usually 10 units regular insulin and 1 ampule D50; consider less insulin if glucose is trending low or 1…","Magnesium sulfate / oxide — Replaces magnesium (IV for very low levels); also constipation, preeclampsia seizure prevention, cardiac rhythm disturbances; magnesium oxide as an antacid and for long-term low magnesium. IV can burn. Overcorrec…","The transcript says dilutional hyponatremia shows \"hypovolemia,\" but describes high BP, weight gain and a bounding pulse from too much fluid; the page lists it as fluid overload.","Skin Disorders: Fungal Infections","Fungi are spore producers that live everywhere. They cause problems through implantation (into an injury), inhalation, or after antibiotics wipe out normal flora. More common in the elderly, the immunocompromised (transplant, chemo) and pat…","Superficial infections affect only surface layers. Dermatophytes cause tinea; Candida albicans (yeast) causes candidiasis: the two most common. Dermatophytes love keratinized tissue (hair, skin, nails) and warm, dark, moist areas; their enz…","Tinea pedis (athlete's foot) — Between the toes most often (also soles, nails): dry, scaling, itchy, macerated, painful skin, fissures. Spread in locker rooms and dorms: shower shoes, clean tubs. Diagnosed by culture and microscopic skin sc…","Tinea capitis — Scalp, eyebrows, eyelashes: scaly red lesions and hair loss (sometimes permanent alopecia). The most common pediatric dermatophyte (the lecture calls it \"cradle cap\"). Oral antifungals twice daily for 4–6 weeks: topicals don…","Tinea versicolor — Overgrowth of yeast normally on the skin: upper back, chest, arms. Risk: hot climate, sweating, oily skin, weak immunity. Not contagious. Bleached-looking white, pink, red or brown patches. Topical antifungals, Selsun Blu…","Candidiasis (thrush, yeast infection) — Candida albicans normally lives on mucous membranes, skin, GI and genital tract; becomes a pathogen with immunosuppression (transplant, HIV, chemo), antibiotics (stronger = more likely), moisture and …","Systemic fungal infections — Internal organs (intestines, urinary tract, lungs, brain); aggressive PO or IV antifungals. Lungs: histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, pneumocystis; lungs and meninges: cryptococcosis.","Skin Disorders: Pigment, Viral & Bacterial","Melasma: dark macules on the cheeks, more in women, pregnancy (\"mask of pregnancy\") and oral contraceptives; avoid sun, bleaching creams, retinoids (tretinoin, Retin-A). A cosmetic nuisance.","Vitiligo: acquired loss of melanin in patches, usually by age 21, can be sudden; possibly linked to autoimmune disorders (pernicious anemia, thyroid disease, diabetes); no treatment.","Herpes zoster (shingles) — Varicella zoster (chickenpox) virus lies dormant in a dermatome and reactivates with immunosuppression, stress or illness (usually over 50). Prodrome of burning and tingling along a dermatome, then vesicles on a r…","Impetigo — Staph and strep carried in the nose; acute, contagious, spreads in daycares and schools. Vesicles and pustules with a honey-colored crust on a red base. Topical antibacterial (Bactroban).","Abscess — Red, raised, tender collection of pus with palpable borders; fluctuant, may drain purulent fluid. Incision and drainage (I&D) plus PO or IV antibiotics; drainage may be cultured.","Furuncle / carbuncle — Furuncle: bacterial infection of one hair follicle (think ingrown hair). Carbuncle: deeper, more painful, several follicles. I&D and antibiotics.","Cellulitis — Staph or strep infection spreading from a wound (animal or insect bite) into surrounding tissue, usually lower extremities: red, swollen, warm, tender, blisters if severe. Not contagious, but untreated it can reach lymph nodes …","MRSA — Staph resistant to methicillin and the penicillin family. Hospital-acquired (surgery, IV tubing, central lines, artificial joints) or community-acquired (starts as a painful boil; wrestlers, childcare workers, crowded living). Warm s…","Skin Disorders: Skin Cancer & Chronic Conditions","Precancerous (benign, sun damage): actinic keratosis (rough, scaly red plaques, light skin) and solar lentigo (liver or age spots). Watch and measure them over time.","Women have higher melanoma risk until about 49; men after 50. White people are about 20 times more likely to be diagnosed than Black people, but Black patients are about 4 times more likely to be diagnosed at an advanced stage (social deter…","A — Asymmetry","B — Border uneven, crusty, notched","C — Color varies (white, brown, blue)","D — Diameter larger than a pencil eraser (6 mm)","E — Evolving: size, shape or color changes; bleeding or scabbing","Basal cell carcinoma — Most common, least malignant, most curable; slow, rarely metastasizes; sun exposure, lighter skin. Small flesh-colored or pink dome, becoming a translucent, shiny, pearly nodule that ulcerates with a shiny border.","Squamous cell carcinoma — Second most common; curable if caught early, otherwise metastasizes to lymph nodes and organs. Red, scaly, keratotic, slightly raised, irregular border, shallow chronic ulcer.","Melanoma — Rare but most deadly, high metastasis. Cancer of melanocytes spreading wide (radially) and deep (vertically) into blood and lymph. Also in the eyes and under the nails. Risk: family history, natural blond or red hair, freckles on…","Eczema","Inflamed, irritated skin; not contagious; most often atopic; inherited tendency; more with asthma or hay fever (overactive immune system). Itchy dry rash on the face, behind the knees, wrists, hands, feet; thick, scaly, red then brown, oozi…","Psoriasis","Chronic, starts in young adults (about 18–24), not contagious, 1–3% of people; overactive immune system, possibly autoimmune; linked to obesity and cardiovascular disease. Skin cells grow too fast (normal skin flakes off about every 4 weeks…","Antifungal Pharmacology","Know which antifungals are topical and which are systemic (PO or IV).","Nystatin (polyene) — Superficial candidiasis: thrush, oral mucosa, vaginal, skin. Cream, powder (skin folds), vaginal, oral liquid (swish and spit or swish and swallow, per order). Too toxic for IV. Topical: few side effects (mild irritatio…","Amphotericin B (polyene) — Agent of choice for systemic mycoses. Binds ergosterol in fungal membranes, making them leaky. High-alert, especially IV: diluted and infused very slowly; very hard on the kidneys (monitor BUN and creatinine); car…","Flucytosine (pyrimidine antifungal) — Inhibits fungal DNA synthesis; rarely alone: given with amphotericin B (synergistic), allowing a lower amphotericin dose and fewer side effects.","Fluconazole (azole) — Azoles block ergosterol synthesis, so the cell wall becomes permeable and the cell dies; superficial and less serious systemic infections (pulmonary). Topical: redness, burning, itching. Systemic: severe GI upset and l…","Griseofulvin — Older, inexpensive. Binds keratin and inhibits fungal mitosis (doesn't affect the wall or membrane). For dermatophyte infections of scalp, skin or nails (scalp ringworm, resistant tinea). Bone marrow suppression, rash, CNS ch…","WBC & CBC Interpretation","Red blood cells (erythrocytes)","Most abundant blood cell; doughnut shape with a thin middle.","Primary cell for tissue oxygenation; hemoglobin on the RBC carries oxygen out to the tissues.","Normal count about 4.2 to 6.2 million/mm³.","White blood cells (leukocytes)","Job = protect: immune function and inflammation.","Granulocytes (granules release inflammatory and immune mediators): neutrophils (most), eosinophils, basophils.","Agranulocytes: lymphocytes, monocytes (with macrophages); they still join immune and inflammatory processes through phagocytosis.","Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas: Neutrophils, Lymphocytes, Monocytes, Eosinophils, Basophils. The percentages in a differential add up to 100%.","Neutrophilssegs = maturebands = immature — Most prevalent WBC; first to arrive at the site of inflammation. — Acute bacterial infection, trauma. More bands than normal = shift to the left (acute bacterial infection).","Lymphocytes (T and B cells) — Primary immune-response cells; mostly in lymphoid tissue, not the bloodstream. — Chronic bacterial infection (memory cells, e.g. a long-lasting MRSA foot wound) and acute viral infection.","Monocytes — Potent phagocytes: \"eat all the junk\"; still secrete some cytokines. — Bacterial infections (clean-up). Can be low with some cancers.","Eosinophils — Phagocytosis tied to antigen–antibody reactions. — Allergic reactions, parasitic infections: \"worms, wheezes and weird diseases.\"","Basophils — Phagocytosis; hypersensitivity and inflammatory reactions. — Allergic reactions.","Hemoglobin (Hgb)M 14–18, F 12–16 — Hemoglobin in the blood: the body's capacity for oxygenation. — Bleeding, folate or B12 deficiency, certain cancers, kidney and liver disease. — Polycythemia, COPD, high altitude, heavy smokers.","Hematocrit (Hct)M 40–52%, F 37–47% — Percentage of blood that is packed RBCs (Hct 40% = 40 mL of packed RBCs per 100 mL of blood). — Anemia, bleeding, bleeding disorders, fluid overload (dilution: more plasma, relatively fewer RBCs). — Poly…","WBC total5,000–10,000 — Degree, or intensity, of the response to a pathological process. — Leukopenia; low neutrophils specifically = neutropenia. — Leukocytosis.","Other RBC labs — RBC count (total erythrocytes); MCV (size of RBCs); MCH (hemoglobin per RBC by weight). Less used at the bedside: know your patient and read up on any flagged value.","The voiceover abbreviates hematocrit as \"HCTZ\". Hematocrit is Hct; HCTZ is hydrochlorothiazide, the thiazide diuretic from the HTN lecture.","CBC = WBC, RBC, hemoglobin, hematocrit and platelets. Normal values are always given on exams and the NCLEX, and abnormal values will be obvious.","CBC with differential: it's all relative","A diff gives the total WBC (absolute number per mm³) plus the percentage of each of the five types; the percentages always add up to 100.","When one type's percentage rises, another's falls, even if its absolute number hasn't changed. Absolute count = total WBC × percentage.","Example: WBC 10,000, lymphocytes 30% = 3,000. A bacterial infection raises WBC to 20,000 with neutrophils 75% and lymphocytes 15%. 15% of 20,000 is still 3,000 lymphocytes: only the proportion changed.","WBC ≈ 13,000, segs 70% ↑, bands 8% ↑ — Bands present = shift to the left: acute bacterial infection.","WBC ≈ 14,000, neutrophils ↓, lymphocytes ↑↑ — Chronic bacterial or acute viral infection.","WBC ≈ 12,000, eosinophils 22% ↑ — Allergic reaction (e.g. asthma attack) or parasitic infection.","Fever is not a disease. It is a protective sign that the body is responding to infection: it slows or stops the growth of some microorganisms (they survive only in a narrow temperature range), improves the immune response and decreases the …","Neutropenia","Measured by the ANC (absolute neutrophil count); neutropenic = ANC < 1,000.","Most often in cancer patients from chemo and radiation.","No neutrophils to fight infection, so bacterial infection can be life-threatening.","Neutropenic precautions: pristine hand hygiene; avoid sick contacts; no raw fruits, vegetables or grains; no fresh flowers; in the hospital, keep the door shut.","Treatment: G-CSF (hematopoietic agents)","Filgrastim (Neupogen): made by recombinant DNA; promotes proliferation, differentiation and activation of granulocyte-making cells. For malignancy, chemo-induced leukopenia, bone marrow transplant, stem-cell harvest, chronic neutropenia. IV…","Pegfilgrastim: long-acting derivative; increases neutrophil production; subQ; bone pain; half-life about 17 h, delayed renal excretion, so dosed less often.","WBC Disorders","Infectious mononucleosis (\"mono\") — Self-limiting lymphoproliferative infection of B lymphocytes, usually from Epstein-Barr virus (EBV). Spread by body fluids, mainly saliva (\"kissing disease\"), also sex, transfusion, transplant. — Adolesce…","Myelodysplastic syndrome (\"bone marrow failure disorder\") — Change in the quantity and quality of bone marrow elements: cells may not mature, may build up in the marrow, or have a short lifespan. — Adults over 65. Hallmark: cytopenias (low …","Hodgkin lymphoma — Neoplasm of lymphoid tissue; biopsy shows Reed-Sternberg cells (from lymph-node B cells), distinctive to Hodgkin. — Across all ages; about 30% of malignant lymphomas. Painless, progressive, rubbery enlargement of a node o…","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma — Neoplasm of lymphoid tissue without Reed-Sternberg cells; can start in any lymphoid tissue. — Mostly older adults. Painless superficial lymphadenopathy plus extranodal disease (skin, GI tract, bone, bone marrow). Feve…","Multiple myeloma (plasma cell myeloma) — Malignancy of mature antibody-secreting B cells (plasma cells): abnormal immunoglobulins, M protein (monoclonal antibody), ↑ osteoclasts, so bone destruction and resorption. Humoral immunity is lost.…","For multiple myeloma the voiceover says it is \"more common in males and females\". It probably means males more than females: check the slide.","Leukemias","Malignant neoplasms from a single hematopoietic cell line. Leukemic cells are immature and undifferentiated: they proliferate fast, live long, can't do the work of mature WBCs, and crowd out normal RBC and platelet maturation. They infiltra…","ALL (acute lymphocytic) — Most common childhood leukemia (about 80% of childhood acute leukemias); peak age 3–7. — 71.7% overall; 91.9% in children","AML (acute myelocytic) — Mainly adults (median age 64; about 80% of adult acute leukemias). — 29.4% adults; 68.7% children","CLL (chronic lymphocytic) — Most common leukemia in adults and older adults; about 30% of US leukemia. Fatigue, weight loss, anorexia, ↑ infections (leukemic B cells make no normal antibodies). — 88.2%","CML (chronic myelocytic) — Adults and children; 15% of US cases; onset usually 40–50. High granulocyte count (track neutrophils) and splenomegaly: fatigue, weight loss, diaphoresis, bleeding, abdominal discomfort. — 69.7%","Acute vs chronic","Acute: sudden, \"stormy\" onset with signs of ↓ mature WBCs, ↓ RBCs, ↓ platelets: bone pain, bruising, fever, fatigue, infection, unexplained pain (a child who suddenly refuses to walk). Blood and marrow show immature WBCs making up 60–100% o…","Chronic: insidious; often found on routine labs.","Treatment (goal = remission)","Cytotoxic chemotherapy first, to destroy rapidly reproducing cells (specific drugs not needed for this class).","Then possibly a bone marrow / stem-cell transplant (volunteer donor, identical twin, or the patient's own), harvested from the iliac crest.","Risks: infection, rejection (graft attacks host), relapse."]},{"s":"Module 6: Coagulation, Stress & Steroids▾","a":"module-6-coagulation-stress-steroids","k":["Module 6: Coagulation, Stress & Steroids▾","Coagulation cascade, anticoagulants and antiplatelets, DVT/VTE, stress response, steroids.","Coagulation Cascade","What blood is made of","Plasma (55%), about 92% water, plus plasma proteins, clotting factors and electrolytes (Na, K, Mg, Ca).","Albumin (about 57% of plasma proteins): carrier molecule; regulates water and solutes across capillaries; drives oncotic pressure.","Globulins: HDL, prothrombin (a clotting factor), hormone-transport proteins.","Fibrinogen: the most plentiful clotting factor.","Most plasma proteins are made in the liver, so liver failure leads to clotting problems.","Serum = plasma without clotting factors (serum + clotting factors = plasma).","Formed elements (45%): RBCs, WBCs, platelets. Blood is only about 8% of body fluid.","Platelets","Not true cells: anuclear cytoplasmic fragments with granules that release adhesive proteins, coagulation factors and growth factors when they sense vessel injury.","Made in the bone marrow; extras are stored in the spleen.","Normal 150,000–400,000. Below about 100,000 = thrombocytopenia, a high bleeding risk (varies by facility).","Platelet activation, step by step","Healthy state: smooth, unactivated platelets circulate freely in the plasma.","A platelet senses vessel damage, so activation begins and adhesion to the injured wall increases.","Its shape and biochemistry change: smooth becomes the spiky dendritic form, which is sticky.","Aggregation: platelets clump with each other and on the vessel wall at the injury.","That activates the clotting system, forming an immobilizing meshwork of platelets + fibrin = a clot.","The clotting cascade","Also called the clotting system, coagulation cascade or coagulation system: groups of proteins activate to form a clot.","Activated by tissue injury (think: cutting your hand while cooking) or infection; platelets are the primary initiators.","End goal: a meshwork of fibrin strands + platelets (often trapping RBCs) that plugs the vessel. Bleeding stopped = hemostasis.","Initiated through the intrinsic or extrinsic pathway; thrombin activates fibrinogen into fibrin and is a major drug target.","Stopping the clot","Antithrombin III (AT-III): circulating inhibitor of thrombin, so fibrinogen is no longer turned into fibrin.","Tissue factor pathway inhibitor (TFPI): made by platelets and endothelial cells; inhibits factor Xa (\"ten a\").","Both also inhibit other clotting factors. Deficiencies in these mechanisms lead to bleeding disorders.","The voiceover says \"antithrombin 383\" and \"TFP\". These mean antithrombin III and TFPI.","Removing the clot: fibrinolysis","The clot forms within minutes; about 20–60 min later the process starts to stop.","tPA (tissue plasminogen activator) and uPA (urokinase-like plasminogen activator) turn circulating plasminogen into plasmin.","Plasmin breaks down fibrin, releasing the trapped old platelets and RBCs to be degraded.","Hear plasmin or tPA, think clot busting.","The Khan Academy coagulation-cascade video (stop at about 13:30; hemophilia not needed) is testable per the lecture, and its intrinsic/extrinsic pathway details aren't in the voiceover. Review it alongside this section.","Coagulation Modifiers","Goals: prevent clot formation (the focus of this course), break apart existing clots (not covered), improve circulation and perfusion, decrease pain, prevent tissue damage. Common indications: atrial fibrillation, heart disease, heart failu…","Anticoagulants","Inhibit the action or formation of clotting factors.","Prevent clots and the extension of an established clot. They have no effect on an existing clot.","Antiplatelets","Prevent the platelet plug by inhibiting aggregation: platelets stop being sticky.","Best for preventing heart attack and stroke. Not anticoagulants.","Both classes are antithrombotic.","Number one concern for every anticoagulant: BLEEDING, external (a cut that keeps bleeding, gums bleeding with a hard toothbrush) or internal (brain or GI bleeds, which may not show until it's an emergency). Know why the patient is on it and…","Know why the patient is on it and where they're at risk of bleeding.","Trend H&H daily: a sudden drop in a stable patient suggests internal bleeding.","Vital signs: tachycardia is the first sign (fluid volume deficit). Later: hypotension, pale or gray skin, ↑ RR and work of breathing.","Skin: bruising, petechiae, hematomas; black tarry stools. Bright red blood on a dressing hours after surgery is probably normal; 4–5 days post-op it's a problem.","Heparin (class: heparins) — Activates antithrombin, indirectly inactivating thrombin and factor Xa, so no fibrin forms. — IV or subQ, when prompt anticoagulation is needed: evolving stroke, PE, large DVT; open-heart surgery and dialysis (bl…","Enoxaparin (Lovenox), low-molecular-weight heparin — Heparin cleaved into small fragments: works on factor Xa only (not thrombin). Greater affinity for Xa, more bioavailable, longer half-life. — SubQ only, prefilled syringes; DVT/clot proph…","Warfarin (Coumadin) — Vitamin K inhibitor: prevents synthesis of the vitamin K–dependent clotting factors. — PO once daily, usually in the evening (about 5 PM). Onset 24 h; lasts 2–5 days. Prevents VTE, DVT, PE; prevents clots in AFib and h…","Apixaban (Eliquis), rivaroxaban (Xarelto) — Direct factor Xa inhibitors. — PO. Before knee or hip replacement; stroke prevention in AFib; post-op thromboprophylaxis; treat DVT/PE. — AEs: bleeding, hematoma, dizziness, rash, GI distress, per…","Argatroban, bivalirudin — Direct thrombin inhibitors: irreversibly bind thrombin, so fibrinogen can't become fibrin. — IV only. Treat HIT; PCI patients at high risk of HIT. — Argatroban: caution in hepatic dysfunction. AE: bleeding. Monitor…","The voiceover says \"anti-TNF\" for the heparin lab. It means anti-Xa (\"anti-ten-a\"). One apixaban interaction on the slide was unclear in the audio (\"fantasia going\"): check the slide.","HIT (heparin-induced thrombocytopenia): in about 5–15% of heparin patients; antibodies form against heparin, causing low platelets plus new thrombi, often in the fingers, toes, nose tip and ears (small vessels). Monitor platelets; stop hepa…","Aspirin (an NSAID; the only NSAID used as an antiplatelet; not a true anticoagulant) — Blocks prostaglandin synthesis via the COX pathways, which also blocks platelet aggregation (less sticky). Prevent or treat MI; prevent ischemic stroke. …","Clopidogrel (Plavix), ticagrelor (Brilinta) — ADP inhibitors: alter the platelet membrane so it never gets the signal to aggregate or turn spiky. Reduce stroke risk; prevent TIA; after MI; sometimes with aspirin. — PO. Same contraindication…","DVT & VTE","Thrombus — A blood clot that stays attached to the vessel wall.","Thromboembolus — A thrombus that has detached and travels.","VTE (venous thromboembolism) — A detached thrombus in the venous system. Clots favor veins: slow, consistent, low-pressure flow (arteries are fast and high-pressure).","DVT (deep vein thrombosis) — A thrombus in a deep vein, usually the lower extremities, most commonly the iliac and femoral veins.","Virchow's triad: three factors that promote venous thrombosis. Venous stasis (slow venous flow): older age, immobility, hospitalized and post-op patients, heart failure. Venous endothelial damage: trauma, certain IV medications, surgery. Hy…","Venous stasis (slow venous flow): older age, immobility, hospitalized and post-op patients, heart failure.","Venous endothelial damage: trauma, certain IV medications, surgery.","Hypercoagulable states: inherited clotting disorders, malignancy, pregnancy, oral contraceptives, HRT, recent surgery; genetic mutations affecting prothrombin or antithrombin (deficiencies).","Pathophysiology & signs","The thrombus usually forms near a venous valve: inflammation there promotes inappropriate platelet aggregation, and the thrombus grows.","Pain, redness, warmth (inflammation), usually in the lower extremity; may be asymptomatic.","Significant obstruction raises venous pressure, causing edema in the affected limb.","Assess: ask about symptoms and onset, and compare limbs side to side.","Risk factors","Clotting disorders; immobility and surgery, especially hip fracture, hip or knee replacement; ortho, spinal cord injury, OB/GYN.","Pregnancy and up to 6 weeks postpartum; oral contraceptives; HRT.","Overweight or obese, smoking, some cancers and chemo, heart failure, IBD.","Personal or family history of DVT; age over 60; varicose (spider) veins.","Pulmonary embolism (PE) — Most serious: a thrombus lodges in the pulmonary circulation and blocks gas exchange.","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension — Rare; repeated lung clots raise pressure in the pulmonary circulation; mainly shortness of breath.","Post-thrombotic syndrome (8–70% of patients) — Chronic inflammation and chronic venous hypertension (the clot raises pressure on the vein wall and damages it). Pain, aching, fatigue, swelling, cramps, itching, heaviness, tingling, bursting …","Phlegmasia cerulea dolens (\"painful blue inflammation\") — Rare; major leg veins totally or nearly occluded: sudden massive swelling, deep pain, intense cyanosis; untreated, it leads to gangrene or amputation.","A clot blocks circulation wherever it lodges: lungs, heart, brain or extremities.","Treatment","Anticoagulation for active clots (see Coagulation Modifiers).","IVC filter (vena cava interruption device): catches clots traveling toward the heart, lungs and brain.","Embolectomy: surgical removal; risky and uncommon.","Prevention","Identify who is at risk and modify risk factors.","SCDs (sequential compression devices), TED hose.","Aggressive mobilization in the hospital and post-op.","Monitor bleeding times (PT/INR) for at-risk patients.","Stress & Adaptation","Homeostasis (\"same\" + \"standing still\") — All body systems balanced around an ideal set point, within a normal range that varies by person (a resting HR of 75 or 55 can both be normal). Examples: pH (H⁺ depends on CO₂, so the respiratory sy…","Allostasis (\"variable\" + \"standing still\") — The dynamic process that gets the body back to homeostasis; the set point can shift for anticipated demand (exercise: RR ↑ to keep oxygenation). Involves many physiologic and psychological variab…","Stress vs stressor — Stress = the physical, chemical or emotional tension felt in body and mind. Stressor = the force causing it (the exam).","Eustress — Positive stress, seen as within coping ability: motivates, focuses, improves performance.","Distress — Negative reaction, seen as outside coping ability: anxiety, concern, decreased performance; short or long term.","Allostatic load / overload — Load = wear and tear from the HPA axis, SNS and immune system being over- or under-activated by chronic stress. Overload = the point of exhaustion: organs and tissues change and stress-induced disease follows.","What shapes coping and adaptive ability","Genetics (a more or less efficient relaxation response; a small part).","Culture and environment (a child of a parent who panics learns to panic).","Prior experiences (a bad dentist visit leads to anticipatory stress).","Pre-existing health status (acute or chronic disease already taxing the body).","Ability to manage stress: support system, coping skills, logical thinking, personality.","Coping mechanisms","Strategies directed at the stressor; effective if they resolve the situation and the feelings about it.","Healthy: meditation, exercise, social support, dinner with friends, calling a parent.","Unhealthy: drug or alcohol use.","Manage stress by avoiding it, changing your reaction, or using healthy coping skills.","General adaptation syndrome: the same response to any stressor","1. Alarm (\"fight or flight\") — Initial arousal: a surge of energy to evade or confront a stressor that may be physical, emotional, real, perceived, positive or negative. SNS / adrenergic: the hypothalamus secretes CRH, which activates the S…","SNS / adrenergic: the hypothalamus secretes CRH, which activates the SNS; the adrenal medulla releases epinephrine and norepinephrine.","HPA axis: CRH stimulates the anterior pituitary to release ACTH, so the adrenal cortex releases glucocorticoids (cortisol). This is a negative-feedback loop.","SNS effects: pupils dilate, dry mouth, ↑ HR, bronchodilation, ↓ peristalsis and gastric secretions, liver makes glucose, bladder contraction inhibited.","2. Resistance — Ongoing stress: the body tries to cope and adjust, but this takes a lot of energy and supplies run out (glucose, epi, norepi, cortisol). Ideally the stressor is removed and the PNS takes over (cholinergic, acetylcholine): th…","3. Exhaustion — The body can't return to homeostasis. The adrenal cortex hypertrophies (constant stimulation) while lymph tissue atrophies, so infections rise. No energy stores are left, adaptation is lost and disease takes over. Can end in…","PNS = rest and digest, the opposite of SNS: saliva, bronchoconstriction, peristalsis resumes, bile released, bladder contracts.","Norepinephrine — Primary vasoconstrictor: regulates blood flow to organs and maintains BP. Dilates pupils (night and far vision). Decreases gastric secretion; inhibits insulin secretion (keeps glucose available).","Epinephrine — ↑ contractility, HR and venous return, so ↑ cardiac output. Bronchodilation. ↑ glycogenolysis (liver releases glucose). Inhibits insulin secretion. Both catecholamines influence cytokine production.","Cortisol (primary glucocorticoid, the body's own steroid) — Works with catecholamines to keep BP and CO normal. Inhibits reproductive hormones (no energy for reproduction). Breaks down muscle, lymphoid and adipose protein into amino acids, …","Aldosterone (primary mineralocorticoid, adrenal cortex; RAAS) — Kidneys reabsorb Na⁺ and excrete K⁺ and H⁺; water follows sodium, so ↑ ECF volume and ↑ BP.","Stress and memory: glucocorticoids promote epinephrine synthesis, and catecholamines plus glucocorticoids form memories in the brain. That helps us avoid a stressor (the rabbit learns to avoid the tiger) but can create traumatic memories, s…","Chronic stress and allostatic overload Examples: living in a war zone, daily racism and discrimination, poverty, caregiving for a sick family member. Immunosuppression (cortisol): thymus atrophy, ↓ T cells, infections, autoimmune disorders,…","Examples: living in a war zone, daily racism and discrimination, poverty, caregiving for a sick family member.","Immunosuppression (cortisol): thymus atrophy, ↓ T cells, infections, autoimmune disorders, poor wound healing. Flares of shingles, cold sores and colds after stress.","Physical symptoms: hair loss, stiff neck, tension headaches, asthma triggers, palpitations, tics, GI upset (nausea, diarrhea, constipation, anorexia), irritable bladder, acne, irregular menses or impotence, sleep disturbances.","Linked diseases: CAD, HTN, stroke, tension headaches, rheumatoid arthritis, autoimmune diseases, IBS, ulcers, sexual disorders, type 2 diabetes.","Stress does not cause bleeding ulcers (an old belief), but it can worsen an existing one.","Prolonged cortisol","Hyperglycemia: ↑ glucose (opposite of insulin), gluconeogenesis, ↓ peripheral glucose use and muscle/fat uptake; also promotes glycogen storage.","Muscle wasting, fragile skin that tears easily, bone breakdown (fragile bones).","Fat redistribution from the extremities to the face and trunk: moon face, buffalo hump.","Sleep deprivation","Increases stress levels; very common in the hospital (noise, frequent waking, lost privacy) and among night-shift nurses.","Irritability, apathy, lack of interest, poor memory and judgment, hallucinations, changes in mentation.","Steroids Pharmacology","Systemic steroids (PO or IV) act body-wide. Inhaled steroids give a localized lung effect with little or no systemic effect.","Why we give steroids","Replacement: the adrenal gland isn't working, or can't keep up after prolonged stress.","Anti-inflammatory: systemic inflammatory response, COPD exacerbation (IV, then PO), chronic IBD, rheumatic disorders.","Immunosuppression: allergies; post-transplant to prevent rejection (weaned off if there's no rejection).","Asthma: usually inhaled.","Replacement dosing: mimic the adrenal gland","Cortisol rises with stress, so steroid doses must increase during illness or extra stress: the patient's adrenal gland won't make more.","Methods: alternate-day therapy, multiple small doses, or ⅔ of the daily dose in the AM and ⅓ in the PM (mimics the dawn burst).","Mineralocorticoids: usually once daily, and often not replaced.","Hydrocortisone — Synthetic glucocorticoid identical to endogenous cortisol: labs can't tell them apart. Has some mineralocorticoid (aldosterone) action but is primarily a glucocorticoid. PO or IV (parenteral). Low dose: minimal or no side e…","Prednisone — PO, typically 5–10 mg/day; for chronic disease such as COPD (e.g. 5 mg/day). No significant aldosterone effect.","Dexamethasone — Used to assess adrenal function: it acts like cortisol but doesn't show up as cortisol in the blood, so any measured cortisol is endogenous. No significant aldosterone effect.","Fludrocortisone — The only mineralocorticoid (aldosterone) replacement. Added to prednisone or dexamethasone to prevent salt wasting (can't hold onto sodium; potassium climbs even with working kidneys).","The voiceover says \"hydrocodone, cortisone\" at one point. The drug is hydrocortisone; hydrocodone is an opioid.","Acute side effects","↑ intraocular pressure (especially with glaucoma).","Fluid retention, high BP (caution in heart failure).","Mood swings.","Weight gain with central fat (abdomen, face, back of neck); intense hunger.","Long-term side effects (months to years)","Cataracts.","Hyperglycemia: diabetes worsens or develops; expect ↑ glucose with IV steroids.","↑ infection risk (immunosuppression).","Osteoporosis and fractures.","Suppressed adrenal hormone production: fatigue, anorexia, nausea, muscle weakness.","Thin, papery skin, bruising, slow wound healing.","Teaching Never stop abruptly: abrupt loss of steroids can be deadly, which is why patients get a taper. Take at the same time each day. Long-term use: medical alert bracelet; monitor weight and blood sugar. Increase the dose during illness …","Never stop abruptly: abrupt loss of steroids can be deadly, which is why patients get a taper.","Take at the same time each day.","Long-term use: medical alert bracelet; monitor weight and blood sugar.","Increase the dose during illness or stress (per the provider)."]},{"s":"Module 7: GI, Hypersensitivity, Urologic & Mental Health▾","a":"module-7-gi-hypersensitivity-urologic-mental-health","k":["Module 7: GI, Hypersensitivity, Urologic & Mental Health▾","GI pharm, hypersensitivities, UTIs and incontinence, depression and anxiety, substance use.","Basic GI Pharmacology","Constipation","Small, infrequent or difficult BMs, generally fewer than 3 per week. Know the patient's own normal pattern.","Causes: low-fiber diet (fiber adds bulk), lack of exercise (exercise stimulates peristalsis), slowed peristalsis in the elderly, obstruction, diverticulitis.","Unrelieved, it leads to fecal impaction: a firm, immovable mass blocks the lower GI tract. Liquid stool can ooze around it, so ask whether it's true diarrhea.","Impaction signs: anorexia, N/V, abdominal distention, cramping, rectal pain.","Diarrhea","↑ frequency and fluidity of BMs; often the first sign of a bigger GI disorder.","Acute: viral or bacterial infection, emotional stress, drugs (antibiotics, antacids, chemo), impaction.","Chronic: lasts more than 4 weeks: chronic GI infection, motility or integrity changes, malabsorption, endocrine disorders.","Episodic: food allergies, irritants (caffeine, tube feedings).","Ask in a sensitive, nonjudgmental way.","Concerns: skin breakdown, fluid and electrolyte loss, nutrition, especially in the very young, very old and debilitated.","Osmotic — Osmotically active solutes pull sodium and water into the gut. — Magnesium sulfate (Epsom salt); concentrated tube feedings.","Secretory — A bacterial toxin ↑ secretion and inhibits reabsorption. — Vibrio cholerae, Staph aureus.","Exudative — Inflamed bowel lumen exudes mucus, blood and protein, which pull water in. — Crohn's, ulcerative colitis.","Motility disturbance — ↓ small-intestine absorption sends excess fluid to the colon. — Dumping syndrome (after gastrectomy), IBS.","Antidiarrheals (all PO)","Adsorbents — Bismuth subsalicylate (Pepto-Bismol): OTC, a form of aspirin. Activated charcoal (also for overdoses). — Coats the GI wall and chemically binds the causative agent so it's eliminated. — Pepto: ↑ bleeding time (aspirin), constip…","Antimotility — Loperamide (Imodium): OTC, antiperistaltic. Diphenoxylate with atropine (Lomotil): Rx only. — Slow peristalsis (less rhythmic contraction and tone); anticholinergics (atropine) also dry secretions. Can combine with adsorbents…","Probiotics — Lactobacillus (Bacid, Culturelle); Saccharomyces boulardii (Florastor) for C. diff. — Replenish normal gut flora (often destroyed by antibiotics) and suppress diarrhea-causing bacteria. — Yogurt usually isn't enough for antibio…","Laxatives","Treatment is individualized (age, severity, contributing factors) and combines more fiber, more activity and medication. Laxatives are among the most misused OTC drugs: misuse causes dependence and bowel damage. Not for long-term use, excep…","Bulk-forming — Psyllium (Metamucil): wafer or powder. — Like dietary fiber: absorbs water, ↑ bulk, distends the bowel, starts movement. GI-only, no systemic effect. — Take with plenty of water (prevents esophageal obstruction or impaction).…","Emollient — Docusate sodium (PO); mineral oil (PO or PR enema). — Lubricates stool and the intestinal wall (docusate lets more fat into the feces), softening stool; keeps water from leaving the intestine. — Docusate: constipation, impaction…","Hyperosmotic — Glycerin (mild; children; suppository); lactulose; polyethylene glycol (GoLYTELY). — ↑ water in the feces, so distention, peristalsis, evacuation. — Lactulose also lowers blood ammonia (liver disease, hepatic encephalopathy).…","Saline — Magnesium salts: mag citrate, magnesium hydroxide (milk of magnesia), magnesium sulfate (Epsom salts). Sodium salts: Fleet enema. — ↑ osmotic pressure draws water into the colon: watery stool in 3–6 h. — Magnesium salts are OTC: ca…","Stimulant — Bisacodyl (Dulcolax) PO or PR; senna (Senokot) PO. — Induce peristalsis along the entire GI tract; effect is proportional to dose. — Most likely to cause dependence. Constipation or full-bowel evacuation; OTC. AEs: nutrient mala…","Hypersensitivities","Hypersensitivity = a normal immune response that is inappropriately triggered, excessive (out of proportion; e.g. sepsis, where the response and its mediators do more damage than the infection), or produces damaging effects. It is triggered…","I: IgE-mediated / immediate — \"How does someone develop an allergy to something cute?\" — B cells, plasma cells, IgE, mast cells — First exposure sensitizes: the B cell becomes a plasma cell, which makes IgE, which binds mast cells and waits…","II: cytotoxic — \"What if a patient received the wrong blood type?\" — IgG, IgM, complement, phagocytes — Antibodies bind antigens on foreign cell surfaces: the complex attacks the cell directly, causing clumping; complement lyses it (hemolys…","III: immune complex — \"What happens when you have rheumatoid arthritis?\" — IgG, IgM, complement, neutrophils, mast cells — Antigen–antibody complexes form in the blood, circulate and deposit in tissues, starting the complement cascade. The …","IV: delayed — \"Ever had poison ivy, a positive TB skin test, a jellyfish sting, a jewelry reaction, Crohn's?\" — T cells (CD8 cytotoxic, CD4 helper), cytokines, macrophages, mast cells; no antibodies — A small incomplete protein (hapten) att…","The voiceover calls type IV T-cell-mediated with no antibodies, then says \"antibodies attack gluten\" when describing gluten and Crohn's. Learn Type IV as T-cell-mediated. It also says \"type II reaction\" while describing type III immune comp…","Type I mediators and symptoms","Vasodilation: stuffy nose, welts, drop in BP.","↑ vascular permeability: fluid leaks out, adding to hypotension and runny nose.","Bronchial smooth-muscle constriction: wheezing, trouble breathing.","Irritant receptors stimulated: pruritus; urticaria (hives), swelling, redness.","Allergy shots build tolerance (↓ sensitivity); allergy medications (antihistamines) block the response.","Local vs systemic Type I","Local (atopic): allergic rhinitis. About 50% of people in the US react to one of the 10 most common allergens. Triggers: pollen, dust, mold, dander, peanuts, penicillin.","Systemic = anaphylaxis, a medical emergency: laryngeal edema (can't breathe) plus vascular collapse with severe hypotension. Most common triggers: medications, bee stings, foods; tiny doses can trigger it within minutes. Itching and hives p…","Treat with antihistamines, glucocorticoids, epinephrine. Teach: MedicAlert bracelet; carry EpiPens.","Transfusion reaction (Type II): stop the transfusion immediately at any sign. Stay with the patient for the first 15 min; take temperature before and 15 min after starting. Fever, chills or flushing are often first; watch HR and BP changes,…","Type II vs Type III: both form antigen–antibody complexes. In Type II the complex attacks the cell directly. In Type III the complexes deposit in tissue and the inflammation causes the damage.","Pharmacotherapy","Immunosuppressants — Decrease the immune response (transplants). — ↑ infection risk. In a transplant with infection, balancing is tricky (e.g. a kidney infection: stop the immunosuppressant and use temporary dialysis).","Antihistamines — Reduce symptoms by suppressing histamine activity. — Don't stop the reaction. May be combined with immunosuppressants in severe reactions.","Epinephrine (sympathomimetic: vasopressor, bronchodilator, anti-asthmatic, vasoconstrictor) — Alpha (arteries) and beta (heart, lungs, arteries, skeletal muscle) agonist: inhibits mast-cell mediator release, constricts vessels, opens bronch…","The voiceover says epinephrine is given \"with severe hypertension\". From context (it constricts vessels and treats anaphylactic shock) this should be hypotension: check the slide.","Epinephrine: giving it safely","Side effects: dysrhythmias, angina or chest pain, hypertension, tachycardia, stroke risk; nervousness, restlessness, tremor.","Routes: every route except PO: IM, IV, subQ (often preferred), even down an ET tube.","Anaphylaxis: lowest dose; adult 0.2–1 mg; peds weight-based 0.01 mg/kg.","1:1,000 (1 mg/1 mL) for anaphylaxis vs 1:10,000 for cardiac arrest. IV solutions are less concentrated: concentrated epi IV can be fatal (severe dysrhythmias or HTN). An IM dose given IV could kill.","Double- and triple-check the dose with a coworker. Monitor VS, rhythm, and whether it's working (easier breathing, less swelling and wheezing).","EpiPen teaching","Teach the patient and family how to use it; use exactly as directed.","Use only when symptoms truly occur (it's a dangerous drug).","Outer thigh, through clothing; hold 10 seconds. One dose per pen; often a 2-pack plus a trainer.","Seek medical attention after every use: short half-life, so symptoms can return.","Carry it at all times (e.g. hiking with a bee allergy); wear a MedicAlert ID.","Intro to Urological Problems","Uncomplicated (lower) UTIs","Bacteriuria — Bacteria in the urine: colonization, not necessarily infection.","Urethritis — Infection of the urethra: dysuria, but commonly asymptomatic.","Cystitis — Infection of the bladder: frequency, urgency, suprapubic discomfort, dysuria. Hematuria is possible in both.","Signs, diagnosis, treatment","E. coli is the most common cause; protein in urine is a fertile breeding ground.","Signs: may be asymptomatic; urgency, frequency, dysuria, hematuria, cloudy or foul urine, fever, chills, fatigue.","Diagnose: H&P, urinalysis (bacteria), urine culture (which organism), CBC (↑ WBC).","Treat: antibiotics; plenty of fluids; avoid irritants (alcohol, caffeine, sometimes sparkling water); loose cotton clothing; urinate when you feel the urge; sometimes probiotics.","Protective factors","Acidic urine (low pH) and urea.","Males: prostatic secretions (more protective). Females: urethral gland secretions.","One-way urine flow, plus the one-way valve where the ureter enters the bladder (prevents backflow).","The immune system.","The voiceover says \"acidic urine, so a high pH or low pH\". Acidic urine is low pH.","Risk factors","Catheterization (CAUTI): a foreign conduit pushed the \"wrong direction\".","Female: shorter urethra, perineal irritation. About 25% develop a UTI in the first year of being sexually active.","↑ age: up to 50% of nursing-home residents have bacteriuria.","Pregnancy; spermicide with diaphragms or condoms (still use protection: a UTI is easier to treat than an STI or unplanned pregnancy).","Obstruction or reflux, immobility (urine stasis), urinary or fecal incontinence, decreased cognition.","Hygiene barriers (homelessness, no resources or restroom access).","Males: less likely to get a UTI but more likely to have recurrent ones (bacteria hide in the prostate); BPH causes retention.","Atypical presentations","Children: fever, irritability, poor appetite, vomiting, diarrhea, \"just don't look well\"; infants can't say what hurts.","Elderly: confusion (the biggest one), anorexia, lethargy, anxiety, falls.","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (Bactrim) — First-line. Not with a sulfa allergy.","Ciprofloxacin — The choice for patients with a sulfa allergy.","Nitrofurantoin — For recurring lower UTIs.","All antibiotics — Finish the whole course, even after feeling better.","Phenazopyridine (Pyridium), urinary tract analgesic — Relieves UTI pain but doesn't cure the UTI. Excreted in urine, it has a topical analgesic effect on the urinary mucosa. Well tolerated; turns urine reddish-orange (can stain undergarment…","Overactive bladder & incontinence","The bladder is smooth muscle (the detrusor) that holds about 300–500 mL before signaling the need to void; pelvic floor muscles underneath help control the bladder and bowel.","Overactive bladder","Bladder muscles contract involuntarily even at low volume, causing urgency.","Causes: neurologic disorders (stroke), diabetes, UTIs, menopause hormone changes, bladder abnormalities (tumors, stones), outflow obstruction.","Signs: sudden urge, urgency incontinence, frequency (8 or more times in 24 h), nocturia (waking more than 2 times a night).","Mirabegron","Bladder antispasmodic: selectively stimulates beta-3 adrenergic receptors, relaxing bladder smooth muscle.","PO, 25–50 mg/day.","Most serious AE: hypertension. Monitor BP; a poor choice for patients with HTN.","Also urinary retention, UTIs, headache.","Incontinence = involuntary loss of urine. It is not a normal part of aging, though age-related changes can contribute.","Urgency — Leakage immediately after a sudden urge, with no time in between; overactive detrusor contracts suddenly (more often with age). — Bladder infection, outlet obstruction (enlarged prostate), CNS conditions (Parkinson's, dementia, MS…","Stress — Leakage with ↑ intra-abdominal pressure (sneezing, coughing, bending, lifting): lost support changes the bladder–urethra angle, so the urethra doesn't fully close. — Age; lost pelvic floor or fascial support from childbirth trauma …","Mixed — Stress + urge together. — Common in older females.","Overflow — The bladder gets so full it leaks. — More common in males with an enlarged prostate.","Functional — The urinary system works, but something stops the person reaching the toilet in time. — Mobility limits, cognitive disorders, no toilet access.","Transient — Sudden and reversible. — UTI, constipation, fecal impaction.","Why incontinence matters","Psychosocial impact.","Acidic urine damages skin (redness, pain, itching, swelling, blisters, dryness), and damaged, damp, warm skin lets bacterial and fungal infection in.","At-risk areas: genitals, between the buttocks, inner thighs. Prevention (good skin care) is the best treatment.","Treatment","Combine medication with bladder training and pelvic floor strengthening (pelvic floor PT).","Anticholinergics block acetylcholine (which drives smooth-muscle contraction). For overactive bladder and incontinence. PO; extended-release forms.","Oxybutynin: the one to know for the exam.","AEs: dry mouth, constipation and other anticholinergic effects.","Depression & Anxiety Pathophysiology","Neurotransmitters are chemicals that cross the synapse and either block or facilitate relay between neurons (more than 200 exist). Dysfunction leads to neurologic and psychiatric disorders, and an imbalance in one affects the others.","Dopamine — Attention, motivation, pleasure, reward. — Parkinson's-like symptoms (slowed reaction time), anergia (abnormal lack of energy), anhedonia (can't enjoy usually enjoyable activities). — Not covered","Serotonin (5-HT) — Obsessions and compulsions. — OCD-like symptoms (excessive thoughts, compulsive behavior); ↑ impulsivity (suicide, aggression); susceptible to cue triggers. — Not covered","Norepinephrine — Alertness and energy; excitatory (fight or flight). — Lack of focus, energy, motivation. — Anxiety, stress, hyperactivity.","GABA — Principal inhibitory neurotransmitter: relaxation. — Dysfunction is linked to anxiety disorders, especially panic, and recently to major depressive disorder.","Glutamate — Abundant neurotransmitter. — Stress hormones stimulate it: an excess build-up is associated with panic.","Low dopamine and low serotonin together affect depression and craving.","Depression","Facts","More than 17 million people in the US; women 2× (men under-report). More common in white people and those never married.","Leading cause of disability worldwide; high suicide risk.","A risk factor for cardiovascular disease: 64% greater risk of CAD.","Etiology: a combination of genetic (family history), biological, environmental and psychological factors; neurochemical, situational, chronic or acute; or a medication side effect.","Types: major depressive disorder, persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia), premenstrual dysphoric disorder, substance- or medication-induced, due to another medical condition, postpartum.","Diagnosis, symptoms, treatment","Requires multiple symptoms intense enough to cause distress and persistently impair psychosocial functioning (not one bad exam).","Symptoms: loss of interest or pleasure, fatigue or restlessness and irritability, poor concentration, low self-esteem, negative thinking, sleep and appetite disturbances (either direction).","Treatment: medication (takes 4–8 weeks for full effect, so keep taking it), psychotherapy (CBT), psychoeducation, support groups, brain stimulation for refractory cases. Combinations tailored to the patient work best.","Anxiety disorders: each is different and treated differently","Panic disorder — Anticipatory anxiety (fear that panic will start) and avoidance anxiety (strategies to avoid triggers and feel in control). Genetics and family history; stressful early-childhood events; stress hormones raise glutamate. Pan…","Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD) — Chronic: anxiety lasting more than 6 months; excessive, uncontrolled, unrealistic worry that spirals (\"fail this test, fail nursing school, get kicked out of my family\"). Muscle tension, autonomic hypera…","PTSD — Chronic stress-response activation after a potentially life-threatening event. Three core symptoms: hyperarousal, avoidance of reminders, re-experiencing. Vivid flashbacks, nightmares or night terrors, emotional blunting, irritabilit…","Social anxiety disorder — Intense fear of criticism, humiliation, negative evaluation and embarrassment in social situations: withdrawal and avoidance, or intense discomfort when attending. Causes: inherited traits, brain structure (amygdal…","OCD — Obsessions = repetitive, unwanted thoughts (contamination, harm). Compulsions = repeated activities or rituals (constant cleaning; turning a doorknob a set number of times; checking the stove again and again). Time-consuming and distr…","Buspirone for GAD: mechanism unknown; no sedation, no respiratory depression, no abuse potential, doesn't intensify CNS depressants; slow anxiolytic effect (not for an attack in the moment).","Depression & Anxiety Pharmacology","Rules for every antidepressant Combination therapy works best (medication plus CBT or EMDR). Most \"antidepressants\" treat both depression and anxiety. SSRIs and SNRIs are first-line because they're the safest. All carry a suicide risk and r…","Combination therapy works best (medication plus CBT or EMDR). Most \"antidepressants\" treat both depression and anxiety.","SSRIs and SNRIs are first-line because they're the safest.","All carry a suicide risk and risk of mental imbalance (they alter neurotransmitters): assess before starting, during adjustment and over time.","Not effective right away: evaluate efficacy over 4–8 weeks. If it isn't working, increase the dose, switch within the class, change class, or add a second drug.","Withdrawal syndrome if stopped abruptly: dizziness, headache, sensory disturbances (hands or feet \"on fire\"), shaking or tremor, anxiety, dysphoria. Patients must talk with the provider before stopping (cost, access, dislike).","No common suffix in any class: memorize which drug belongs where.","SSRI (selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor) — Fluoxetine (Prozac): the only SSRI tested (more than 20 exist; know the others for the NCLEX) — Blocks reuptake of serotonin into the presynaptic nerve ending, so more serotonin stays in the s…","SNRI (serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor) — Venlafaxine (Effexor) — Blocks reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine. — Like SSRIs: nausea, headache, anorexia, insomnia or somnolence, sexual dysfunction, withdrawal syndrome; also sw…","TCA (tricyclic antidepressant) — Amitriptyline (Elavil) — Blocks reuptake of two monoamines: norepinephrine and serotonin (works a bit differently from SSRIs and SNRIs). — Used for more than 50 years; good efficacy, tolerable side effects, …","MAOI (monoamine oxidase inhibitor) — Phenelzine (Nardil) — MAO normally turns norepinephrine, serotonin and dopamine into inactive products; inhibiting it leaves more of all three available. — Used for refractory depression; works better fo…","Serotonin syndrome","Usually from two drugs that both raise serotonin (an overload); more likely with MAOIs and other serotonergic drugs.","Onset 2–72 h after treatment: altered mental status, confusion.","Check that a patient on an SSRI isn't on another serotonergic drug.","Anticholinergic effects (TCAs)","Hot as a hare: overheated.","Dry as a bone: dry mouth and eyes, all secretions.","Blind as a bat: blurred vision.","Red as a beet: flushed.","Mad as a hatter: confusion.","Atypical antidepressants","Bupropion (Wellbutrin) — Structurally similar to amphetamine: a stimulant effect, ↓ appetite. Full effect in 1–3 weeks (faster). Mechanism unclear. Also for smoking cessation, ADHD, neuropathic pain. AEs: seizures, agitation, headache, dry …","Ketamine — Traditionally for extreme pain (given by a physician or provider in the ICU). Low-dose intranasal for refractory or extreme depression, with a rapid effect (helps suicidal patients quickly). AEs: perceptual disturbances, dissocia…","Trazodone — Second line for depression; blocks serotonin reuptake; minimal antidepressant effect, so mostly used for anxiety or insomnia (a sleep aid).","Benzodiazepines (\"the -lams and -pams\", not always)","How and when","Enhance GABA's inhibitory effect in the CNS, so more relaxation.","For GAD and panic disorder, short-term only: temporarily stops symptoms (breakthrough anxiety or panic) but doesn't treat the underlying disorder.","Small prescriptions (e.g. 10 pills), no automatic renewals.","Schedule IV, moderate abuse risk; teratogenic (not in pregnancy).","Dangers & antidote","CNS depression, ↓ LOC, respiratory depression (more with IV); addictive, with withdrawal; memory loss.","Don't combine with other CNS depressants: Benadryl, alcohol, opioids, barbiturates (can stop breathing).","CYP450: avoid grapefruit and fatty foods (affect absorption).","Antidote: flumazenil.","Substance Use Disorder & Withdrawal","Addiction is a disease","Substance use disorder (SUD) is a disease of brain and behavior: a complex interaction of environmental, biological, genetic and brain-chemistry factors. It is not a personal failure or weakness.","Dopamine (reward): substances cause a big, rapid dopamine surge far beyond normal pleasure. Over time normal pathways downregulate, so more and more frequent use is needed for the same surge.","Also linked to prefrontal cortex changes.","Brain scans: dopamine-transporter activity is low after 1 month without meth and returns by 14 months, so brain changes are reversible.","Use person-first language (\"a person using meth\").","Risk vs protective factors","Risk: aggressive behavior in childhood, lack of parental supervision, poor social skills, drug experimentation, drug availability at school, community poverty.","Protective: good self-control, parental monitoring and support, positive relationships, good grades, school anti-drug policies, neighborhood resources.","Think upstream: these are proxies for social determinants of health. Addiction also depends on the drug itself (type, route, effect, age at first use, availability, cost).","Treatment principles","No single treatment fits everyone: individualize and treat the whole person.","Therapy is the crux: CBT, contingency management and positive reinforcement, motivational enhancement (readiness to change), family therapy, 12-step programs. Medication goes hand in hand with these.","Medications differ by substance: opioids (methadone, buprenorphine, naltrexone, lofexidine), nicotine (buprenorphine, nicotine replacement patches and gum), alcohol (naltrexone, disulfiram). The full chart isn't required.","Medically assisted detox and screening","Medically assisted detox: withdrawal in a structured, medically supervised setting; the safe way to manage acute, possibly life-threatening withdrawal. Not every rehab is medically assisted.","Screen for HIV/AIDS, hepatitis B and C, TB, endocarditis: no access to clean needles and equipment spreads bloodborne pathogens. Endocarditis is common with long-term IV drug use.","Methadone — Synthetic opioid, mu agonist: occupies the empty, craving opioid receptors, preventing cravings and withdrawal. Medication-assisted treatment for opioids, including heroin (people often move from prescription opioids to cheaper,…","Buprenorphine + naloxone (Suboxone) — Buprenorphine = partial opioid agonist (blocks receptors, prevents cravings; alone it is Subutex). Naloxone (Narcan) = opioid antagonist. The agonist–antagonist combination aids recovery and abstinence.…","Withdrawal timelines (know these for exams)","Opioids (heroin, prescription painkillers) — 12–24 h after the last dose — 72 h — 5–10 days; lessening at about 1 week, better and better by 1 month — No: miserable (\"feels like dying\") but not life-threatening.","Benzodiazepines (Xanax, Klonopin, Valium, Ativan; Ambien on the slide) — 6–12 h (one graphic says 1–4 days) — Week 2 — Subsides after about week 2 — Yes","Alcohol — About 8 h after the last drink — 1–3 days — Tapers off in about 1 week, up to several weeks — Yes","The benzodiazepine graphics differ: one says onset 1–4 days after the last dose, the other says 6–12 h with symptoms lasting 1–4 days. Both peak around week 2. Check which your slide emphasizes.","Opioid withdrawal","Severe flu-like symptoms: N/V, stomach cramps, diarrhea, goosebumps, depression, cravings, anxiety, sweating, muscle aches, fever.","Supportive treatment: treat each symptom (antiemetics, antipyretics) plus medication-assisted therapy.","Benzo & alcohol withdrawal (both act on GABA, treated the same)","Manageable: sleep disturbance, dry retching, irritability, nausea, tension, weight loss, anxiety, palpitations, panic attacks, headache, muscle aches, sweating, poor concentration, perceptual changes.","Dangerous: seizures, hallucinations, delirium tremens (DTs), Wernicke's encephalopathy.","Treat with a benzodiazepine taper (prevents seizures, lessens DTs), plus 12-step or therapy.","Alcohol: vitamin supplementation, especially thiamine (chronic stomach inflammation impairs absorption).","Wernicke's encephalopathy triad","Profound disorientation, indifference, inattention.","Oculomotor dysfunction: nystagmus, lateral rectus palsy, conjugate gaze palsy.","Gait ataxia.","Delirium tremens (DTs)","Hallucinations, disorientation.","Tachycardia, hypertension, fever.","Agitation, diaphoresis."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"final-nur326-review.html","t":"NUR 326 Review","secs":[{"s":"NUR 326 Review","a":"","k":["Final Exam Review · NUR 326","Whole-Course Quick Reference","Week 1: Antimicrobials & HIV","Week 2: Respiratory & Hematology","Week 3: Renal, Male Reproductive & Sensory","Week 4: Liver, Gallbladder & Pancreas","Week 5: GI & Endocrine","Week 6: Cardiovascular","Week 7: Cancer, Chemotherapy & Musculoskeletal","Self-Test Flashcards","This review is built only from your NUR 326 lectures (Weeks 1–7 voiceovers). Grey notes flag spots where a voiceover misspoke: check those against your slides. Use the hide toggles on any table to quiz yourself."]},{"s":"Whole-Course Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-course-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Course Quick Reference▾","Antidotes & Rescue Drugs","Acetaminophen overdose — Acetylcysteine — 2, 4","Iron overdose (common childhood poisoning) — Deferoxamine (chelates iron) — 2","Theophylline toxicity — Activated charcoal — 2","Hepatic encephalopathy (high ammonia) — Lactulose (titrate to 2–3 soft stools a day) — 4","Digoxin toxicity or overdose — Digoxin immune Fab — 6","Isoniazid peripheral neuropathy (prevention) — Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) — 2","Methotrexate (folate antagonist) — Folic acid supplement — 7","Numbers to Know","AIDS diagnosis — CD4 below 200 or any AIDS-defining condition (treated goal about 500–600, undetectable viral load) — 1","PEP — ART for 28 days after exposure; HIV tests at 6 and 12 weeks — 1","Flu antivirals — Start before or within 48 h of symptom onset — 1","Epoetin alfa — Give only if Hgb below 10 (black box) — 2, 3","GFR / oliguria — Normal above 90 / below 400 mL per day — 3","Levothyroxine — Empty stomach about 30 min before breakfast — 5","Ejection fraction — Normal 55–65%; HFrEF below 40% — 6","Digoxin — Apical pulse a full minute; hold if below 60 — 6","Sublingual nitroglycerin — Every 5 min, up to 3 doses; no relief 5 min after the first: call 911 — 6","Ankle-brachial index — Normal about 1–1.4; 0.5 or less = severe arterial disease — 6","Chemo nadir / prophylactic levofloxacin — About 7–14 days after a dose / ANC below 500 for more than 7 days — 7","HPV vaccine / colorectal screening — Best at 11–12 (up to 45) / start at 45 — 7","DEXA T-score — −1 or higher normal; −1 to −2.5 osteopenia; −2.5 or lower osteoporosis — 7","Raloxifene — Stop at least 72 h before a procedure or immobility — 7","Methotrexate for RA — Once WEEKLY (daily dosing can kill) — 7","Fracture nonunion — Not healed 4–6 months after the fracture — 7"]},{"s":"Week 1: Antimicrobials & HIV▾","a":"week-1-antimicrobials-hiv","k":["Week 1: Antimicrobials & HIV▾","Antibiotic principles, cell wall and intracellular antibiotics, antivirals, HIV and AIDS.","Intro to Antimicrobials","Bactericidal vs bacteriostatic — Bactericidal kills the bacteria; bacteriostatic only inhibits growth.","Broad spectrum — Effective against many organisms (gram + and gram −): the \"shotgun\" approach, used when the organism isn't known yet (not cultured).","Narrow spectrum — Effective against a few species: the \"BB gun\" approach; you need to know the organism and its sensitivity.","Selective toxicity — Toxic to specific cells while sparing normal cells nearby.","Resistance — The organism survives the antimicrobial or makes it ineffective: innate (always been that way) or acquired (mutated).","Superinfection — An infection during treatment for a primary infection: candidiasis, C. diff.","Prophylactic antibiotics — Prevent infection: high-risk surgery (knee replacement, cardiac, abdominal), history of endocarditis, immunocompromised or neutropenic patients (HIV, chemo).","Mechanisms (how the classes are grouped): inhibit cell wall synthesis; increase cell wall permeability; inhibit protein synthesis (kills the cell, or only stops function/reproduction); inhibit DNA synthesis or alter DNA function; disrupt a …","Choosing an antimicrobial","Community-acquired (easier to treat, more often viral) vs hospital-acquired (more often bacterial, more often resistant).","Susceptibility patterns and the suspected organism (the number one way to limit resistance).","Site: can the drug penetrate it (bone, lung, kidney; some concentrate in urine, good for UTIs)?","Patient: allergies, age extremes (lower doses), renal and liver function.","Dose and length depend on host defenses, site (an abscess takes longer than a UTI), organism, local vs systemic (sepsis).","Give it early; some drugs need peak and trough levels (e.g. vancomycin).","Cultures before antimicrobials","Goal: get cultures before starting, since the drug can keep the organism from growing in the culture.","Exceptions: critically ill or severe sepsis, and meningitis (start a drug that crosses the blood-brain barrier).","A culture order is stat: delegate if you're busy.","Sputum, urine or blood. Blood: two sets, aerobic and anaerobic bottles, preferably a peripheral stick (lines and ports carry surface bacteria); clean the skin and bottles well, since nurses often contaminate cultures.","Disk diffusion: no growth around the disk = effective. MIC = lowest concentration that inhibits growth; MBC = lowest that kills 99.9%. Reports usually say only \"susceptible\" or \"resistant\".","Allergy vs adverse effect","Nausea and vomiting are usually adverse effects (intolerance), not allergy.","True allergy: rash, welts, angioedema, anaphylaxis.","Cross-reactivity: penicillin allergy may mean cephalosporin allergy, especially if anaphylactic.","Penicillin and sulfa allergies are common; allergies can develop at any age.","Resistance & hospital infections","Driven by over-prescription. MRSA (methicillin resistant), CRE (whole carbapenem class), MDRO (multiple classes).","Mechanisms: innate, shared genetic material, mutation from exposure, not taking the full course (\"survival of the fittest\").","Hospital risk: illness, surgery, invasive devices (CLABSI, CAUTI, G-tubes), skin breakdown. Post-op: atelectasis raises pneumonia risk; wound dehiscence or evisceration.","Upper respiratory infections are mostly viral: about a 1 in 4,000 chance an antibiotic helps vs 1 in 1,000 chance of harm.","Environment: C. diff spores survive 5+ months on surfaces; VRE contaminates cushions; gowns 37–65% contaminated after care; white coats 69%.","Treatment failure: wrong drug, started too late, dose too low, too short, can't reach the site (blood-brain barrier).","Antibiotics: Cell Wall Inhibitors","Penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems and vancomycin all inhibit or damage cell wall synthesis: the weakened wall lets extracellular fluid rush in, so the cell swells and bursts (lysis and death). Learn them as classes and put each gener…","Beta-lactamase and its inhibitors: penicillins, cephalosporins and carbapenems share a beta-lactam ring. Some bacteria secrete beta-lactamase, an enzyme that breaks that ring so the antibiotic doesn't work. Beta-lactamase inhibitors (sulbac…","The voiceover calls these \"beta-lactam antibiotics\" and calls the enzyme \"beta-lactam\". It means beta-lactamase inhibitors and beta-lactamase.","Penicillins","Inhibit transpeptidase, so the wall becomes fragile and the bacteria autolyze; bacteria must be growing and dividing. Low toxicity, good for outpatients. Uses: UTIs, STIs, pneumonia, peritonitis, sepsis, meningitis; mostly gram-positive (st…","Natural — Penicillin G, penicillin V — Usually IV; gram-positive; half-life about 30 min (longer with kidney problems). Sometimes with aminoglycosides (work together). IM for STDs.","Penicillinase-resistant (anti-staph) — Nafcillin (drug of choice) — IV only; resists penicillinase, an enzyme common in staph.","Aminopenicillins — Ampicillin, amoxicillin (often with a beta-lactamase inhibitor) — Ampicillin: one of the first broad-spectrum drugs; PO or IV; diarrhea and rash; used less because of resistance. Amoxicillin: fewer side effects, common in…","Extended spectrum — Piperacillin (+ tazobactam = Zosyn) — Broadest penicillin; always with a beta-lactamase inhibitor; good for Pseudomonas. Can affect platelet function; caution in renal dysfunction.","Cephalosporins","Structurally similar to penicillin, same mechanism (transpeptidase, autolysis), broad coverage, low toxicity. Cross-sensitivity with penicillin allergy is rare (1–4%); avoid if anaphylactic to penicillin. Five generations: spectrum widens w…","The voiceover says cephalosporins are \"often resistant to beta-lactamase, meaning that they don't work against bacteria that secrete beta-lactamase,\" then says some bacteria secrete \"cephalosporinase.\" Check the slide for the intended wordi…","1st — Cefazolin (IV only); cephalexin (Keflex, PO or IV) — Gram-positive, staph; not enterococcus or strep. Cephalexin: skin infections, UTIs. Cefazolin: surgical prophylaxis. No CNS coverage (minimal CSF penetration).","2nd — Cefuroxime, cefoxitin, cefotetan — More gram-negative, still gram-positive; IV and PO. Not for anaerobes, CNS or Pseudomonas, so rarely used; some intestinal (abdominal) infections.","3rd — Ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, ceftazidime — Strong gram-negative, little gram-positive; mostly IV/IM. Ceftriaxone: very long acting, once a day, crosses the blood-brain barrier (meningitis); avoid without a working liver. Ceftazidime: Pseu…","4th — Cefepime (the only one) — Gram-negative and positive, very broad: often started for the very sick patient with an unknown infection; crosses the BBB; covers Pseudomonas.","5th — Ceftaroline — Multidrug-resistant staph; not Enterobacter, Pseudomonas, ESBL or Klebsiella. IV only, renally dosed: monitor BUN and creatinine.","Carbapenems (\"-penem\")","Broadest spectrum of all antibiotics: saved as a last resort to avoid resistance.","Unique AE: seizures. Use caution with a seizure history, the elderly, and other seizure-inducing drugs. All are IV, infused over 60 minutes.","Imipenem-cilastatin: very broad, resists beta-lactamase, crosses the BBB. Cilastatin inhibits renal dihydropeptidase, which would break imipenem down too fast.","Meropenem: a bit less coverage, doesn't degrade in the kidney (no cilastatin needed), less seizure activity, so used more often; some GI distress.","CRE (carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae): a deadly public health emergency, killing about 50%; a major infection-prevention concern, with very few drug options left.","Vancomycin (glycopeptide)","Immediate cell wall inhibition (not by autolysis). Gram-positive, MDR staph (MRSA), penicillin-resistant organisms. Not for meningitis/CNS.","PO vancomycin treats C. diff and pseudomembranous colitis only (it stays in the gut).","Nephrotoxic (IV): monitor renal labs, especially with IV contrast or other nephrotoxic drugs. Also ototoxic at high levels (reversible), immune thrombocytopenia (monitor platelets), caution with neuromuscular blockade.","Red man syndrome: from infusing too fast: rapid flushing, itchy rash, possibly tachycardia and hypotension. Slow the infusion; premedicate with Benadryl next time; tell the pharmacist or provider.","Peak and trough: they keep the drug in its therapeutic range. Peak = highest level, drawn about 30 min after the IV infusion is finished (infusion done at 1:00 PM, so draw about 1:30). Trough = lowest level, drawn about 30 min before the ne…","Antibiotics: Protein Synthesis & DNA Inhibitors","These drugs don't touch the cell wall: they alter protein synthesis (transcription or translation: nucleus, mRNA, ribosomes) or DNA, so the bacteria die, stop reproducing, or stop doing damage.","Aminoglycosides (\"-mycin\") — Gentamicin, tobramycin — Bind bacterial ribosomes, preventing protein synthesis. Potent, gram-negative (gram-positive usually only with other antibiotics). For complicated infections: hard-to-clear UTI or pyelon…","Lincosamides — Clindamycin — Binds ribosomes; bactericidal or bacteriostatic depending on concentration. Complicated infections: chronic bone, GU, intra-abdominal, anaerobic pneumonia. Enterobacteria are resistant (won't work for VRE/CRE). …","Macrolides — Erythromycin, azithromycin — Bind ribosomes; mostly bacteriostatic (kill at high concentration). STIs (gonorrhea), upper and lower respiratory and skin infections; unusual organisms that live inside host cells: Legionnaires', L…","Tetracyclines — Tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline — Bacteriostatic; bind ribosomes; broad spectrum but much resistance. Unusual infections: Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Rickettsia), chlamydia, trichomoniasis, Lyme disease, cholera. Do…","Fluoroquinolones — Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin (Levaquin) — Alter bacterial DNA (gyrase and topoisomerase enzymes), so they work on rapid- and slow-growing organisms. Potent, broad (mostly gram-negative), excellent oral absorption (can go h…","Sulfonamides — Sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim (Bactrim) — Bacteriostatic; blocks folic acid synthesis. First order for uncomplicated UTIs; respiratory infections, salmonella, shigellosis. Often given in HIV. — More adverse reactions in HIV. …","Metronidazole (Flagyl) — Metronidazole — Inhibits DNA synthesis; anaerobes and protozoa: amebiasis, giardiasis, trichomoniasis, intra-abdominal infections, H. pylori, Crohn's, and C. diff (with PO vancomycin). — No alcohol for 24 h before a…","The voiceover says gentamicin with a neuromuscular blocker can cause \"profound respiratory distress condition called Myasthenia Gravis\". The page keeps the respiratory distress warning; myasthenia gravis is a separate disease. It also calls…","Antivirals (Non-HIV)","Few antivirals exist: viruses replicate only inside host cells (no cell wall of their own), so a drug that kills the virus can kill healthy cells too. Most antivirals work only during the replication phase, which is often over by the time s…","Acyclovir (also valacyclovir, famciclovir) — Interferes with viral nucleic acid synthesis, prevents the virus binding to cells, and activates the immune system. Oral, genital herpes, shingles, chickenpox (varicella): initial and recurrent o…","Oseltamivir (Tamiflu) — Inhibits neuraminidase (a flu enzyme), stopping replication. Best for influenza A, some B; also approved for swine flu. Prophylaxis or treatment, started before or within 48 hours of symptom onset. Given mainly to th…","Ganciclovir — For CMV (cytomegalovirus), a concern only in the immunocompromised (AIDS, transplants); incurable and recurrent, and often fatal in lung transplant patients. CMV retinitis: can be injected into the eye. Inhibits viral DNA poly…","Herpes facts from the lecture","About 1% of people have genital herpes; it's twice as common in women.","HSV-1 above the waist, HSV-2 below.","Shingles (herpes zoster) affects 1 in 3 Americans; more concerning in the elderly.","Shingles vaccine","Shingrix (recombinant): for people 50 and older.","Reduces risk and the frequency and severity of outbreaks.","A very thick injection: it's painful.","The voiceover also calls the shingles vaccine \"a live vaccine\" and \"still a live virus\" while describing the newer recombinant vaccine. Check the slide.","HIV & AIDS","Definitions & epidemiology","HIV (human immunodeficiency virus): a retrovirus that destroys CD4 T cells. HIV-1 is the most prevalent and the one seen in the US; HIV-2 is less pathogenic and mostly confined to West Africa.","AIDS (acquired immunodeficiency syndrome) is caused by HIV: not everyone with HIV has AIDS, but everyone with AIDS has HIV.","About 1.1 million infected in the US. 76% of adults and adolescents with HIV are men. Black men who have sex with men are the highest-risk group; new cases are rising among women. Use \"men who have sex with men\" (less stigmatizing).","How HIV takes over","Binds cells with CD4 receptors (mainly T lymphocytes; also monocytes and macrophages), fuses and enters.","Reverse transcriptase makes a DNA copy of its RNA; integrase inserts it into the cell's DNA; protease helps assemble new virus, which buds out to infect more cells. These enzymes are the drug targets.","Infected for life. About 10 billion particles are made daily. Anti-HIV antibodies form but are ineffective: they are only a marker of infection.","Losing CD4 cells means losing cell-mediated (memory) immunity, then opportunistic infections and cancers.","1. Early (acute) infection — Rapid replication; 10–60% notice no symptoms, or nonspecific flu-like ones. Infectious from day one. Seroconversion: antibodies become detectable, the person is highly infectious, and flu-like symptoms can last …","2. Clinical latency (chronic) — Viral levels stabilize; asymptomatic or mild. Untreated, 3–12 years; with treatment it can last for decades. Eventually CD4 drops and viral load rises: they move inversely.","3. Symptomatic HIV, then AIDS — Fever, sore throat, swollen lymph nodes, rash, muscle aches, night sweats, mouth ulcers, chills, fatigue. Testing is driven by risk factors, since the symptoms are nonspecific.","AIDS is diagnosed by either: (1) CD4 count below 200, or (2) any AIDS-defining condition regardless of labs. Ones to memorize: Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP), TB (Mycobacterium), Kaposi sarcoma, invasive cervical cancer, pervasive (invasive) …","Oral health & dementia","Oral manifestations (fungal, viral, bacterial, cancerous; oral hairy leukoplakia on the tongue; periodontal disease) appear as CD4 drops, a sign of progression toward AIDS. Teach oral care and dental visits; consider masks in public.","HIV-associated dementia (AIDS dementia complex): HIV crosses the blood-brain barrier; once 40–60% of patients, now much less with treatment. AIDS-defining.","Transmission","Blood and body fluids: anal or vaginal sex without a condom (high risk; oral sex low to none); perinatal (25–30% untreated vs under 2% treated); shared needles; transfusions or transplants (rare now).","Not from toilet seats, hugging, saliva or kissing.","Risk rises with frequency and duration of contact and with viral load (high early, often before diagnosis), plus host immune status and genetics.","Health care exposure: splashes are near zero risk; a needlestick is still under 1%. Always report it; PEP may be needed.","Antiretroviral therapy (ART)","Know the principles, not individual drug names. Typical regimen: two NRTIs plus a third agent from another class. This has dramatically reduced morbidity and mortality, keeping patients in clinical latency for decades.","Fusion inhibitors, CCR5 antagonists — Block the virus from entering the cell.","NRTIs and NNRTIs — Inhibit reverse transcriptase, so viral RNA can't be incorporated into the host DNA. NRTI AEs: peripheral neuropathy, pancreatitis, lipoatrophy, hepatic steatosis; black box: lactic acidosis (monitor lactate for the first…","Protease inhibitors, integrase inhibitors — Block those viral enzymes.","Monitoring & goals","Start as soon as possible after diagnosis.","Goal: an undetectable viral load (effectively no transmission, but still HIV positive).","Monitor CD4 count (want it high; treated patients ideally about 500–600) and viral load (want it low). Low CD4 with high viral load is bad.","PrEP vs PEP","Pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP): daily antiretroviral (a 2-in-1 pill) for someone in a stable relationship with an HIV-positive partner; cuts transmission by more than 90%; not given to just anyone (resistance).","Post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP): ART for 28 days after an exposure, then HIV tests at 6 and 12 weeks. Also used after high-risk health care exposures."]},{"s":"Week 2: Respiratory & Hematology▾","a":"week-2-respiratory-hematology","k":["Week 2: Respiratory & Hematology▾","URIs, pneumonia, TB, obstructive airway disease and its drugs, anemias and polycythemia.","Upper Respiratory Infections","Sputum is mucus that traps particles; cilia move it out with coughing. Sputum can be cultured, but yellow sputum alone doesn't mean an antibiotic is needed. Purulent usually means infection.","Acute (simple) bronchitis — Inflammation of the bronchi and bronchioles (not alveoli); about 80% viral; no airflow obstruction. Starts as a cold: sore throat (pharyngeal erythema), runny nose, aches, productive cough, sometimes wheezing or …","Influenza — Viral; types A and B most common; they mutate. Rapid onset: fever, chills, body aches. — The vaccine changes yearly; it prevents severe illness (not all infection), cutting severe outcomes including death in adults by about 36%.…","Allergic rhinitis — IgE on mast cells triggers histamine release: sneezing, runny nose, itching, congestion, watery itchy eyes; upper and lower airways and eyes. — Avoid triggers; antihistamines.","Sinusitis — Swollen sinuses obstructed by fluid and edema let bacteria grow; more likely with nasal polyps, a deviated septum or allergies. Distinctive: headache and facial pain or pressure, plus ear and dental pain, decreased smell, cough.…","Pharyngitis / tonsillitis — Inflammation of the palate, tonsils and uvula. White spots suggest bacterial (strep); viral usually has none. Swollen tonsils can nearly touch, making swallowing hard. — Determine whether it's strep.","Laryngitis — Inflammation of the larynx (vocal cords). Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis): the classic barking cough. — Goes along with other URIs.","Epiglottitis: an emergency. Mostly children. Rapid fever, severe sore throat, difficulty swallowing, drooling, inspiratory stridor, retractions, muffled voice, prefers sitting up. The absence of a barking cough distinguishes it from croup. …","Histamine","Stored in mast cells (skin, soft tissue) and basophils (blood).","Causes hives and itching, vasodilation (redness, low BP if systemic), bronchoconstriction, sleep–wake effects, ↑ gastric acid.","Responsible for most allergic reaction symptoms (drugs, foods, contact).","H1 receptors: smooth muscle contraction and capillary dilation (the target of antihistamines). H2: heart rate and gastric acid (H2 blockers for GERD).","Bronchospasm","Sudden constriction of bronchial muscles.","Triggers: asthma history, a URI, especially before anesthesia (procedures may be delayed); ventilated and PACU patients.","Sudden trouble breathing, dropping sats, wheezing.","Treat with an inhaled bronchodilator; an inhaled steroid and anticholinergic longer term.","URI pharmacology (mostly OTC: know that patients take them and teach safe use)","H1 antihistamines, 1st generation (sedating) — Diphenhydramine (Benadryl); meclizine, promethazine, dimenhydrinate (motion sickness, vertigo, nausea) — Block H1 receptors; palliative (treat symptoms, not the cause). Mild allergic reactions,…","H1 antihistamines, 2nd generation (non-sedating) — Loratadine, fexofenadine, cetirizine — Same uses; fewer side effects and less drowsiness (some CNS effect at high doses); can be taken in the morning.","Decongestants (sympathomimetics) — Pseudoephedrine, phenylephrine — Stimulate alpha receptors, causing vasoconstriction that shrinks nasal vessels and opens passages; drying. SNS/CNS stimulation: agitation, anxiety, feeling \"wired\". No more…","Antitussives (cough suppressants) — Dextromethorphan (OTC), codeine (opioid, Rx), benzonatate (Tessalon Perles, Rx) — Suppress the cough reflex in the brain. Coughing is usually helpful; suppress it for a dry, nonproductive or painful cough…","Expectorant — Guaifenesin (Mucinex) — Reduces the surface tension of secretions so mucus is easier to cough up; effectiveness questionable. Hydration is the best expectorant. Some GI upset; caution with chronic cough and asthma.","Mucolytic — Acetylcysteine — Lowers mucus viscosity; for chronic bronchopulmonary disease and cystic fibrosis (also the acetaminophen-overdose antidote). Smells like rotten eggs. Best by nebulizer or tracheostomy. Can cause bronchospasm: mo…","Antihistamine cautions from the lecture (they can raise BP): closed-angle glaucoma, cardiac and kidney disease, hypertension, bronchospasm, COPD, peptic ulcer disease, seizures, BPH, pregnancy. Controlled hypertension is usually fine.","Pneumonia","Pneumonia = any infection of the lower respiratory tract after the upper airway defenses (cilia, mucus, cough) fail. It can be viral, bacterial, fungal, protozoal or parasitic. Inflammation fills the alveoli with purulent material, fibrin a…","Risk factors","Age extremes: children under 5, adults over 70–80.","Compromised immunity: long-term steroids, transplants, HIV.","Underlying lung disease (COPD, pulmonary hypertension); smoking impairs ciliary clearance.","Aspiration risk: alcoholism, altered LOC, impaired swallowing.","Nursing home residents, immobility, hospitalization, thoracic or abdominal surgery, anesthesia, intubation.","Influenza: the most common cause.","CAP vs HAP","CAP (community-acquired): a leading reason for hospitalization; usually easier to treat. HCAP (health care–associated: nursing homes, dialysis, chemo) falls under CAP.","HAP (hospital-acquired): develops 48+ hours after admission; worse outcomes, ICU-associated, more virulent and deadly. VAP (ventilator-associated) in intubated patients.","Aspiration of oropharyngeal secretions is the most common route; droplets are the other.","Patho: the pathogen reaches the lower airway, triggering an inflammatory reaction; lung vessels dilate; goblet cells secrete mucus that accumulates around the alveoli and pulmonary capillaries; alveoli can't open and close properly, so gas …","Presentation","Usually preceded by a URI. Fever, chills, cough, malaise, pleuritic pain; severe cases bring dyspnea and hemoptysis.","Bacterial (usually hospital-acquired): productive, purulent sputum: green, rusty, or \"currant jelly\".","Viral (usually community-acquired): nonproductive, scant cough.","Respiratory distress: still oxygenating, but through more work (accessory muscles, frequent breaks, ↑ HR, can't talk well). Respiratory failure: can no longer compensate, so expect arrest and CPR.","Diagnosis","Assessment: wet breath sounds (rhonchi), inspiratory crackles, dullness on percussion over consolidation, ↑ tactile fremitus, egophony (prolonged \"E\" heard over the lung), chest pain, exercise intolerance.","Chest X-ray: an infiltrate (white consolidation where the lung should be black).","CBC with diff: WBC rise (bacterial vs viral).","Gold standard: sputum culture and sensitivity (identifies the pathogen and the drug).","Gram-positive bacterial — Staph aureus (MRSA): enters through the bloodstream via IV routes; the most common gram-positive HAP. Strep pneumoniae (pneumococcal): the most frequent CAP bacterium; brown or rusty sputum.","Gram-negative bacterial — Make you sicker and are harder to treat: Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella. Hospital-acquired, often from central lines; also IV drug use.","Aspiration pneumonia — GI contents enter the alveoli; more acidic aspirate means more inflammation, so PPIs are given to reduce acidity. Risk: altered LOC, alcohol, immobility, NG tubes, decreased gag reflex, slow gastric emptying. Aspirati…","Viral — Influenza is the most common cause (and the top cause of CAP); also adenovirus, RSV, parainfluenza. It weakens lung defenses, so secondary bacterial pneumonia can follow. Supportive care (Tylenol or ibuprofen, decongestants, mucolyt…","Atypical: Pneumocystis (PCP) — A yeast-like fungus in immunosuppression, most common in HIV.","Atypical: Mycoplasma pneumoniae — \"Walking pneumonia\": mild; persistent cough, headache, earache; has features of both bacteria and viruses.","Atypical: Legionella (Legionnaires') — Gram-negative; spread by water systems (old air conditioners, misted produce, hot tubs); can be deadly. Report to the health department to find the source.","Fungal: Aspergillus — From old building walls, reconstruction, stored grains, dead leaves, compost; deadly in the immunocompromised.","Treatment: antibiotics for bacterial pneumonia; supportive care for viral (unless a secondary bacterial pneumonia develops). Adequate ventilation and oxygenation, hydration, pulmonary hygiene, nebulizers. Vaccines: PCV13 (13 pneumococcal st…","Tuberculosis","Patho","Any infection by Mycobacterium; M. tuberculosis is most common (M. leprae = leprosy).","Aerobic rod-shaped bacillus that needs lots of oxygen, so it favors the lungs (can also affect bone and brain).","Granulomas (tubercles): walled-off clusters of macrophages and lymphocytes around cheesy caseous tissue breakdown.","Airborne droplets from coughing and sneezing (mainly human-to-human); spreads to the lungs through blood and lymph.","Slow-growing, which makes it hard to treat.","Latent vs active","Normal immunity usually contains it, with no disease.","Latent TB: bacilli dormant in granulomas, no signs or symptoms. It can reactivate when immunity drops: HIV, immunosuppressants (RA, autoimmune disease), poor nutrition, renal failure.","Active TB: gradual, often unnoticed until advanced: fatigue, weight loss, lethargy, anorexia, low-grade afternoon fever, slowly worsening productive cough over months, night sweats (most suggestive), anxiety. Later: dyspnea, chest pain, hem…","Extrapulmonary TB: neurologic signs or meningitis (brain), bone pain, urinary problems.","Screening: high-risk people get the interferon-gamma release assay (blood draw); the non-high-risk public (including students) gets the TB skin test. A positive test is not a diagnosis: next a chest X-ray (granulomas), then a sputum stain a…","Drug therapy principles: first-line drugs (the 5 below) are used unless resistance is suspected; second-line drugs are reserved for resistant cases and are less effective (names not needed). Start a broad 4-drug first-line regimen, then nar…","Isoniazid (INH) — Most widely used; disrupts mycobacterial cell wall synthesis. PO. — Peripheral neuropathy (give vitamin B6, pyridoxine); liver-metabolized, so hepatotoxicity (monitor liver enzymes; black box: hepatitis); optic neuritis (v…","Rifampin — Inhibits protein synthesis. Also for meningitis, HIV-related infections, leprosy. PO or IV. — Hepatitis (monitor LFTs, especially with INH); hematologic problems (bleeding, bleeding times). Red-orange-brown urine, tears, sweat (h…","Ethambutol — Bacteriostatic; suppresses RNA, then protein synthesis. PO only; combined with INH and rifampin (a 3-in-1 pill exists). — Retrobulbar (optic) neuritis: monitor vision, frequent eye exams. Not for children under 13.","Pyrazinamide (PZA) — Static or cidal by concentration; thought to inhibit lipid and nucleic acid synthesis. Never alone. PO. — Hepatotoxicity and hyperuricemia: contraindicated in severe liver disease and acute gout. Contraindicated in preg…","Streptomycin — The first TB drug; an aminoglycoside causing faulty protein synthesis. — Ototoxic, nephrotoxic, blood dyscrasias (bleeding times). IM only (daily injection); caution with anticoagulants.","Drug-resistant TB (MDR-TB): a growing national and global problem, especially in HIV/AIDS and other at-risk groups (homeless, malnourished, substance users, cancer, immunosuppressed, crowded or poorly sanitized housing). Higher risk among i…","Obstructive Airway Disease: Asthma, Chronic Bronchitis & Emphysema","Obstruction = narrowed airways; worse on expiration, so air gets trapped. The result: ↑ work of breathing (accessory muscles), V/Q mismatch, hypoxemia, hypoventilation and hypercapnia, and hyperinflation on chest X-ray. Measured by FEV1 (ai…","What it is — Chronic inflammation of the bronchial airways (not alveoli); hyperresponsive, variable, usually reversible obstruction — Mucus hypersecretion and productive cough 3 months a year for at least 2 consecutive years (often winter);…","Main cause / risk — Childhood, allergy, family link (100+ genes), urban living, pollution, tobacco smoke, viral infections, GERD, immune imbalance — Smoking (about 90%); smoking plus vaping = 6× risk — Smoking and secondhand smoke, air poll…","Patho — A trigger causes inflammation (mast cells, eosinophils and others): mucus hypersecretion, smooth-muscle bronchoconstriction, swelling. Repeated, untreated attacks cause airway remodeling (chronic, irreversible). Inflammation is the …","Signs — Wheezing, cough, dyspnea, chest tightness. Severe: accessory muscles, diminished or absent breath sounds, diaphoresis, can't speak, silent chest (respiratory failure). — Persistent productive cough (purulent if infected); frequent r…","Diagnosis — History; PFTs (gold standard): ↓ expiratory flow and FEV1. Often misdiagnosed (heart failure, CF, vocal cord dysfunction). — History, exam, chest imaging, PFTs (↓ FEV1). Usually irreversible by the time patients seek care. — PFT…","Treatment — Avoid triggers; peak flow meter and action plan (green, yellow, red, by % of personal best); SABA for mild; oral corticosteroids for exacerbations; ICS, long-acting inhalers, leukotriene antagonists; immunotherapy (allergy shots…","Asthma triggers & the late response","Most common trigger: exercise (people with asthma can still exercise, with awareness). Also secondhand smoke, climate, dust mites, pet dander, pollen.","Early response: mediators cause vasodilation, capillary permeability, edema, bronchoconstriction and mucus.","Late response: recruited eosinophils, neutrophils and lymphocytes release mediators again hours later. Teach: after an attack, keep meds close and limit the trigger.","Patients often underestimate their severity: education matters.","Status asthmaticus","An unrelenting attack: silent chest (no air movement); blood CO₂ above 70.","A life-threatening emergency; often needs IV epinephrine.","Mainstays for acute attacks: bronchodilators, corticosteroids, oxygen.","The voiceover places the late asthmatic response \"48 hours later\", then describes it happening at dinnertime the same day. Check the slide for the timing.","\"Blue bloaters\" vs \"pink puffers\": chronic bronchitis (blue bloater) patients tend to be heavier, cyanotic, edematous, wheezy, with elevated hemoglobin, and are more hypoxic. Emphysema (pink puffer) patients are thin, with severe dyspnea, q…","Obstructive Airway Pharmacology","Rescue vs prevention: the only rescue drug is the short-acting beta agonist (albuterol). A patient who is wheezing and can't breathe right now gets albuterol. Everything else (LABAs, anticholinergics, xanthines, inhaled corticosteroids, leu…","Bronchodilators (relax bronchial smooth muscle)","Beta-2 agonists (\"-terol\") — SABA: albuterol, levalbuterol. LABA: salmeterol, formoterol. — Mimic the SNS: stimulate beta-2 receptors in the lungs, causing bronchodilation (selective beta-2 is preferred; non-selective drugs like epinephrine…","Anticholinergics — Ipratropium (+ albuterol = Combivent / DuoNeb) — Block acetylcholine (PNS), so less bronchoconstriction and mucus. Prophylactic, not rescue; 3–4 times daily or once daily. Anticholinergic effects: dry as a bone, hot as a …","Xanthine derivatives (methylxanthines) — Theophylline, aminophylline — Inhibit phosphodiesterase, so ↑ cAMP: smooth muscle relaxation, and inhibits IgE-driven mediator release. Second line (toxicity, many interactions). Metabolized to caffe…","Inhaler teaching","Inhaled delivery minimizes systemic effects.","MDI (metered dose): not breath-activated; inhale slowly and deeply; harder to use.","DPI (dry powder): breath-activated; easier for patients with cognitive problems or young children.","Nebulizer: common in the hospital for acute exacerbations.","Order of inhalers","Take the bronchodilator first, then the inhaled corticosteroid: the opened airways absorb the steroid better.","Rinse the mouth after inhaled steroids.","Anti-inflammatories & others","Leukotriene receptor antagonists (modifiers) — Montelukast (Singulair, kids over 12 months), zafirlukast (kids over 5) — Block leukotrienes (released by mast cells), which cause inflammation, bronchoconstriction and mucus. PO, chewables or …","Inhaled corticosteroids — Beclomethasone, budesonide, fluticasone — Suppress the immune response in the airway with fewer systemic effects than PO steroids (PO prednisone is used short-term for exacerbations). Nebulizer or MDI. Take on a re…","Mast cell stabilizer — Cromolyn — Stabilizes mast cell membranes, preventing mediator release. Take 15–20 min before a known trigger (a game, the gym, a pumpkin patch).","Monoclonal antibody — Omalizumab — Binds IgE, limiting mediator release in allergic asthma; add-on only; injection. Monitor closely for hypersensitivity: significant anaphylaxis risk.","Selective PDE4 inhibitor — Roflumilast — Potent anti-inflammatory in the lungs; prevents COPD exacerbations, best for chronic bronchitis with frequent exacerbations. PO; not a bronchodilator. AEs: N/V/D, headache, muscle spasms, ↓ appetite,…","Anemia","Anemia = too few RBCs, or poor quality or quantity of hemoglobin, from impaired production, blood loss, RBC destruction, or a combination. Whatever the cause, less oxygen reaches the tissues. Hemoglobin (heme with iron at its center) revers…","Normocytic, normochromic — Normal size and color — Blood loss, sickle cell, aplastic","Microcytic, hypochromic — Small and pale (less hemoglobin); ↓ MCV — Iron deficiency (also thalassemia)","Macrocytic (megaloblastic), normal color — Large, abnormal, immature; ↑ MCV — B12 or folate deficiency (both needed for RBC DNA synthesis)","The voiceover calls iron deficiency \"a macrocytic anemia because those red blood cells are smaller than normal\". Small cells are microcytic. It also describes B12 cells as \"pale, large\", while the size and color chart lists macrocytic as no…","Hemoglobin is the best indicator of RBCs.","Hematocrit is much more affected by fluid volume.","Normal values are given on exams.","People usually don't seek care until levels are very low, because the body compensates well.","Signs of every anemia (all from low O₂ delivery)","Mild: none.","Mild to moderate: fatigue, weakness, slight tachycardia, SOB.","Moderate to severe: tachycardia, tachypnea, low BP, pallor (blood shunted from skin to vital organs), faintness, cardiovascular symptoms on exertion, palpitations.","Very severe: bone pain (chronic), chest pain, heart failure.","Speed matters: a slow GI bleed is better tolerated than a sudden 2 L loss at the same Hgb.","Iron deficiency (most common in the US) — ↓ intake, impaired absorption, ↑ demand, or loss (GI bleed, menstruation). Toddlers, adolescent girls, women of childbearing age, pregnancy, poverty. — Glossitis (smooth tongue), cheilosis (mouth so…","B12 deficiency (pernicious anemia) — Most common in the elderly. Atrophic gastritis, or autoimmune destruction of parietal cells, means no intrinsic factor, so B12 can't be absorbed. Also Crohn's, celiac, bacterial overgrowth, parasites. Cl…","Folate (B9) deficiency — Decreased intake (not absorption; no intrinsic factor needed). Pregnancy and breastfeeding (most common group); malnutrition; alcoholism and cirrhosis (folate is stored in the liver). — Low folate in pregnancy: low …","Anemia of CKD — Kidneys can't make erythropoietin, so the bone marrow isn't signaled to make RBCs. — Worse kidney failure means worse anemia (Hgb tracks kidney function); kidney failure signs, no unique anemia signs.","Aplastic anemia — Bone marrow failure (rare, dangerous): mostly autoimmune attack on stem cells; congenital or acquired; idiopathic; toxins (chemo, radiation, benzene, insecticides), marrow cancer, after viral hepatitis or mono. — Pancytope…","Acquired hemolytic anemia — Premature RBC destruction by an external agent: autoimmune, blood incompatibility, drug reactions, hereditary, burns, microangiopathies (like type II/III hypersensitivity). — Low Hgb, ↑ reticulocyte count (immatu…","Sickle cell anemia — Genetic, autosomal recessive; hemoglobin S; mostly people of African (sub-Saharan) descent (7–13% of African Americans per the lecture). Cells sickle when oxygen is low, die early and clog vessels. — Swelling of hands a…","Thalassemia — Genetic, autosomal recessive; lacks alpha or beta globin. Beta: Greek, Italian, Jewish; alpha: Asian (Chinese, Vietnamese, Cambodian). More severe than sickle cell. — Microcytic, hypochromic; delayed growth, fatigue, enlarged …","Blood loss — Hemorrhage (trauma) or internal bleeding: gross (obvious) or occult (GI, retroperitoneal). — Symptoms depend on speed: rapid loss can't be compensated (about 50% loss means shock and death; about 20% may cause no symptoms at re…","The voiceover says aplastic \"a\" means \"all\". The page follows its point (all blood cell lines are affected, i.e. pancytopenia). It also says \"we give everyone A negative\" when it means O-negative.","Anemia Pharmacology & Polycythemia","Severe or rapid-loss anemia often needs a blood transfusion. These drugs treat longer-term nutritional deficiencies and CKD anemia.","Ferrous sulfate (PO iron) — Treat and prevent iron deficiency anemia (diet is usually enough; supplements in pregnancy and for menstruating females who are deficient). — Best absorbed on an empty stomach (food at first if GI upset is bad, t…","Iron dextran (IV/IM) — Severe deficiency or can't tolerate PO. — The dextran can cause fatal anaphylaxis: give a test dose first with epinephrine available (not given at home). IM: Z-track (stains skin).","Cyanocobalamin (B12) — B12 deficiency. Diet: meat, salmon, cod, milk and dairy (if the stomach works). — Large daily oral doses (1–2 mg) work as well as injection; also intranasal, subQ or IM (weekly until near normal, then monthly; faster …","Folic acid — Folate deficiency; every pregnant person (prenatal vitamins). Diet: leafy greens, legumes, organ meat, fortified grains. — OTC at least 400 mcg/day; Rx 1–5 mg. Very well tolerated. Folic acid can fix the anemia but mask a B12 d…","Epoetin alfa (Epogen), erythropoiesis-stimulating agent (longer-acting: darbepoetin) — Anemia of CKD (kidneys don't make erythropoietin): stimulates erythropoiesis, ↑ Hgb and reticulocytes. — Black box: give only if Hgb is below 10; goal ab…","Polycythemia (too many RBCs)","Relative — Hemoconcentration from dehydration (low plasma volume): a false elevation of Hgb, Hct and RBC percentage. — Correct the cause (fluids).","Primary: polycythemia vera — Rare, slow-growing blood cancer / stem cell disorder (chronic myeloproliferative): one mutated stem cell overproduces RBCs, WBCs and platelets (not lymphocytes). Over 60; men 2×. — Headache, fatigue, weight loss…","Secondary — Compensatory response to tissue hypoxia: the kidneys secrete erythropoietin and the marrow makes more RBCs. Causes: COPD (biggest group), high altitude (chronic mountain disease), long-term smoking, genetics, chronic gas exposur…","The main danger in absolute (primary or secondary) polycythemia: clots. Thick, high-volume blood brings HTN, headache, poor concentration, ruddy color (sometimes cyanosis of lips and nails), DVT, angina, TIAs, stroke and MI; also hypermetab…","The voiceover lists \"long-term exposure to carbon dioxide\" for tunnel workers, coal miners and garage attendants. Check the slide: it may mean carbon monoxide."]},{"s":"Week 3: Renal, Male Reproductive & Sensory▾","a":"week-3-renal-male-reproductive-sensory","k":["Week 3: Renal, Male Reproductive & Sensory▾","Pyelonephritis, stones, glomerular disease, AKI and CKD, BPH and ED, eye and ear disorders.","Renal: Pyelonephritis, Obstruction & Kidney Stones","Review your A&P and the NUR 325 lower UTI content: per the lecture, it is testable.","Pyelonephritis (upper UTI / kidney infection)","Inflammation of one or both kidneys; usually ascending from a lower UTI (or from a bloodstream infection). E. coli is most common.","Kidneys swell with infectious exudate; if severe, the renal artery is compressed: necrosis, abscess. Chronic or recurrent infection can cause CKD and sepsis.","Risk: pregnancy, recurrent lower UTIs, antibiotic-resistant strains.","Sudden onset: looks systemically sick, with fever and chills, CVA (costovertebral angle) tenderness, plus lower UTI symptoms (dysuria, frequency, urgency, hematuria), N/V, anorexia.","Treatment: antibiotics (community-acquired: Bactrim, ciprofloxacin, or nitrofurantoin), short course, or 7–14 days if recurrent; IV inpatient if severe. Urinalysis and culture for sensitivity; start broad, then narrow.","Complication: urosepsis (high mortality), especially in the elderly. Pregnancy complicates drug choice (usually not Bactrim or cipro).","Urinary obstruction","Anywhere in the tract: renal pelvis (mostly stones), ureter (stones, pregnancy, tumors), bladder or urethra (bladder cancer, neurogenic bladder, prostate, stricture).","Complications: urinary stasis leading to infection (recurrent UTIs or pyelonephritis); back-up pressure causing hydroureter / hydronephrosis and post-renal AKI. Fix the blockage (often surgery; may be an emergency).","Presentation depends on site, size, internal vs external cause, and speed of onset (the main factor in severity): a stone in the roomy renal pelvis may not hurt; once it enters the narrow ureter, pain is severe and comes in waves. A slowly …","Nephrolithiasis (renal calculi / kidney stones)","The most common cause of urinary obstruction. Crystals form in the nephron (kidney level) when urine is supersaturated with a solute; size ranges from tiny to golf ball, smooth or jagged. Promoters: pH changes (frequent UTIs), high urinary …","Calcium (oxalate or phosphate) — Most common (70–80%); often idiopathic; family history, high blood calcium, high-oxalate diet, high sodium and protein intake. — Thiazide diuretics","Struvite (\"staghorn\") — Related to UTIs. — Antibiotics (plus pain control and fluids)","Uric acid — Related to gout. — Treat the gout: allopurinol","Cystine — Less than 1% (no detail needed). — —","Renal colic","Flank pain radiating to the lower abdomen or groin.","Spasmodic: 20–60 min, relief, then again (ureteral spasms); sharp (the stone scrapes the ureter).","N/V, difficulty urinating, hematuria, pale, diaphoretic.","Fever and chills only if infected, which suggests pyelonephritis from urine backing up.","Treatment","Pain is the main goal: NSAID (IV ibuprofen) for some; most need an opioid (morphine).","Hydration / IV fluids dilute the urine, prevent re-forming, and help pass the stone.","Recurrent stones: prevention by stone type (above).","Renal: Urologic Cancers & Glomerular Disease","Kidney cancer (renal cell carcinoma)","85% of kidney cancers; kidney cancer is under 5% of all cancers.","Risk: smoking (#1), obesity, older age, men, genetics.","No early signs: usually found after metastasis (sometimes by chance on a CT), so 5-year survival is about 10% once metastasized.","Late signs: CVA tenderness, cola-colored hematuria, palpable abdominal mass. Metastasizes to bone and lung (dyspnea, cough, bone pain).","Usually chemo-resistant. Not metastasized: remove the kidney (the other compensates). Metastasized: chemo and radiation.","Bladder cancer (urothelial carcinoma)","The 4th most common cancer in men.","Risk: smoking (#1), male, occupational toxins (rubber, paint), low fluid intake (toxins sit concentrated in the bladder; more fluid flushes them).","Early: pink or red hematuria (may be microscopic, found on UA). Later: UTI-like frequency, urgency, dysuria.","Stage 1: intravesical therapy; advanced: systemic chemo.","BCG (intravesical, for early bladder cancer): a live vaccine instilled into the bladder (empty the bladder, in-and-out catheter) that triggers a local inflammatory response so the immune system attacks cancer cells. Weekly for 6–12 weeks. K…","Glomerular disease","The glomerulus: arterioles in Bowman's capsule; the afferent arteriole brings blood in, the efferent takes it away; the pressure gradient pushes wastes into the urine. Filtration takes a lot of oxygen (the kidneys get about 20% of cardiac o…","Glomerulonephritis — Inflammation of the glomeruli (focal or diffuse); 3rd leading cause of kidney failure in the US (about 25% of end-stage cases); immune-mediated. Type III (most common): circulating antigen–antibody complexes deposit in …","Signs: HARP — Hematuria, Azotemia (waste build-up), Retention of sodium and water (edema, ↑ BP), Proteinuria; ↓ GFR.","Triggers — Post-strep (most common): antibodies mistake the GBM for an antigen; other bacterial, viral or parasitic infections; Berger's disease (IgA builds up in the glomerulus); systemic autoimmune disease: lupus, vasculitis, Goodpasture …","Chronic glomerulonephritis — Inflammation lasting months to a year: scar tissue can't filter, a slow progression to ESRD and dialysis.","Nephrotic syndrome — The glomerulus becomes too permeable to plasma proteins: more than 3 g of protein/day on a 24-hour urine. Causes: glomerulonephritis, uncontrolled diabetes. Albumin loss means ↓ oncotic pressure, so severe edema / third…","Glomerulopathy (diabetic, hypertensive) — Diabetes thickens the GBM (filters poorly); hypertension scars (sclerosis) the glomerulus. Hematuria, oliguria (under 400 mL/day), fluid retention, harder-to-control HTN, proteinuria with low blood …","For glomerulopathy the voiceover says the BUN:creatinine ratio will be \"higher than 20 to one\" and that this points to a kidney issue. Compare with the AKI lecture's pre-renal vs intrarenal BUN:creatinine teaching.","Acute Kidney Injury & Chronic Kidney Disease","What the kidneys do","Maintain fluid and electrolyte homeostasis (BP, third spacing).","Excrete water-soluble wastes and drugs in urine.","Endocrine: make erythropoietin (RBCs), activate vitamin D (calcium, bone), make renin (RAAS, BP).","Very \"needy\": about 1 L/min of blood (about 20% of cardiac output) for 0.5% of body mass, so injury can happen within hours of poor perfusion; often reversible.","GFR is the indicator of kidney function: normal above 90; below 60 suggests a problem; about 25% of normal (about 25–30) = renal insufficiency. Prolonged insufficiency progresses to chronic disease.","AKI classifications","Pre-renal (most common): volume loss before the kidney: blood loss, dehydration, third spacing (sepsis).","Intrarenal: direct kidney damage: acute tubular necrosis (ATN) from toxins, overdose or nephrotoxic drugs; acute glomerulonephritis; vascular disease (diabetes, HTN).","Post-renal (least common): obstruction (stone, tumor, blockage).","The injury also triggers an inflammatory response that can cause more cell death.","The voiceover says insufficiency is \"25 to 30 mL per hour\" when describing GFR (GFR is per minute), and first says oliguria is \"less than 400 mL an hour\" before correcting to 400 mL in 24 hours.","AKI presentation","Oliguria: under 400 mL/24 h (under 200 mL a shift, about 30 mL/h), beginning about 1 day after a hypotensive event and lasting 1–3 weeks.","Severe: fluid volume excess, metabolic acidosis, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, azotemia / uremia (wastes build up), neurologic changes.","Treatment: fix the cause (replace volume; antidote or flush for toxins), treat electrolytes, avoid nephrotoxic drugs, monitor labs.","CKD causes & staging","Staged by GFR: stages 1–2 usually asymptomatic; moderate brings HTN; severe brings the other manifestations; stage 5 = uremic (\"urine in the blood\").","ESKD causes: diabetes about 50%, hypertension about 30%, other about 20% (glomerulonephritis, AKI). Best treatment is prevention: control glucose and BP.","Risk: family history, CAD, hyperlipidemia, atherosclerosis, over 60, men, Black Americans, smoking, obesity.","Cycle of worsening: proteinuria (leaky GBM; proteins injure tubules and drive inflammation) and ↑ angiotensin II (vasoconstriction, HTN, hyperfiltration, more proteinuria). Watch BP and urine.","Fluid and electrolyte balance — Edema, third spacing, pulmonary edema (the biggest concern; often the signal to start dialysis); hyperkalemia (big one), hyperphosphatemia, hypermagnesemia; metabolic acidosis.","Excreting waste — Anorexia and malnutrition, N/V, GI bleeding and gastritis; pruritus, dry scaly skin, uremic frost; confusion and mental status changes (azotemia crosses the BBB); fatigue, headache, sleep problems, encephalopathy; anxiety …","Making erythropoietin — Chronic anemia (Hgb can reach 5–6, often well tolerated because it develops slowly).","Activating vitamin D — Renal osteodystrophy: weak bones, fractures.","Renin / RAAS — HTN, leading to heart failure, CAD; also pericarditis, PAD.","CKD pharmacotherapy (slow progression, treat complications; nothing reverses CKD)","Blood pressure — ACE inhibitor or ARB (never both), others as needed — Goal systolic below 140 (aim about 110–130), diastolic below 90; lower slowly (the kidneys are used to higher pressure).","Lipids — Statin, diet — Cholesterol below 200.","Volume overload — Loop diuretic (furosemide), 2 g sodium diet; dialysis — Know loop diuretic complications and how to judge effect.","Hyperkalemia — Diuretics, potassium-lowering drugs, dialysis — Dysrhythmia risk.","Metabolic acidosis — Sodium bicarbonate (PO) — Alkaline agent that raises bicarb and pH. Start when bicarb is below 15; goal 18–20. Slows CKD, protects bone, improves nutrition. AE: bloating.","Hyperphosphatemia — Calcium carbonate (Tums) — Binds phosphate (calcium and phosphate move inversely). Take with meals. Can cause hypercalcemia: monitor calcium and phosphate.","Renal osteodystrophy — Calcitriol (activated vitamin D) — Stimulates intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate for bone mineralization. AEs: hypercalcemia (know its signs: GI upset, nausea) and hyperphosphatemia.","Anemia — Epoetin alfa — Know the black box (only if Hgb below 10).","Renal dosing: poor kidney function means drugs are eliminated slowly, so doses and frequency are adjusted: digoxin, diabetic drugs (glyburide, metformin), vancomycin (renal dosing protocol), and opioids. The most concerning drug in severe C…","The voiceover says sodium bicarbonate will \"decrease the pH\" and that \"acidosis is a higher pH\", and calls calcium carbonate a treatment for \"hypophosphatemia\". Bicarbonate raises a low pH, and calcium carbonate treats high phosphate.","Male Reproductive Disorders","Testicular cancer — About 1% of male cancers but the major cancer in men 15–34; a known cause of male sterility. Risk: cryptorchidism (undescended testis; about 10% of cases; the higher it sits, the higher the risk; uncorrected or corrected…","BPH (benign prostatic hyperplasia) — Non-cancerous enlargement (normal walnut-sized 4–20 g; BPH 50–80 g). Epithelial overgrowth causes mechanical obstruction; smooth muscle overgrowth causes dynamic obstruction of the urethra. Risk: age (bi…","Prostate cancer — Most common male cancer in the US; 2nd to lung in cancer deaths. Highest in Black men, lowest in Asian and Native American men; rises after 50 (over 80% of cases after 65); a 1st- or 2nd-degree relative raises risk 8×; hig…","PSA screening controversy — PSA finds cancer 5–15 years before a digital rectal exam, but only 1 in 3 with a positive PSA has cancer; it drives unnecessary biopsies and aggressive treatment harms (ED, incontinence, bowel problems) for a sma…","Erectile dysfunction — Can't achieve or sustain an erection; about 30 million US men; linked to chronic illness. Primary (rare, lifelong). Secondary: organic (PVD, drugs such as antidepressants and antihypertensives, low testosterone, pelvi…","Finasteride (also dutasteride), 5-alpha reductase inhibitors — Block testosterone's conversion to DHT (blood DHT drops about 70%; circulating testosterone doesn't change), so prostate epithelial tissue regresses: relieves mechanical obstruc…","Tamsulosin (Flomax), alpha-1 blocker — Relaxes smooth muscle in the bladder neck, prostate and urethra, relieving dynamic obstruction. Selective for prostate alpha receptors: works rapidly; lifelong. — Well tolerated; doesn't lower BP like …","Sildenafil, PDE5 inhibitor (drug of choice for ED) — Inhibits PDE5, preserving cGMP; enhances the response to sexual stimulation only (nothing happens without it; no effect in men without ED). Also pulmonary HTN and BPH. Take up to 4 h befo…","The voiceover says finasteride works best in very large prostates, then that \"it's not going to have very much effect at all if their prostate is enlarged\". It most likely means \"not very enlarged\".","Visual & Sensory Disorders","Know each condition's risk factors, visual changes and any treatment named. The only drugs covered are for glaucoma.","Cataracts (cloudy lens) — Age (biggest; almost everyone eventually), eye trauma, congenital, premature babies, diabetes, chronic corticosteroids, smoking, alcohol — Gradual, painless blurry vision (one or both eyes), halos, altered color, g…","Diabetic retinopathy — About 40% of diabetics over 40 — Non-proliferative: capillary microaneurysms, retinal swelling, hard exudates, macular edema, dot/blot hemorrhages. Proliferative: all of that plus new fragile, leaky vessels. — Control…","Hypertensive retinopathy — High BP — Blockages in retinal vessels; no change at first; severe sustained HTN causes sudden vision loss (optic disc swelling, ischemia). — Treat the HTN: vision can be restored.","Retinal detachment — Myopia (nearsighted), over 40, head trauma, eye tumors, cataract surgery history — Usually spontaneous: a tear lets vitreous humor flow behind the retina, so it peels from the choroid. Sudden, painless, unilateral loss;…","Age-related macular degeneration — Retinal aging, genetics, UV light (pilots, flight attendants, sunny areas), hyperopia (farsighted), smoking, light-colored eyes. Protective: dark green leafy vegetables. — Most common cause of irreversible…","Glaucoma = elevated intraocular pressure plus vision changes or optic nerve damage","Open-angle (chronic, usually both eyes)","An abnormal trabecular meshwork slows aqueous humor drainage into the canal of Schlemm, so IOP rises.","Risk: elevated IOP, older Black Americans (3–4×), family history, myopia, diabetes, HTN, migraines.","Few changes until advanced: progressive loss of sight, vague eye pain, halos, tunnel vision.","Treated with eye drops that ↓ aqueous production and/or ↑ drainage.","Closed-angle (acute, narrow-angle): an EMERGENCY","An abnormal iris–cornea angle completely blocks outflow when the pupil dilates.","Risk: Asian ethnicity, female, hyperopia, family history, older age. Biggest trigger: anticholinergic drugs.","Usually unilateral: severe eye pain, N/V, blurry vision, halos, red eye, dilated nonreactive pupil, cloudy cornea.","Outcome depends on time to treatment; needs surgery and avoiding triggers (the drops below are for open-angle).","Either type can cause blindness: pressure on inner structures cuts optic nerve blood flow.","Beta blockers: timolol (non-selective), betaxolol (beta-1 selective) — ↓ aqueous humor production only. — Maintenance. Transient burning; systemic effects if absorbed. Same contraindications as oral beta blockers: no timolol with asthma, se…","Prostaglandin analog: latanoprost — ↑ drainage (outflow). — Open-angle or ocular hypertension; very well tolerated.","Alpha-2 agonist: brimonidine — ↓ production and may ↑ drainage. — Open-angle only (not narrow-angle). Burning or stinging on instillation; if systemic: dry mouth, fatigue, headache, blurry vision.","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor: dorzolamide — ↓ production. — Second line; can be combined with timolol. Stinging, bitter taste, allergic reactions.","All eye drops: apply nasolacrimal pressure (press the inner corner of the eye) for 2 minutes to keep the drug in the eye and prevent systemic side effects.","Ménière's disease (endolymphatic hydrops)","Episodic: excess endolymph distends the hearing and balance structures, disrupting vestibular and hearing function. Usually starts at 20–40; one or both ears.","Recurrent vertigo with N/V, hearing loss, tinnitus, ear fullness.","Triggers: high salt, caffeine, alcohol, stress, MSG, allergies. Nearly all need sodium restriction; drugs only treat the dizziness.","Lecture slips to check","Ménière's is called \"a disease of the middle ear\", though it describes the endolymph of the inner ear.","Brimonidine is said to \"increase ocular pressure\" right after \"used for open-angle\": check the slide."]},{"s":"Week 4: Liver, Gallbladder & Pancreas▾","a":"week-4-liver-gallbladder-pancreas","k":["Week 4: Liver, Gallbladder & Pancreas▾","Jaundice, hepatitis, cirrhosis and liver failure, gallbladder disease, pancreatitis.","Liver Function, Jaundice & Viral Hepatitis","The liver","Largest organ (about 3 lb), right upper (epigastric) area, 5–7% of cardiac output. Lobules of hepatocytes around a central vein; hepatocytes can regenerate.","Kupffer cells line the sinusoids and remove bacteria and toxins.","Functions: metabolizes and stores fat, carbohydrate, protein, vitamins and minerals; blood volume reservoir; blood filter (removes bilirubin); makes clotting factors (prothrombin, fibrinogen); drug metabolism and detoxification. Liver failu…","Portal circulation: the portal vein brings nutrient-rich deoxygenated blood from the stomach, intestines, spleen and pancreas, which is the basis of the first-pass effect (oral doses are higher than IV).","Liver labs in liver disease","LFTs = liver enzymes AST, ALT, alk phos: rise (often not concerning until over about 150), but they're not a good measure of severity.","Bilirubin (total, direct, indirect): rises.","Ammonia: rises (the liver breaks it down).","Total protein and albumin: fall (the liver makes them).","PT: prolonged (no prothrombin), so bleeding risk.","Jaundice (icterus)","Yellowing from ↑ bilirubin (a heme breakdown product), usually visible at about 2–2.5 (normal about 0.6–1). Look at the sclera (best in darker skin), palms, soles and mucous membranes. Also dark urine, ↑ liver enzymes, normal or clay-colore…","Hemolytic — Too many RBCs broken down (hemolysis, bleeding). — Indirect (unconjugated): overproduction","Hepatocellular — The liver can't take up or conjugate bilirubin. — Indirect (unconjugated): impaired liver function","Obstructive — The liver conjugates, but bile can't flow out: gallstones, bile duct obstruction. — Direct (conjugated)","Conjugated = direct (about 30%); unconjugated = indirect (about 70%). Some liver conditions and hepatitis raise both.","Viral hepatitis","A systemic virus that inflames the liver: an immune response and inflammatory mediators lyse infected cells, causing edema, hypoxia and hepatocyte death. (Hepatitis also has non-viral causes: alcohol, drugs, chemicals, bacteria. Hep D and E…","Prodromal — About 2 weeks after exposure: fatigue, anorexia, malaise, N/V, headache, hyperalgesia, cough, low-grade fever. Highly transmissible.","Icteric — Starts with jaundice 1–2 weeks later, lasts 2–6 weeks: dark urine, clay stools, enlarged, tender liver, worsening fatigue and abdominal pain.","Recovery — Jaundice resolves; LFTs normalize about 2–12 weeks after jaundice onset; the liver stays enlarged and tender for a while.","Hepatitis A — Fecal–oral, foodborne (poor sanitation, food handlers not washing hands); also IV drug use, sex — Acute onset, fever, usually mild; never chronic — Hand hygiene; vaccine (2 doses 6 months apart, children from 12 months, high-r…","Hepatitis B — Blood and sex (IV drug use, dirty needles, unsafe sex) — Insidious; incubation 60–180 days; about 10% become chronic — Vaccine (3 doses): all infants and required for health care workers; safe sex. Chronic HBV (only if high ri…","Hepatitis C — Like B (IV drugs, sex), plus mother to fetus and medical mishaps — Insidious; about 80% become chronic; leads to hepatocellular carcinoma and liver transplant — No vaccine. Direct-acting antivirals (DAAs) make it nearly curabl…","Acetaminophen in hepatitis: allowed at under 2 g/day (vs 4 g normally) for fatigue and malaise; avoid Tylenol and NSAIDs in advanced liver disease. Hepatitis drug names don't need to go on the matrix: just recognize them.","Cirrhosis, Acute Liver Failure & Liver Pharmacology","Hepatitis (inflammation) can be reversible; cirrhosis is irreversible inflammatory fibrotic scarring (death rate about 26%). Leukocytes release mediators that drive fibrosis; chaotic fibrosis blocks bile channels and blood flow (jaundice, p…","Causes","Hepatitis B and C.","Excessive alcohol: alcoholic cirrhosis is the most common single type (still only about 25%).","NAFLD / NASH (nonalcoholic fatty liver / steatohepatitis).","Autoimmune, hereditary metabolic, idiopathic.","Alcohol & the liver","Alcohol becomes acetaldehyde, which alters hepatocytes and activates stellate cells (fibrosis); blocks protein export, causing vitamin and mineral problems and malnutrition. Kupffer cells recruit neutrophils; gut bacteria translocate; immun…","Alcoholic fatty liver: mild, asymptomatic, reversible if drinking stops.","Alcoholic steatohepatitis: fat, inflammation and hepatocyte degeneration; irreversible fibrosis begins; anorexia, nausea, jaundice, edema.","Cirrhosis: necrosis, fibrosis, scarring.","Early (vague, insidious) — N/V, anorexia, flatulence, bowel changes, fever, weight loss, palpable liver: usually diagnosed late.","Jaundice — Bilirubin build-up.","Peripheral edema, ascites — No albumin or protein, so lost oncotic pressure and third spacing; ascites also from portal hypertension (can mean 20–50 lb of fluid; mostly untreatable; drained).","Spider angiomas (spider telangiectasias) — Vascular skin lesions of end-stage liver disease.","Bleeding — No clotting factors (prolonged PT); small veins burst from portal pressure.","Endocrine — Women: anovulation, amenorrhea. Men: hypogonadism, impotence, infertility (especially alcoholic cirrhosis).","Esophageal and anorectal varices — Distended veins from portal HTN; rupture means hemorrhage with high mortality.","Hepatic encephalopathy — The liver can't clear toxins: confusion, then coma and death.","Portal hypertension","Fibrosis resists portal blood flow (like paper towels stuffed in a pipe), so everything below backs up.","Systemic hypotension, vascular underfilling, RAAS activation, plasma volume expansion, ↑ cardiac output.","Asymptomatic until complications: variceal hemorrhage, ascites, gut bacteria translocation (peritonitis), hepatorenal syndrome (liver and kidneys failing), heart failure.","No drug reverses it: prevent and treat complications; liver transplant is the only cure.","Hepatic encephalopathy","In 30–45% of cirrhosis patients.","Diagnosed by level of consciousness (behavior change, then confusion, then coma), not by a lab value.","Correlates with ↑ ammonia, a neurotoxin that crosses the BBB, but never diagnose from the ammonia level alone.","Acute (fulminant) liver failure: not from cirrhosis; a sudden failure, most often from acetaminophen overdose (antidote: acetylcysteine), also viral hepatitis or metabolic disease. Edematous hepatocytes, patchy necrosis and inflammation; si…","The timing of acute liver failure is given as both \"six to eight weeks\" and \"five days to eight weeks\" after an acetaminophen overdose (and 6–8 weeks after viral hepatitis). Check the slide.","Drugs for hepatic encephalopathy (the only liver drugs on the matrix)","Lactulose (first line) — A sugar not digested until the colon, where bacteria make acids: hyperosmotic (draws water, laxative) and acidic, converting ammonia to ammonium, which is trapped in the gut and excreted, so blood ammonia falls. — P…","Rifaximin (second line) — Antibiotic that inhibits bacterial RNA synthesis; originally for GI infections; reduces encephalopathy; sometimes preventive. — Ammonia isn't monitored with it. Risk of C. diff: monitor.","Gallbladder: Cholelithiasis & Cholecystitis","Cholelithiasis (gallstones)","Impaired metabolism of cholesterol, bilirubin and bile acids: crystals saturate and aggregate in the gallbladder.","Cholesterol stones about 70%; pigmented (black, hard) and mixed are less common.","Risk: developed countries, obesity, female, around 40, oral contraceptives, rapid weight loss, Native Americans (60–70%) vs 10–15% of white Americans, genetics, pre-existing pancreatic or liver disease. The old mnemonic: \"fair, fat and fort…","Stones sitting in the gallbladder are asymptomatic. Pain comes when a stone lodges in the cystic or common duct during a contraction.","Biliary colic: severe epigastric / RUQ pain radiating to the back, about 30 min to a few hours after a fatty meal; intermittent or steady. Also fatty-food intolerance (and cabbage), heartburn, flatulence.","Obstructed bile flow (stone in the bile duct)","Obstructive jaundice (bile can't reach the duodenum).","Dark amber urine that foams when shaken (water-soluble conjugated bilirubin).","Clay-colored stools (bilirubin can't reach the gut to become urobilinogen).","Steatorrhea (no bile salts, so no fat digestion); fatty-food intolerance.","Pruritus (bile salts deposit in the skin).","Bleeding tendency (no bile, no vitamin K absorption, fewer clotting factors).","Cholecystitis (inflamed gallbladder): usually a stone lodged in the cystic duct; acute or chronic; the gallbladder becomes edematous, hyperemic, distended, and loses function over time. Pain is like biliary colic, but pressure on the wall c…","Pain — IV ketorolac (Toradol) (NSAID) first; opioids (morphine, Dilaudid) if not controlled.","Nausea and vomiting — Antiemetics: ondansetron (Zofran), Phenergan.","Spasm — Anticholinergics: ↓ gallbladder secretion and spasm, ↓ risk of lodging more stones.","Dissolving stones — Bile acids: uncommon; mainly when the patient can't have surgery.","Pancreatitis","Diabetes is the endocrine pancreas; pancreatitis is exocrine dysfunction.","Acute pancreatitis","Most cases are mild and resolve on their own; about 20% become severe and need hospitalization.","Causes: obstruction of enzyme outflow, usually a gallstone in the common bile duct (shared with the pancreas); also alcohol, some drugs, viral infections.","Trapped enzymes autodigest the pancreas: vascular damage, coagulopathy, necrosis, fat necrosis.","Trapped enzymes and their damage","Trypsin: edema, necrosis, hemorrhage.","Elastase: hemorrhage.","Phospholipase A: fat necrosis.","Kallikrein: edema, ↑ vascular permeability (fluid leaks into the abdomen), smooth muscle contraction (pain), shock.","Lipase: fat necrosis (breaks down pancreatic cells). Amylase and lipase are the enzymes most often discussed.","Presentation & labs","Hallmark: sudden, severe epigastric / mid-abdominal pain, often radiating to the back; tenderness and guarding (from edema distending the ducts and capsule, plus peritoneal irritation).","N/V, abdominal distention, fever, hypoactive bowel sounds; severe cases: hypotension and tachycardia (fluid loss); jaundice if the bile duct is blocked.","Labs: ↑ pancreatic enzymes (amylase, lipase), glucose up or down (monitor closely), ↑ WBC.","Hemorrhagic pancreatitis (severe, rare): Grey Turner sign = flank bruising; Cullen sign = bruising around the umbilicus (the lecturer's memory trick: vampires don't have belly buttons, think of the Cullens from Twilight).","Complications","Pulmonary: shallow breathing from pain, pleural effusion, diaphragm irritation.","Hypovolemic shock.","Hypocalcemia leading to tetany: fat necrosis makes free fatty acids that bind calcium.","Pseudocyst: a fluid sac outside the pancreas (necrotic products and secretions) causing more inflammation and scarring; may be a palpable epigastric mass; rupture causes peritonitis.","Abscess: fluid cavity inside the pancreas from extensive necrosis; infection and perforation; very high fever, leukocytosis, mass.","Chronic pancreatitis","Progressive fibrotic disease. Most common cause: alcohol (acinar cells make a toxic metabolite and cytokines, destroying the pancreas including the islets of Langerhans). Also genetics, chronic gallstone obstruction, smoking, autoimmune, tu…","Repeated acute attacks plus ongoing dysfunction; constant, hard-to-treat pain (a chronic pain condition).","Weight loss and fat malabsorption (no enzymes); diabetes (islet loss); pseudocysts.","Treatment","Pain control first: opioids (hydromorphone, morphine).","Dicyclomine (anticholinergic antispasmodic): ↓ secretions, relaxes ductal smooth muscle.","Antacids and H2 blockers: ↓ hydrochloric acid, which ↓ pancreatic enzyme secretion.","Insulin or glucagon as blood sugar requires.","Pancrelipase (chronic): replaces the missing pancreatic enzymes; essentially no adverse effects; take with every meal and snack (often 3–4 capsules).","The voiceover calls an enzyme \"calcine / Halcrine\" (kallikrein), says pancrelipase is \"used to reduce the secretion of pancreatic enzymes\" (it replaces them), and says abdominal fluid \"can actually cause acts\" (ascites)."]},{"s":"Week 5: GI & Endocrine▾","a":"week-5-gi-endocrine","k":["Week 5: GI & Endocrine▾","Upper and lower GI disorders, GI drugs, adrenal, pituitary and thyroid disorders.","Upper GI Disorders","Upper GI = esophagus, stomach and the start of the small intestine; lower GI = the rest of the small intestine, colon, rectum and anus. Dysphagia (difficulty swallowing, solids first, then liquids): mechanical causes (stenosis, strictures, …","GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease)","The LES doesn't close properly, so acidic stomach contents flow back into the esophagus.","Causes: weak LES closure or ↑ abdominal pressure: fatty, spicy, tomato-based, citrus foods, caffeine, heavy alcohol, smoking, sleep position, obesity, pregnancy, drugs.","Signs: heartburn (pyrosis), dyspepsia, regurgitation, chest pain, dysphagia, pulmonary symptoms; tooth decay, gingivitis, ear problems, hoarseness, chronic sore throat, throat clearing, laryngitis.","Complications: esophageal ulcers, scarring, strictures, and Barrett's esophagus: premalignant metaplasia with 3× the risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma (very low survival).","Treat the GERD (avoid triggers, drugs) to prevent complications.","Hiatal hernia","The diaphragm opening for the esophagus is too large, so part of the stomach moves into the thorax.","Sliding (less severe; peritoneum intact). Paraesophageal / rolling (part of the stomach stays above the diaphragm permanently; thin peritoneum forms a true sac). Types III and IV are mixed or complex.","Risk: older age, diaphragm injury, repeated abdominal pressure (severe coughing, vomiting, constipation and straining), obesity, smoking.","Often no symptoms; otherwise belching, dysphagia, chest or epigastric pain: looks like GERD (they often coexist).","Conservative care: small frequent meals, don't lie down after eating, avoid tight clothing or abdominal binders, weight control, GERD drugs; surgery if needed.","Acute gastritis — Temporary inflammation of the stomach lining only, lasting 2–10 days: irritants (alcohol), NSAIDs (block prostaglandins, which normally make protective mucus and inhibit acid), infection (H. pylori most common; stomach vir…","Chronic gastritis — Progressive inflammation for weeks to years: autoimmune (parietal cells attacked) or chronic H. pylori. Leads to peptic ulcer disease, bleeding ulcers, anemia, ↑ gastric cancer.","Gastritis signs — Vomiting, stomach burning or upset, anorexia; severe cases: hematemesis or black stool; postprandial discomfort (soon after eating).","H. pylori — Gram-negative spiral bacterium that thrives in acid; often asymptomatic, but overgrowth causes persistent inflammation: chronic gastritis, PUD, stomach cancer. Spread person to person (saliva, feces, vomit) or by contaminated fo…","Acute gastroenteritis — Stomach and small intestine: viruses (norovirus, rotavirus), bacteria (E. coli, Salmonella, Campylobacter), parasites; \"food poisoning\". Lasts 1–3 days (up to 10). Watery (maybe bloody) diarrhea, abdominal pain, N/V,…","Peptic ulcer disease (esophageal, gastric or duodenal ulcers)","An open sore in the lining when aggressive factors (H. pylori, NSAIDs / aspirin, excess acid, pepsin, heavy alcohol, smoking, family history) overwhelm defenses (mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow, prostaglandins). Stress doesn't cause ulcers b…","Who — Later, about 50–70 (long-term NSAIDs, steroids, anticoagulants, systemic illness) — Most common site; any age, often earlier adulthood (H. pylori, NSAIDs)","Pain timing — 1–2 hours after eating — 2–4 hours after eating","Pain character — Burning, cramping or gas-like pain in the epigastrium or back, worse with an empty stomach. Non-bleeding ulcers may cause no symptoms (or N/V, anorexia, weight loss).","PUD complications: \"HOP\": Hemorrhage (vascular areas), Obstruction (scar tissue and strictures), Perforation, leading to peritonitis.","The voiceover says NSAIDs cause \"decrease[d] gastric acids\". NSAIDs block prostaglandins, and prostaglandins normally inhibit acid, so the net effect is less protective mucus and more acid.","Lower GI Disorders","Appendicitis","An obstructed appendix becomes inflamed. The danger is the complications: rupture, gangrene, abscess, peritonitis.","Peak age 10–12. Dull, steady pain that worsens over 4–6 hours, periumbilical, then RLQ; fever, nausea, anorexia.","Rebound tenderness in the RLQ (pain when pressure is released).","Sudden pain relief is a bad sign: possible rupture, so high peritonitis risk.","Diagnosis: signs, ultrasound, sometimes exploratory laparotomy.","Peritonitis","Inflamed peritoneum (the serous membrane lining the abdomen), usually after something ruptures or perforates: gastric or duodenal ulcer, gallbladder, pancreatitis, spleen, bladder, appendix.","Causes third spacing, leading to hypovolemic shock and sepsis; ↓ peristalsis leads to paralytic ileus and obstruction.","Sudden, severe pain (can't move, talk or breathe easily), tenderness, a rigid, board-like abdomen (not the same as distended), N/V, fever, ↑ WBC, ↑ HR, ↓ BP (pain and fluid shifts).","Treatment: anti-inflammatories; fix the cause.","Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS): chronic altered bowel pattern from changes in motility: IBS-C (constipation) or IBS-D (diarrhea). Distention, fullness, flatus, bloating, intermittent pain worsened by stress and relieved by defecation, urgen…","Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD): chronic inflammation with exacerbations and remissions","More common in women, Caucasians, people of Jewish descent and smokers; thought to be autoimmune, triggered by infection. Both raise VTE / DVT risk.","Where — Anywhere, but mainly the small intestine and upper large intestine; can reach the rectum — Mucosa of the rectum and colon: starts in the rectum and extends continuously; may involve the whole colon","Pattern — Lymph structures blocked, causing deep linear fissures and ulcers in patchy \"skip lesions\", cobblestone appearance, granulomas, all layers involved — Large ulcerations, epithelial necrosis, crypt abscesses; fragile granulation tis…","Onset / who — Often before the 3rd decade — Usually the 3rd decade; white people of European descent (especially Ashkenazi Jewish); rare in Asian populations","Signs — Crampy lower abdominal pain, often RLQ; watery diarrhea; weight loss, fatigue, anorexia, fever, malabsorption; RLQ mass; mouth ulcers; fistula signs — Abdominal pain, bloody diarrhea (hallmark); weight loss, fatigue, anorexia, fever","Complications — Malnutrition, anemia, scar tissue and obstruction, fistulas (abnormal connections), ↑ cancer — Hemorrhage, perforation, malnutrition, anemia, strictures, fissures and abscesses, toxic megacolon (rapid, life-threatening colon…","Diverticulosis","Small pouches (diverticula) bulge through weak spots in the colon wall, usually the descending colon; not inflamed.","Congenital or acquired: low-fiber diet and chronic constipation.","Usually asymptomatic; found by accident or when diverticulitis develops.","Diverticulitis","A pouch becomes inflamed, usually from lodged fecal material.","LLQ pain, fever, ↑ WBC, constipation or diarrhea; sometimes a large amount of frank blood (may resolve on its own).","Complications: perforation, peritonitis, obstruction.","GI Pharmacology","Most GI care is diet modification and sometimes surgery. Review the NUR 325 constipation and diarrhea drugs (laxatives, antidiarrheals) too. For GERD and PUD, drugs either increase protective factors (antacids, sucralfate) or decrease aggre…","H. pylori treatment — Combination of several antibiotics + an acid inhibitor — Combination limits resistance (H. pylori thrives in acid, where most antibiotics don't). Confirm infection first (breath test). 10–14 days; adherence is poor: ab…","H2 receptor antagonists — Famotidine (more common), cimetidine — Block parietal-cell histamine (H2) receptors: ↓ acid 60–70%, ↑ stomach pH. PO or IV; OTC. GERD, PUD, and ulcer prophylaxis in hospitalized / ICU patients (less acidic contents…","Proton pump inhibitors (\"-prazole\") — Omeprazole, pantoprazole, esomeprazole — Irreversibly turn off the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase (proton pump), so no HCl. Work better than H2 blockers. OTC or Rx; meant for short-term PUD and GERD. Long-term use: ↑ ris…","Mucosal protectant — Sucralfate — Sucrose + aluminum hydroxide; in stomach acid it forms a sticky gel barrier. FDA-approved for duodenal ulcers (also gastric ulcers, chronic gastritis). PO (tablet or suspension), taken before meals. Few sid…","Antacids — Aluminum, calcium (Tums), magnesium, aluminum + magnesium — Chemically neutralize about 50% of acid: heartburn, indigestion, GERD, PUD, stress ulcers. Aluminum and calcium: constipation. Magnesium: diarrhea. Al + Mg combined bala…","The voiceover says H2 blockers for ulcer prophylaxis \"decrease the pH of gastric acid\". They raise stomach pH (less acidic), as it says earlier in the same lecture.","Antiemetics","Serotonin (5-HT3) blocker — Ondansetron (Zofran) — Blocks serotonin in the chemoreceptor trigger zone and the vagal afferents of the stomach and small intestine. PO or IV. Made for chemo and radiation nausea; now used for any nausea. Mild h…","Antihistamines (H1) — Dimenhydrinate (Dramamine), hydroxyzine (Vistaril), meclizine — Block H1 in the inner ear: motion sickness, dizziness, nausea. Significant sedation and anticholinergic effects: fall risk in the elderly. Never give hydr…","Dopamine antagonist / prokinetic — Metoclopramide (Reglan) — Blocks dopamine, ↑ LES tone and ↑ peristalsis. Nausea from chemo, radiation and opioids; GI motility problems (e.g. cystic fibrosis, bowels slow to wake after surgery). Sedation. …","Antidiarrheals (review): slow peristalsis and reduce effluent; watch for constipation and drowsiness (falls, driving). The atropine-containing one is anticholinergic: drying and bradycardia.","Inflammatory bowel disease drugs","Sulfasalazine (5-aminosalicylic acid; also mesalamine) — A sulfonamide split in the gut into 5-ASA (anti-inflammatory) and sulfapyridine. Mild to moderate IBD. — Nausea, fever, rash, headache, hematologic disorders. Not with a sulfa allergy…","Infliximab (Remicade), a DMARD / monoclonal antibody — Neutralizes TNF-alpha (an inflammatory mediator), turning down the immune response. Used for many autoimmune conditions. — Immunosuppression, so infection (the main effect), ↑ cancer, h…","Endocrine: Pituitary–Adrenal Axis & Cushing Syndrome","Endocrine is hard because hormones affect nearly every cell: review the A&P slides and draw out the feedback loops. Anterior pituitary (six hormones; focus on TSH and ACTH). Posterior pituitary: ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin. ACTH acts on …","The 3 S's of the adrenal cortex: Sugar = cortisol (glucocorticoid), Salt = aldosterone (mineralocorticoid), Sex = androgens. Too much = Cushing; too little = Addison's.","Primary — The adrenal cortex itself secretes excess cortisol.","Secondary — The anterior pituitary makes too much ACTH, causing excess cortisol.","Exogenous — Steroid drugs (prednisone, dexamethasone), especially at high doses for long periods: the most common cause in the US.","Excess cortisol drives most symptoms (aldosterone and androgens are also high). Because cortisol is always high, it doesn't rise with stress: surgical patients may need ACTH or cortisol given pre-op. Chronic suppression of ACTH can make the…","↑ glucose availability — Hyperglycemia / glucose intolerance (\"diabetic\"): ↑ gluconeogenesis, cortisol blocks insulin.","Maintain the vascular system — Hypertension (also sodium and water retention from aldosterone); fragile capillaries cause bruising (ecchymosis).","Break down protein and fat — Muscle wasting and weakness, thin fragile skin, osteoporosis and bone pain (↓ bone formation, ↑ resorption, fractures); fat redistribution: central obesity (\"apple shape\"), moon face, buffalo hump, thin limbs; p…","Suppress immune and inflammatory responses — Impaired wound healing, ↑ infection risk.","Alert the brain — Mood swings, insomnia.","(Androgens) — Hirsutism (hair on women's chin, chest, areolas), thinning scalp hair, gynecomastia.","These look like the effects of long-term steroid use. Treatment is aimed at the cause: surgery or radiation for pituitary or adrenal tumors; for exogenous steroids, taper slowly. Drugs that suppress steroid synthesis are temporary adjuncts …","Aminoglutethimide — Blocks the first step of all adrenal steroid synthesis (glucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids, androgens all fall); cortisol drops about 50%. — Temporary (e.g. awaiting surgery or radiation), usually no more than 3 months;…","Ketoconazole (\"-azole\" antifungal) — Also inhibits glucocorticoid synthesis (blocks adrenal hormone formation). — Same use. Hepatotoxic: no alcohol, avoid with liver disease. Not in pregnancy (fetal thyroid damage, per the lecture).","The voiceover says ketoconazole \"isn't actually an antifungal drug, which you should know from the -azole suffix\". It is an azole antifungal that also blocks steroid synthesis. It also says \"HCTZ\" where it means ACTH.","Endocrine: Addison's Disease & Adrenal Crises","Addison's disease = adrenal cortex disease with hyposecretion of all three cortical hormones (cortisol, aldosterone, androgens); the most severe effects come from the lack of cortisol. Cause: idiopathic, autoimmune (most common), or a damag…","Low aldosterone","Loses sodium and water, keeps potassium: hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, dehydration.","Low BP: ↓ vascular tone, cardiac output and circulating volume.","Salt craving.","The lecture adds that low aldosterone signals more ADH, which could lead to fluid volume excess.","Low cortisol","Low energy, hypoglycemia, weakness, fatigue.","High ACTH.","Hyperpigmentation (\"tan\") from MSH stimulating melanocytes.","Hydrocortisone (drug of choice) — Both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid activity. IM, IV or PO. (Prednisone or dexamethasone are alternatives, usually PO.)","Fludrocortisone — The only mineralocorticoid: works like aldosterone (salt and water balance); PO, once daily.","Replacement therapy teaching (review the NUR 325 steroid drug matrix) Lifelong, regardless of cause. Correct dosing should make the patient look like a normal, functioning person; over- or underdosing causes problems. Mimic the normal patte…","Lifelong, regardless of cause. Correct dosing should make the patient look like a normal, functioning person; over- or underdosing causes problems.","Mimic the normal pattern: some prescribers give the whole dose at bedtime; others give ⅔ in the morning and ⅓ in the afternoon. Be consistent every day; use the smallest effective dose (much smaller than for non-endocrine uses).","Never stop abruptly (and never run out): this can cause Addisonian crisis.","Increase the dose during any stress (infection, surgery, trauma): failing to can be fatal. The 3 × 3 rule: about 3 times the usual dose for 3 days, with the provider.","Always carry an injectable plus an oral supply; wear medical ID.","Severe Cushing syndrome (emergency)","Massively high random serum cortisol, or 24-h urine free cortisol more than 4× the upper limit; severe hypokalemia.","Linked to sepsis, opportunistic infection, uncontrolled HTN, heart failure; can be deadly.","Treat the metabolic and electrolyte problems; find the cause.","Addisonian crisis (acute adrenal insufficiency)","Insufficient serum corticosteroids: sudden loss of the adrenal glands, sudden stress in chronic disease, or sudden stopping of corticosteroids.","The body \"crashes\": a medical emergency (more in the practice-readiness course).","Prevented by adequate dosing and teaching.","Endocrine: Pheochromocytoma, SIADH & Diabetes Insipidus","Pheochromocytoma","A rare tumor of the adrenal medulla overproducing epinephrine and norepinephrine; 90% benign; young and middle-aged adults.","Epinephrine stimulates alpha and beta receptors; norepinephrine stimulates almost entirely alpha. Alpha activation is the main cause of the hypertension.","Hallmark: hypertension (intermittent or persistent, can be extreme) with the classic triad: headache, diaphoresis, tachycardia.","Episodes are triggered by anything that stimulates the SNS: stress, excitement, exercise, smoking. Keep a low-stimulus, relaxed environment.","Danger: stroke and cardiac events. Treatment: surgical removal; alpha blockers before surgery (tumor handling can release a massive catecholamine surge) or long term if inoperable.","Phenoxybenzamine","An old alpha blocker, used now only for pheochromocytoma.","Noncompetitive, irreversible alpha blockade: long-lasting BP lowering (wanted here).","Given 10–14 days before surgery and briefly after.","AEs: orthostatic hypotension, reflex tachycardia, nasal congestion, sexual side effects in men.","ADH (antidiuretic hormone), from the posterior pituitary, is released for high serum osmolality or hypotension; it makes the kidneys retain water. \"ADH stops the pee.\"","Causes — Malignant tumor (most common): small cell lung carcinoma secreting ADH; CNS disorders (head trauma, stroke, brain tumor); drugs (morphine, SSRIs, some chemo); hypothyroidism, infection — Neurogenic (central): hypothalamus or pituit…","Serum osmolality — Low (diluted) — High (concentrated)","Serum sodium — Low: dilutional hyponatremia — High: hypernatremia","Urine osmolality / specific gravity — High (concentrated) — Low (like straight water)","Urine output / weight — Low output, weight gain — Very high output (up to 15 L/day), weight loss","Signs — Hyponatremia (neurologic): fatigue, confusion, lethargy, dulled sensorium, muscle twitching, seizures; impaired taste, anorexia, vomiting. Sodium below 115: severe, irreversible neuro damage. Water intoxication: cells (brain) swell.…","Treatment — Treat the cause (remove the tumor, stop the drug, wait out head trauma); fluid restriction; loop diuretics only if Na is above 125; salt tablets; chronic cases: demeclocycline — Neurogenic: desmopressin (DDAVP), synthetic ADH. N…","Demeclocycline (chronic SIADH)","A tetracycline antibiotic that makes the kidneys less sensitive to ADH.","Not first line (restrict fluids and reverse the cause first).","Tetracycline effects: photosensitivity, tooth staining, nephrotoxic.","Monitor sodium constantly (also with loop diuretics).","Desmopressin (DDAVP)","Synthetic ADH replacement for neurogenic DI.","Nasal spray (common; nasal irritation), PO, IV or subQ, in small doses.","Large doses cause the opposite problem (overdose: water retention).","For SIADH the voiceover says \"we can give medications that stimulate the release of ADH\", and says demeclocycline \"was originally used as an antifungal\". In SIADH, drugs reduce ADH's effect, and demeclocycline is a tetracycline antibiotic. …","Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders","The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped gland in the neck, the body's metabolism \"volume knob\". It makes T4 (thyroxine), which is mostly inactive and bound and acts as the regulator, and T3, the active form converted from T4 in the body. Iodine i…","Goiter = thyroid enlargement, with or without dysfunction (\"nontoxic\" if no symptoms). Causes: excess pituitary TSH; low iodine (low hormone, so TSH climbs and the gland enlarges); goitrogens (foods or drugs, e.g. lithium).","Main cause — Primary (95%); Hashimoto's thyroiditis (autoimmune; most common cause). Secondary: the pituitary doesn't make enough TSH. — Graves' disease (autoimmune: thyroid-stimulating antibodies make the gland enlarge and keep producing).…","Risk — Women over 50, Caucasian, pregnancy, other autoimmune disease (lupus, RA), family history, amiodarone, lithium, treatment for hyperthyroidism (neck radiation, radioactive iodine) — Family history of Graves', over 40, Caucasian, amiod…","Labs — High TSH, low T3 and T4 (secondary: TSH also low). Hashimoto's: anti-thyroid peroxidase (hallmark) and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies. — Low TSH, high T3 and T4; antibodies; Doppler ultrasound shows ↑ blood flow; radioactive iodine up…","Signs: metabolism turned — Down: cold intolerance, weight gain, fatigue, lethargy, poor memory and attention, ↑ cholesterol (often on a statin), muscle cramps, constipation, puffy face, hair loss, brittle nails, menstrual problems and ↓ fer…","Extreme — Myxedema: severe, long-untreated hypothyroidism (skin thickening, extreme hair loss); myxedema coma (loss of brain function; life-threatening; rare) — Thyroid storm (thyrotoxic crisis): triggered by a major stressor (severe infect…","Treatment — Levothyroxine (synthetic T4, converted to T3); lifelong (also after thyroid removal) — PTU, radioactive iodine (more common), or surgery (then lifelong thyroid hormone)","Levothyroxine (Synthroid)","Synthetic T4; very long half-life (keep doses steady); symptoms improve in about a month.","Too much causes hyperthyroid symptoms; too little, hypothyroid symptoms.","Food and other drugs reduce absorption: take on an empty stomach about 30 min before breakfast (keep it at the bedside).","Increases warfarin's effect: bleeding risk.","Lifelong.","PTU (propylthiouracil)","Blocks thyroid hormone synthesis and suppresses T4-to-T3 conversion.","Black box: hepatotoxic.","Usable with caution in the first trimester; switch drugs in the 2nd and 3rd.","Parathyroid (four small glands within the thyroid tissue)","Parathyroid hormone (PTH) is released for low calcium: it raises calcium and promotes vitamin D production by the kidney.","Hypoparathyroidism","Too little PTH, causing hypocalcemia; rare; usually from damage or removal during thyroid surgery (watch post-thyroidectomy patients).","Muscle cramps, irritability, tetany, convulsions; Trousseau's and Chvostek's signs.","↑ phosphate (less excreted): monitor it.","Lifelong synthetic PTH; normalize calcium and vitamin D.","Hyperparathyroidism","Excess PTH, causing hypercalcemia and bone breakdown.","Muscle weakness, poor concentration, HTN, kidney stones.","Lower calcium: diuretics (excrete calcium), calcitonin, bisphosphonates (↓ bone breakdown), vitamin D; surgery (e.g. a PTH-secreting tumor).","In the hyperthyroid section the voiceover says amiodarone \"can cause hypothyroidism\" and that exophthalmos is \"common with a hypothyroid\". In context both refer to hyperthyroidism. It also says \"synthroid and morphine\" when it means warfari…"]},{"s":"Week 6: Cardiovascular▾","a":"week-6-cardiovascular","k":["Week 6: Cardiovascular▾","CAD, heart failure, dysrhythmias, PVD, valve disease and cardiac drugs.","Coronary Artery Disease & Stable Angina","Every heart problem is electrical (conduction), plumbing (blocked or spasming arteries, valves), or pump (dysfunctional muscle). CAD = atherosclerosis of the coronary arteries (which branch off the aorta to feed the heart): plaque narrows t…","Non-modifiable risk","Advanced age.","Family history (genes and shared environment); gene polymorphisms.","Males earlier; women's risk equals men's after menopause (estrogen protects).","Black, Hispanic and Indigenous Americans more than white or Asian/Pacific Islander; a death disparity for Black Americans (structural racism, access).","Modifiable risk","Hypertension (2–3× risk).","Smoking and secondhand smoke: damage the endothelium; nicotine raises catecholamines, lipids and BP.","Diabetes (insulin resistance damages the endothelium, adds inflammation).","Obesity (abdominal / android) and inactivity: inflammation, adipokines.","Diet (excess fat, trans fat, carbs; DASH diet protects).","Hyperlipidemia (high TG and LDL, low HDL): a key drug target (statins).","Depression, stress, anxiety (inflammation).","Endothelial dysfunction: diseased coronary arteries constrict when they should dilate (driven by diabetes, hyperlipidemia, HTN, smoking). Combined with plaque, the heart gets less blood exactly when it needs more.","Angina","The main symptom of CAD (some patients are asymptomatic). Chest pain plus heartburn, palpitations, weakness, dizziness, anxiety, nausea, cold sweats, burning in the chest, shoulder or abdomen.","Stable angina (the focus here): a supply–demand imbalance through a partially open artery. Brought on by exertion (shoveling snow, stairs, a big meal) and relieved by rest; lasts 2–5 minutes; can be severe; often mistaken for indigestion.","Complete occlusion = MI.","Cardiac vs non-cardiac chest pain","Always rule out the heart first for pain in the chest, left arm, jaw or left shoulder, diaphoresis, pallor.","Cardiac: diffuse pressure or tightness (\"an elephant on my chest\"), hard to pinpoint.","Non-cardiac: focal, well localized.","Women often have hot or burning discomfort or tenderness, not always in the chest, plus indigestion, heartburn, nausea, fatigue.","Chest pain suggesting MI (not stable angina): occurs at rest (at a desk, waking from sleep), may radiate, is not relieved by rest, with N/V, shortness of breath, diaphoresis. Teach: if you sit down and the pain isn't better in about 5 minut…","When describing stable angina, the voiceover at one point says \"unstable angina is where the coronary blood flow is diminished but not completely blocked\". The page follows the rest of the lecture: that describes stable angina, which is rel…","Cardiomyopathy & Heart Failure","Dilated — Ischemia, valve disease, alcohol and drugs, peripartum, infection; often genetic. Leads to HFrEF.","Hypertrophic — Thick ventricle (often from hypertension); deadly dysrhythmias and sudden cardiac death; the chamber shrinks and EF eventually falls.","Restrictive (least common) — Rigid, non-compliant ventricles that resist filling; usually presents as right-sided HF with systemic congestion; often from amyloid disease.","Heart failure is a pump problem: a chronic, progressive condition in which the heart can't pump enough blood to meet the body's needs. The most common cause of hospitalization (dyspnea or volume overload), especially over 65. Cardiac output…","Causes","Repeated ischemia (CAD, unstable angina), a big MI (e.g. papillary muscle rupture).","Chronic hypertension (the greatest risk factor).","COPD (right ventricular failure).","Dysrhythmias, valve disorders (mitral insufficiency, aortic stenosis), pulmonary emboli.","Risk factors","HTN; diabetes (endothelial dysfunction, inflammation).","MI: within 6 months, 22% of men and 46% of women develop HF.","Age; men earlier, post-menopausal women equal; higher in Black Americans (more HTN); genetics; obesity.","Severe anemia, congenital defects, viruses (myocarditis, e.g. COVID), alcohol, cocaine / crack, kidney disease.","Stable angina itself isn't an HF risk factor (it is for CAD and MI).","Backs up into — Pulmonary circulation — Systemic circulation (vena cava)","Most common cause — Poorly controlled hypertension — Severe COPD","Signs — Cough, crackles, wheezes, frothy (maybe blood-tinged) sputum (pulmonary edema), paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, orthopnea (tripod), restlessness, confusion, tachycardia, exertional dyspnea, fatigue, cyanosis — JVD, dependent edema, we…","For exams and the NCLEX, left vs right is dichotomized; in practice HF usually progresses to affect both sides.","Ejection fraction (normal 55–65%, by echo) — Below 40% (can be as low as 10%) — Normal or modestly ↓ (about 40–50%)","Problem — Impaired contraction: a weak, big, boggy LV can't generate pressure or stroke volume — Ventricles can't relax and fill: stiff, thick or restricted, with high filling pressures","Who — Younger males, CAD, diabetes — Chronic HTN, older females, diabetes, obesity","Course & remodeling","Chronic, progressive, with episodes of decompensated HF (new or worse signs, usually volume overload, ER visits); acute new-onset HF is less common.","Ventricular remodeling: the weak heart releases angiotensin II, aldosterone, endothelin, TNF-alpha and catecholamines, meant to help but causing apoptosis, hypertrophy, collagen deposits and myocardial fibrosis: ventricles enlarge and dilat…","S3 gallop","Low-pitched sound after S2, in early diastole (rapid ventricular filling).","From high end-diastolic volume (fluid left in the ventricle) and ↑ pressure.","Abnormal over age 40: indicates heart failure.","Best prevention: control HTN, glucose, lipids, obesity.","Conduction & Dysrhythmias","Cardiac cell properties","Automaticity: generates an impulse.","Excitability: responds to an outside impulse.","Conductivity: receives and passes an impulse on.","Contractility: shortens (\"squeezes\") in response.","The EKG","Depolarization (\"light switch on\") is contraction (systole); repolarization is recharging. Driven by the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump.","P wave = atrial depolarization (from the SA node). QRS = ventricular depolarization (AV node, then bundle of His and Purkinje fibers); atrial repolarization is hidden in the QRS. T wave = ventricular repolarization.","Flat baseline = isoelectric line. Each small box = 0.04 sec; count the QRSs on a 6-second strip × 10 for the rate.","Normal sinus rhythm: rate 60–100, regular (equal R-to-R), upright rounded P before every QRS, PR 0.12–0.20 sec, narrow QRS under 0.12 sec. Sinus arrhythmia (also normal): the same, but the rate varies with breathing or autonomic changes; co…","Why dysrhythmias matter: they change rate (and sometimes filling or contractility), so they change cardiac output. Mechanisms: inappropriate automaticity (a rogue cell fires: MI, electrolyte imbalance, especially potassium, also calcium and…","Sinus bradycardia — Sinus, under 60, regular, normal PR and QRS — Electrolytes (high K⁺), vagal stimulation, digoxin toxicity, late hypoxia, beta blockers, CCBs, amiodarone (hold parameters), MI near the SA node; fit people may run slow — ↓…","Sinus tachycardia — Sinus, 100–150, regular, normal PR (P may be hard to see), narrow QRS — Exercise, pain, anxiety, strong emotion (catecholamines), fever, fluid volume deficit (often the first sign), epinephrine, albuterol, caffeine, nico…","PSVT (paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia) — 150–250, starts above the ventricles (not the SA node); P waves hidden or abnormal; narrow QRS; sudden bursts — Re-entry; overexertion, emotional stress, stimulants, digoxin toxicity, heart d…","PACs (premature atrial contractions) — An early, odd-looking P wave; normal PR; QRS follows — — — Harmless alone; frequent PACs raise the risk of A-fib: check electrolytes and O₂; not the time to discharge.","Atrial flutter — Repetitive re-entry: sawtooth P (flutter) waves, atrial rate up to about 250, slower ventricular rate (2:1, 3:1, 4:1), narrow QRS, regular — \"Sick heart\": CAD, cardiomyopathy, valve or congenital disease, myocarditis, HTN, …","Atrial fibrillation — Many chaotic atrial impulses: irregularly irregular, no discernible P waves (fibrillatory waves), no PR, usually about 100–175 — Electrolytes, hypoxia, cardiovascular disease (CAD, prior MI). New onset: check electroly…","PVCs — Early wide QRS (over 0.12) with no P wave from a ventricular ectopic focus; up or down; bigeminy (every 2nd beat), trigeminy (3rd), quadrigeminy (4th) — Electrolytes (biggest), caffeine, nicotine, hypoxia, fever, exercise, emotional …","Ventricular tachycardia — 3 or more PVCs in a row (described as \"a 12-beat run\"); fast, regular, wide, no P waves — MI, CAD, severe electrolyte abnormality, HF, drug toxicity — Deadly rhythm: severely ↓ CO. Check a pulse: no pulse means CPR…","Ventricular fibrillation — Chaotic quivering wave; no effective contractions; no measurable rate — — — No cardiac output: look at the patient and check a pulse (telemetry artifact happens), then start CPR.","The voiceover reads the PR interval as \"12 to 20 seconds\" (it means 0.12–0.20 sec) and says atrial flutter \"always originate[s] in the AV node\", overriding the SA node. Check the slide wording for flutter's origin.","Peripheral Vascular Disease","PVD is the umbrella term for peripheral circulatory disease: PAD (arterial only), venous disease, or both. Mostly atherosclerosis of the extremity vessels (usually the legs): thick, hard vessels and plaques cause ischemia, then pain, mobili…","Smoking (biggest): over 80% with PAD are current or former smokers; about 4× risk, earlier onset, faster progression, 2× amputation, poorer survival.","Men and women are affected equally (sex isn't a risk factor).","Uncontrolled diabetes, high cholesterol, existing heart disease or stroke, age over 50.","Other causes","Thrombus (clots in the legs).","Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger's disease): autoimmune inflammation of peripheral arteries, mostly in smokers; permanent occlusion.","Vasospasm: Raynaud's (temperature sensitivity causes peripheral vasoconstriction; linked to scleroderma, pulmonary HTN, genetics; cause unknown); other autoimmune conditions.","Problem — Arterial plaque causes local ischemia (most common site: femoral artery) — Vein walls and valves fail, so blood pools (venous stasis); about 40% of the US; chronic","Pain — Intermittent claudication: calf or buttock cramping with a predictable walking distance, relieved by rest (\"angina of the leg\"); numbness, burning, heaviness — Aching, heaviness, tiredness","Skin, pulses, color — Shiny thick skin, hair loss, diminished pulses, cool legs, pallor on elevation, cyanosis, dependent rubor (reactive hyperemia: red when dangling), ↓ sensation, ED — Edema, leathery, dark tan skin, flaky itchy skin, var…","Ulcers — Toes, top of the foot; non-healing — Stasis ulcers on the ankles","The 5 P's (arterial): Pain, Pulselessness, Palpable coolness, Paresthesias, Paresis (weakness). Ankle-brachial index (leg vs arm BP): normal about 1–1.4; 0.5 or less = severe arterial disease.","Treatment (PAD and PVD treated the same)","Smoking cessation (the biggest).","↑ physical activity (builds collateral circulation), weight loss, stress reduction, control diabetes and HTN.","Occlusion: bypass, balloon or stent.","Drugs: antiplatelets (aspirin, clopidogrel), anticoagulants (warfarin, apixaban), thrombolytics, statins, antihypertensives: review the NUR 325 matrix.","Drugs for intermittent claudication","Cilostazol (Pletal): platelet inhibitor plus vasodilator. AEs: headache (common), dizziness, diarrhea and abnormal stools, palpitations, peripheral edema. CYP450 interactions: report it on new prescriptions.","Pentoxifylline: vasoactive; ↑ blood flow and O₂ delivery, so ↑ walking distance and duration. PO three times a day. AEs: N/V, dizziness.","Valve Disease & Infective Endocarditis","Blood flows through the valves in order: tricuspid → pulmonic → mitral → aortic (\"TPMA\"; \"try before you buy\": tricycle before bicycle). Tricuspid = right atrium to right ventricle; pulmonic = RV to pulmonary artery; mitral = left atrium to…","The voiceover says \"the tricuspid and aortic valves open during systole\". The valves that open during ventricular systole are the pulmonic and aortic (the outflow valves). Check the slide.","Problem — Stenosis = stiff, tight leaflets that don't open or close well; the heart works harder (the aortic valve is most often affected) — Regurgitation = floppy, loose leaflets; when the LV contracts, blood flows backward into the left a…","Signs — Often asymptomatic until severe. Triad: syncope, lightheadedness, chest pain (the coronary arteries just past the valve get less flow); SOB, pulmonary edema (crackles, cough, congestion) — Fatigue (less forward flow), SOB and pulmon…","Both are left-sided (higher pressure, so more problems). Diagnosis: echocardiogram (TTE, or TEE to see the back of the heart). Treatment: drugs for contractility and rate, but usually valve replacement. Causes are mostly genetic, childhood …","Infective endocarditis","What happens","Infection on a valve forms vegetations (infected masses seen on echo) that keep the valve from opening and closing.","Septic emboli: vegetations stick to damaged endothelium and attract WBCs and platelets; the coagulation cascade and fibrin enlarge them; pieces break off with each contraction.","Tricuspid valve most common (the first valve bloodstream bacteria reach), so emboli go to the lungs (PE). Mitral or aortic: emboli go to the body (brain, coronaries). Any valve can be affected.","Who is at risk","Prosthetic valves attract bacteria: prophylactic antibiotics before dental work.","Pacemaker wires.","IV drug use (Staph aureus, Staph epidermidis via contaminated needles or skin, entering through the tricuspid first).","Any bloodstream infection (e.g. stepping on glass). Not every patient uses drugs; treat everyone the same.","Systemic — Looks very sick: fever, chills, weight loss, anorexia, muscle and joint pain, new murmur (can be hard to hear).","Embolic ischemia / infarction — Extremities, spleen, kidney, bowel, brain: often the first sign. Embolic stroke in about 40%; also meningitis, seizures, encephalopathy, brain abscess.","Petechiae — Emboli in small skin capillaries.","Splinter hemorrhages — Linear streaks in the nail beds.","Janeway lesions — Non-tender red or discolored spots on the palms and soles.","Osler's nodes — Tender nodules on the finger pads (tissue dying).","Roth spots — Retinal changes (emboli in the eye).","Severe — Can lose a nose or fingertips.","Treatment: replace the valve; clear the bloodstream infection first with IV antibiotics around the clock before and after surgery.","Cardiac Pharmacology: Antianginals & Heart Failure Drugs","Much of cardiac pharmacology is NUR 325 drugs (antihypertensives, diuretics, statins, antiplatelets): pull those forward from your matrix.","Relieve chest pain — Nitrates (dilate veins, ↓ preload), beta blockers (↓ HR and contractility), CCBs (↓ afterload, HR, contractility), ranolazine. First line: a beta blocker or CCB plus a nitrate, reassessed every 3 months.","Slow atherosclerosis — Statins; aspirin, clopidogrel (antiplatelets).","↓ morbidity and mortality — ACE inhibitors or ARBs.","Nitroglycerin (prototype nitrate)","Dilates veins mainly (all vessels): ↓ preload, ↓ cardiac workload.","AEs: headache (short; Tylenol OK), hypotension / dizziness, reflex tachycardia; tolerance develops quickly.","Sublingual (Nitrostat) for active angina: sit down; let it dissolve (don't swallow); repeat every 5 min, up to 3 doses; take only as many as needed.","No relief within 5 minutes of the first dose: call 911 (a second dose is OK, but still call).","Patch: hairless chest or thigh, rotate sites, on in the morning, off at night. Ointment (Nitro-Bid): chest or thigh under its paper. IV: ICU only (glass bottle, special tubing).","Severe hypotension with sildenafil, some antihypertensives, alcohol.","Isosorbide & ranolazine","Isosorbide: long-acting nitrate taken daily for prevention (also used in HF). For chest pain, take sublingual nitro, not another isosorbide. Taper to stop (stopping abruptly can cause coronary vasospasm).","Ranolazine (Ranexa): mechanism unknown (probably helps the myocardium use energy efficiently); PO. Prolongs the QT (lethal dysrhythmia risk); caution with renal or liver failure; no BP or HR drop; headache, dizziness, nausea, constipation. …","Heart failure drugs","The foundation for all HF types (HFpEF responds less): ACE inhibitor / ARB / ARNI + beta blocker + mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist + SGLT2 inhibitor.","RAAS inhibitors: ACE inhibitor, ARB, or ARNI: sacubitril-valsartan (ARB + neprilysin inhibitor) — ↓ preload and afterload, suppress aldosterone, ↓ cardiac remodeling; ↓ mortality in HFrEF. ARNIs are thought best (neprilysin inhibition), but…","Beta blocker: carvedilol (most used; beta and alpha blockade) — Blocks harmful SNS (neurohormonal) activation; helps dysrhythmias and remodeling. — Watch for hypotension (patients check their BP).","MRA: spironolactone (a potassium-sparing diuretic) — Suppresses sodium and water retention, offloading the LV (\"keep them dry\"); ↓ hospitalizations and deaths. — Watch the kidneys and hyperkalemia: potassium changes plus a sick heart mean d…","SGLT2 inhibitors (\"-gliflozin\") — Cardioprotective even without diabetes: unload the ventricle (natriuresis, osmotic diuresis), cardiac metabolism. ↓ readmissions, mortality, morbidity. — The mechanism in HF is still being studied.","Loop diuretic: furosemide — Treats volume overload (most HF admissions); PO, IV push or IV drip. — No survival benefit (symptom relief only). Watch potassium (dysrhythmias; low K⁺ with digoxin means toxicity) and hypotension.","Other inotropes: dopamine, dobutamine, milrinone — IV drips that make the heart squeeze harder (↑ contractility, ↑ CO). — More in complex care.","Digoxin (digitalis), a cardiac glycoside / inotrope: from foxglove. Inhibits the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase, so calcium builds in cardiac cells, causing ↑ contractility; also ↑ renal blood flow and ↓ SNS stimulation (↓ HR). No longer first line (little …","Toxicity risk: older age, women, diuretics, LOW POTASSIUM (the NCLEX favorite).","Signs: bradycardia (hallmark), headache, dizziness, confusion, nausea, visual disturbances.","Prevent: lowest effective dose, weekly levels (like an INR), potassium supplements if kidneys work. Take the apical pulse for a full minute; hold if below 60.","Antidote: digoxin immune Fab (severe toxicity or overdose).","Cardiac Pharmacology: Rate & Rhythm Control","Goal: heart rate under 100 and sinus rhythm when possible. Beta blockers (usually first) and CCBs treat sinus tachycardia not explained by a fixable cause (fever, volume) and keep A-fib or flutter rate-controlled (they can also help convert…","Amiodarone (antidysrhythmic) — For almost any dysrhythmia: A-fib, flutter, SVT, ventricular. Prolongs the action potential and refractory period; blocks alpha and beta receptors. IV drip when emergent, then PO. — About 75% have adverse effe…","Atropine (anticholinergic / antimuscarinic) — Symptomatic sinus bradycardia only: blocks the vagus nerve (vagolytic). Works only for vagal bradycardia (about 30% of cases), not a \"sick heart\" conduction problem. — IV push 1 mg every 3–5 min…","Adenosine — PSVT (or SVT): slows conduction through the AV node; very short half-life (may need repeat doses). — Causes a brief asystole (flat line) before rhythm returns: have shock pads on. IV only: 6 mg rapid push, then 12 mg, then 12 mg…","Dofetilide (antidysrhythmic) — Converts A-fib / flutter to sinus rhythm and maintains it (a home maintenance drug); blocks cardiac potassium channels. — Risk of torsades de pointes (polymorphic V-tach: deadly; start CPR) and SVT; headache, …"]},{"s":"Week 7: Cancer, Chemotherapy & Musculoskeletal▾","a":"week-7-cancer-chemotherapy-musculoskeletal","k":["Week 7: Cancer, Chemotherapy & Musculoskeletal▾","Cancer biology, four common cancers, chemotherapy, osteoporosis, fractures, OA, RA, gout and lupus.","Cancer Biology, Staging & Causes","Cancer = diseases of cellular transformation (e.g. genetic mutation), uncontrolled growth, invasion and metastasis. Cancer cells lack differentiation, skip DNA-error checkpoints, don't undergo apoptosis, and ignore signals from other cells.","Contact inhibition — Stay out of others' space — Invade","Cohesiveness — Stick together — Leave and spread","Communication — Signal neighbors — Ignore signals","Growth / life span — Controlled; apoptosis — Uncontrolled; \"immortal\" or unpredictable death","Appearance — Uniform, organized — Large, variably shaped nuclei (high nucleus-to-cytoplasm ratio), varied sizes, lost features, disorganized stacking, poorly defined boundary","HLA antigens — \"Self\" marker — Non-self marker, so the immune system may attack","Differentiation — Well differentiated, resembles the tissue of origin — Poorly differentiated / anaplastic","Growth — Slow, progressive — Erratic: slow or very rapid","Spread — Encapsulated, demarcated, movable; doesn't invade or metastasize; no necrotic core — Invasive, infiltrates tissue, metastasizes; may have a necrotic core, hemorrhage","Danger — Usually harmless, but can be lethal if it presses on a critical organ (brain, lung) — Cancer","Cell cycle (chemo targets phases)","G0: resting (most normal cells); cancer cells don't rest, and cells in G0 aren't affected by cycle-specific drugs.","G1: enters the cycle; DNA-synthesis enzymes made.","S: synthesis (DNA copied; the lecture describes the 46 chromosomes reorganizing into two sets of 23 pairs).","G2: prepares to divide (RNA, specialized proteins).","M: mitosis makes two daughter cells.","Generation time = time through one cycle (hours to days). Cancer cells cycle constantly with no restriction point, so no DNA repair and constant mutation.","Grading & staging","Grade (microscope): 1 = well, 2 = moderately, 3 = poorly differentiated / anaplastic.","TNM (international, biopsy-based): T = primary tumor size and involvement (T0 none, Tis = in situ, only in the layer it began; T1–T4 larger or more invasive); N = lymph nodes (N0 none, N1 closest few, N2 distant or numerous); M = metastasis…","Stages: I confined to the organ of origin (most treatable), II locally invasive, III regional spread, IV distant sites.","Metastasis","Malignant cells invade, then travel by lymph or blood and need their own blood vessels (angiogenesis) to grow. Primary tumor = site of origin; secondary tumors look like the primary tissue under the microscope (breast cells in bone). Seedin…","Lung — Bone, brain","Colon — Liver","Breast — Bone, brain, liver, lung","Prostate — Vertebrae","Melanoma — Brain","Risk factors","UV exposure (skin), low-fiber diet (GI); age (except reproductive cancers like cervical and uterine).","Males: lung, urinary (lifestyle, environment).","Genetic and ethnic: Burkitt lymphoma (African descent), nasopharyngeal (Chinese descent).","Oncogenic viruses: HPV, HIV, herpes, Epstein-Barr.","Occupational and environmental carcinogens and mutagens.","Genes","Tumor suppressor gene = the brake pedal: restrains division. Defective means uncontrolled growth. p53 controls apoptosis of cells with damaged DNA.","Proto-oncogene = the gas pedal: normal growth and division. Mutated into an oncogene = a gas pedal stuck down (constant division, no rest).","Carcinogen — Alters cell DNA; proven to cause cancer; usually needs prolonged exposure; damage is cumulative. Known: tobacco, asbestos, alcohol, HPV, HIV. Probable: night shift work (about 60% higher breast cancer risk). Possible: engine ex…","Promoter — Helps already-mutated cells proliferate: high-fat diet, alcohol, tobacco, hormones (more lifetime menstrual cycles means more estrogen: early first period, late first pregnancy, little or no breastfeeding). Some agents are both c…","Lung, Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer","Historically the four highest-prevalence cancers in Kentucky (the most common overall is non-melanoma skin cancer). The lecture mentions racial, ethnic and rural disparities in diagnosis and survival; you don't need the statistics for the e…","Lung cancer","Leading cause of cancer death in the US and Kentucky. Most are diagnosed late (advanced or metastatic) because early stages have few symptoms. Stage I five-year survival is over 60%; stage IV is below 10%.","Screening: low-dose CT. Average age at diagnosis is about 70 (often over 65).","Etiology: cigarette smoking (80–90% of cases; both a carcinogen and a promoter). Risk rises with pack-years (packs per day × years smoked; 2 packs a day from age 20 to 60 = 80 pack-years). Passive smoke (+20–30%), COPD (chronic inflammation…","Pathogenesis: smoke and toxins paralyze the cilia, so carcinogens build up along the airway. Cancer starts in an area of dysplasia (thickened bronchial mucosa), erodes the epithelium, and grows into the lumen or forms a mass.","Types: non-small cell (85–90%, slower growing) vs small cell (faster, metastasizes sooner, responds to initial treatment but relapses faster).","Signs and symptoms (overlap with smoking): a cough that won't go away or gets worse, hemoptysis or rust-colored sputum, chest pain worse with deep breathing, coughing or laughing, hoarseness (tumor pressing the laryngeal nerve), loss of app…","Breast cancer","Most common cancer in the US (outside non-melanoma skin cancer); about 1 in 8 women. Men can get it too.","Non-modifiable risks: age over 50; first period before 12, menopause after 55 (longer hormone exposure); dense breast tissue; family history of breast or ovarian cancer; prior chest radiation; mother took DES (given 1940–1971 to prevent mis…","Modifiable risks: overweight or obesity after menopause (fat stores estrogen); hormone replacement therapy (estrogen plus progesterone for more than 5 years); some hormonal oral contraceptives; first pregnancy after about 30, not breastfeed…","BRCA mutations also raise ovarian, colon and pancreatic (and, in males, prostate) cancer risk. Genetic counseling; some choose a preventive mastectomy or oophorectomy.","Lecture slip: the voiceover says first pregnancy \"after 20\"; this is usually taught as after 30. Check your slide.","Cervical cancer","Much less common now thanks to Pap smears and the HPV vaccine. Almost 100% of cervical cancer patients test HPV-positive.","Other risks: smoking; sexual history and STIs (chlamydia or gonorrhea compound HPV risk); immunosuppression (HIV/AIDS); genetics; DES.","HPV vaccine: 97% effective at preventing cervical cancer; 100% effective against genital warts; safe in decades-long studies. Best at age 11–12, can be given up to 45.","Colorectal cancer","Fourth most common cancer and fourth leading cause of cancer death; higher incidence and death in males.","Risks: age; inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn's, ulcerative colitis); family history of colorectal cancer or polyps; genetic syndromes (FAP, Lynch syndrome/HNPCC); sedentary lifestyle; diet low in fruits, vegetables and fiber, high in fat a…","Polyps: non-cancerous growths that can become cancer; usually asymptomatic and found on screening. Adenomatous = most common and higher cancer potential; hyperplastic = usually don't become cancer.","Symptoms (nonspecific): change in bowel habits, blood in stool, diarrhea or constipation, feeling the bowel doesn't empty (more rectal), abdominal pain or cramping, unexplained weight loss.","Screening starts at 45 (earlier with IBD, family history or genetic conditions). Flexible sigmoidoscopy sees about a third of the colon; colonoscopy usually every 10 years if normal.","Chemotherapy Principles","Antineoplastic drugs inhibit growth and spread of malignant cells. Cytotoxic chemotherapy kills cells, especially rapidly dividing ones. Chemo is one of three treatments, alongside surgery (removes the tumor) and radiation (high-energy radi…","Cell-cycle specific vs nonspecific","Phase-specific: work only in one phase (antimetabolites in S, mitotic inhibitors in M). They can't touch G0 cells, so they need to stay in the blood a long time (prolonged infusion or frequent small doses).","Phase-nonspecific: toxic in any phase, including G0, but less selective for proliferating cells.","Usually combined: one specific plus one nonspecific.","What affects success","Works best on fast-growing tumors with a high growth fraction (short doubling time).","Cancer type, correct drug, dose and schedule.","Drug resistance (can explain relapse).","The patient's general health.","General principles","No single drug works on all cancers. Combination therapy usually beats a single drug, avoiding overlapping toxicity (e.g. not two cardiotoxic drugs).","Low therapeutic index: a fine line between therapeutic and toxic.","Rapidly dividing normal cells get hit: hair follicles (alopecia starts 7–14 days after chemo, reversible, regrows 1–2 months after therapy ends), GI (nausea and vomiting, which can occur several days after chemo; stomatitis), bone marrow (n…","Most chemo drugs don't need routine therapeutic drug monitoring.","Toxicities, adverse effects and even allergies are usually not contraindications (premedication or desensitization). Reasons to delay or adjust: very low WBC/neutrophils, active infection, poor nutrition or hydration, liver or kidney dysfun…","Fertility: any chemo can impair it. Discuss sperm banking or egg freezing before treatment. Most chemo drugs are pregnancy category D or X.","Myelosuppression","Bone marrow suppression (chemo or radiation): fewer WBCs, RBCs and platelets. Biggest risk is infection from neutropenia.","Nadir (lowest counts) about 7–14 days after the dose: highest risk of infection, anemia and bleeding. A key teaching point.","Prophylactic antibiotic (levofloxacin) for high-risk prolonged neutropenia: ANC below 500 for more than 7 days.","Stomatitis & extravasation","Stomatitis = inflammation of the oral mucosa. Magic mouthwash (prescription): viscous lidocaine, Mylanta, diphenhydramine, nystatin, prednisolone, distilled water. Swish, gargle, spit (swallow if the esophagus is involved). Treats symptoms;…","Extravasation = the drug leaks into tissue during IV administration, causing severe tissue damage. Established protocols; chemo is usually given through a port or tunneled line.","Lecture slip: the nadir is said as \"between seven and 12 days\" and then \"seven, 14 days\" (and \"10 to 14 days\" right after). Go with your slide.","Chemotherapy Drug Classes","You won't be asked drug names; questions give the class. All cytotoxic agents disrupt DNA synthesis, target high-growth-fraction cells, are hazardous to handle (certification, double gloves, special disposal) and many are vesicants. Hormona…","Alkylating agent (cyclophosphamide) — Nonspecific — Extreme vesicant; hemorrhagic cystitis (metabolite acrolein): watch for blood in the urine; sterility with prolonged use; skin and nail discoloration; resistance develops over time.","Antimetabolite (methotrexate) — Specific: S phase — Mimics folic acid (blocks dihydrofolate reductase). Nephrotoxic at high doses (keep hydrated); hepatotoxic with long-term use (liver enzymes); category X, teratogenic. Resistance develops.…","Antitumor antibiotic (doxorubicin) — Nonspecific — From Streptomyces bacteria. Cardiotoxicity, cumulative (lifetime max dose): heart failure, can be fatal, acutely or years later. Baseline echocardiogram and EF monitoring.","Mitotic inhibitor / vinca alkaloid (vincristine) — Specific: M phase — From the periwinkle plant; blocks microtubules. Hallmark: peripheral neuropathy (neurotoxic). Significant vesicant. Usually doesn't cause much bone marrow suppression, s…","Biologics / targeted therapy (checkpoint inhibitors, CAR T-cell, monoclonal antibodies, cancer vaccines, immune modulators) — Not cell-cycle based — Harness the immune system. Flu-like symptoms (fever, chills, fatigue, headache, muscle and …","Lecture slip: vincristine is said to act in the \"end phase\"; the slide context is M phase (mitosis).","Antiemetics","Ondansetron (Zofran)","Blocks 5-HT3 (serotonin type 3) receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone; made for chemo-induced nausea and vomiting.","Effective alone or with others (especially steroids). PO or IV.","Adverse effects: headache, diarrhea, dizziness.","Promethazine (Phenergan)","First-generation antihistamine (chemo, post-op, general nausea).","Major effect: sedation; can cause respiratory depression. Many drug interactions.","Give IV piggyback (diluted); check the IV works before pushing. Black box: children under 2, and gangrenous extravasation.","Lecture slip: ondansetron is called a serotonin \"agonist\", but the same sentence says it blocks 5-HT3 receptors (an antagonist). Check your slide.","Osteoporosis & Its Drugs","Women have lower peak bone density and rapid bone loss in the early post-menopausal years, so they hit the fracture threshold earlier. From about age 30, osteoclast resorption exceeds osteoblast formation. Lifetime loss in women is about 35…","−1 or higher — Normal","Between −1 and −2.5 — Osteopenia (low bone mass; trabecular matrix thinning)","−2.5 or lower — Osteoporosis (\"porous bones\"): low density plus microarchitectural deterioration. Most common in the hip, vertebrae and wrist (trabecular bone).","−2.5 plus a fragility fracture — Severe osteoporosis (fragility fracture = breaking a bone falling from standing)","Age; female (post-menopausal); Caucasian or Asian.","Adult fracture history; first-degree relative with a fragility fracture; genetics.","Low body weight, thin small frame; no weight-bearing exercise.","Smoking, alcohol, excess caffeine.","Long-term corticosteroids (inhibit osteoblasts).","Low calcium or vitamin D; eating disorders; gastric bypass (malnutrition).","Low estrogen or testosterone.","Manifestations & hip fracture","Early: none (screen high-risk patients with DEXA). Later: fractures, pain, loss of height, stooped posture (kyphosis).","Hip fracture (most common in women over 65; proximal third of the femur): mortality rises 2.5–4 times, from immobility complications (pneumonia, clots, skin breakdown, sepsis), not the fracture itself. Independent living is often lost.","Presentation: sudden hip pain, can't walk, severe groin pain, the leg is externally rotated and shortened, little bruising (encapsulated). A fragility fracture may present as vague knee, groin or thigh pain with no trauma.","Drug goal = reduce fractures. Prevention: calcium and vitamin D (vitamin D increases calcium absorption). Treatment mostly decreases bone resorption. Hormone replacement therapy is not recommended for osteoporosis (clots, breast and other c…","Alendronate (Fosamax), bisphosphonate — First line; prevention and treatment. Binds permanently to bone and inhibits osteoclasts. Cuts fractures about 50%. — Bioavailability only about 1% (juice or coffee cut it about 60%): take in the morn…","Raloxifene (Evista), SERM — Mimics estrogen on bone; safer than estrogen. Post-menopausal osteoporosis only. Reduces spinal fractures (about 50%), not hip. — Hot flashes, leg cramps, stroke and clot risk: stop at least 72 h before a procedu…","Calcitonin, hormone — Inhibits osteoclasts. Treatment only, not prevention; not long term. Reduces spinal fractures only. — Unique analgesic effect (acute vertebral or hip fracture pain). Given intranasally: nasal irritation. Expensive, not…","Lecture slip: one line says osteoporosis applies when trabecular breaks \"have not occurred\", which contradicts the slide definition (actual breaks in the matrix). Check your slide.","Fractures & Complications","A fracture is a break in bone continuity when stress exceeds what the bone can absorb. Causes: traumatic (fall), indirect (massive muscle contraction, rare), fatigue/stress (repeated stress, marathon runners), pathologic (osteoporosis, bone…","Types to know","Open (compound): bone breaks through the skin. Closed (simple): doesn't.","Transverse: straight line at 90°; common with traumatic falls.","Spiral: twisting injury.","Comminuted: more than one fracture line and more than two fragments (e.g. landing on the feet from a height).","Greenstick: incomplete; the bone bends. Most common in children.","Stress: repeated use.","Presentation & healing","PED: Pain, Edema (a natural splint), Deformity, plus loss of function and abnormal mobility. Splint in the position of comfort and go to the ED.","Healing phases: inflammatory (hematoma), reparative (fibrocartilage callus, ossification), remodeling. Complications come from this process going wrong.","Delayed union — Identified 3 months to a year out: bone pain and tenderness increase when it should have healed. Risks: tobacco, older age, severe anemia, uncontrolled diabetes, low vitamin D, hypothyroid, poor nutrition, infection, complic…","Malunion — Heals in improper alignment (unequal muscle pull and gravity); often from weight bearing too soon.","Nonunion — Not healed 4–6 months after the fracture (diabetes, local infection).","Impaired bone growth — Pediatric: a fracture through the epiphyseal (growth) plate.","Compartment syndrome — Increased pressure in a limited space (crush injury, cast too tight, swelling or bleeding): a tourniquet effect compresses vessels and nerves, causing hypoxia and necrosis. Signs: pain out of proportion to the injury,…","Fat embolism — Rare; after a long-bone fracture or major trauma. Fat from marrow blocks lung vessels: sudden, possibly respiratory arrest. Classic triad: hypoxemia, altered LOC, petechial rash (often last). Treatment is supportive (ventilat…","Osteomyelitis — Probably the most common fracture complication: a pus-producing bone infection, usually Staph aureus. Risks: recent trauma (open fracture more than closed), diabetes, hemodialysis, IV drug use, splenectomy. Routes: direct (o…","The transcript garbles the empiric osteomyelitis antibiotics (\"naphthal encephalol and a rankamycin\", likely nafcillin or cefazolin plus vancomycin). Check your slide for the exact drugs.","Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease","Synovial joints: outer fibrous capsule, inner synovial membrane, articular cartilage (prevents friction) and synovial fluid (lubricates). Arthropathy = joint disorder; with inflammation of one or more joints it's arthritis.","OA basics","Degeneration from aging and wear and tear; inflammation is local, not systemic. Most common arthritis and the leading cause of chronic disability in the US.","Sites: cervical and lumbosacral spine, hip, knee, hands, big toe. Usually spared: wrists, elbows, ankles.","More common in men before 45, in women after 45.","Risks: age (cartilage loss after 40), obesity (10 lb overweight adds 30–60 lb of force on the knee per step), long sports participation, repetitive heavy occupational work, prior trauma, misalignment (wider female hips, leg-length differenc…","Pathophysiology","Pressure wears away cartilage, exposing subchondral bone; cysts form and erode the remaining cartilage.","Chondrocytes make proteoglycans to repair, but the excess fluid causes swelling.","Osteoblasts build osteophytes (bone spurs), the hallmark; they can break off into the joint.","Synovial fluid thickens; joint space narrows: bone on bone.","Manifestations: deep aching pain with exertion, relieved by rest; worse in cold weather; stiffness that improves once moving; crepitus; altered gait; hard swelling; limited ROM; deformity. Hands: Heberden's nodes (distal joints) and Bouchar…","Treatment: no drug stops or reverses OA, so manage pain, keep mobility, minimize disability (physical therapy, canes and walkers, weight loss, joint replacement). Mild to moderate: acetaminophen first, then OTC NSAIDs; topical capsaicin (fr…","Degenerative disc disease","The same process in the spine: lumbar (L4, L5, S1) or cervical. Lifting and twisting (nurses!). Back pain is the second most common reason for doctor visits and the top cause of work disability under 45. Discs compress with age, so nerves a…","Rheumatoid Arthritis","RA is a systemic autoimmune disease (a type III hypersensitivity): immune complexes deposit in the synovium and destroy the joint. Cause unknown: genetic predisposition plus a trigger (infection, injury, hormonal or reproductive factors).","Risk & patho","Diagnosed mostly at 40–60; women 2.5 times more than men; tobacco; strong family history.","B cells and macrophages release cytokines; they make rheumatoid factor (RF), an antibody against your own IgG. Diagnosis: RF plus symptoms and exam; CRP and sed rate for inflammation.","Lysosomal enzymes destroy cartilage; very vascular, inflammatory scar tissue called pannus forms and keeps destroying the joint.","Manifestations","Early: vague systemic signs (fatigue, anorexia, weight loss, generalized stiffness).","Joints affected symmetrically: heat, swelling, tenderness, morning stiffness lasting more than an hour; swelling is soft, spongy and warm (OA swelling is hard).","Advanced: subluxation (partial loss of contact, can't be fixed), swan-neck deformity (no Heberden's or Bouchard's nodes).","Systemic: fatigue and malaise, pericarditis or myocarditis, lung nodules, Sjögren's syndrome (dry eyes and mouth), rheumatoid nodules (firm subcutaneous granulomas near joints; about 25%).","NSAIDs — Immediate symptom relief.","Prednisone (glucocorticoid) — Short-term for severe flares not controlled by NSAIDs; rapid suppression of inflammation. If long term, low dose (below 10 mg/day, as little as 2.5 mg) with DMARDs.","Methotrexate (DMARD) — First line; slows disease. Interferes with folate metabolism, so folic acid supplement. Once WEEKLY (daily dosing can kill). GI effects; hepatotoxic (AST, ALT, alk phos; no alcohol); bone marrow suppression (CBC; repo…","Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil) (DMARD) — Antimalarial; dampens macrophage, T-helper and cytokine activity. Better tolerated than methotrexate; best combined. About 1% retinopathy (can cause blindness): report blurry vision.","Biologics (biologic response modifiers) — Target the immune system; expensive. Risks: severe skin or lung infections, skin cancers, serious allergic reactions. Names not needed.","Lecture slip: says methotrexate \"used in smaller doses\" is for cancer; it's the other way around (low weekly doses for RA, higher doses for cancer). Check your slide.","Gout","A disorder of uric acid metabolism: urate crystals deposit in and around joints (gouty arthritis, acute and very painful). Purines break down to uric acid, which the kidneys excrete. High-purine foods: alcohol (beer, red wine), organ meats,…","Risk & phases","Mostly men; obesity; HTN, diabetes, renal disease, sickle cell; alcohol use; high-purine diet; diuretics; Black Americans.","Phases: 1) asymptomatic hyperuricemia; 2) acute flare; 3) clinically inactive (still hyperuricemic); flares come closer together until chronic gouty arthritis.","Excruciating pain, classically the big toe (first MTP joint, about 50%): can't tolerate a sheet or sock.","Burning, redness, swelling, warmth, fever; lasts days to weeks.","Tophi: hard nodules of urate crystals under the skin around joints; may drain chalky material.","High risk of kidney stones.","NSAIDs (steroids if NSAIDs can't be used or don't work) — Drug of choice for acute attacks — Anti-inflammatory symptom relief.","Allopurinol — Prophylaxis only (no help in an acute attack); xanthine oxidase inhibitor, so less uric acid is produced — Usually well tolerated; most common effect is rash, but watch for Stevens-Johnson syndrome and TEN. Agranulocytosis and…","Colchicine — Oldest; second line; mainly for flares (prophylaxis if allopurinol isn't tolerated) — Inhibits mitosis and leukocyte migration, so less inflammation; short-term leukopenia and marrow suppression. GI symptoms (stomach-flu-like, …","Probenecid — Uricosuric: blocks uric acid reabsorption (more excreted); alone or with allopurinol — Take with food and plenty of fluids. Report kidney problems (hematuria, change in urine amount, rapid weight gain) and liver signs. Many dru…","Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)","A complex autoimmune disease: autoantibodies, mainly ANA (antinuclear antibody), against the body's own DNA form immune complexes that can land in any organ and destroy it. The most common target is the kidney (glomeruli). In RA, complexes …","Who & triggers","Genetic; females; ages 20–40; most common in Black Americans.","Sun exposure (triggers flares); hormones (estrogen-containing birth control raises risk 50%; menarche before 10 doubles it); smoking; antibiotic allergies.","Course: exacerbations and remissions. Flare warning signs: more fatigue, pain, headache. Triggers: sunlight, infection, abruptly stopping a medicine, stress.","Fatigue (most common), photosensitivity, butterfly (malar) rash (hallmark), edema (legs, around the eyes), fever, hair loss.","Raynaud's: small distal capillaries constrict with cold (painful); calcium channel blockers.","Any organ: brain (headache, seizures, stroke), lungs (pleuritis, effusion), heart (myocarditis, endocarditis), kidneys (about half; may need dialysis or transplant), vasculitis, anemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, arthritis.","Multi-organ disease = poor prognosis; most have mild disease and do well.","Treatment depends on the systems involved: NSAIDs (headache, musculoskeletal pain, pleuritis, pericarditis); steroids (high dose for severe kidney or CNS disease, low dose for arthritis); hydroxychloroquine (skin and joints, and helps preve…","The transcript's \"brain nods\" is Raynaud's phenomenon (the context is cold-triggered constriction of distal capillaries)."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"final-nur406-review.html","t":"NUR 406 Review","secs":[{"s":"NUR 406 Review","a":"","k":["Final Exam Review · NUR 406","Whole-Course Quick Reference","Week 1: Cardiovascular","Week 2: Acid-Base Balance & ABGs","Week 3: Neuro I","Week 4: Neuro II","Week 5: Endocrine Emergencies","Self-Test Flashcards","This review pulls together your NUR 406 lectures (Weeks 1–5), taken from the comprehensive study guide for each week, so the whole course is on one page. Grey notes are the same lecture-slip notes as on the week pages. Use the hide toggles …"]},{"s":"Whole-Course Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-course-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Course Quick Reference▾","The points to have cold before the final, one row per lecture part.","Wk 1 · 1. Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — Myocarditis is mostly viral (Coxsackie A and B) and can disrupt conduction. Normal pericardial fluid is 15–50 mL; about 200 mL compresses the heart into cardiac tamponade, a medical …","Wk 1 · 2A. Acute Coronary Syndrome — ACS = unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI; time is tissue. Prinzmetal's is vasospasm, often at rest, treated with nitrates. Unstable plaque: big lipid core, thin fibrous cap, inflammation (CRP). First-time ch…","Wk 1 · 2B. Myocardial Infarction — MI is irreversible ischemic necrosis; ECG changes stay and troponin trends up. Myocardial cells can't store ATP: contraction drops in 1–2 minutes, irreversible injury from 30 minutes to 4 hours, so reperfu…","Wk 1 · 3. Acute MI Pharmacology — Take vital signs before every drug. Aspirin: chew the first dose; decreases mortality in STEMI. Morphine: watch respiratory depression and hypotension; may inhibit antiplatelets. Nitroglycerin: reduces prel…","Wk 1 · 4. Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection — Primarily from hypertension; turbulent flow, a bruit, rupture risk. Saccular (one side, wide neck), berry (small neck, circle of Willis; drain, clip or coil), fusiform (whole circumference, monitore…","Wk 2 · 1A. pH, Hydrogen & Acids — More hydrogen ions = lower pH; the scale is logarithmic (each number is 10 times). Normal serum pH 7.35–7.45; below 7.35 is acidosis, above 7.45 alkalosis; below 6.8 or above 7.8 is incompatible with life. …","Wk 2 · 1B. How the Body Defends Its pH — Buffers act in seconds, the lungs in minutes to hours, the kidneys in hours to days. Bicarbonate–carbonic acid is the main extracellular buffer; phosphate and protein buffers are intracellular. Know …","Wk 2 · 2A. ABGs & the Four Disorders — Know the numbers: pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO2 35–45, HCO3 24–29 (BMP CO2 = bicarb). ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Metabolic alkalosis: vomiting, NG suction, loop diuretics, antacids (treat with …","Wk 2 · 2B. ABG Interpretation — Step 1 pH (7.40 is the midpoint), step 2 PaCO2, step 3 HCO3, step 4 compensation. The value that matches the pH is the problem; the other one moving the same direction is compensating. Uncompensated: one abno…","Wk 3 · 1. Migraines — 4 to 72 hours; at least two of unilateral, throbbing, worse with activity, moderate to severe, plus at least one of nausea and vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia. Without aura is most common; chronic = 15 days a month …","Wk 3 · 2A. Parkinson's Disease — Substantia nigra loss → low dopamine → relative excess acetylcholine. Triad: rest tremor (pill-rolling, better with movement, worse with stress), rigidity (cogwheel) and bradykinesia (the major cause of disa…","Wk 3 · 2B. Parkinson's Pharmacology — Correct the imbalance: boost dopamine or block acetylcholine. Sinemet is the gold standard (levodopa → dopamine; carbidopa protects levodopa); months to work; on-off and wearing-off loss of effect; avoi…","Wk 3 · 3. Myasthenia Gravis — Autoimmune attack on nicotinic ACh receptors at the neuromuscular junction (ACh supply is fine). Fluctuating weakness that improves with rest; eyes and face first; chewing and swallowing (aspiration); respirato…","Wk 3 · 4. Multiple Sclerosis — T cells cross the blood-brain barrier and attack CNS myelin; oligodendrocytes are lost; scarring; axon death means permanent loss. Age 20–40, female (males worse), cool climates, white, family history. RRMS is…","Wk 4 · 1A. ALS — Lou Gehrig's disease; unknown cause; fatal from respiratory failure, about 3 years after diagnosis. Ages 40–70 (peak 60–69), males, 10% familial, possibly smoking. Upper and lower motor neuron degeneration → voluntary muscl…","Wk 4 · 1B. Guillain-Barré Syndrome — Autoimmune demyelination of peripheral nerves (acute ascending polyneuritis). Trigger: viral respiratory or GI infection; also Campylobacter jejuni, surgery, rarely flu vaccine. Weakness and tingling asc…","Wk 4 · 2. Neuro Infectious Diseases — Meningitis: S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis; respiratory droplets; fall and winter; dorms, prisons, military. Triad: fever, headache, nuchal rigidity; photophobia; seizures in 30%; non-blanching petec…","Wk 4 · 3A. TIA — Focal, acute, one vessel; under 24 hours, no infarction, no residual effects. Stroke risk highest in the first month. Carotid: weakness, numbness, vision loss. Vertebrobasilar: blurry vision, vertigo, ataxia. Bell's palsy m…","Wk 4 · 3B. Stroke & Ischemic Stroke — 87% ischemic, 13% hemorrhagic; leading cause of disability. Risks: HTN, atherosclerosis, tobacco, diabetes, OCs, age (higher risk, better recovery), males, sickle cell, A-fib. Non-contrast CT first: blo…","Wk 4 · 3C. Hemorrhagic Stroke & Bleeds — Ruptured vessel; untreated HTN; 38% mortality; ages 30–60; edema and ICP → herniation. Prognosis: age, location and size, bleeding speed and midline shift. Worst headache of their life; meningeal sig…","Wk 4 · 3D. Stroke Treatment & Consequences — ABCs (GCS below 8 → intubate), blood sugar, non-contrast CT first. Hemorrhagic: reverse anticoagulation, manage BP and ICP (HOB up, antipyretics, neutral head). Ischemic: alteplase (plasminogen →…","Wk 5 · 1A. DKA & HHS: Physiology & Labs — DKA: type 1, under 65, ketosis (30% of cases in type 2, especially SGLT2 inhibitors). HHS: type 2, over 65, no ketosis, rare. Insulin isn't working; counter-regulatory hormones rise; osmotic diuresi…","Wk 5 · 1B. Presentation, Treatment & Resolution — Triggers: infection (most common), MI, CVA, sepsis, pancreatitis, poor insulin compliance (check A1c). DKA evolves over about 24 hours: N/V and abdominal pain, confusion, volume depletion, f…","Wk 5 · 2A. Thyroid Storm — Overwhelming thyroid hormone release after a major stressor; death within about 48 hours untreated. Risks: long-standing untreated hyperthyroidism, acute event, irregular use or stopping antithyroid drugs. Fever 1…","Wk 5 · 2B. Myxedema Coma — Severe hypothyroidism; high mortality, uncommon. Risks: poorly controlled hypothyroidism plus infection, MI, surgery; sedatives, especially opioids. Usually confusion or lethargy, not coma; hypothermia (lower temp…","Wk 5 · 3A. Addison's Disease & Replacement — Three S's: sugar (cortisol), salt (aldosterone), sex (androgens); cortisol loss is the most severe. Mostly autoimmune; ACTH and MSH rise without feedback; symptoms at 90% destruction. Low aldoste…","Wk 5 · 3B. Addisonian Crisis — Sudden corticosteroid insufficiency; life-threatening. Triggers: surgery, infection, trauma, severe illness, sudden stop of long-term steroids (taper). Sudden back, leg and abdominal pain; severe vomiting and …"]},{"s":"Week 1: Cardiovascular▾","a":"week-1-cardiovascular","k":["Week 1: Cardiovascular▾","Pericardial disease, acute coronary syndrome, MI, acute MI pharmacology, aneurysms and aortic dissection.","Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","Topic 1, the first lecture of NUR 406: myocarditis, then the pericardium, how inflammation leads to pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade, the bedside signs, pulsus paradoxus, and treatment.","Myocarditis","Think back to normal A&P: the myocardium is the muscle layer of the heart, the muscle cells. Myocarditis is an inflammatory disease of the myocardium.","Range of severity — A wide variety of symptoms: it can be a pretty mild disorder with little to no discomfort, all the way up to heart failure and a lethal condition.","What happens to the cells — The inflammation comes with degeneration and necrosis of the cardiac myocytes. The inflammation causes direct injury to the myocytes, and over time it can create fibrosis and scarring that lead to structural chan…","Conduction — We often see a disruption in conduction through the heart. The tissue changes, inflammatory cytokines and T cell activation in the myocardium can result in abnormal electrical conduction.","Cause — Typically viruses. In the United States and Europe, viruses are the leading cause of myocarditis. The Coxsackie virus A and B are the majority of cases.","Treatment — Depends on how severe the symptoms are. Treated by giving antivirals; anti-inflammatories can also be used.","The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid","The pericardium is a sac-like membrane that surrounds the heart and the roots of the major blood vessels, and it helps the heart function properly. Between its inner and outer layers is the pericardial space.","Normal — A small amount, about 15 to 50 mL. — Acts like a lubricant between the inner and outer layers, for normal function of the heart.","Pericardial effusion — When the pericardium is inflamed, fluid accumulates in excess of what is normal. Effusion is that excess fluid accumulation. — Can compress the heart chambers and limit cardiac filling.","Cardiac tamponade — About 200 mL. — The fluid compresses the heart. The chambers are restricted and can't stretch and fill with blood as they normally would.","Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency. The fluid has to be removed quickly, because the heart chambers can't stretch and fill with blood.","Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade","The lecture describes what acute pericarditis looks like, then what changes once it progresses to cardiac tamponade and cardiac output falls.","Acute pericarditis","Chest pain: a sharp pain that worsens with deep breathing.","Fever, because of the inflammatory process.","Dyspnea (the lecture glosses it as \"some painful breathing\").","Pericardial friction rub on auscultation: a scratching sound, almost like rubbing on a balloon.","Muffled heart sounds, because you are listening to the heart pump through a layer of fluid.","ECG changes: elevations and also depressions.","Pulsus paradoxus is also named as a sign of acute pericarditis.","Cardiac tamponade","Signs of decreased cardiac output: hypotension, and the usual signs of a patient who is not being perfused appropriately, such as confusion and dizziness.","Jugular vein distension, from blood backing up in the system because of the decreased cardiac output.","Muffled heart sounds, from listening through a layer of fluid.","Pulsus paradoxus occurs in about 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","Pulsus Paradoxus","What it is — A decrease in the systolic blood pressure when the patient breathes in (with inspiration).","Why it's a \"paradox\" — Typically your blood pressure should increase a little bit when you breathe in. Seeing a drop instead is the paradox.","What it indicates — The heart is not pumping properly, and something is impacting cardiac filling.","How you see it — Most clearly with an arterial line (an IV catheter in an artery), which gives real-time blood pressure: you can watch the pressure change as the patient breathes in.","How often — About 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","The recording garbles the exact size of the systolic drop (\"a decrease in the systolic blood pressure of more and more\"), so no number is given here. If the slide shows a value, it can be added.","Pericarditis on the ECG","The ECG in pericarditis shows elements of ST depression as well as ST elevation. In some cases it looks similar to a patient having an MI, but the clinical symptoms will be different.","Treating Pericarditis","NSAIDs (anti-inflammatories) — Anti-inflammatories are used. Aspirin may be seen occasionally.","Corticosteroids — \"Give or take\": sometimes they work really well, sometimes not so much.","Colchicine — Typically a gout medication, but recently found to work well for pericarditis that is not responding to NSAIDs.","Pericardiocentesis — For cardiac tamponade caused by the pericarditis. A needle is put directly into the pericardium and the fluid is drawn out. Because tamponade is a medical emergency, this happens quickly.","Acute Coronary Syndrome","Topic 2, first half: what ACS is, the spectrum from Prinzmetal's angina to STEMI, what makes plaque unstable and why it ruptures, unstable angina, and how ACS differs from stable angina.","What ACS Is","Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)","An umbrella term for situations in which the blood supply to the heart muscle is suddenly blocked or compromised.","It is certainly a medical emergency.","The blockage can come and go, or it can be sudden and complete. Either way, cardiac tissue is dying.","ACS covers three conditions: unstable angina, non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), and ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).","The three have a similar pathophysiology, so the signs and symptoms can look similar. The ECG is what helps determine the next step: send the patient to the cath lab for reperfusion, or treat medically?","\"Time is tissue.\" The instructor says it several times: treat the blockage as quickly as possible to save as much tissue as possible.","The ACS Spectrum","The slide shows a progression from a normal vessel to complete occlusion. Stable angina has been covered before; ACS is the part of the picture where stable angina has become unstable.","Asymptomatic plaque — Some buildup of atherosclerotic plaque. — Nothing. They don't know it's there unless we go looking for it.","Stable angina — Atherosclerotic plaque buildup. — Signs and symptoms with exertion (strenuous activity).","Unstable angina — The clot is able to stay really small, and blood supply is still somewhat maintained. — Any new or changing chest pain.","NSTEMI — A larger buildup; blood flow is even more decreased. Some blood still gets through, but not enough. — A myocardial infarction without a change in the ST segment on the ECG.","STEMI — The vessel is completely and totally occluded by the clot. — A myocardial infarction with elevation of the ST segment on the ECG.","On the other side of the spectrum slide sits Prinzmetal's variant angina, which is a vasospasm of the coronary artery rather than plaque buildup (next section).","Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina","Mechanism — The coronary artery vasospasms. It is not necessarily related to atherosclerotic plaque buildup; the spasm reduces oxygenated blood flow to the cardiac tissue. The spasms can increase a person's risk for myocardial ischemia.","Cause — A lot of times, damage to the endothelium of the coronary vessel. The spasms narrow the artery and decrease blood flow.","Plaque — These patients may or may not have atherosclerotic plaque.","When it happens — Oftentimes at rest: sleeping, sitting around, minimal activity. These are not the people who are overexerting themselves.","ECG — May show ST elevation. Monitor closely to see whether treatment is working and the ECG changes return to normal.","Treatment — Oftentimes nitrates, because they relax the spasm.","Outlook — Usually benign, but it can cause some serious dysrhythmias to be on the lookout for.","What Makes Plaque Unstable","Usually, when plaque becomes unstable, people develop ACS. These are people who already have atherosclerotic plaque in their coronary vessels.","Stable plaque","A pretty thick fibrous cap on top of the fat, which contributes to its stability.","Unstable plaque","The lipid core (the fat inside the vessel) gets bigger.","As the core grows, the fibrous cap gets thinner.","There is a component of inflammation; C-reactive protein (CRP) is a marker of inflammation in the body.","Smooth muscle cells proliferate into the intima (the lecture describes the intima as the middle layer of the blood vessel). These cells create the fibrous cap and bring in inflammatory components, increasing the risk of plaque rupture.","Assessing Unstable Plaque","A study that puts dye into the vessel shows how much narrowing is happening, so we can roughly estimate whether the lipid core is larger or smaller.","CRP levels show how much inflammation is going on.","Why Plaque Ruptures","Rupture risk is higher with unstable plaque: a large lipid core, a thin fibrous cap, and ongoing inflammation. The slide walks through the theory step by step.","1. Trigger — Psychological stress, exercise, or circadian rhythms stimulate the sympathetic nervous system.","2. Sympathetic response — Epinephrine and norepinephrine are released: blood pressure and heart rate rise, and the heart works harder with stronger contractions.","3. Force on the vessels — Pushing out more blood with stronger contractions puts more force on the vessels, including the coronary vessels themselves.","4. Rupture — That force can lead to rupture of the plaque.","5. Platelets — Platelets come to the site and adhere to the ruptured plaque. They release substances that attract more platelets and can contribute to vasospasm.","6. Thrombus — A thrombus forms in that same area.","Unstable Angina","What counts as unstable angina","Chest pain occurring for the first time. Consider it unstable immediately, until proven otherwise; it is a medical emergency because no pattern or history has been established.","In a patient who normally has stable angina, pain that is more severe than normal: for example, they have taken their first nitro and five minutes later the pain hasn't stopped or is getting worse.","Pain that is different: usually in the chest, but this time down one arm, down both arms, or radiating into the jaw.","It may happen at rest; the person may be awakened in the middle of the night.","What's happening in the vessel","The plaque ruptures and a thrombus forms. That rupture and clot formation is the real worry.","The size of the clot and of the blockage determine how much blood gets past to perfuse the tissue on the other side. A 30% occluded artery may still allow some flow around a clot; a 90% blockage leaves little room.","If there is no infarction, the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved, and some tissue was still perfused.","The ECG changes and then goes back to normal, and we typically don't see troponin changes.","Unstable angina is a medical emergency. Teach patients to recognize the signs, recognize it as an emergency, and call 911 right now. They should most definitely not drive themselves to the ER.","Stable Angina vs ACS","Teach patients the difference so they can identify it and seek care. The big difference is the pain: its severity and its duration.","Stable angina","Pain should last about 5 minutes, and be gone by maybe about 15 minutes.","May feel like a little bit of chest tightness.","Flares up with strenuous activity.","ACS","Chest pain lasts a lot longer.","No relief with nitroglycerin.","More severe: the sensation of an elephant sitting on your chest.","Other symptoms: diaphoresis, shortness of air, nausea and vomiting.","An impending sense of doom: they know something is not right but can't pinpoint it. Take them very, very seriously.","Signs in Men and Women","Research over roughly the last 50 years shows different signs and symptoms of ACS between men and women. These symptoms aren't limited to one or the other.","Typically associated with men","Chest pain.","Tingling or discomfort in the back, arms, neck or jaw.","Other symptoms (often reported by women)","Dizziness, nausea, vomiting, tiredness.","Heartburn: a lot of women with ACS say they took some Tums that morning because they thought it was heartburn.","Some of these can feel similar to stable angina. The particular concern is when the experience changes or gets worse.","Myocardial Infarction","Topic 2, second half: the signs of MI, what an acute MI is, what happens to heart tissue over time, the zones of damage, STEMI vs NSTEMI, and the LAD.","Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction","What the lecture lists","Diaphoresis and dyspnea.","Extreme anxiety; restless, can't sit still, moving around.","Impending sense of doom (\"I know something's wrong\") without being able to pinpoint it.","Levine sign: the fist to the chest, the \"Hollywood\" heart attack sign.","Pallor and weak pulses, from perfusion issues because the heart isn't working right.","Retrosternal (behind the sternum) crushing chest pain that can radiate to several parts of the body; the elephant on the chest.","Acute Myocardial Infarction","What an acute MI is","Ruptured plaque plus thrombus that severely reduces or completely blocks blood flow to the heart muscle.","Think of ACS as a continuum; MI is at the end of that continuum.","It is ACS with prolonged ischemia that results in the death of myocardial tissue: an irreversible ischemic necrosis. These cells are never going to function properly again.","Infarction happens either because blood flow disruption goes on over a very long period of time, or because of a complete and total blockage.","How much tissue is lost depends on: where the infarction is (smaller or larger vessel, left or right side of the heart), how long blood supply has been affected, and how complete the blockage is. Partial flow over a few days can damage tiss…","MIs are classified by ECG findings: STEMI and NSTEMI.","ECG — Changes, then eventually goes back to normal. — Changes that stay; a repeat ECG won't go back to normal.","Troponin — Typically no change in cardiac enzymes. — Increases. Serial troponins trend up during the infarction, then trend down after the occlusion has been rectified.","Tissue — No infarction: the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved. — Death of myocardial tissue, which is irreversible.","Ischemia, Infarction & ATP","Ischemia","When the oxygen supply does not meet the heart's metabolic demands.","Infarction","Tissue death that results from that ischemia.","Why ATP Matters","ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a molecule that stores and transfers energy in cells; it is the main energy source for cellular function.","Myocardial cells cannot store ATP. They must be continuously fed ATP, so they need a continuous supply of oxygen to make it.","When oxygen goes down, ATP supply goes down, but the heart can't stop beating while ATP isn't being produced.","What Happens to Heart Tissue Over Time","Acute ischemia — ATP depletes instantly.","1 to 2 minutes — Decreased ability of the heart to contract.","30 minutes up to 4 hours — Irreversible injury to cardiac tissue. By 4 hours, necrosis has begun and the tissue begins to die.","1 to 2 weeks — The necrotic tissue is cleared away; the myocardium is very weak and susceptible to rupture.","About 6 weeks — The necrotic tissue has been replaced by tough scar tissue.","Reperfusion: as quickly as possible, at least within 4 hours. Most guidelines state reperfusion therapy should be done within four hours. Within 30 minutes would be ideal, since irreversible injury can occur that quickly.","Scar tissue is thickened and tougher, so electrical impulses have a harder time traveling through it. That is why ECGs after an MI can show permanent changes.","Zones of Damage","Infarction — Necrosis; dead cells, where the MI happened. — No. The goal is to stop it from increasing.","Injury — Not dead yet, but the tissue has suffered injury. — Some recovery is still possible; it can be perfused and restored to be viable.","Ischemia — The outer zone. — Full recovery is possible if it is perfused again.","The goal is to not extend the size of the infarct: increase oxygen and decrease the demand on the heart. The instructor compares this with an ischemic stroke: the same concept of tissue dying from lack of oxygen, where time is of the essenc…","Three Factors That Decide the Extent of Damage","The location or level of the occlusion in the coronary artery.","The length of time it has been occluded.","The availability of collateral circulation. In people with atherosclerotic plaque the heart can start to develop collateral circulation. The lecture calls it \"typically a bad thing,\" but here it creates ways to bypass the blockage and still…","STEMI vs NSTEMI","The main difference is the ST segment on the ECG.","ST segment — Elevated. — Normal, or a slight ST depression.","QRS complex — Usually a wide QRS that develops over hours. — Normal.","T wave — Peaked, then over time less peaked, then inverted. — Inverted the entire time.","Troponin — Elevated; still elevated after 2 hours, with a sharper increase than in NSTEMI. — Elevated.","Infarct size — Typically larger; more blockage, more tissue dying. — Smaller.","Outcomes — Worse. — Better than STEMI.","The \"tombstone.\" Nurses describe the ST elevation of a STEMI as looking like a tombstone, because the prognosis is not good if it isn't treated very quickly. Seeing it on an ECG should throw up red flags: investigate, and do it quickly.","On the ECG images, an NSTEMI can show ST depression or T wave inversion; a STEMI shows ST elevation.","The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"","Coronary blood supply named in the lecture","The right coronary artery.","The left main artery, which branches into the left circumflex artery and the left anterior descending (LAD) artery.","The LAD supplies almost the entire front side of the heart, much of the sidewall, and most of the left ventricle.","The LAD is the vessel most commonly involved in MI, and one of the most damaging. It is a really large vessel feeding most of the left ventricle, so a blockage there is particularly detrimental and often very lethal if not treated very quic…","Acute MI Pharmacology","Topic 3, the second part of the same ACS PowerPoint: the initial drugs, fibrinolytic therapy with alteplase, nitroglycerin safety, reperfusion injury, and the key takeaways. Diagnostics and surgical reperfusion are covered in med-surg, not …","Initial Drug Treatment","The lecture goes through these drugs twice, once briefly and once as a recap with more detail. Both passes are combined here.","Oxygen — Increases oxygen delivery to the ischemic myocardium. — New research shows that if the patient is not hypoxic, oxygen may not have that benefit. Whether it is ordered depends on the provider.","Morphine — Decreases pain. Some research suggests it dilates blood vessels to help blood flow, and there is some thought that it helps preserve ischemic tissue. — Side effects: respiratory depression and hypotension, so check vital signs an…","Aspirin — Makes platelets less sticky and suppresses platelet aggregation, so the thrombus is less likely to form or get bigger. — Routinely used. Have the patient chew the first dose to speed absorption. Research shows aspirin decreases mo…","Nitroglycerin — Dilates the veins to decrease preload; reduces preload and afterload, which helps limit infarct size. — It does not reduce mortality. Given sublingually, or IV if the patient can maintain their blood pressure.","Beta blockers — Reduce heart rate and contractility (and blood pressure), which reduces overall oxygen demand. This can reduce pain, reduce infarct size and help with mortality. — Only if there are no contraindications.","Thrombolytic agent — The biggest line of treatment. — Only if the patient is eligible, with no contraindications; for example, not after a recent hemorrhagic stroke. Ideally given within about 4 to 6 hours of the chest pain starting. These …","Take vital signs before giving any of these. Note heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory effort before administration. These medications can push someone over the edge if they aren't stable to begin with.","Which Drugs Affect Mortality","Aspirin — Decreases mortality in STEMI.","Beta blockers — Can help with mortality.","Nitroglycerin — Does not reduce mortality (it limits infarct size).","Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)","When it's used","Often used in a STEMI.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) is the first choice for treating a STEMI. If a cath lab is not available, fibrinolytic therapy is effective.","These are the \"clot busters.\"","Alteplase (tPA)","The prototype and the drug of choice; it is the most effective.","Dissolves the clot by converting plasminogen into plasmin; plasmin is what actually dissolves the clot.","Works best when given within 30 to 70 minutes of the onset of symptoms (when the chest pain started).","Drugs Given Alongside","In a STEMI where fibrinolytic therapy is anticipated, clopidogrel is also given at presentation, and heparin might be started.","Anyone who has had fibrinolytic therapy is given heparin and antiplatelet therapy (like clopidogrel) to help prevent new clots from forming. Alteplase dissolves the current clot; the others keep new ones from forming.","The main adverse effect is bleeding. We are dissolving a clot, so watch for bleeding.","The lecture gives two time windows: thrombolytics \"ideally\" within about 4 to 6 hours of chest pain starting, and alteplase working best within 30 to 70 minutes of symptom onset. Both are recorded here exactly as stated.","Nitroglycerin Safety","Sildenafil (Viagra) — Do not give nitroglycerin with it. Both cause vasodilation of the veins and can reduce preload, leading to really profound hypotension.","Other nitrates — Severe hypotension, especially when taken with other nitrates.","Common effects — Headache and flushing.","Route — Sublingual, or IV if the blood pressure tolerates it.","Reperfusion Injury","What the lecture says","When myocardial tissue that has been deprived of oxygen suddenly receives oxygenated blood, it can become stunned, and that can cause heart failure.","Why: white blood cells come to the infarcted, damaged tissue (that's their job), and they release oxidized free radicals that cause the reperfusion injury.","These patients need to be monitored closely.","Also monitor for reperfusion dysrhythmias, such as ventricular tachycardia (VTach) and ventricular fibrillation.","Key Takeaways","Acute MI by the numbers","Acute MI is a leading cause of death in the United States and throughout the world.","A big reason for that mortality is hesitating or delaying care (\"it might pass,\" \"it's not serious\").","With treatment, survival is great: about 90 to 95% for treated patients in the US. Teach patients: if you suspect an MI, seek treatment. Better safe than sorry.","About half of people with MI are younger than 65.","People older than 65 and people with diabetes can have an MI and not know it: a silent or asymptomatic MI, found later, with no signs or symptoms at the time.","Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","Topic 4: what an aneurysm is and why it forms, the types by location and shape, assessing a AAA, and the two big complications, rupture and aortic dissection.","What an Aneurysm Is","Definition and cause","A localized dilation or outpouching of a vessel wall.","A true aneurysm involves all three layers of the arterial wall. There are also false aneurysms (below).","Aneurysms are primarily a result of hypertension. Prolonged, uncontrolled high blood pressure puts constant pressure on the arterial wall: the vessel dilates, the wall weakens, a pouch or bulge forms, and eventually the pouch can burst.","Aneurysms cause turbulent blood flow, which makes them susceptible to rupture.","Most commonly seen in the aorta and the cerebral arteries, but they can occur elsewhere.","Listen for a bruit. Because of the turbulent flow, a stethoscope over an aneurysm may pick up a bruit: a whooshing-type sound.","Risk Factors","Think of anything that damages blood vessels. The primary drivers are atherosclerosis and hypertension.","Named in the lecture","Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Diseases of the blood vessels","Trauma","Tobacco use","Older adult age (over 65)","A hereditary component","Types of Aneurysms","By location","Aortic aneurysms, the most common type: abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA, \"triple A\"), thoracic aortic aneurysm, and dissecting aortic aneurysm.","Cerebral aneurysms: anywhere in the brain.","False or pseudoaneurysms.","By shape","Saccular","Berry (a subtype of saccular)","Fusiform","Saccular — Balloon-shaped. Involves only one side of the vessel wall, not the entire circumference. A wide neck at the base. — Seen on an angiogram (dye is injected and watched). — Not specified separately in the lecture.","Berry — A subtype of saccular: the same pouch or balloon look, but a small neck at the base. — Located at bifurcations. A very common location is the circle of Willis, which connects the anterior and posterior circulation at the base of the…","Fusiform — Involves the entire circumference of the vessel; no longer limited to one wall. Usually a gradual, progressive dilation of the arterial wall. Can have potentially extensive involvement. — Often related to diffuse sclerotic change…","False (pseudo) — A localized dissection or tear in the inner artery wall; the outer artery walls are still intact. Considered a type of hematoma. — Often a result of vascular interventional procedures, such as leakage between a vascular gra…","Berry Aneurysm Surgery","Drain — Takes the blood out of the aneurysm.","Clip — Places a clip around the base of the aneurysm.","Coil — Fills the aneurysm from the inside with a coil.","Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)","Auscultation — Listen with the stethoscope over the abdominal aorta. A bruit there suggests a AAA may be present.","Inspection — In particularly thin patients with low body fat, you may even see a pulsatile mass in the abdomen.","Light palpation — You could feel a mass that might indicate a AAA.","Suspected AAA: no deep palpation. Deep palpation is a risk for rupture, so keep it to light palpation.","The aorta is the largest vessel in the body and supplies a lot of really important places, so the clinical manifestations of an aortic aneurysm depend on where it is located. An aneurysm above the kidneys looks different from one below the …","Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection","Rupture","Especially in the aorta, where the pressure in the vessel is very high.","If a AAA ruptures, the patient will bleed out very, very quickly, with a lot of blood involved.","Aortic dissection (dissecting aneurysm)","A tear in the inner layer of the artery; blood enters between the layers of the artery wall.","The turbulent blood flow tears away at the layers, separating them.","A medical emergency. These patients can also rupture, and without quick intervention it can be fatal.","An aneurysm increases the risk for dissection: a weakened wall can keep weakening, eventually tear, and turn into a dissection.","Not tested: the slides showing the different types of dissections and aneurysms. The instructor said, \"you do not need to know these. So don't memorize them. They're just for you to look at.\"","Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection","Aneurysm","Can be completely asymptomatic.","Aortic dissection","Sudden, severe tearing pain: the most common manifestation.","Most likely felt in the chest, but it can also be in the neck, jaw, back or abdomen.","Clinical Manifestations of Aortic Dissection","Early stages — Blood pressure is elevated, as the body tries to compensate for the loss of blood.","Later stages — Blood pressure is \"in the trash\" and may be completely unattainable, because of significant bleeding. Blood between the walls of the artery isn't in the arteries getting where it needs to be.","Other signs — Syncope, total unresponsiveness, hemiplegia, paralysis of the lower extremities.","Without intervention — Progresses to total cardiovascular collapse, shock and death.","Managing Aortic Dissection","Aortic dissection is a surgical emergency; minutes matter. Emergent evaluation is needed, and surgical intervention is the priority and the most important goal.","Diagnosis — CT scan, or MRA (an MRI with angiogram).","Beta blockers — Decrease heart rate and blood pressure to prevent further tearing or further dissection of the artery.","Nitrates — May also be seen.","Surgery — The most important goal of treatment."]},{"s":"Week 2: Acid-Base Balance & ABGs▾","a":"week-2-acid-base-balance-abgs","k":["Week 2: Acid-Base Balance & ABGs▾","pH and buffers, compensation, the four acid-base disorders and ABG interpretation.","pH, Hydrogen & Acids","Topic 1, first voiceover (Acid base 1): what pH is, why hydrogen matters, normal serum pH and how pH varies in the body, what pH affects (including oxygen delivery), the survivable limits, and volatile vs non-volatile acids.","What pH Is","pH, acids and bases","pH is the potential, or the power, of a hydrogen ion. It is a scale that identifies whether a solution is more acidic or more alkaline, based on the hydrogen ions present.","The bottom of the scale is more acidic, the middle is neutral, and the higher the number, the more basic (alkaline).","pH is inversely related to hydrogen ion concentration: when hydrogen ions go up, pH goes down (more acidic); when hydrogen ions go down, pH goes up (more basic).","The scale is logarithmic, not linear: each number is 10 times more acidic or alkaline than the one before it. A pH of 5 is 10 times more acidic than a pH of 6; going from 7 to 6 is a tenfold increase in hydrogen ions.","An acid can donate (release) hydrogen ions. A base can accept hydrogen ions; bases have fewer hydrogen ions and give up a hydroxide ion (OH⁻).","A strong acid completely dissociates in solution (gives up all its hydrogen ions). A weak acid only partially dissociates.","Where Common Things Fall on the Scale","Acidic side (lower numbers)","Milk, coffee, tomato, lemon","Stomach acid (hydrochloric acid)","Battery acid","Neutral and alkaline side","Water: 7, neutral","Blood: on the alkaline side; the slide shows it at 8, but it is really a little lower than 8","Baking soda, stomach tablets (like Alka-Seltzer), soap","Drain cleaner: 14","Why Hydrogen Matters","What hydrogen does in the body","Helps maintain cell membranes.","Helps with enzymatic activity.","Is a component of water and keeps the body hydrated.","Helps with energy production.","Is a component of the sugars, proteins, starches and fats we consume.","The body works to stay in homeostasis and functions best within certain ranges, like a heart rate of 60 to 100 or sodium of 135 to 145. pH is one of those ranges.","Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body","Normal serum (blood) pH: 7.35 to 7.45. Most of the body's enzymes function within that very narrow range.","pH isn't the same everywhere. The stomach is the most acidic place in the body, with hydrochloric acid at a pH more like 1. The stomach is uniquely designed for it: mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate at the cell surface to protect t…","Serum (blood) — 7.35 to 7.45","Saliva — 6.5 to 7.5","Stomach — As low as 1.5 on the GI chart (about 1 in the narration), then pH increases as contents move through the GI tract. The GI tract's differences aid digestion.","Skeletal muscle — 6.9 to 7.2","Bone — 7.4","Liver — 7.2","Pleural fluid — 7.6","What pH Affects","Acid-base balance affects","Electrolytes: sodium, potassium in particular, and chloride.","Hormones.","Oxygen transport and delivery, which is greatly affected.","Acidic environment","Hemoglobin gives up oxygen more readily at the tissue.","Alkalotic environment","Hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and doesn't give it to the tissue, which can result in inadequate tissue oxygenation.","The slide shows this on the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve: the relationship between oxygen saturation of the blood and oxygen partial pressure, which shifts as the blood becomes acidotic or alkalotic.","Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits","Below 7.35 — Acidosis.","7.35 to 7.45 — Normal.","Above 7.45 — Alkalosis.","Below 6.8 or above 7.8 — Incompatible with life, because of pH's profound effects on cellular metabolism, enzyme activity and oxygen delivery.","The body can function outside the normal range for a short time, but because pH affects so much (neurologic, respiratory, GI and cardiovascular function), the goal is to get back to normal as quickly as possible.","Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids","Volatile acids","Can be eliminated as carbon dioxide (CO2) gas, by the lungs (pulmonary ventilation).","The only volatile acid in the body is carbonic acid (H2CO3).","Carbonic acid is a weak acid: it does not easily release its hydrogen ion.","In the presence of the enzyme carbonic anhydrase, it readily dissociates into CO2 and water.","Non-volatile acids","Can be eliminated only by the kidneys.","Most are strong acids: they readily release their hydrogen ions.","Examples: lactic, phosphoric, sulfuric, acetoacetic and beta-hydroxybutyric acid (table below).","Lactic acid — A byproduct of anaerobic metabolism (metabolism without oxygen). A little different: it is metabolized by the liver and kidneys, and once oxygen is reestablished to the tissue it can be reconverted and used for energy again.","Phosphoric acid — Made from phosphorus; works with calcium to help with strong bones, and supports kidney function.","Sulfuric acid — Results from protein metabolism.","Acetoacetic acid — Produced by the liver.","Beta-hydroxybutyric acid — Increases with exercise, calorie restriction and fasting. Its measurement is used as a diagnostic for people in DKA (ketoacidosis).","Why the Body Leans Acidic","At baseline, the body produces more acids than bases: from the food we eat and the drinks we consume, from the metabolism of lipids and proteins, and from cellular metabolism, whose waste product is CO2.","We want to avoid alkalosis, especially if a person is ill, because hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and tissue oxygenation suffers. An alkaline pH is also really difficult to correct.","How the Body Defends Its pH","Topic 1, second voiceover (Acid base 2): the three lines of defense, the buffer systems, the carbonic acid equation, cellular compensation and potassium, respiratory and renal control, and compensation.","The Three Lines of Defense","1. Chemical buffer systems — Almost instantaneously (seconds) — Primarily intracellular. Chemicals combine with acid or base to resist a change in pH. Makes only small changes; a relatively short, not sustainable fix.","2. Respiratory system — Several minutes to hours — Changes the rate or depth of breathing to blow off or retain CO2. The lungs are the primary controller of the carbonic acid supply.","3. Renal (kidney) control — Several hours to days — The long-term regulator. Excretes hydrogen ions and retains or excretes bicarb. The kidneys are the primary controller of bicarb (the base).","The lungs and kidneys work together. The lungs respond relatively quickly; the kidneys need more time, but their mechanisms are more long-term.","Buffer Systems","Buffers are chemicals that combine with an acid or base to change the pH and bring it back to normal. They can release or accept a hydrogen ion, and they work almost instantaneously, but they make small, short-term corrections only.","Bicarbonate–carbonic acid — The main buffer outside the cells (extracellular fluid) — CO2 from cell metabolism combines with water to form carbonic acid, which carbonic anhydrase breaks down in the lungs and kidneys (see the equation below)…","Phosphate — The main intracellular buffer — Picks up a hydrogen ion inside the cell to balance the pH inside the cell. It can't be measured. You don't need to memorize its equation.","Protein — Primarily intracellular (most proteins are in cells) — Most proteins can buffer. Carboxyl groups act as weak acids and release hydrogen ions; amino groups act as weak bases and accept them. When pH increases, proteins release hydr…","Hemoglobin — A protein buffer in the blood (some call it a fourth buffer system) — As pH increases, hemoglobin loses hydrogen ions, and the reverse when pH decreases. It also binds CO2 to form carbaminohemoglobin, which carries CO2 to the l…","The Carbonic Acid Equation","CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 (carbonic acid) ⇌ H⁺ + HCO3⁻ (bicarb). The instructor: \"You really want to be familiar and comfortable with the equation and know this backwards and forwards.\"","Lungs — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into CO2 and water, and the lungs exhale the CO2.","Kidneys — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into hydrogen ions and bicarb, and the kidneys excrete the hydrogen ions (and can generate bicarb).","The equation goes both directions: carbonic acid can break down into CO2 and water or form from them, and the same on the bicarb and hydrogen side.","A patient retaining CO2, like a COPD patient, pushes the equation in the other direction and increases bicarb.","CO2 also rises with respiratory depression, such as narcotic use or an overdose: the respiratory rate is so low the lungs can't remove the CO2, which changes the pH.","When a patient is acidotic and not compensating through the respiratory system, sodium bicarb is often given as a quick fix to raise the pH. This happens a lot in the ICU.","Cellular Compensation & Potassium","Cells like a neutral electrical balance. Hydrogen and potassium are both positively charged, and potassium is found primarily inside the cell.","In acidosis — Hydrogen ions move into the cell to balance the pH, and potassium moves out of the cell, which can raise blood potassium.","With kidney dysfunction — The kidneys can't get rid of the excess potassium: hyperkalemia, which can cause dysrhythmias.","When pH returns to normal — Hydrogen ions move back into the blood and potassium moves back into the cells.","With working kidneys — The kidneys will already have excreted the excess potassium, so this can lead to hypokalemia (to be revisited with DKA later in the course).","Cellular compensation is a temporary correction of the pH, not a permanent solution.","Respiratory Control of pH","The body produces CO2 continuously, which forms carbonic acid; in the lungs it breaks back down into water and CO2, and the excess CO2 is exhaled.","This works only on volatile acids, not on non-volatile acids like lactic acid.","The lungs adjust serum pH by changing the rate or depth of breathing. The brain and arterial receptors are stimulated by the pH change.","On mechanical ventilation in the ICU, these adjustments can be made through the ventilator settings.","pH too low (acidosis)","Breathing rate or depth increases → more CO2 blown off → less carbonic acid → pH rises.","pH too high (alkalosis)","Breathing rate or depth decreases → CO2 retained → more carbonic acid → pH falls.","Renal Control of pH","The kidneys are the last to alter pH and are the long-term regulators. They can eliminate large amounts of acid by getting rid of hydrogen ions, using an active transport mechanism.","They can also excrete bicarb (a base) if the pH is too high, or retain bicarb if the pH is too low.","Example: in a COPD patient retaining CO2, over time the kidneys compensate by holding on to more bicarb, so this patient has higher CO2 and higher bicarb to balance the serum pH.","Kidney function is extremely important: in renal failure, patients can no longer maintain a normal pH and often take bicarb daily.","Acidosis — Increased — Increased — Raises pH back to normal","Alkalosis — Decreased — Decreased — Lowers pH back to normal","The kidneys handle acidosis better than alkalosis. It is harder for the kidneys to excrete bicarb than to hold on to it.","Compensation","If one side of the equation is the problem, the body pulls toward the other side to rebalance. That movement is compensation: the body's response to an acid-base imbalance, aimed at bringing the pH back to normal. It is evaluated with arter…","Underlying problem is metabolic","The respiratory system compensates.","Underlying problem is respiratory","The renal system compensates, bringing about metabolic changes (metabolic compensation).","Uncompensated — Nothing has changed in response to a pH outside normal limits.","Partial compensation — Bicarb and CO2 levels have changed, but the pH is still outside normal limits.","Complete compensation — Bicarb or CO2 levels are abnormal, but the pH is back within normal limits.","What an ABG Measures","The lecture ends by previewing the ABG: pH, CO2 (PaCO2), PO2, HCO3 (bicarb) and O2 saturation. \"You do need to know these numbers.\" They are taught in the next lecture (Part 2A).","ABGs & the Four Acid-Base Disorders","Topic 2, first voiceover (ABGs): why ABGs are drawn, the normal values, the key terms, ROME, and each of the four disorders with its ABG pattern, causes, signs and treatment.","Why We Draw ABGs","Purpose of an arterial blood gas","To assess acid-base status, determine the adequacy of oxygenation and ventilation, and decide which treatment would be best.","Arterial blood gives the best representation of the pH and PaCO2 in the body.","A venous blood gas (VBG) can be drawn, but the measurements differ: the CO2 is a bit higher (it's venous blood), which changes the pH measurement. If an arterial draw isn't possible, a VBG can gauge where things stand, but for critically il…","Normal ABG Values","\"You do need to know these numbers.\" pH 7.35–7.45 · PaCO2 35–45 · HCO3 24–29.","pH — 7.35 to 7.45 — The balance of hydrogen ions.","PaCO2 — 35 to 45 — The respiratory parameter: the partial pressure of CO2 in the artery (the carbonic acid that dissolves into CO2 and water).","HCO3 (bicarb) — 24 to 29 — The metabolic parameter. It is calculated on the ABG, not directly measured.","Serum CO2 (BMP) — 24 to 29 — On a regular venous chemistry panel (BMP), the CO2 level is equivalent to the bicarb.","The ABG also reports PO2 and O2 saturation, and the slide compares ABG and VBG values (a VBG reports PvCO2, the partial pressure of CO2 in the vein, with a slightly different pH). The transcript doesn't read those numbers aloud, so they are…","Key Terms","Primary event — The problem that initiates the acid-base imbalance: the root cause. — Hypoventilation, hyperventilation, vomiting, diarrhea.","Primary disorder — What happens as a result of the primary event. — Hypoventilation retains CO2 → respiratory acidosis. Vomiting up stomach acid → metabolic alkalosis.","Compensatory mechanisms — The processes the body uses to bring acid-base balance back, without correcting the underlying cause. — A lung problem → the kidneys compensate (and can keep it up longer, if they function properly). A kidney probl…","Metabolic cause — The bicarb level changes because of metabolic alterations (think kidneys). — Metabolic acidosis and metabolic alkalosis.","Respiratory cause — The carbonic acid (CO2) level changes because of alterations in the lungs. — Respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis.","ROME & the Four Primary Disorders","ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Respiratory: pH and CO2 move in opposite directions (pH up, CO2 down = alkalosis; pH down, CO2 up = acidosis). Metabolic: pH and bicarb move in the same direction (both up = alkalosis; both down …","Metabolic acidosis — Bicarb ↓ — ↓ — CO2 ↓","Metabolic alkalosis — Bicarb ↑ — ↑ — CO2 ↑","Respiratory acidosis — CO2 ↑ — ↓ — Bicarb ↑","Respiratory alkalosis — CO2 ↓ — ↑ — Bicarb ↓","Notice the compensating value always moves in the same direction as the primary change.","Metabolic Alkalosis","Too much bicarb, or not enough carbonic acid (acid).","ABG — pH high; bicarb high (greater than 29); CO2 typically normal if uncompensated, and high once the lungs compensate.","Causes — Excessive baking soda or antacids (Alka-Seltzer): too much base. The lecture links this to hypokalemia, with hydrogen shifting out of the cell and potassium going in. Prolonged vomiting (losing hydrochloric acid, so hydrogen ions).…","Manifestations — CNS over-excitability, tremors, muscle cramps, confusion up to coma, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and respiratory depression (the body holding on to CO2 to counteract the alkalosis).","Cardiac risk — With a high pH, hydrogen ions come out of the cell to buffer, and potassium goes into the cell, so less potassium circulates: hypokalemia, with its classic cardiac issues.","Compensation — The lungs decrease the rate and depth of breathing to hold on to CO2.","Treatment — Treat the cause, such as the vomiting. Drugs that reduce gastric hydrochloric acid secretion: H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors.","Respiratory Alkalosis","The lecture introduces it as a \"bicarb deficit in our extracellular fluid,\" most often caused by hyperventilation (the primary event), with too much CO2 blown off.","ABG — pH high; CO2 low (blowing off CO2); bicarb relatively normal, because the kidneys are not yet compensating.","Why the kidneys can't help acutely — The kidneys take hours to days. A person hyperventilating in a panic attack often passes out; their breathing then regulates and CO2 builds back up.","Causes — Hyperventilation (anxiety, panic attack); increased metabolic demand (fever, infection, sepsis); some medications that increase breathing rate; head injuries, brain lesions or chronic brain injury; pulmonary diseases; ventilator se…","Manifestations — Similar to metabolic alkalosis: CNS over-excitability, light-headedness, syncopal episodes, up to coma.","Treatment — Help retain CO2 and decrease respirations: anti-anxiety medications, calming the patient, breathing into a paper bag (inhaling CO2). On a ventilator, change the settings.","Respiratory Acidosis","Can be acute or chronic. Excess CO2 means too much carbonic acid, so too high a hydrogen ion concentration. Primarily caused by hypoventilation: CO2 rises and pH drops.","Acute (e.g., respiratory arrest)","pH low.","CO2 high.","Bicarb pretty normal: the kidneys haven't had time to compensate.","Chronic (e.g., COPD retaining CO2)","The kidneys have had time to compensate.","Bicarb high.","pH closer to normal, or normal when fully compensated.","The COPD Example: Acute on Chronic","This client's baseline (chronic) — 60 — 32 — Normal (chronic, compensated)","Now with an infection (breathing worse) — 75 — Still 32: the kidneys haven't had a chance to compensate — Drops: more acidic","Causes — New-onset CNS depression and hypoventilation: overdose, found down, too much narcotic or sedative given or taken; respiratory arrest; head injury; anesthesia and post-anesthesia (the body can take longer to metabolize the drugs); p…","Manifestations — Difficulty breathing or respiratory distress; slow, shallow breathing; dysrhythmias and ECG changes; decreased level of consciousness, eventually coma. The patient feels like they can't catch their breath.","Interventions — Improve ventilation (increase the rate and depth of breathing to blow off CO2): stimulate the patient, encourage slow and deep breathing; on a ventilator, increase the respiratory rate or the tidal volume.","Metabolic Acidosis","Too much acid or not enough bicarb: a bicarb deficit in the blood.","ABG — pH low (below 7.35); bicarb low (same direction as pH). CO2 somewhat normal early, before the lungs compensate; once they do, the lungs breathe faster to blow off CO2, lowering it.","Causes — Renal disease or failure (can't excrete hydrogen ions; dialysis patients take daily bicarb); fistulas (bicarb lost with fluid and electrolytes); diabetes, especially type 1, with DKA; lactic acidosis (cells without enough oxygen); …","Manifestations — Lethargy, confusion, headache; tremors, muscle cramps, muscle twitching; paresthesias (tingling of fingers, toes, perioral); hypotension; hyperkalemia (hydrogen ions pulled into the cell, potassium out); warm, flushed skin;…","Compensation — Compensatory hyperventilation: deep breaths called Kussmaul respirations, common in DKA, as the lungs try to blow off CO2 and reduce the acidosis.","Treatment (DKA) — DKA's high ketones are acids. Treat by bringing the blood glucose down with insulin to decrease the acid in the body (more later in the course).","One line in the recording says that if the lungs are compensating, the CO2 \"is going to be increased.\" The rest of the lecture (the arrow table, Kussmaul breathing to blow off CO2, and the worked example of 7.35 / 33 / 20) shows the CO2 goi…","ABG Interpretation","Topic 2, second voiceover (ABG Interpretation): how compensation works, the four levels of compensation, the step-by-step method, every worked example, and the two shortcut methods.","How Compensation Works","Respiratory compensation (lungs)","Triggered by a metabolic problem.","Rapid: within a few minutes to a few hours.","Hyperventilation (blow off CO2) or hypoventilation (conserve CO2), depending on whether it's correcting an acidosis or an alkalosis.","Metabolic compensation (kidneys)","Triggered by a respiratory problem.","Slow: hours to days.","Retains or excretes hydrogen ions and bicarb as needed.","The Four Levels of Compensation","Uncompensated — Abnormal — Only one (the acid or the base component) is abnormal, not both.","Partially compensated — Still abnormal — Both are abnormal: the body has started to respond, moving the second component to pull the pH back.","Compensated — Within normal limits — Still abnormal, but balanced. The imbalance is neutralized, which is not the same as corrected.","Corrected — Within normal limits — Also within normal limits, after whatever caused the imbalance has been corrected.","Not everyone returns to \"corrected.\" A COPD patient holds on to CO2, and their bicarb stays high to balance it; because COPD is chronic, you will never see a fully corrected ABG.","Interpreting an ABG Step by Step","1. pH — Acidic, basic or normal? If the pH is normal but the CO2 and bicarb are not, compensation has occurred. Then decide which way the normal pH leans: treat 7.40 as neutral; 7.35 to 7.40 leans acidic, above 7.40 to 7.45 leans basic (e.g…","2. PaCO2 (35 to 45) — Acidic (high), basic (low) or normal? If it is abnormal and matches the pH, it's a respiratory problem: an inverse relationship with pH (Respiratory Opposite).","3. HCO3 (24 to 29) — A calculated value. Basic (high), acidic (low) or normal? If it matches the pH, it's a metabolic problem: a direct relationship with pH (Metabolic Equal).","4. Compensation — Is the body trying to restore the pH by altering the system that is not involved in the imbalance? If compensation has occurred, that value moves in the same direction as the other component. Use the four levels above.","With compensation, the pH points the way. When the pH is normal, the side it leans toward (acidic or basic) tells you which value is the primary problem and which is compensating.","Worked Examples From the Lecture","Cover the interpretation column with the eye button and work each one yourself. Values are pH / PaCO2 / HCO3.","7.25 / 56 / 24 — Respiratory acidosis (no compensation) — pH down, CO2 up (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.35 / 56 / 35 — Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the high CO2; the high bicarb is compensating.","7.60 / 25 / 24 — Respiratory alkalosis (no compensation) — pH up, CO2 down (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.45 / 30 / 20 — Fully compensated respiratory alkalosis — pH normal but leaning basic, matching the low CO2; the low bicarb is compensating.","7.26 / 40 / 18 — Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — pH down, bicarb down (equal); CO2 normal.","7.35 / 33 / 20 — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the low bicarb; the low CO2 is compensating.","7.46 / 37 / 26 — Metabolic alkalosis (as given in the lecture) — pH basic, CO2 normal; the lecture calls the bicarb of 26 \"on the higher end.\"","Normal pH leaning basic / high CO2 / 30 — Compensated metabolic alkalosis — Leaning basic matches the high bicarb; the high CO2 is compensating.","7.30 / 52 / 30 — Respiratory acidosis, partially compensated — pH and CO2 both acidic; the kidneys are retaining bicarb, but the pH is still abnormal.","7.60 / 31 / 26 — Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Tic-tac-toe: pH and CO2 line up in the basic column; bicarb is normal.","Below 7.35 / 29 / 21 — Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — pH and bicarb both acidic; the CO2 is abnormal (basic), but the pH is not yet normal.","7.52 / 48 / 39 — Metabolic alkalosis, partially compensated — pH and bicarb both basic; CO2 abnormal (acidic); pH still abnormal.","The lecture ends with a slide of extra practice ABGs to work through on your own; their values aren't read aloud in the transcript.","Two Shortcut Methods","\"It's important to know this information and to rationalize all these steps.\" These are extra ways to get there, if they fit how you think.","ABG tic-tac-toe","Draw a board with three columns: Acidic, Normal, Basic.","Place the pH, CO2 and bicarb in the column each one falls in.","The value that lines up with the pH names the problem (CO2 = respiratory, bicarb = metabolic), and the pH's column names acidosis or alkalosis.","If the third value is in the normal column, it's uncompensated.","The instructor's three lines","Draw a line each for pH, CO2 and bicarb, always in that order.","pH: low end Acidic → high end Basic.","CO2: opposite, low is Basic → high is Acidic.","Bicarb: low is Acidic → high is Basic.","Memorize \"AB, BA, AB,\" mark where each value falls, and see what matches the pH."]},{"s":"Week 3: Neuro I▾","a":"week-3-neuro-i","k":["Week 3: Neuro I▾","Migraines, Parkinson's disease and its drugs, myasthenia gravis and multiple sclerosis.","Migraines","Topic 1: the definition and diagnostic criteria, types, risk factors and triggers, pathophysiology, the four phases, abortive therapy (analgesics, triptans, CGRP antagonists, antiemetics) and preventive therapy.","What a Migraine Is","Migraines are the most common complaint neurologists see day to day. A migraine is an episodic neurologic disorder characterized by a headache that lasts anywhere from 4 to 72 hours.","At least TWO of these","Unilateral head pain (characteristic of migraine)","Throbbing pain","Headache that worsens with activity","Pain rated moderate to severe","Plus at least ONE of these","Nausea and vomiting","Photophobia: sensitivity to (avoidance of) light, such as sunglasses, dark rooms, pulled curtains","Phonophobia: avoidance of sound; everything feels loud and grating","Types of Migraine","With aura — An aura is present (about a third of people with migraines).","Without aura — No aura. The most common kind of migraine.","Chronic — Migraines on about 15 days a month over 3 months. They usually start out episodic, and their frequency increases over time until they reach that threshold.","Risk Factors & Triggers","Risk factors","Family history.","Estrogen and progesterone: females are more susceptible. Migraines can occur more right before and during menstruation, and often decrease during pregnancy and menopause. The theory is that cyclical withdrawal of these hormones over the men…","Other genetic and environmental factors.","Common triggers","Fatigue; sleep disturbances, meaning too little or too much sleep (e.g., 15 hours when that isn't normal)","Missing a meal; overexertion","Weather changes (rainy, cold weather)","Stress; hormonal changes","Alcohol (red wine is a common one)","Bright lights or strong smells (stores with strong odors)","Pathophysiology","Not well understood; there is no well-established, identifiable pathology. Migraines are associated with changes in brain metabolism and blood flow, and current theories involve changes in neurotransmitter levels and blood vessel tone.","1 — Increased neuronal depolarization results in hyperactivity.","2 — Excitatory neurotransmitters are released in the brain.","3 — They activate the vascular system, stimulating vasodilation, which results in the migraine.","The Four Phases of a Migraine","1. Premonitory — Hours or days before the headache; about a third of people with migraines — Fatigue, irritability, loss of concentration, stiff neck, food cravings.","2. Aura — Only in people with aura (about a third); lasts up to about an hour — Visual: shimmering lights, flashing spots, blind spots. Sensory: numbness, tingling, pins and needles. Motor: weakness, difficulty with speech, coordination pro…","3. Headache — 4 to 72 hours — Throbbing pain, typically starting on one side and spreading to the whole head; fatigue, nausea, vomiting, dizziness, hypersensitivity to touch on the head.","4. Recovery — A few hours up to a few days to fully resolve — Irritability, fatigue, depression.","Abortive (Symptomatic) Therapy","Abortive therapy treats the symptoms, and it is more effective when given early in the course of the headache.","Mild to moderate, no severe nausea or vomiting — Basic analgesics: aspirin, acetaminophen, NSAIDs, or a combination. Tried first because they can be really effective, are readily available and are inexpensive. If they don't work alone, a tr…","Moderate to severe, no nausea or vomiting — An oral migraine-specific agent: the triptans, often combined (e.g., sumatriptan with naproxen), since the combination is more effective.","Can't take or tolerate triptans — A CGRP antagonist (calcitonin gene-related peptide antagonist).","Severe nausea and vomiting (any severity of migraine) — Antiemetics are added, such as ondansetron or promethazine (Phenergan).","Triptans (Serotonin 1B/1D Receptor Agonists)","How they work — Promote vasoconstriction of the intracranial blood vessels (vasodilation has a role in causing migraine). They also suppress inflammatory neuropeptides in the brain and block the pain pathway.","Routes — Subcutaneous, PO or intranasal. Nasal spray has fewer side effects than subQ. SubQ works most quickly. PO has side effects similar to subQ, but they occur less often.","Side effects — Injection site reactions (subQ), chest pressure, flushing, weakness, headache; a bad taste with the nasal form. These make sense for a drug that constricts blood vessels.","Avoid in — Ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina.","No triptans for clients with ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina. Triptans work by constricting blood vessels.","CGRP Antagonist: Rimegepant (Nurtec)","Used when triptans aren't effective or are contraindicated: CAD, heart disease, angina, ischemic stroke.","Given PO.","Side effects: GI upset (abdominal pain, nausea, dyspepsia).","A CYP substrate: use caution with any inhibitors or inducers of that system.","Preventive Therapy","When it's used","More than 4 migraines in a month, or","Migraines that last longer than 12 hours.","Drugs named","Beta blockers: propranolol","Tricyclic antidepressants: amitriptyline","Anti-epileptic drugs","Estrogen","Goals of preventive therapy","Reduce migraine frequency, severity and duration.","Improve the response to treatment of acute attacks.","Improve function and reduce disability.","Prevent episodic migraines from turning into chronic migraines.","Parkinson's Disease","Topic 2, first voiceover: what PD is, primary vs secondary parkinsonism, risk factors, the dopamine–acetylcholine imbalance, the clinical manifestations and the classic triad, and complications.","What Parkinson's Disease Is","Parkinson's disease (PD)","A complex motor disorder with other systemic non-motor and neurologic symptoms.","Progressive and degenerative: it keeps getting worse, and some symptoms worsen faster than others.","Characterized by tremors, rigidity and bradykinesia. The tremor is notably more present when the muscles are at rest.","A disorder of the basal ganglia, which help produce smooth, coordinated movements (the lecture places them in the cerebellum). The concern is the substantia nigra inside the basal ganglia, which contains the cells that produce dopamine.","Primary vs Secondary Parkinsonism","Parkinsonism is the umbrella term. Parkinson's disease is the leading cause of parkinsonism.","Primary parkinsonism","The etiology is unknown (idiopathic); this is Parkinson's disease.","Also includes genetic components and sporadic onset.","Defining feature: no defining event led to the onset of symptoms.","There is damage to the dopamine-producing cells.","Secondary (acquired) parkinsonism","Results from something else: an event that caused the onset.","Infection, head trauma, drug intoxication.","Some antiemetics and some antidepressants are the most common cause. Symptoms usually go away after the medication is stopped.","Any drug that interferes with dopamine transmission can cause it.","Who Gets Parkinson's Disease","Age — Often begins after about 50; incidence rapidly increases after about 60 and peaks around 70. Typically diagnosed in the elderly, but it can happen young (Michael J. Fox, diagnosed in his early 30s).","Genetics — Familial or genetic forms account for about 10% of primary parkinsonism, with both dominant and recessive patterns.","Sex — Seen more often in males.","Environment — Pesticides, air pollution, farm and agricultural work.","Depression — Can make PD develop earlier, because of its association with dopamine changes.","Head trauma — Makes PD more likely (Muhammad Ali: repeated head trauma from boxing, diagnosed in his late 30s).","Decreased estrogen — A hysterectomy, or anything that decreases estrogen, is a risk factor.","Coffee — A protective factor.","Dopamine, Acetylcholine & the Imbalance","Dopamine","Has both inhibitory and excitatory effects in the brain, depending on the receptor it binds.","In PD, it's the inhibitory effects that are lacking.","Helps control movement and balance by inhibiting unnecessary movements, which improves coordination, balance and muscle control.","Acetylcholine (ACh)","An excitatory neurotransmitter: stimulates muscle movement and can cause uncoordinated movement.","Essential for all muscle function, and works with dopamine. It's all about the balance.","1 — The substantia nigra in the basal ganglia is destroyed.","2 — Dopamine levels decrease, because the dopamine-producing cells are in the substantia nigra.","3 — An imbalance develops: a relative excess of acetylcholine.","4 — Extra muscle movements that dopamine would normally inhibit are no longer inhibited: loss of controlled movement and balance.","Clinical Manifestations","They may develop alone or in combination, and diagnosis often takes a while because onset is insidious. At first it may involve only one side of the body; it typically becomes bilateral as the disease progresses. Later, all of them appear.","Common signs","Bradykinesia: slowness of movement.","Cogwheel rigidity: stiff muscles; movements like bending the elbow aren't smooth.","Resting tremor.","Shuffling (Parkinsonian) gait: they don't lift their feet.","Mask-like expression: loss of small facial movements, a flat affect.","Postural instability.","The classic triad, the hallmark of PD: Tremor, Rigidity, Bradykinesia.","Tremor","Often the first sign. It is a rest tremor: most noticeable when the body part is supported against gravity but not doing purposeful activity, and it decreases with movement.","An early sign is the pill-rolling tremor: the thumb looks like it's rolling a small object against the finger. In the dominant hand, handwriting may suffer.","Worse with stress or concentration, such as trying to control the movement.","Cause — Faulty neurological impulses — Dopamine deficiency and the ACh imbalance","When — Occurs with motor function; fine motor movements are really difficult — Occurs at rest; tends to improve with movement","Other signs — No other manifestations of Parkinson's — Comes with the other manifestations of Parkinson's","Rigidity","Resistance to passive movement: when the provider flexes the client's arm at the elbow, there's unintentional resistance.","Cogwheel rigidity: slow, jerky movements like a cogwheel.","Cause: excess acetylcholine stimulates the muscles continuously, so they never get to relax (sustained contraction). Clients report muscle soreness, aches and pains.","Often unilateral at first, becoming bilateral over time.","Bradykinesia","Loss of automatic movement, or slowness of movement. Arguably the major cause of disability in Parkinson's: a lack of the spontaneous movements we take for granted.","Blinking — Dry eye, more susceptible to abrasion.","Arm swing while walking — Reduced or absent, adding to balance problems.","Swallowing saliva — Drooling.","Facial micro-expressions — Flat affect, mask-like expression.","Postural Instability & Gait","Postural instability: a decreased ability to prevent falling while moving, from impaired postural reflexes.","Gait disturbances: short steps, or shuffling without picking up the feet.","Complications","Dementia — About 40% develop it. Lewy bodies spread from movement areas of the brain into memory and cognition areas. Can also cause hallucinations.","Fatigue — From sleep disturbances: the tremor picks up while lying at rest trying to fall asleep.","Depression — A risk factor and also a complication; they may become dependent on others for the first time.","Anxiety — The second most frequent psychiatric disturbance in PD, after depression.","Decreased mobility — Malnutrition (trouble preparing meals or feeding themselves); aspiration and pneumonia from swallowing problems; UTIs from urinary retention; skin breakdown from sitting more.","Drug-related — The drugs are very effective but come with complications (below).","Parkinson's Pharmacology","Topic 2, second voiceover: the goals of treatment, Sinemet and its loss of effect, adverse effects and interactions, Duopa, the dopamine agonists, rotigotine and apomorphine.","Goals & Strategy of Drug Therapy","Goal: help clients keep motor control as long as possible, decrease symptoms, and maintain as much independence as possible. Because PD is progressive and degenerative, treatment is staving it off as much as possible.","When to start: once symptoms affect the dominant hand or interfere with work, ADLs, or social or leisure activities.","Strategy: correct the imbalance, either by enhancing dopamine (dopaminergic drugs) or by blocking acetylcholine (anticholinergic drugs).","Sinemet (Levodopa-Carbidopa)","The gold standard of treatment for Parkinson's disease.","Levodopa","Once it reaches the brain, it is converted to dopamine and activates the dopamine receptors, increasing dopamine activity.","Carbidopa","Prevents levodopa from being broken down before it reaches the brain, so more dopamine is sustained for longer.","One of the most effective PD drugs, but it takes several months for symptoms to improve while dopamine builds up. Teach clients it can take up to months.","It does not work long-term: as PD progresses, fewer dopaminergic terminals survive to take up levodopa and convert it to dopamine, so the effect is lost.","Loss of Drug Effect","Abrupt loss (\"on-off\" phenomenon) — Like flipping a switch: the drug is working, then suddenly it isn't and the symptoms are back. Impossible to predict, can happen at any point in the dosing interval, and over time the \"off\" episodes get m…","Gradual loss (\"wearing off\") — Each dose wears off sooner and sooner, even as the dose is increased; symptoms gradually return. Differs between individuals. — Shorter dosing intervals (taking it more often), or changes in the dose amount.","Adverse Effects (Mostly From the Levodopa)","Nausea and vomiting — Take with a small amount of food. Eating does reduce absorption, so it's a balance, but it keeps clients taking the drug. Avoid high-protein food.","Dyskinesias — From annoying up to disabling, sometimes so disabling the client needs a different drug.","Postural (orthostatic) hypotension — Teach clients to change positions slowly.","Dysrhythmias — Monitor cardiac function, especially when the dose has been increased over time.","Psychosis — In 20%: hallucinations, nightmares, even paranoia.","Darkened sweat and urine — Not harmful, but annoying; it can stain clothing.","Malignant melanoma — Sinemet can activate malignant melanoma: do a good skin assessment before starting, to identify suspicious lesions.","Decrease levodopa's effect","Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine)","Antipsychotics","Protein","Increase levodopa's effect (to toxicity)","Anticholinergics","MAOIs","Duopa (Levodopa-Carbidopa Infusion)","What it is — Another levodopa-carbidopa combination, as a gel infused into the small intestine through a feeding tube or J-tube. Continuous infusion for up to 16 hours a day; it can be stopped while sleeping at night.","Why — Keeps a consistent, continuous blood level. For clients who don't respond to the PO combination, or whose PO dose doesn't last long enough, or who have more or longer \"off\" periods; it helps minimize on-off episodes.","MAOIs — Do not take within 2 weeks of an MAOI for depression: it can cause levodopa toxicity.","Interactions — Antihypertensives, MAOIs, antipsychotics, metoclopramide, isoniazid, iron, vitamins, high protein. Do a good med rec.","Side effects — Falling asleep without warning (like narcolepsy), so not for clients who drive a truck or pilot an aircraft for a living; orthostatic hypotension; hallucinations (visual, auditory, tactile); unusual urges; depression; dyskine…","Tube risks — Placement is a surgery: infection, tube dislodgement, and the other risks of tube placement.","Dopamine Agonists & Other Drugs","Pramipexole (Mirapex) — A dopamine receptor agonist: it binds and stimulates dopamine (D2) receptors. Usually monotherapy in younger clients early in PD; combined with levodopa-carbidopa as the client ages or the disease advances (levodopa …","Ropinirole (Requip) — A dopamine receptor agonist for idiopathic PD. Exactly how it works isn't known; animal studies show increased nerve impulses in the substantia nigra. — Similar to other Parkinson's drugs; with long-term use, an increa…","Rotigotine (Neupro) — A once-daily patch, brought back into the U.S. in 2012. — Not described in the lecture.","Apomorphine (Apokyn) — A short-acting subQ injection for fast relief: useful during the \"off\" phase of the on-off phenomenon to regain control of symptoms. — Not described in the lecture.","Myasthenia Gravis","Topic 3: what MG is, the neuromuscular junction attack, clinical manifestations, myasthenic crisis, immunosuppressants and cholinesterase inhibitors, cholinergic crisis, and how to tell the two crises apart.","What Myasthenia Gravis Is","Myasthenia gravis (MG)","An acquired autoimmune disease characterized by fluctuating weakness of certain muscle groups.","Commonly affected: ocular muscles (the eyes), bulbar muscles (brainstem region), and the limb and respiratory muscle groups.","The weakness comes from an antibody-mediated attack on the postsynaptic membrane of the neuromuscular junction: a breakdown in communication between nerve cells and muscle cells.","How It Progresses","Variable from person to person. It often appears first as a mild, insidious case that's easy to push off (\"just a little muscle weakness\").","It can spontaneously go into remission (back to baseline), stabilize (plateau), or cycle through relapses, stable periods and symptom-free periods lasting weeks to months.","It can also progress severely and rapidly.","Because the course is hard to predict, being honest up front with clients about that is key.","Who Gets It","Rare; more common in females and in people aged 10 to 65 (a broad range).","Why the antibodies attack isn't known; there seems to be a genetic component and possible environmental triggers.","Manifestations often first appear during pregnancy or postpartum, or after exposure to certain types of anesthesia.","What Happens at the Neuromuscular Junction","1 — Antibodies form against the nicotinic acetylcholine postsynaptic receptors at the neuromuscular junction of skeletal muscle; the receptors are no longer recognized as self.","2 — Anti-acetylcholine antibodies attach to the receptor sites and block them from binding acetylcholine.","3 — Eventually the antibodies destroy the receptor sites.","4 — The body makes enough acetylcholine, but there aren't enough sites for it to bind, so nerve impulse transmission to the muscle is diminished.","Acetylcholine is an excitatory neurotransmitter responsible for muscle contraction (the lecture says smooth muscle). It also has a role in vasodilation, increasing body secretions, and slowing heart rate, which matters for the drugs below.","Pattern — Insidious onset with fluctuating skeletal muscle weakness; clients can feel weakness starting, rest, and the strength comes back. Symptoms can be vague.","Eye, face, mouth, throat, neck — Often affected first: drooling, difficulty chewing and swallowing, which affects nutrition and raises aspiration risk.","Respiratory muscles — Weak diaphragm and intercostal muscles can impair the ability to breathe or ventilate appropriately.","Shoulders and hips — Not typically affected, but muscle fatigue after exercise can occur there.","The video (Carrie) — Slow speech, slow blinking, a tracheostomy to help clear secretions, a possibly weak cough, difficulty swallowing.","Myasthenic Crisis","An acute exacerbation of muscle weakness","Can develop as MG progresses, typically triggered by a stressor: infection, surgery, emotional distress, pregnancy, a menstrual cycle, or inadequate pharmacotherapy or other drugs (not on enough medication, or not taking it appropriately).","In the second video, a simple cold sent Carrie into crisis: difficulty expanding her lungs, difficulty breathing, needing oxygen.","Myasthenic crisis: watch the respiratory muscles. The big concern is respiratory insufficiency, and these clients are at higher risk for respiratory arrest.","Drug Therapy","Drug therapy is not a cure; the hope is to alter and alleviate symptoms. Several administration routes can be used.","Immunosuppressants","MG is autoimmune, so immunosuppressants, particularly steroids, are used frequently to calm the immune response.","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Cholinesterase is the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine.","Blocking it lets acetylcholine stay in the synaptic junction longer, so it can hopefully find a receptor the antibodies haven't damaged.","They also intensify the effects of the acetylcholine released, hopefully increasing muscle strength.","Neostigmine","A cholinesterase inhibitor (anticholinesterase drug), so essentially a cholinergic drug, used to treat MG symptoms.","Facilitates impulse transmission across the neuromuscular junction, increasing cholinergic action.","At therapeutic levels it affects both muscarinic and nicotinic receptors.","Nicotinic receptors","Adrenal and skeletal muscle.","These are the receptors affected by MG.","Muscarinic receptors","Sweat glands and other glands (and the effects on the GI tract, bladder, heart, lungs and eyes below).","Neostigmine stimulates these too, which causes most of its side effects.","Cholinergic vs Anticholinergic Effects","GI tract — Increased motility, diarrhea — Decreased motility, constipation","Secretions — Increased — Decreased (dries secretions)","Bladder — Urinary urgency — The opposite","Heart rate — Slows — Increases","Lungs — Bronchial constriction — The opposite","Pupils — Miosis (constriction) — The opposite","Use the symptoms to pick the drug: symptomatic bradycardia → an anticholinergic like atropine raises the heart rate. Atropine is also used to dry up secretions, for example for clients on comfort care or hospice who are choking on secretion…","SLUDGE: the cholinergic side effects. Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Diaphoresis, GI distress, Emesis.","Adverse Effects of Neostigmine","Muscarinic stimulation","Increased secretions and GI motility (diarrhea).","Urinary urgency.","Slowed heart rate.","Bronchoconstriction: consider carefully before giving to clients with asthma or other lung disease.","Miosis.","Nicotinic (neuromuscular) stimulation","Therapeutic dose: increased muscle contraction, the desired effect.","Toxic dose: decreased contraction; the overstimulated muscles wear out and can fail, leading to a cholinergic crisis.","Cholinergic Crisis","Extreme muscle weakness or paralysis, plus signs of excessive muscarinic stimulation: increased intestinal motility (diarrhea, cramping), bradycardia, pupil constriction, lots of saliva, diaphoresis.","It arises from anticholinesterase medications: essentially a drug toxicity, from excessive acetylcholine accumulation.","Risk of respiratory arrest; the client may need mechanical ventilation.","Antidote for the muscarinic signs: atropine (an anticholinergic).","Teach clients with MG to wear a medical alert bracelet at all times.","Myasthenic Crisis vs Cholinergic Crisis","Both present with respiratory insufficiency and extreme muscle weakness, heading the same direction for opposite reasons.","The problem — Not enough stimulation or acetylcholine — Too much acetylcholine (too much neostigmine)","Why the muscles fail — Too weak, leading to respiratory failure — Overstimulated and worn out, leading to respiratory failure","Typical cause — A stressor, or inadequate medication — Anticholinesterase drug toxicity","After edrophonium — Improves (acetylcholine rises temporarily) — Worsens (already too much acetylcholine)","Edrophonium is a very short-acting cholinesterase inhibitor that raises acetylcholine temporarily. Give it when one of the two crises is suspected: improvement means myasthenic crisis; worsening means cholinergic crisis.","Multiple Sclerosis","Topic 4: what MS is, risk factors, pathophysiology, the types of MS, clinical manifestations, disease-modifying drugs, acute relapse treatment and symptom management.","What Multiple Sclerosis Is","Multiple sclerosis (MS)","A chronic, immune-mediated inflammatory disease involving degeneration of CNS myelin (loss of the myelin sheath), scarring (sclerosis or plaque formation) and loss of axons.","A progressive disease, with several types.","Features: lots of inflammation, especially in the white matter of the brain and spinal cord; demyelination of nerve fibers; and scar tissue called gliosis. Glial cells myelinate neurons in the CNS; on imaging, the white spots are areas of s…","Demyelinating disorders damage the myelin sheath and impair neural signal transmission. MS affects the central nervous system; Guillain-Barré affects the peripheral nervous system (a later lecture).","Who Gets MS","The etiology is largely unknown, most likely a combination of genetic predisposition, an immune system problem and an environmental trigger (such as an infection).","Age — Onset typically between 20 and 40.","Sex — More common in females, but males who get MS typically have a more severe, progressive course.","Location — Higher prevalence in moderately cool climates, especially the northern U.S.: above the 40th parallel north and below the 40th parallel south. Areas in between have lower prevalence.","Race — People who are white are more likely to have MS.","Family history — Increases risk.","Possible (associated) — Smoking, vitamin D deficiency, obesity, infection. Not concrete risk factors, but there is evidence of an association.","MS is a diffuse, progressive CNS autoimmune inflammatory disease affecting white and gray matter throughout the brain and spinal cord. The attack targets the myelin sheath and involves T lymphocytes and antibodies.","1 — T lymphocytes migrate to the CNS, cross the blood-brain barrier, and increase inflammation and breakdown of the myelin sheath.","2 — An antigen-antibody reaction in the CNS attacks the myelin, releasing inflammatory mediators.","3 — The oligodendrocytes (myelin-producing cells) are lost.","4 — Axons are demyelinated, and plaque and scar tissue form (sclerotic lesions).","5 — The axons die: nerve signals are first delayed, then lost. Neurons die, and the brain atrophies overall.","Normal — An axon wrapped in an intact myelin sheath. — Quick, almost instantaneous transmission.","Inflamed (early) — The nerve fiber is inflamed but intact. — Still transmitted, maybe a little slower; the client may notice some weakness.","Detached — The myelin detaches and the underlying axon is damaged; scarring and axon death follow. — Completely blocked: a permanent loss of function.","By diagnosis, clients are usually quite symptomatic. Looking back, many realize they'd had weakness much longer than they thought, but brushed it off until they couldn't ignore it.","Types of MS","Most types have exacerbations (relapses or flares): a temporary worsening of symptoms, mild or serious, lasting several days to several weeks, which may improve and may be followed by progressively worsening symptoms.","Benign MS — Exacerbations, but after each flare the client returns to baseline: no loss of function.","Relapsing-remitting (RRMS) — The most common type, about 80–90% of cases. Long remissions with a few exacerbations; pretty stable between them, with minimal disability or progression. They don't necessarily return fully to baseline: a littl…","Primary progressive (PPMS) — Gradual, steady progression from the start, with no periods of remission or exacerbation (no flare-ups).","Secondary progressive (SPMS) — Starts with relapses and remissions, with some loss of function each time, then shifts to a more progressive course without remissions.","Progressive relapsing (PRMS) — Gradual progression of symptoms with exacerbations, and fewer remissions overall.","The type is determined by how often exacerbations happen and how long remissions last.","Relapses vary: for about 20% of people they last less than a week; for others 1 to 2 weeks, or 2 weeks to a month.","If relapses get shorter after starting treatment (e.g., from a month down to a week or less), that suggests the medication is working.","No finding is unique to MS, but some are highly characteristic. The big takeaway: symptoms depend on which nerves are affected.","Common initial symptoms","Paresthesias of the face, trunk and limbs; numbness, tingling, weakness, loss of muscle function.","Optic neuritis and unilateral vision loss.","Impaired gait.","Urinary incontinence.","Some develop seizures.","Symptoms to monitor closely","Cognitive \"cog fog\": difficulty thinking clearly and concentrating, not necessarily a memory problem.","Vision problems.","Depression, related to loss of function.","Fatigue: needs more rest periods.","Pain: chronic (continuous) or acute (with exacerbations); burning or stabbing.","Bowel: constipation most common, possibly incontinence. Bladder: incontinence or retention.","Sexual dysfunction; muscle stiffness or spasms.","Walking and balance issues, vertigo: high fall risk; some eventually need a wheelchair.","Clients with MS are at high risk for falls. Most have walking and balance issues, and some have vertigo.","Goals of Drug Therapy","There is no cure. Goals: slow disability and keep the client functioning at the highest possible capacity.","Decrease relapse frequency, lengthen remissions, and reduce new brain lesions (which lead to more dysfunction and symptoms).","Three jobs: modify the disease process, treat acute exacerbations, and manage symptoms.","Disease-Modifying Drugs","Injectables","Interferon beta (Avonex) — Interferons occur naturally in the immune system and can be pro- or anti-inflammatory. Here they keep pro-inflammatory T cells from crossing the blood-brain barrier, reducing inflammation. Decreases relapse rates …","Glatiramer acetate (Copaxone) — Increases anti-inflammatory T cells, which cross the blood-brain barrier and suppress inflammation. Efficacy similar to interferons. — Injection site reactions. A reaction right after injection: flushing, pal…","Oral Drugs","Fingolimod — Retains lymphocytes in the lymph nodes so they don't migrate across the blood-brain barrier, decreasing brain inflammation. Commonly used for RRMS.","Dimethyl fumarate — Developed specifically for MS. Thought to inhibit immune cells, decreasing inflammation and preventing nerve damage.","Infusions","Natalizumab — Keeps circulating T cells from leaving the blood vessels, so they can't cross the blood-brain barrier. Very effective: reduces relapses by up to 68%. Also used for Crohn's disease. Monotherapy only; used for RRMS. — Most commo…","Alemtuzumab — Reserved for poor response to two or more MS drugs: a last effort, not first-line. — Not described in the lecture.","Mitoxantrone — For secondary progressive MS, progressive relapsing MS, and worsening RRMS that never gets a complete remission. — Not described in the lecture.","Natalizumab and PML: only through the TOUCH program, only as monotherapy, and the PML risk is significantly higher with other immunosuppressants like steroids.","Treating an Acute Relapse","High-dose IV glucocorticoids (\"pulse-dose steroids\") — MS is autoimmune, so very high doses calm the immune response: about 500 mg to 1 g daily for 3 to 5 days (vs 40 to 60 mg for asthma). Not for long-term or frequent use; usually reserved…","IV gamma globulin (IVIG) — For clients who can't tolerate high-dose glucocorticoids.","ACTH (H.P. Acthar Gel) — An adrenocorticotropic hormone gel with prolonged ACTH release after injection. Mechanism not clear, but it treats acute relapses. For clients who can't tolerate glucocorticoids, or when steroids aren't (or weren't)…","Plasmapheresis — A dialysis-like process: blood is removed through a catheter, the plasma is separated from the red cells and replaced with donor plasma, and the blood is reinfused.","Managing MS Symptoms","Symptoms vary from client to client, so medications are chosen based on the presentation.","Urinary frequency — An anticholinergic.","Urinary retention — A cholinergic.","Constipation — Hydration and fiber first, then a bulk-forming laxative.","Fatigue — Amantadine, plus teaching to plan the day around rest periods.","Muscle spasms — Muscle relaxants.","Severe cognitive dysfunction (cog fog) — Donepezil (Aricept), normally an Alzheimer's medication."]},{"s":"Week 4: Neuro II▾","a":"week-4-neuro-ii","k":["Week 4: Neuro II▾","ALS, Guillain-Barré, neuro infections, TIA, ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke, and stroke treatment.","Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)","Topic 1, first half: what ALS is, its risk factors, the motor neuron pathology and the excitotoxicity (glutamate) hypothesis, the clinical manifestations, and riluzole.","ALS: What It Is","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)","Also called Lou Gehrig's disease (after the Yankees baseball player who had it).","A neurodegenerative, progressive muscle weakness. The cause is unknown.","It is fatal, usually from respiratory failure caused by the progressive muscle weakness. Death usually occurs about 3 years after diagnosis.","Age — Typically diagnosed between 40 and 70; peaks around 60 to 69.","Sex — More diagnoses in males.","Genetics — A familial form with a genetic mutation accounts for about 10% of diagnoses.","Smoking — Some research suggests it is a risk factor.","ALS: Pathology & Cause","The principal feature is degeneration of the upper and lower motor neurons, so the voluntary muscles are affected.","Large motor neurons decrease in the spinal cord, brainstem and cerebral cortex, followed by degeneration of the remaining motor neurons.","Motor neuron death leads to axonal degeneration, demyelination and sclerosis (scarring). Damaged motor neurons stop sending messages, so the muscles can't function.","The excitotoxicity hypothesis (the current working hypothesis): excessive levels of glutamate, an excitatory neurotransmitter, start a series of events that eventually kill the neuron. It came from finding elevated glutamate in the CSF of p…","ALS: Clinical Manifestations","Signs vary greatly from person to person, depending on which neurons are affected. Initial symptoms relate to upper motor neuron dysfunction, lower motor neuron dysfunction, or both.","Spinal form (about half) — Weakness begins in the arms and legs and progresses to muscle atrophy: spasticity, lost dexterity, impaired gait, trouble walking, tripping and falling, hand weakness.","As it progresses — Difficulty chewing, swallowing and speaking; cognitive and behavioral changes in some; constipation (immobility, diet, muscle weakness); sleep problems.","Eventually — Breathing becomes the main issue: ALS paralyzes the breathing muscles, and respiratory failure is why people with ALS die.","ALS: Riluzole","Status — The only FDA-approved drug for ALS.","How it works — A glutamate inhibitor: blocks glutamate receptor sites and helps reduce damage to the motor neurons.","Benefit — Prolongs survival and slows the deterioration of function, but not by long: about 6 months to a year at most.","Side effects — Dizziness, GI upset, nausea, vomiting. Hepatotoxic: watch liver function.","Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS)","Topic 1, second half: what GBS is and what triggers it, the ascending pattern and its timing, the manifestations, and treatment and prognosis.","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: What It Is","Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS), also called acute ascending polyneuritis","A rare autoimmune disorder that causes demyelination: autoantibodies damage the myelin sheath.","Myelin covers the axons and lets impulses travel almost instantaneously. As it is destroyed, impulses are delayed or completely absent.","Viral infection — The usual trigger, a few days to weeks earlier, typically after a respiratory tract or GI infection.","Bacterial infection — Some cases; particularly Campylobacter jejuni, a foodborne illness.","Surgery — Occasionally, days to weeks after surgery.","Flu vaccination — Rarely.","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Pattern & Manifestations","The ascending pattern","Symptoms usually start in the lower extremities (weakness and tingling, pins and needles) and ascend up the body.","Recovery goes the opposite direction: strength returns from the top down (upper body, lungs, then the legs).","Severity ranges from tingling and weakness to full leg paralysis, complete quadriplegia, and respiratory insufficiency.","Timing","Most worsen over hours to weeks.","Some decline rapidly, over 24 hours; these clients may end up intubated on a ventilator.","Recovery is just as variable; some need a tracheostomy after weeks of ventilation.","The concern is the ascending weakness reaching the breathing muscles. Once they're affected, GBS can cause respiratory insufficiency and ultimately respiratory failure needing mechanical ventilation.","Manifestations","Uncoordinated movements, loss of strength, difficulty walking: a high fall risk.","Decreased sensation, so clients may not notice injury.","Loss of bowel and bladder control.","Blurred vision, which doesn't always fit the ascending pattern and can be an early sign.","Problems breathing, swallowing and chewing, so an aspiration risk.","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Treatment & Prognosis","No cure. The goal is to manage severity and speed recovery.","Acute phase: steroids (high dose, titrated down as the client recovers) and IV immunoglobulin (IVIG), which shorten the severity and duration of symptoms; high-dose IVIG can shorten hospital stays.","Most people get GBS only once; some have recurrences.","Survive — 95%","Return to baseline function — 75%","Residual weakness after about 3 years — 25 to 30%; may never return to baseline","Relapses of weakness and tingling many years later — About 3%","Neuro Infectious Diseases","Topic 2: meningitis (the meninges, causes, who gets it and how it spreads, pathophysiology, signs and the Kernig and Brudzinski tests, bacterial vs viral, treatment and prevention), encephalitis, and brain abscess.","Meningitis: What It Is & What Causes It","The lecture defines meningitis as \"an inflammation of the brain or the spinal cord.\" It is primarily an infection of the pia mater and subarachnoid space, the ventricular system and the CSF.","Infectious meningitis is caused by bacteria: from an infection elsewhere (such as pneumonia), the bloodstream, or a penetrating wound that lets bacteria reach the CSF or brain.","Most common pathogens in adults: Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococci) and Neisseria meningitidis (meningococci).","It may also be caused by viruses (enteroviruses in particular), fungi, parasites or toxins, but bacteria are the most common.","Pia mater — The layer closest to the brain.","Arachnoid mater — The middle layer; the subarachnoid space is filled with cerebrospinal fluid.","Dura mater — The outer layer: really tough and fibrous.","Who Gets Meningitis & How It Spreads","When and who","Typically fall or winter, when people stay inside with the windows closed.","Typically follows otitis or sinusitis (an ear or sinus infection), an immunocompromised state, or pneumonia.","Pneumococcal meningitis mostly affects young people and those older than 40 (as stated in the lecture).","Outbreaks in college dorms, prisons, military bases: anywhere large groups live and sleep together. It is very communicable.","How it spreads","Respiratory droplets, or contact with contaminated saliva or respiratory secretions.","Some carriers of meningococcal bacteria never develop the disease but can still pass it on.","Meningitis: Pathophysiology","1 — Meningococci or pneumococci are inhaled and attach to the epithelial cells of the nasopharynx.","2 — They cross the mucosal barrier and enter the bloodstream.","3 — They travel to the brain, cross the blood-brain barrier and infect the meninges, starting in the arachnoid mater and the CSF of the subarachnoid space.","4 — Large numbers of neutrophils are recruited; the inflammation causes brain edema, and the exudate thickens the CSF and can impair its flow.","5 — Increased intracranial pressure: an adult's skull is a closed system with no room for more.","Meningitis: Clinical Manifestations","The classic triad: Fever (infection), Headache (meningeal irritation; often throbbing), and Stiff neck / nuchal rigidity (tucking the chin to the chest is painful because it stretches the inflamed meninges).","Other manifestations","Change in mental status.","Nausea and vomiting (can happen with increased ICP).","Severe photophobia.","Decreased consciousness, up to coma or death, from increased ICP.","Seizures in about 30% of cases.","Specific to meningococcus: a petechial or purpuric rash covering the skin and mucous membranes.","Tests for Meningeal Irritation","Kernig sign — Client supine; one leg extended, the other flexed 90° at the hip and 90° at the knee. Support the heel and knee of the bent leg and try to straighten it toward the ceiling. — Pain (the client may even flex the neck).","Brudzinski sign — Client supine; place a hand under the base of the skull and flex the neck. — Neck flexion causes the hips and knees to flex, to reduce the pain of meningeal irritation.","Bacterial vs Viral Meningitis","Bacterial","More common, with a higher fatality rate if treatment isn't started quickly: it can kill within hours.","The petechial rash is associated with this form. It is non-blanchable: press a glass over it and a petechial or purpuric rash won't fade.","Can cause septic emboli: tiny microemboli that lodge in capillaries and block blood flow, so adults and children can lose fingers or limbs.","Long-term effects: hearing loss, seizures, brain damage.","Viral (acute viral meningitis)","The milder form.","Often no elevated white cell count in the CSF on lumbar puncture.","Typically no long-term effects.","Bacterial meningitis: give antibiotics as soon as possible and do not hold them for any reason. It can kill within a matter of hours.","Treating & Preventing Meningitis","IV antibiotics — Aggressive therapy with multiple IV antibiotics; two of the most common are ceftriaxone and vancomycin (vancomycin for a MRSA form).","Acyclovir — Given frequently to prevent herpetic encephalitis, which is deadly, in case the cause is viral. Stopped once the CSF results show it isn't viral.","Steroids — To reduce inflammation.","Anti-seizure medications — Often during the acute phase of meningitis and encephalitis, because increased ICP and inflammation raise seizure risk.","Vaccines (the best prophylaxis) — Available for meningococcus, pneumococcus and H. influenzae. The meningitis vaccine is first given around age 11 or 12, with a booster around 16; a different version is given to people who are immunocomprom…","Encephalitis","Inflammation of the brain tissue","An acute condition, usually caused by a virus.","Causes: West Nile virus (from mosquitoes; the lecture mentions a 2016 outbreak), measles, chickenpox, mumps. It can happen very rapidly, and patients can die.","Herpes simplex virus 1 causes herpetic encephalitis: very severe, with rapidly appearing manifestations, unlike viral meningitis, which is relatively mild.","With severe symptoms, clients are often started on both antibiotics and acyclovir until the cause is known.","Onset — Signs typically appear about 2 to 3 days after infection, once enough virus builds up.","Manifestations — From mild up to mental status changes and coma; fever and headache, nausea and vomiting; CNS changes such as seizures (common); others depending on where the infection is. These are vague and nonspecific; you don't need to …","Acyclovir — For the HSV type in particular, but not effective on other viruses. In herpetic encephalitis it reduces mortality but doesn't necessarily reduce neurological complications.","Supportive care — Fluids, acetaminophen, antiemetics for vomiting, and often anti-seizure medications.","Brain Abscess","What it is — An accumulation of pus within the brain tissue, from a localized infection (like encephalitis) or a systemic infection.","Common sources — Ear, tooth, mastoid or sinus infections, because of their proximity to the brain.","Major culprits — Streptococci and Staphylococcus aureus, bacteria found on the skin that typically cause ear, tooth and sinus infections.","Imaging — The abscess walls are visible, and the midline can be shifted by the pressure from pus and inflammation.","Treatment — Drain the abscess to reduce pressure (the brain is a closed system, so this lowers the risk of secondary injury from swelling), then put antibiotics into the area.","Manifestations — Similar to meningitis and encephalitis: headache, fever, nausea, vomiting; signs of increased ICP (drowsiness, confusion, up to seizures). Other symptoms depend on location; for example, a temporal lobe abscess causes visua…","Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)","Topic 3, first voiceover (TIA): what a TIA is, carotid vs vertebrobasilar presentations, the Bell's palsy mimic, the key terms, and TIA diagnosis and treatment.","A type of cerebrovascular disease: a focal, acute cerebral insufficiency. Usually only one vessel gets closed off, clotted off or has a spasm, so only that area of the brain is affected.","Typically lasts less than 24 hours: a transient episode of neurologic dysfunction with no acute infarction. Symptoms are often gone by the time the client reaches the ED, with no residual effects.","A stroke, by contrast, causes neurological deficits that last longer than 24 hours.","Fairly common; seen more often in males.","After a TIA, the client is at increased risk for a stroke, especially within the first month. Monitor and teach them to go to the ED if they suspect another TIA, because it might be a full-blown stroke.","Presentation depends on which vessel is involved (a vessel supplying the speech area causes difficulty forming words). Recurrent TIAs usually produce the same symptoms each time. Onset is abrupt, and recovery is abrupt and rapid.","The lecture also says a CT can show blood in the brain tissue with a TIA, though \"not as clear as it is with an ischemic stroke.\" That is kept as stated; check the slide wording.","Carotid area","Weakness or heaviness in the arms, legs or face.","Numbness.","Vision loss.","Vertebrobasilar area (back of the brain)","Blurry vision.","Vertigo.","Ataxia.","Motor or sensory changes.","TIA mimic: Bell's palsy","A problem with the facial nerve causes facial paralysis (drooping mouth, drooping face), so these clients are often evaluated for a stroke or TIA. Bell's palsy recovers, but it takes time and requires treatment; a TIA recovers much more qui…","Key Terms: Dysphagia, Dysphasia, Contralateral & Ipsilateral","Dysphagia (with a G) — Difficulty swallowing. Memory trick: G for gastric. — Keep NPO: high risk for aspiration (a foreign substance entering the lungs from eating and drinking without swallowing properly), which can inflame and damage the …","Dysphasia (with an S) — Difficulty with speech. — A communication problem.","Contralateral — Crossing the midline: the opposite side. — Manifestations appear on the opposite side of the body from the brain damage.","Ipsilateral — Staying on the same side. — Manifestations appear on the same side as the damage.","TIA: Diagnosis & Treatment","History and physical — Is there a pattern of other vascular problems?","Quick CT scan first — Rule out hemorrhage, which is easy to see on CT. Then look for other evidence of CVA, such as lacunar infarcts (tiny infarcts that may cause no symptoms).","MRA (MRI with angiography) — Uses dye to highlight aneurysms.","Carotid Doppler studies — Look for buildup in the carotid arteries reducing blood flow to the brain.","Echocardiogram — Look for a cardiac cause, such as a clot on the left side of the heart that could break off and travel to the brain.","Treatment depends on the cause; the goal is reducing the risk of a full stroke","Small doses of aspirin.","Clopidogrel (Plavix): an antiplatelet that keeps platelets from sticking together.","If the TIA is cardiac-related: heparin or warfarin to prevent coagulation.","If caused by carotid narrowing or stenosis: carotid endarterectomy (opens the artery by removing the plaque), or carotid angioplasty (a balloon catheter fed up through usually the femoral artery and inflated, with a filter to catch plaque p…","Education on lifestyle changes, especially the modifiable risk factors for stroke.","Stroke & Ischemic Stroke","Topic 3, second voiceover (Stroke and hemorrhage 1): stroke types and statistics, risk factors, signs and the first CT, ischemic stroke (thrombotic and embolic), and the penumbra.","Stroke (CVA): Overview & Statistics","Also called a cerebrovascular accident (CVA), the term used in hospitals, or a \"brain attack\" (a term that didn't stick).","The result of a problem with blood supply to the brain. Two types: ischemic, about 87% of strokes, and hemorrhagic, about 13%.","About 800,000 strokes a year; for over half a million people it's their first stroke. Risk rises with age, but about a third of people with a first stroke are under 65.","A leading cause of disability. Third leading cause of death in women, fifth in men, fourth overall. Someone has a stroke about every 40 seconds and someone dies from one every 4 minutes; about $36.5 billion in healthcare costs a year.","Stroke Risk Factors","Hypertension — Especially uncontrolled or undiagnosed. Commonly leads to hemorrhagic stroke, often by way of an aneurysm that ruptures, or by weakening the arterial wall until it ruptures. More than doubles the risk of intracranial hemorrha…","Atherosclerosis and hyperlipidemia — Vessel damage leads to plaque formation and clots, in brain vessels as in the heart.","Tobacco use — Damages the endothelial lining of blood vessels.","Diabetes — Damages blood vessel walls, which can lead to plaque formation.","Oral contraceptives — Particularly estrogen and progesterone: increase plasma fibrinogen and coagulation factor activity.","Age (starred) — Advanced age: higher risk of stroke but a better chance of recovery, because brain atrophy leaves more room in the closed skull for swelling and bleeding.","Male sex — Males are at higher risk overall.","Sickle cell anemia — In crisis, blood cells clump and can lodge in brain vessels.","Atrial fibrillation — Quivering atria let blood pool and clot; the clot can travel to the brain. Often treated with anticoagulants such as warfarin, which bring their own bleeding risk.","Signs of a Stroke & the First Test","Clinical manifestations","Numbness or weakness of the face, arm or leg, usually on one side (asymmetrical weakness).","Sudden confusion; difficulty speaking or understanding speech; slurred speech (dysarthria).","Vision trouble in one or both eyes.","Trouble walking, balance problems, dizziness, lack of coordination (ataxia).","A sudden, severe headache with no known cause, particularly important in hemorrhagic stroke.","CT without contrast, done very quickly","Part of the stroke alert protocol (with attention to airway and breathing as needed); it drives the rest of the treatment plan.","Blood shows up right away as white; an ischemic stroke shows as a darkened area.","We must know ischemic vs hemorrhagic: never anticoagulate or give a thrombolytic to a hemorrhagic stroke. It makes the bleeding worse and could kill the client.","Ischemic Stroke","An occlusion of a cerebral artery by a thrombus or an embolus; everything distal to the occlusion loses its blood supply.","Both types typically present the same way, based on which part of the brain isn't perfused, but the etiology guides treatment: atherosclerosis? a hypercoagulability problem? a thrombus or embolism elsewhere?","On CT, a darkened area that isn't prominent at first but becomes more noticeable if blood flow isn't restored.","A tiny vessel occlusion may go unnoticed (evidence of an old stroke is sometimes found on an unrelated CT). A larger vessel occlusion produces symptoms based on its location.","Neuro deficits start within about one minute of oxygen deprivation; without reestablished blood flow, the damage becomes irreversible.","Hemorrhagic conversion: after an ischemic stroke, blood from the peripheral circulation extravasates across a disrupted blood-brain barrier into the brain tissue once reperfusion is established, naturally or as a result of therapy. A hemorr…","Thrombotic — Arterial obstruction from thrombus formation. Atherosclerosis: vessel-lining damage leads to plaque that narrows or blocks the vessel; if the plaque ruptures, a clot forms. Hypercoagulable states: cancer, oral contraceptives, a…","Embolic — Look at the heart first: Mural thrombus: a clot in the ventricle (the image shows the left ventricle); a piece breaks off and travels through the aorta and carotids to the brain. Atrial fibrillation: a left atrial thrombus from po…","Mural thrombus: a clot in the ventricle (the image shows the left ventricle); a piece breaks off and travels through the aorta and carotids to the brain.","Atrial fibrillation: a left atrial thrombus from pooled blood. Usually arterial clots, on the left side of the heart.","Patent foramen ovale: a birth-defect hole in the septum between the atria lets a venous clot (such as a DVT piece) pass from the right atrium to the left atrium instead of going to the lungs as a PE; the left ventricle then sends it to the …","Valve infection, particularly the mitral valve: vegetation forms, and a piece breaks off as an embolus.","Mechanical valve replacement (more than a porcine valve): clots can form around the valve.","Carotid artery plaque can rupture, and the clot travels to the brain.","The lecture describes the mitral valve as \"between the right and left ventricle.\" It is kept out of the table as a likely slip; check the slide.","Assessment clues for embolic risk","Carotid bruit: a swishing sound on auscultation from turbulent flow through a vessel narrowed by a growing plaque.","ECG: in A-fib, little quivering lines replace the P waves (the atria aren't contracting properly). These clients are at higher risk for embolic stroke, especially if newly diagnosed and not yet anticoagulated long enough.","Time Is Tissue: The Penumbra","Dead tissue (core)","Injured beyond repair.","Penumbra","The extended area of injured but salvageable tissue around the core.","Goal for ischemic stroke: treatment within 3 hours of symptom onset, to reestablish blood flow and save the penumbra. Once a stroke protocol is activated, the client goes to CT first (often bypassing a room), labs are drawn quickly and the …","Hemorrhagic Stroke & Intracranial Bleeds","Topic 3, third voiceover (Stroke and hemorrhage 2): hemorrhagic stroke, epidural, subdural and subarachnoid bleeds, subarachnoid hemorrhage management, berry aneurysms and AVMs, and BE FAST.","Hemorrhagic Stroke","A ruptured vessel with bleeding within the brain tissue (the parenchyma).","Leading cause: untreated hypertension; over time the pressure weakens vessels until they rupture. Clients with aneurysms are also at increased risk.","About 38% mortality, within minutes to hours; in the brainstem, death can occur within seconds. Mortality depends on the size of the area affected.","Blood is very irritating to brain tissue, so inflammation causes edema; blood plus edema in the confined skull sharply increases ICP. The brain tissue can collapse on itself (herniation), which usually results in death.","Usually ages 30 to 60, younger than ischemic stroke clients. High morbidity and mortality, with serious disabilities.","Prognosis depends on","Age: older clients have brain atrophy and more room, so a better prognosis.","Location and size: the brainstem has a very poor prognosis.","How rapid the bleeding is, and whether it causes brain distortion or shift. A midline shift (a bleed pushing one side across the center line, seen on a CT report) means a very poor prognosis.","Presentation","The worst headache of their life.","A rapid change in level of consciousness.","With subdural, epidural or subarachnoid bleeds, the client presents like meningitis from meningeal irritation: nuchal rigidity and photophobia.","Other Intracranial Hemorrhages","Bleeding in the layers covering the brain, named by location. A bleed in the brain tissue itself is a hemorrhagic stroke.","Epidural — Between the skull and the dura mater. — Usually with skull fractures (think trauma). Tend to be arterial, so even a less severe injury can mean a lot of blood loss.","Subdural — Below the dura. — Tend to be venous, e.g., injury to bridging veins. Severe injury can cause rapid decline, but venous blood may accumulate slowly: a client hits their head, seems fine, and symptoms appear later.","Subarachnoid — Between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater, where the CSF lives. — Usually from an aneurysm or an AVM. Causes bloody CSF.","Treatment for epidural and subdural bleeds is to evacuate the collecting blood and relieve pressure on the brain. Blood bleeding into the brain tissue cannot be evacuated.","Subarachnoid Hemorrhage","Diagnosis by lumbar puncture","Normal CSF is clear or slightly yellow. The first tube may have a little blood from needle trauma, but it should clear. With a subarachnoid bleed, the fluid stays bloody. A subarachnoid hemorrhage is identified by lumbar puncture about 12 h…","Bed rest and BP control — Avoid big BP fluctuations: keep it managed to prevent further bleeding, but high enough to perfuse the brain. The nurse is often given very specific BP parameters.","Surgery — If an aneurysm is the culprit, surgical intervention (clipping or coiling), usually done ASAP.","Anticonvulsant — Blood irritates the brain, so seizure risk rises.","Calcium channel blocker — After clipping or coiling, to help treat the symptoms of the subarachnoid hemorrhage.","Triple H — Keeps the brain perfused: pressors to keep BP high enough, colloids and a hypervolemic state, and some expected hemodilution.","The lecture describes the calcium channel blocker as working \"by narrowing and slowing that blood flow.\" It is kept out of the table as stated; check the slide for the intended mechanism.","Berry Aneurysms, AVMs, Clipping & Coiling","Berry aneurysm — The most common aneurysm that ruptures in the brain: little berries hanging off the artery that rupture when big enough.","AVM (arteriovenous malformation) — Normally arteries divide into arterioles, capillaries, venules, then veins. In an AVM the junction is a jumbled clump, so high-pressure arterial blood goes directly into the vein without slowing through ca…","Clipping — A clip is placed around the aneurysm to block blood flow into it.","Coiling (endovascular embolization) — A catheter inserted in the groin is threaded into the aneurysm, and detachable platinum coils reduce blood flow into the sac, lowering the chance of rupture.","The lecture also says \"berry aneurysms are often located in the veins,\" which conflicts with the same slide (\"hanging off the artery\") and with Week 1. It is kept out of the table; check the slide.","BE FAST & Last Known Normal","B: Balance — Headache, dizziness, loss of balance, trouble walking; falls, especially in the elderly.","E: Eyes — Sudden vision loss in one or both eyes; unequal pupils.","F: Face — Facial asymmetry: have them smile. Differentiate from Bell's palsy.","A: Arm — Weakness: both arms held up perpendicular to the body; does one drift?","S: Speech — Difficulty speaking or slurred words.","T: Time — Call 911, get to the hospital, activate a stroke alert.","Find out exactly when symptoms started: the \"last known normal.\" Treatment must happen within a certain time frame.","Stroke Treatment & Consequences","Topic 3, fourth voiceover (Stroke treatment): the first steps, hemorrhagic vs ischemic treatment, alteplase, and the consequences of stroke.","Stroke Treatment: First Steps","ABCs: can they maintain their airway, are they breathing, is there a pulse? A GCS below 8 raises concern about airway protection, so the client is intubated. Stroke clients may also have decreased respiratory drive.","Stroke alert / protocol: labs, CT, the neuro team, and a blood sugar check, because hypoglycemia can mimic a stroke.","CT scan without contrast, done first to exclude hemorrhagic stroke: fast, and best in the acute stage. An MRI gives better images later, but it is slower and less available, so it isn't the first choice in an emergency.","Hemorrhagic stroke","Reverse anticoagulation: is there a reversal agent or antidote for the client's medications?","Manage hypertension, the likely cause.","Manage ICP: elevate the head of the bed, give antipyretics for fever, keep the head in a neutral position.","Ischemic stroke","Thrombolytics (clot busters) to open the occlusion and protect and reperfuse the penumbra.","Mechanical thrombectomy: a surgeon removes the clot manually (also called a penumbra procedure).","Alteplase (tPA) for Ischemic Stroke","Heparin and antiplatelets don't dissolve a clot; they only prevent one from forming. Fibrinolytics actually break up clots.","Alteplase (tPA, tissue plasminogen activator), the most commonly used fibrinolytic, converts plasminogen to plasmin; plasmin digests the fibrin.","Used in severe thrombotic events: acute MI, PE, ischemic stroke, occasionally femoral artery clots or a clotted central line. Given IV.","Monitor BP — It can cause hypotension.","Monitor HR and rhythm — Arrhythmias can develop with reperfusion.","Bleeding risk — Increased risk of intracranial bleeding. Don't give it to clients at high risk for bleeding (such as a previous hemorrhagic stroke or a GI bleed).","CT first — Always rule out hemorrhagic stroke before giving it.","With heparin — Often given with heparin to prevent new clots from forming.","Antidote — Aminocaproic acid, an antithrombolytic: blocks plasminogen binding and activation to plasmin, reducing fibrinolysis to stop bleeding. Give it only if the hemorrhage is life-threatening.","Consequences of Stroke","Left side of the brain affected","Difficulty with communication (verbal and written) and with more analytical activities.","Right side of the brain affected","Difficulty with things like imagination, music and art.","Dysphagia — A swallow evaluation comes before giving food or advancing a diet. Speech pathology does a barium swallow study under real-time x-ray with different thicknesses of food and liquid, then recommends a diet: regular, thickened liqu…","Motor and sensory deficits — Neglect of body parts no longer getting sensory input; poor coordination (fall risk); loss of muscle groups, which atrophy over time.","Flaccidity → spasticity → contractures — Flaccid (limp, weak) muscles on the contralateral side become spastic (painful), then contractures (permanently shortened muscles, joints stuck in abnormal positions), typically within the first 6 we…","Homonymous hemianopia — Contralateral field blindness: loss (or blurring) of up to half the vision, on the same side of both visual fields.","Aphasia — The global term for affected language: dysphasia (speech), receptive aphasia (understanding), reading and writing problems; one or all.","Depression — Very common, from significant disabilities and loss of independence.","Memory problems — Even well-known things like family members' names.","Behavior changes — Increased emotional response (upset, angry or crying more easily); slower reaction times; hesitance or caution; confusion or disorientation; apathy.","The lecture also says clients \"can underestimate their own abilities\" and calls it a big safety concern, alongside slower reaction times (fall risk). That is kept as stated; check the slide wording."]},{"s":"Week 5: Endocrine Emergencies▾","a":"week-5-endocrine-emergencies","k":["Week 5: Endocrine Emergencies▾","DKA and HHS, thyroid storm, myxedema coma, Addison's disease and Addisonian crisis.","DKA & HHS: Physiology & Labs","Topic 1, first half: what DKA and HHS are and who gets them, the insulin and counter-regulatory hormone background, why DKA makes ketones and HHS doesn't, why HHS glucose runs higher, the anion gap, plasma osmolality and sodium, and potassi…","DKA vs HHS: What They Are & Who Gets Them","Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)","A crisis that occurs as an acute metabolic complication of diabetes, with ketosis.","More common in people under 65, and characteristically associated with type 1 diabetes.","Can occur in type 2 under extreme stress: really serious infections, sepsis, trauma, cardiovascular or other emergencies. Type 2 diabetics still make insulin, which is typically enough to prevent fat breakdown into ketones unless the situat…","About 30% of all DKA cases occur in people with type 2 diabetes, particularly those taking SGLT2 inhibitors (the \"-flozins,\" like dapagliflozin).","Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS)","Also called hyperosmolar hyperglycemic syndrome; in the past, hyperglycemic hyperosmolar non-ketotic (HHNK). HHS is the name you'll see most.","Another life-threatening complication of diabetes, but with no ketosis.","Mostly type 2 diabetes, and more common over 65.","Pretty rare.","Background: Insulin, Glucagon & Counter-Regulatory Hormones","Extracellular glucose is primarily regulated by two hormones: insulin and glucagon.","After a meal, glucose enters the pancreatic beta cells, which leads to insulin release. Insulin restores normal blood glucose by reducing hepatic glucose production (less glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis) and by increasing glucose uptake …","Gluconeogenesis turns non-carbohydrate substrates (amino acids, lactate, glycerol) into glucose. Glycogenolysis breaks glycogen down into glucose, which the body can use for energy.","In times of stress, more insulin is needed than usual; in diabetics that insulin isn't available, and DKA or HHS can develop.","In both, the main problem is that insulin isn't working properly: in DKA the body isn't making enough; in HHS the insulin that is available isn't working effectively.","On top of that, the counter-regulatory hormones (glucagon, adrenaline, cortisol, growth hormone) go up. They increase hepatic glucose production and impair the peripheral tissues' ability to use glucose.","Dehydration and electrolyte imbalances occur primarily from the osmotic diuresis caused by glycosuria (glucose in the urine).","The lecture uses the word \"gluconeogenesis\" twice in a row; the second definition (glycogen broken down into glucose) is glycogenolysis, which is how it is listed here.","Why DKA Makes Ketones & HHS Doesn't","DKA is marked by increased gluconeogenesis, lipolysis and ketogenesis, with reduced glycolysis: that is the \"keto\" in ketoacidosis.","The insulin deficiency is absolute in type 1 and relative in type 2, and the excess counter-regulatory hormones add to it.","Suppressing lipolysis and ketogenesis is more sensitive to insulin than suppressing gluconeogenesis. In HHS, the residual insulin is enough to minimize ketoacidosis but not enough to control the hyperglycemia.","1 — Insulin deficiency and resistance mean glucose can't get into the cells, which need it for energy.","2 — Without glucose, the body breaks down fat for energy (lipolysis), releasing free fatty acids and glycerol from peripheral fat stores.","3 — The free fatty acids travel to the liver, are taken up by hepatocytes and activated.","4 — They are converted into acetyl-CoA, enter the ketogenic pathway and form ketone bodies.","Why not in HHS? One theory: there may be enough insulin activity to minimize lipolysis (and so ketone formation), but not enough to block gluconeogenesis, promote glucose use and prevent the hyperglycemia.","Why HHS Glucose Runs Higher","DKA","Clients present earlier, with symptoms of ketoacidosis: shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.","Because they seek treatment earlier, glucose is usually 350 to 450 (can be as high as 800).","Tend to be younger with a higher GFR, so a greater capacity to excrete glucose.","HHS","Clients present later, without those early ketoacidosis symptoms.","Glucose is typically higher: it can exceed 1,000.","Glycosuria first limits the rise in glucose, but the osmotic diuresis causes volume depletion and a lower GFR, which limits glucose excretion. The effect is more pronounced in HHS.","Anion Gap, Plasma Osmolality & Sodium","Anion gap — DKA typically presents as an elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis. The anion gap is the difference between negatively and positively charged electrolytes, calculated from sodium, chloride and bicarb (sodium minus chloride plus …","Beta-hydroxybutyrate — Ketones are no longer really measured directly; in DKA, beta-hydroxybutyrate (one of the main metabolic products in ketoacidosis) is measured instead.","Closing the gap — Ketoacid anion excretion depends on volume status and GFR. Isotonic IV fluids correct the hypovolemia, increase renal excretion of ketoacid anions and reduce the anion gap.","Plasma osmolality — Increased in both, in proportion to the glucose; usually not as high in DKA because the glucose isn't as high.","Sodium — Hyperglycemia pulls water out of the cells and expands the extracellular fluid, so plasma sodium falls: a dilutional hyponatremia. It isn't treated directly, because it corrects as the hyperglycemia is corrected.","Potassium: High on Arrival, Depleted Underneath","Most body potassium is in the cells. Clients with DKA and HHS present with a normal to elevated serum potassium but a significant total body potassium depletion, for three reasons:","Acidosis — In metabolic acidosis, hydrogen ions move into the cells and potassium moves out to balance the charge, raising serum potassium. Working kidneys can offset this by excreting the excess.","Insulin deficiency — Insulin normally moves potassium into the cells; without it, potassium stays in the extracellular fluid, raising serum levels further.","Hyperosmolarity — The osmotic diuresis from high glucose causes massive urinary losses, and potassium is lost continuously in the urine, depleting the total body supply.","Serum potassium can be 5, 6 or beyond on arrival. Don't bring it down unless it's causing problems and the client is symptomatic: it returns toward normal as the pH is corrected.","Once treatment starts, insulin drives potassium back into the cells and the serum level falls quickly, which can cause severe hypokalemia. If the kidneys have already excreted potassium, there is even less left to move back in.","As soon as the potassium reaches a normal level, replacement typically starts, because it will keep moving into the cells and the serum still needs a normal level to prevent cardiac problems.","Watch the client: are they symptomatic, and are there EKG changes of hyper- or hypokalemia (including dysrhythmias)?","DKA & HHS: Presentation, Treatment & Resolution","Topic 1, second half: precipitating factors, how each presents, the treatment steps, how you know each has resolved, and why hydration matters so much in HHS.","Precipitating Factors","A precipitating factor can often be identified.","Infection is the most common: severe pneumonia or a UTI without an insulin adjustment.","Inflammatory events: MI, CVA, sepsis, pancreatitis, without an insulin adjustment.","Poor compliance with insulin, or increased insulin needs that aren't met. Compliance can be checked with a hemoglobin A1c.","Clinical Presentation","DKA: evolves rapidly, over about 24 hours","GI: nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, from delayed gastric emptying or an ileus induced by the metabolic acidosis.","Neurologic: slight confusion up to obtunded, with severe acidosis.","Volume depletion: decreased skin turgor, decreased urine output, dry oral mucosa, tachycardia, hypotension.","Fruity breath (some say it smells like nail polish remover) from exhaled acetone.","Kussmaul respirations: really deep respirations and hyperventilation, compensating for the metabolic acidosis.","HHS: begins several days before admission","Marked hyperglycemia with the typical signs: polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, plus weakness.","As it progresses: lethargy, obtunded, up to coma. Neurologic symptoms are most common in HHS because of the hyperosmolarity.","Extracellular volume depletion: dry mucous membranes, poor skin turgor, sunken eyes, hypotension, tachycardia.","Symptoms of a coexisting illness that may have precipitated it (pneumonia, UTI) are usually present.","Treatment","Hospitals usually have a protocol and algorithm; follow your hospital's protocol. The lecturer cites the University of Kentucky (UK) protocol for the potassium numbers.","1. Fluid replacement — Usually first: expands extracellular volume and stabilizes the client hemodynamically. Treat volume depletion rapidly (normal saline); fluid resuscitation in HHS should be pretty aggressive.","2. Correct the potassium deficit — Per the UK protocol, a DKA client receives potassium replacement if potassium is less than 5 before insulin is started, to prevent the hypokalemia that follows. If potassium is less than 3.3, replace it fi…","Sodium bicarb — May be given if the pH is low (around 7.1 to 7.2), depending on how the client presents.","3. IV insulin — An IV insulin infusion, with blood sugar checked regularly (typically once an hour at first) and the rate adjusted to the glucose. Continue until the anion gap is closed and the condition is resolving.","Add dextrose — When glucose reaches about 200 to 250, fluids change from no glucose to D5W/normal saline (dextrose added to the saline) while the insulin infusion continues, to prevent hypoglycemia if the gap is still open.","Monitor — Glucose very closely and frequently (it shouldn't fall too rapidly), electrolytes and the anion gap.","Insulin drives potassium into the cells. Check potassium before insulin: below 3.3, replace potassium first. Watch for EKG changes of hyper- and hypokalemia throughout treatment.","How You Know It's Resolved","DKA: look at the labs","Make sure the ketoacidosis has resolved: venous pH or the anion gap, and the level of ketoacidosis by beta-hydroxybutyrate, which falls as the acidosis is corrected.","HHS: look at the patient","Are they mentally alert and back to baseline? Also check the plasma osmolality: the goal is about 315, which the lecture calls a normal level.","Once the client can eat regularly, IV insulin is changed to subQ insulin.","HHS: Why Hydration Matters So Much","If adequate hydration isn't maintained, dehydration and worsening renal function follow.","Clients with poor fluid intake from altered thirst mechanisms or inability to access fluids (as may be seen in the elderly) are at increased risk of severe dehydration.","Diminished renal filtration further impairs glucose excretion, which exacerbates the hyperglycemia.","Thyroid Storm","Topic 2, first half: the two extremes of the thyroid spectrum, then thyroid storm: what it is, risk factors, clinical manifestations and treatment.","The Two Extremes of the Thyroid Spectrum","Thyroid storm (thyrotoxic crisis)","The hyperthyroid extreme: an overwhelming release of thyroid hormones.","Myxedema coma","The hypothyroid extreme: severe hypothyroidism.","Many manifestations are the same as ordinary hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism, just more dramatic or severe.","Thyroid Storm: What It Is","An overwhelming release of thyroid hormones that puts an intense stimulus on the body's metabolism.","Typically a reaction to a major stressor (a big infection, major surgery, a car wreck). The stressor releases stress hormones such as adrenaline, everything in the body is \"revved up,\" and the body can't tolerate this for long.","Life-threatening: untreated, it can cause death within about 48 hours.","Long-standing untreated hyperthyroidism — The lecture calls this Graves' disease.","A precipitating acute event — Surgery, trauma or infection, which may or may not involve the thyroid.","Irregular use or stopping antithyroid drugs — Commonly reported before a thyroid storm.","Thyroid Storm: Clinical Manifestations","Fever (the most notable) — Body temperature rises greatly, to 104 or 105; excessive sweating; the client is hot and can't tolerate heat.","Tachycardia — Often above 140.","Blood pressure — Hypertension at first. With sustained tachycardia the heart can't fill properly, so BP may start to drop. Untreated: hypotension, heart failure and pulmonary edema that can lead to death.","Dysrhythmias — A-fib and atrial flutter are the most common.","Other — Nausea, vomiting, agitation, tremors, psychosis, up to stupor or coma.","The transcript says \"heat tolerance\"; with fever and excessive sweating, the page reads it as not tolerating heat.","Thyroid Storm: Treatment","Treatment hits several points where thyroid hormone acts. These clients are in the ICU and very closely monitored.","Beta blocker — Controls the symptoms and signs of the increased adrenergic tone; adjusted as needed to keep a proper heart rate and blood pressure.","Thionamide (PTU) — Blocks new hormone synthesis and suppresses conversion of T4 to T3. Synthesis is blocked within about 1 to 2 hours; within 24 hours, T3 levels can drop up to about 45%.","Iodine solution — Blocks the release of thyroid hormone. Given about 1 hour after the first dose of PTU.","Glucocorticoids — Reduce T4 to T3 conversion. Evidence is conflicting, so they're given when there is clear evidence of thyroid storm, because they rapidly reduce active thyroid hormone and its effects.","Bile acid sequestrants — Thyroid hormones are metabolized in the liver and excreted in bile; free hormone released into the intestines is reabsorbed. Sequestrants decrease enterohepatic circulation and recycling of thyroid hormone.","Enterohepatic circulation is the circulation of biliary acids, bilirubin and other substances from the liver to the bile, stored in the gallbladder before reaching the small intestine.","The lecture says \"thionamide, which is also called PTU.\" PTU is the thionamide named here.","Myxedema Coma","Topic 2, second half: myxedema coma's risk factors, clinical manifestations and treatment, then thyroid storm vs myxedema coma side by side.","Myxedema Coma: What It Is","Severe hypothyroidism leading to decreased mental state, hypothermia and slowed function of other organs.","A medical emergency with a high mortality rate, but uncommon, because hypothyroidism can be diagnosed earlier and kept from getting worse.","Severe, long-standing or poorly controlled hypothyroidism — The baseline risk, as with thyroid storm on the other end.","A precipitating acute event — Infection, MI or major surgery on top of poorly controlled hypothyroidism.","Sedative drugs — Especially opioids, which can trigger myxedema coma in someone who already has hypothyroidism.","Myxedema Coma: Clinical Manifestations","Altered consciousness — Despite the name, clients don't often present in coma: confusion, lethargy, obtunded, some with psychosis. Untreated, it can progress to coma.","Hypothermia — Decreased thermogenesis from decreased metabolism. The lower the temperature, the higher the mortality.","Hypotension and bradycardia — Thyroid hormone helps maintain BP homeostasis; severe hypothyroidism causes bradycardia, decreased contractility, low cardiac output and hypotension.","Hyponatremia — About 50% of the time. When present it is severe and adds to the decreased mental status. Impaired free water excretion causes a dilutional hyponatremia.","Hypoglycemia — Can be caused by hypothyroidism alone and may be worse in myxedema coma.","Hypoventilation — Overall depression of the ventilatory drive and decreased responsiveness, leading to hypoxia and hypercapnia (respiratory acidosis). Some clients need a mechanical ventilator.","The lecture words it as hypoventilation \"results from a respiratory acidosis\"; the page follows the rest of that sentence (depressed ventilatory drive leading to hypoxia and hypercapnia).","Myxedema Coma: Treatment","If myxedema coma is suspected, treat before lab confirmation.","Thyroid hormones (combination) — Levothyroxine (T4) plus liothyronine (T3): the combination works better than T4 alone. Given IV until the client can take T4 orally. Thyroid levels are monitored daily to confirm the therapy is working.","Glucocorticoids — Clients often have a coexisting adrenal insufficiency (decreased pituitary function, secondary adrenal insufficiency, or ACTH secretion blunted by severe hypothyroidism). IV hydrocortisone is given until adrenal insufficie…","Supportive measures — IV fluids, electrolyte replacement as needed, mechanical ventilation for hypoventilation, glucose monitoring, and treating any underlying infection.","Thyroid Storm vs Myxedema Coma","End of the spectrum — Hyperthyroid — Hypothyroid","Underlying risk — Long-standing untreated hyperthyroidism; irregular use or stopping antithyroid drugs — Severe, long-standing or poorly controlled hypothyroidism; sedatives, especially opioids","Trigger — Infection, surgery, trauma — Infection, MI, surgery","Temperature — Fever, 104 to 105 — Hypothermia","Heart rate and BP — Tachycardia above 140; hypertension, then hypotension — Bradycardia and hypotension","Mental status — Agitation, tremors, psychosis, up to stupor or coma — Confusion, lethargy, obtunded, psychosis, up to coma","Key drugs — Beta blocker, PTU, iodine, glucocorticoids, bile acid sequestrants — IV levothyroxine plus liothyronine, IV hydrocortisone","Addison's Disease & Replacement Therapy","Topic 3, first half: the three S's, causes and the ACTH feedback loop, aldosterone vs cortisol deficiency, lifelong replacement therapy, and stress dosing and teaching.","Addison's Disease: The Three S's","Addison's disease is a disease of the adrenal cortex that causes hyposecretion of all three adrenocortical hormones.","Sugar — Cortisol — Stimulates gluconeogenesis and raises blood sugar; mobilizes fat stores; helps break down proteins.","Salt — Aldosterone — Assists in fluid and electrolyte balance.","Sex — Androgens — Male sex hormones.","The most severe effects come from the lack of cortisol.","Long-term effects: immune suppression and bone breakdown (bone density problems).","Causes & the Feedback Loop","It can be idiopathic, but the most common cause is autoimmune. The adrenal gland can also be damaged or destroyed by disease or trauma.","In primary adrenocortical insufficiency (primary Addison's), the adrenal gland stops secreting cortisol, so there is no negative feedback. The anterior pituitary increases MSH and ACTH to try to stimulate cortisol, but despite very high lev…","Manifestations don't become evident until about 90% of the adrenal cortex is destroyed, so Addison's is usually advanced at diagnosis, or diagnosed during an Addisonian crisis.","Aldosterone Deficiency vs Cortisol Deficiency","Low aldosterone (hypoaldosteronism)","Aldosterone regulates sodium, and so water retention. Low aldosterone means loss of water and sodium, with potassium retention.","Hyponatremia, hyperkalemia and dehydration, with the signs of all three.","Hypotension from decreased vascular tone, cardiac output and circulating blood volume.","Salt craving.","The lecture adds that hypoaldosteronism signals more ADH, which stimulates water reabsorption and could lead to fluid volume excess in some cases.","Low cortisol (hypocortisolism)","Low energy: a lack of stress hormones.","Hypoglycemia, since cortisol normally raises blood glucose.","Hyperpigmentation: the pituitary increases ACTH to raise cortisol, and ACTH also stimulates melanin secretion by melanocytes, so unsuppressed ACTH darkens the skin.","Lifelong Replacement Therapy","Adrenal insufficiency requires lifelong replacement with the appropriate corticosteroids.","Hydrocortisone (Cortef) — The drug of choice: has both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid activity. IM, IV and PO.","Prednisone — Glucocorticoid, PO.","Dexamethasone — Glucocorticoid, IV or PO.","Fludrocortisone — Added when extra mineralocorticoid activity is needed; the only mineralocorticoid available. Works like aldosterone, so expect an impact on sodium and water balance. PO.","Dosing should mimic the body's normal pattern of corticosteroid secretion. Mineralocorticoids can be given once a day; glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid doses should approximate what the adrenal glands normally secrete.","Never stop therapy abruptly.","Stress Dosing & Teaching","Healthy adrenal glands increase glucocorticoid output in response to stress. These clients don't get that boost, so it has to be given exogenously: increase the dose in times of stress.","Supplemental dosing follows guidelines for specific medical conditions and surgical procedures, so it varies. Example: for a mild or febrile illness, the 3 x 3 rule (loosely, three times the usual dose for three days).","Keep an emergency supply: an injectable preparation plus an oral preparation.","Wear a medical alert bracelet so emergency personnel know about the glucocorticoid needs.","Not following the regimen (stopping abruptly, or not increasing the dose under stress) can cause an Addisonian crisis, which can be fatal.","Addisonian (Adrenal) Crisis","Topic 3, second half: what an Addisonian crisis is and what causes it, its symptoms, and its treatment.","Addisonian (Adrenal) Crisis: What It Is & What Causes It","A complication of Addison's disease: acute adrenal insufficiency, a sudden insufficiency of serum corticosteroids. A severe, life-threatening endocrine emergency.","More common with primary adrenal insufficiency, but can occur with secondary and tertiary.","Primary — The adrenal gland itself stops secreting cortisol (Addison's disease).","Secondary — ACTH deficiency, usually from conditions affecting the hypothalamus or pituitary.","Tertiary — Typically excessive glucocorticoid exposure.","Triggers","A sudden loss of the adrenal glands.","Increased stress in someone with underlying adrenal problems: surgery, infection, trauma, severe illness.","Sudden cessation of long-term steroid therapy, which is why clients stopping steroids are tapered.","Addisonian Crisis: Symptoms","Symptoms stem mainly from the lack of cortisol and aldosterone.","Sudden pain — Lower back, legs and abdomen; the lecture attributes it to hyponatremia from the lack of mineralocorticoids.","Severe vomiting and diarrhea — Lead to dehydration and hypotension; also tied to the mineralocorticoid deficiency.","CNS changes — Confusion, slurred speech, up to loss of consciousness.","Electrolyte imbalances — Hyperkalemia and hyponatremia, with their signs and symptoms. Put the client on an EKG and watch for dysrhythmias.","The lecture's video example shows shaking and decreased level of consciousness in a girl with no prior diagnosis: Addison's is often first diagnosed when the person has an Addisonian crisis, and family may not recognize it as an emergency.","Addisonian Crisis: Treatment","Untreated, it can be fatal. Rapidly replace fluid, salt and glucocorticoids, and give glucose.","Hydrocortisone IV — About a 100 mg initial bolus, then about 50 mg every 6 hours IV, until vital signs stabilize and the client can take food and medications orally. Hydrocortisone has both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid actions.","Normal saline — Replaces lost sodium: about 1 to 3 liters IV, or 5% dextrose in normal saline, based on volume status and urine output.","Once stable — Switch to PO medications, continue fluids, and taper to maintenance doses as the condition resolves."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]}];
